fn documentChunk(j: usize) -> StringView => match (j) {
    0 =>
        "# Nova Documentation — Milestone 0 / C0 Deliverable\n\nDate: 2026-10-22. Status: **M0/C0 complete; all decisions accepted ([ADR 0002](decisions/0002-milestone-0-decision-set.md)).**\n**M1 and C1 complete**: Nova compiles to C and runs, including natively inside CosmoOS on x86-64 and AArch64.\n**M2 (ownership and borrowing) and M3 (safe heap) complete** ([milestone-2.md](milestone-2.md), [milestone-3.md](milestone-3.md), [ADR 0003](decisions/0003-m3-built-in-generic-types.md)). **C2 complete** ([cosmoos/c2-report.md](cosmoos/c2-report.md)). **M4 (regions) complete** ([milestone-4.md](milestone-4.md), [ADR 0004](decisions/0004-m4-regions.md)). **M6 (generics and collections) complete** (2026-10-23): generics, interfaces, optionals, `StringView`, `Vector`, `String`, `SlotMap`, `StableVector`, property tests, and the [OD-3 design](od3-design.md) ([milestone-6.md](milestone-6.md), [ADR 0005](decisions/0005-m6-generics-and-built-in-collections.md)); **M6x (region parameters, OD-3) complete** ([milestone-6x.md](milestone-6x.md), [ADR 0006](decisions/0006-od3-region-parameters.md)). **C3 (collections and system calls) complete** ([cosmoos/c3-report.md](cosmoos/c3-report.md)). **M5 (escape and placement analysis) complete** ([milestone-5.md](milestone-5.md)). **M7 (production C backend) complete** (2026-10-24; [milestone-7.md](milestone-7.md)): source maps, structured C, the NIR interpreter, the random program generator, MSVC, and a 24-hour fuzz campaign with no miscompile. **C4 (freestanding compilation in CosmoOS) complete** ([cosmoos/c4-report.md](cosmoos/c4-report.md)). **C5 (kernel memory integration) complete** ([cosmoos/c5-report.md](cosmoos/c5-report.md)). **C6 (kernel components in Nova beside their C originals: LZ4, fbvalid) complete** ([cosmoos/c6-report.md](cosmoos/c6-report.md)), with C struct types imported from headers (type-system.md §31, [ADR 0008](decisions/0008-c-struct-bindings-from-headers.md)). **C7 (kernel concurrency) complete** (2026-10-25; [cosmoos/c7-report.md](cosmoos/c7-report.md), [ADR 0009](decisions/0009-c7-effects-and-embedded-c-state.md)): context effects, C state embedded by value, lock guards, kernel objects and grace periods (type-system.md §32–§34; `integrations/cosmoos/c7/`), and an in-kernel stress test under lockdep on both arches. **C8 (experimental module) complete** (2026-10-28): 1,000 load/unload cycles against CosmoOS itself, whose hook was merged as #457 ([cosmoos/c8-report.md](cosmoos/c8-report.md)). **CosmoOS roadmap 1.2 A5 in Nova complete** (2026-10-28): CPU time, nice values and a weighted fair share, written in Nova inside CosmoOS (#461, #476, #477; [cosmoos/a5-report.md](cosmoos/a5-report.md)). **M12 (tooling for AI agents) complete** (2026-10-26; [milestone-12.md](milestone-12.md)): JSON diagnostics with fixes, `novac fix`, `novac explain`, and `novac mcp`, an MCP server ([tooling.md](tooling.md) §5–§6). **C9 (gradual adoption)**: C9a, CosmoOS's GPT parser and writer in Nova, complete (2026-10-26; [cosmoos/c9-report.md](cosmoos/c9-report.md)), with C arrays in imported structs and exported constants ([ADR 0014](decisions/0014-c9-c-arrays-and-exported-constants.md)). **M9 (performance) complete** (2026-10-25; [milestone-9.md](milestone-9.md)): 9a, a benchmark suite of fifteen workloads in Nova, C, C++, Rust, Zig and Goose ([benchmarks.md](benchmarks.md)); 9b, checked bulk slice operations (`copyFrom`, `fill`); 9c, bounds-check elimination ([optimisations.md](optimisations.md), [ADR 0010](decisions/0010-m9c-pointers-from-references.md)); 9d, overflow-check elimination and the gcc gap; 9e, placement of non-escaping boxes on the stack by default; 9e′, one-pointer optional boxes and references, and boxes built in place. **M11 (language gaps) complete** (2026-10-26; [milestone-11.md](milestone-11.md)): nested patterns, `for` over non-`Copy` elements, borrow-checker precision for cursors, character literals, `wrapping*`/`checked*`/`saturating*` arithmetic, `String.appendNumber`, ",
    1 =>
        "`if` and `match` expressions, and references to optionals (`borrow (T?)`) are done; `f32`/`f64` (type-system.md §35), printed as the shortest text that reads back, are in (with `nbody`, a sixteenth suite workload), and so are function values (`fn(T) -> R`), closures (`(x) => x > limit`), effect lists on their types (`@effects() fn(i32)`, usable in atomic contexts) and library methods taking them (`sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all`, `retain`; §36, [ADR 0012](decisions/0012-m11-closures.md)); floats were accepted in [ADR 0011](decisions/0011-m11-floating-point.md). **M10 (tooling) complete** (2026-10-26; [milestone-10.md](milestone-10.md)): T1, the formatter `novac fmt` ([formatting.md](formatting.md)), T2, the documentation generator `novac doc` ([tooling.md](tooling.md) §1, [api/core.md](api/core.md)), and T3, the language server `novac lsp` with a VS Code extension (`editors/vscode`, [tooling.md](tooling.md) §2), T4, debugging with lldb and gdb ([tooling.md](tooling.md) §3), and T5–T6, packages: names across packages and `nova.toml` with path and git dependencies ([ADR 0013](decisions/0013-m10-modules-and-packages.md), [tooling.md](tooling.md) §4), are done. **M8 (systems and freestanding) complete** (2026-10-24; [milestone-8.md](milestone-8.md), [ADR 0007](decisions/0007-m8-systems-and-freestanding.md)): unsafe and raw pointers ([unsafe.md](unsafe.md)), the C FFI (type-system.md §28), the freestanding profile ([cosmoos/profiles.md](cosmoos/profiles.md) §4), and systems capabilities (`lib/core`, MMIO, C function pointers; type-system.md §29). **M13 (the documented gaps) complete** (2026-10-28; [milestone-13.md](milestone-13.md)): vector and string growth through `realloc` with a fast path; `dyn Interface` and owning closures (`move` literals, `Box<dyn fn(…) -> R>`; [ADR 0015](decisions/0015-m13-dynamic-dispatch-and-owning-closures.md)); userland threads with `Send`/`Share`, `Arc`, `Mutex` and atomics in the hosted library `lib/std` ([ADR 0016](decisions/0016-m13-userland-threads.md), type-system.md §38); field-precise closure captures, constant-index disjointness and `splitAtMut`; and a package registry with version solving and publishing ([ADR 0017](decisions/0017-m13-package-registry.md), [tooling.md](tooling.md) §4.6–§4.8).\nImplemented-feature specs: [grammar.md](grammar.md) (M1 grammar), [type-system.md](type-system.md)\n(M1 static semantics), [intermediate-representation.md](intermediate-representation.md) (HIR/NIR as\nimplemented) and [diagnostics.md](diagnostics.md) (error codes).\n\n## Master prompt §23 deliverables\n\n| § | Deliverable | Document |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Language vision | [vision.md](vision.md) |\n| B | Goose technical analysis | [research/goose.md](research/goose.md) (Goose commit `f605b5a`) |\n| — | Comparative research (Rust, Zig, Cyclone, Swift, Nim, Vale, escape analysis, regions, UB, fuzzing) | [research/comparative.md](research/comparative.md) |\n| C | Hybrid memory model, comparison of three architectures, recommendation | [memory-model.md](memory-model.md) |\n| D | Language syntax, 19 example programs, MVP vs future | [language-spec.md](language-spec.md) |\n| E | Formal safety design: invariants, rejection, runtime checks | [ownership.md](ownership.md) |\n| F | Compiler passes and IRs | [architecture.md](architecture.md), [c-backend.md](c-backend.md) |\n| G | Implementation language decision | [decisions/0001-compiler-implementation-language.md](decisions/0001-compiler-implementation-language.md) |\n| H | Repository structure | [architecture.md §6](architecture.md) |\n| I | Milestone roadmap | [roadmap.md](roadmap.md) |\n| J | Risk register | [risks.md](risks.md) |\n| K | Open architecture decisions | [open-decisions.md](open-decisions.md) |\n| L | First executable milestone | [milestone-1.md](milestone-1.md) |\n\n## CosmoOS prompt §12 deliverables\n\n| # | Deliverable | Document |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Integration feasibility report | [cosmoos/feasibility.md](cosmoos/feasibility.md) |\n| 2 | Rev",
    2 =>
        "ised Nova memory architecture | [memory-model.md](memory-model.md) + [cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md](cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md) |\n| 3 | Kernel and userland profile specs | [cosmoos/profiles.md](cosmoos/profiles.md) |\n| 4 | ABI compatibility report | [cosmoos/abi.md](cosmoos/abi.md) |\n| 5 | Allocator integration design | [cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md §2–3](cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md) |\n| 6 | Ownership and quiescence design | [cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md §4–7](cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md) |\n| 7 | Freestanding runtime design | [cosmoos/profiles.md §3.3](cosmoos/profiles.md) |\n| 8 | Compiler/backend integration design | [cosmoos/build-integration.md](cosmoos/build-integration.md) |\n| 9 | Security and correctness risk register | [risks.md](risks.md) |\n| 10 | Detailed milestone roadmap | [roadmap.md](roadmap.md) |\n| 11 | Acceptance-test matrix | [testing.md](testing.md) |\n| 12 | Recommendation for first integration | [cosmoos/feasibility.md](cosmoos/feasibility.md); result: [cosmoos/c1-report.md](cosmoos/c1-report.md) |\n\nPrimary-source audits: [research/cosmoos-kernel-audit.md](research/cosmoos-kernel-audit.md),\n[research/cosmoos-platform-audit.md](research/cosmoos-platform-audit.md)\n(CosmoOS HEAD `30d34b29`, main `4527d16d`; read-only).\n\nDesign records: [od3-design.md](od3-design.md) (lifetime-parameterised aggregates; implemented in M6x).\n\nUnsafe code (M8a): [unsafe.md](unsafe.md), the contract unsafe code must keep and what is checked.\n\n**M14 (proposed, 2026-10-29):** [milestone-14.md](milestone-14.md), what a kernel written in Nova needs from Nova, for\nCosmoOS's roadmap 2.0: global and per-CPU state, typed calls across compilation units, a kernel library, novac for a\nself-hosted build, hot-path performance and an outside audit.\n\n**M15 (proposed, 2026-10-30):** [milestone-15.md](milestone-15.md), what a compiler written in Nova (OD-33) needs from\nNova:\n- fixes for the five defects its probes found (#1–#5);\n- turning bytes into text;\n- the host services `novac build` uses.\n\nSafety audit pack: [safety-audit.md](safety-audit.md), the guarantees, where they are enforced, the riskiest code, and the\nfirst review's results (2026-10-29), for an independent audit.\n\nBenchmarks (M9): [benchmarks.md](benchmarks.md), the method, machines and toolchains of `benchmarks/suite/`; results in [milestone-9.md](milestone-9.md).\nOptimisations (M9, normative): [optimisations.md](optimisations.md), the preconditions and checks of each optimisation (bounds-check elimination).\n\nTooling (M10; [milestone-10.md](milestone-10.md)): [formatting.md](formatting.md), the canonical style of `novac fmt`; [tooling.md](tooling.md), the other commands (`novac doc`). The generated reference of `lib/core`: [api/core.md](api/core.md).\n\nC struct types (ADR [0008](decisions/0008-c-struct-bindings-from-headers.md)): imported from a C codebase's own headers (CosmoOS, Linux, the C library), never re-laid-out in Nova.\n\n## Topics without a file of their own\n\nAllocators and escape and placement analysis are specified in [memory-model.md](memory-model.md) (§3, §5), the runtime\nsurface in [c-backend.md](c-backend.md) §6, and the FFI in [type-system.md](type-system.md) §28 with `c-backend.md` §2.3.\nA topic gets its own file (`allocators.md`, `escape-analysis.md`, `runtime.md`, `ffi.md`) when it outgrows its section\n(master prompt §20).\n",
    3 =>
        "# lib/core\n\n- [struct PhysAddr](#struct-physaddr)\n- [struct VirtAddr](#struct-virtaddr)\n- [struct Layout](#struct-layout)\n- [interface Allocator](#interface-allocator)\n- [enum LoadOrdering](#enum-loadordering)\n- [enum StoreOrdering](#enum-storeordering)\n- [enum Ordering](#enum-ordering)\n- [struct AtomicU64](#struct-atomicu64)\n- [struct AtomicU32](#struct-atomicu32)\n- [struct AtomicUSize](#struct-atomicusize)\n- [struct AtomicBool](#struct-atomicbool)\n- [struct Volatile](#struct-volatile)\n- [struct MmioPtr](#struct-mmioptr)\n- [struct SliceHalves](#struct-slicehalves)\n- [struct SliceHalvesMut](#struct-slicehalvesmut)\n- [struct SliceSplitting](#struct-slicesplitting)\n- [struct SliceAlgorithms](#struct-slicealgorithms)\n\n## struct PhysAddr\n\n```nova\npublic struct PhysAddr\n```\n\nA physical address.\n\n### PhysAddr.of\n\n```nova\npublic fn of(bits: u64) -> PhysAddr\n```\n\nThe address whose value is `bits`.\n\n### PhysAddr.bits\n\n```nova\npublic fn bits(this) -> u64\n```\n\nThe address's value.\n\n### PhysAddr.offset\n\n```nova\npublic fn offset(this, n: u64) -> PhysAddr\n```\n\nThe address `n` bytes further on; panics if that overflows a `u64`.\n\n## struct VirtAddr\n\n```nova\npublic struct VirtAddr\n```\n\nA virtual address.\n\n### VirtAddr.of\n\n```nova\npublic fn of(bits: u64) -> VirtAddr\n```\n\nThe address whose value is `bits`.\n\n### VirtAddr.bits\n\n```nova\npublic fn bits(this) -> u64\n```\n\nThe address's value.\n\n### VirtAddr.offset\n\n```nova\npublic fn offset(this, n: u64) -> VirtAddr\n```\n\nThe address `n` bytes further on; panics if that overflows a `u64`.\n\n## struct Layout\n\n```nova\npublic struct Layout\n```\n\nThe size in bytes and the alignment (a power of two) of a block of memory.\n\n## interface Allocator\n\n```nova\npublic interface Allocator {\n    fn allocate(borrow this, layout: Layout) -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;\n    unsafe fn deallocate(borrow this, p: *mut u8, layout: Layout);\n}\n```\n\nAn allocator a `Box<T, A>` can use: a struct without fields that implements this (type-system.md §30.2).\n\n## enum LoadOrdering\n\n```nova\npublic enum LoadOrdering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Acquire,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic load (ADR 0021): C11's memory_order_relaxed, _acquire and _seq_cst.\n\n## enum StoreOrdering\n\n```nova\npublic enum StoreOrdering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Release,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic store: C11's memory_order_relaxed, _release and _seq_cst.\n\n## enum Ordering\n\n```nova\npublic enum Ordering {\n    Relaxed,\n    Acquire,\n    Release,\n    AcquireRelease,\n    SequentiallyConsistent,\n}\n```\n\nThe ordering of an atomic read-modify-write (swap, compare-exchange, add, subtract): C11's memory_order_relaxed,\n_acquire, _release, _acq_rel and _seq_cst. A compare-exchange that fails orders as a load: AcquireRelease as\nAcquire, Release as Relaxed, the others as themselves.\n\n## struct AtomicU64\n\n```nova\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicU64\n```\n\nA u64 that threads may read and change at once: every operation is atomic and sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicU64.create\n\n```nova\npublic fn create(value: u64) -> AtomicU64\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU64.constant\n\n```nova\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u64) -> AtomicU64\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static N: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64.constant(0);`.\n\n### AtomicU64.load\n\n```nova\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u64\n```\n\nSafety: the runtime accesses `value` only atomically, and safe code reaches it only through these methods.\n\n### AtomicU64.store\n\n```nova\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u64)\n```\n\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.swap\n\n```nova\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.compareExchange\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64) -> Result<u64, u64>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAdd\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, valu",
    4 =>
        "e: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nAdds, wrapping; returns the old value.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchSub\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: u64) -> u64\n```\n\nSubtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.loadWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u64\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.storeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.swapWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nswap, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.compareExchangeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u64, desired: u64, order: Ordering) -> Result<u64, u64>\n```\n\ncompareExchange, with an ordering; a failure orders as Ordering says.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchAddWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchAdd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU64.fetchSubWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u64, order: Ordering) -> u64\n```\n\nfetchSub, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicU32\n\n```nova\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicU32\n```\n\nA u32 that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64.\n\n### AtomicU32.create\n\n```nova\npublic fn create(value: u32) -> AtomicU32\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicU32.constant\n\n```nova\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: u32) -> AtomicU32\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4): `static N: AtomicU32 = AtomicU32.constant(0);`.\n\n### AtomicU32.load\n\n```nova\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.store\n\n```nova\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: u32)\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.swap\n\n```nova\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.compareExchange\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: u32, desired: u32) -> Result<u32, u32>\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAdd\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchSub\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: u32) -> u32\n```\n\nSafety: as AtomicU64.load.\n\n### AtomicU32.loadWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> u32\n```\n\nload, with an ordering (ADR 0021).\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.storeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nstore, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.swapWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nswap, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.compareExchangeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: u32, desired: u32, order: Ordering) -> Result<u32, u32>\n```\n\ncompareExchange, with an ordering; a failure orders as Ordering says.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchAddWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchAdd, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n### AtomicU32.fetchSubWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: u32, order: Ordering) -> u32\n```\n\nfetchSub, with an ordering.\nSafety: as load.\n\n## struct AtomicUSize\n\n```nova\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicUSize\n```\n\nA usize that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64 (usize is 64 bits on every target, §29.3).\n\n### AtomicUSize.create\n\n```nova\npublic fn create(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.constant\n\n```nova\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicUSize.load\n\n```nova\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> usize\n```\n\nThe value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSiz",
    5 =>
        "e.store\n\n```nova\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: usize)\n```\n\nStores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.swap\n\n```nova\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nStores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicUSize.compareExchange\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: usize, desired: usize) -> Result<usize, usize>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAdd\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nAdds, wrapping; returns the old value.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchSub\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize\n```\n\nSubtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\n\n### AtomicUSize.loadWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs load, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.storeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nAs store, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.swapWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs swap, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.compareExchangeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(\n    borrow this,\n    expected: usize,\n    desired: usize,\n    order: Ordering,\n) -> Result<usize, usize>\n```\n\nAs compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchAddWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchAdd, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicUSize.fetchSubWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize\n```\n\nAs fetchSub, with `order`.\n\n## struct AtomicBool\n\n```nova\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicBool\n```\n\nA bool that threads may read and change at once.\n\n### AtomicBool.create\n\n```nova\npublic fn create(value: bool) -> AtomicBool\n```\n\nA new atomic holding `value`.\n\n### AtomicBool.constant\n\n```nova\n@constant\npublic fn constant(value: bool) -> AtomicBool\n```\n\nThe same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n\n### AtomicBool.load\n\n```nova\npublic fn load(borrow this) -> bool\n```\n\nThe value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.store\n\n```nova\npublic fn store(borrow this, value: bool)\n```\n\nStores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.swap\n\n```nova\npublic fn swap(borrow this, value: bool) -> bool\n```\n\nStores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n\n### AtomicBool.compareExchange\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool) -> Result<bool, bool>\n```\n\nOk(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n\n### AtomicBool.loadWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> bool\n```\n\nAs load, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.storeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn storeWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: StoreOrdering)\n```\n\nAs store, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.swapWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn swapWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: Ordering) -> bool\n```\n\nAs swap, with `order`.\n\n### AtomicBool.compareExchangeWith\n\n```nova\npublic fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool, order: Ordering) -> Result<bool, bool>\n```\n\nAs compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n\n## struct Volatile\n\n```nova\n@noncopy\npublic struct Volatile<T: Copy>\n```\n\nA cell read and written only by volatile accesses: a hardware register, or memory shared with a device.\nNot Copy: a copy would read the register with an ordinary access, once, instead of each time it is used.\n\n### Volatile.read\n\n```nova\npublic fn read(borrow this) -> T\n```\n\nSafety: `this.value` is a live, aligned `T` (it is a field), and the read is a single volatile access.\n\n### Volatile.write\n\n```nova\npublic fn write(borrow mut this, v: T)\n```\n\nSafety: as `read`; `borrow mut this` makes the write exclusive.\n\n## struct MmioPtr\n\n```nova\n@noncopy\npublic struct MmioPtr<T>\n```\n",
    6 =>
        "\nThe address of a memory-mapped `T`, typically a `@repr(C)` struct of `Volatile` registers. Not Copy: two\ncopies would be two owners of the same registers.\n\n### MmioPtr.at\n\n```nova\npublic unsafe fn at(address: usize) -> MmioPtr<T>\n```\n\nSafety: `address` is mapped, aligned for `T`, and no other `MmioPtr` or reference aliases the registers.\n\n### MmioPtr.get\n\n```nova\npublic fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T\n```\n\nSafety: `at`'s contract makes `base` a live `T` reached only through this value; the result borrows `this`.\n\n### MmioPtr.getMut\n\n```nova\npublic fn getMut(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T\n```\n\nSafety: as `get`; `borrow mut this` makes the access exclusive.\n\n### MmioPtr.address\n\n```nova\npublic fn address(borrow this) -> usize\n```\n\nThe address of the registers, as given to `at`.\n\n## struct SliceHalves\n\n```nova\npublic struct SliceHalves<region r, T> {\n    public left: Slice<r, T>;\n    public right: Slice<r, T>;\n}\n```\n\nThe two parts of a slice that `splitAt` divides (type-system.md §16.5): the elements before the split, and from it on.\n\n## struct SliceHalvesMut\n\n```nova\npublic struct SliceHalvesMut<region r, T> {\n    public left: SliceMut<r, T>;\n    public right: SliceMut<r, T>;\n}\n```\n\nThe two parts of a mutable slice that `splitAtMut` divides: disjoint, so both may be written at once.\n\n## struct SliceSplitting\n\n```nova\npublic struct SliceSplitting<region r, T>\n```\n\nSplitting a slice in two (type-system.md §16.5): `s.splitAt(mid)` is `SliceSplitting<T>.splitAt(s, mid)`. The\nstruct's region ties the halves to the slice they come from; it has no fields and is never made.\n\n### SliceSplitting.splitAt\n\n```nova\npublic fn splitAt(s: Slice<r, T>, mid: usize) -> SliceHalves<r, T>\n```\n\nThe elements before `mid`, and from `mid` on; panics if `mid` is past the end.\n\n### SliceSplitting.splitAtMut\n\n```nova\npublic fn splitAtMut(s: SliceMut<r, T>, mid: usize) -> SliceHalvesMut<r, T>\n```\n\nAs splitAt, for a mutable slice: two mutable slices that do not overlap.\nSafety: slicing `s[mid..]` checks `mid` against the length (it panics past the end), so `[0, mid)` and\n`[mid, length)` are inside `s` and disjoint, and each is lent for as long as `s` is.\n\n## struct SliceAlgorithms\n\n```nova\npublic struct SliceAlgorithms<T>\n```\n\nThe algorithms over a slice of `T`, as static functions: the struct has no fields and is never made.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.sortBy\n\n```nova\n@effectsOf(cmp)\npublic fn sortBy(s: SliceMut<T>, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32)\n```\n\nOrders `s` so that `cmp` of each element and the next is at most zero, for a consistent `cmp`. Not stable.\nAn introsort: quicksort with a median-of-three pivot, insertion sort for short runs, and heapsort past a\ndepth of 2·log₂ n, so O(n log n) calls of `cmp` in the worst case. Allocates nothing; the recursion is on\nthe shorter side, so the stack is O(log n). An inconsistent `cmp` leaves some permutation of `s`.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.forEach\n\n```nova\n@effectsOf(visit)\npublic fn forEach(s: Slice<T>, visit: borrow mut fn(borrow T))\n```\n\nCalls `visit` on each element, in index order.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.any\n\n```nova\n@effectsOf(test)\npublic fn any(s: Slice<T>, test: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> bool\n```\n\nDoes `test` return true for some element? Stops at the first that does.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.all\n\n```nova\n@effectsOf(test)\npublic fn all(s: Slice<T>, test: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> bool\n```\n\nDoes `test` return true for every element? Stops at the first that does not.\n\n### SliceAlgorithms.retain\n\n```nova\n@effectsOf(keep)\npublic fn retain(s: SliceMut<T>, keep: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> usize\n```\n\nMoves the elements for which `keep` returns true to the front, in order, calling it once on each element in\nindex order; returns how many there are. `v.retain(keep)` then truncates the vector to that length.\n",
    7 =>
        "# Nova Compiler Architecture (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. Implementation language: C#/.NET 10 (ADR 0001,\npending approval). Normative semantics: `ownership.md`, `language-spec.md`.\n\n## 1. Pipeline\n\n```text\n source files\n     │  Lexer            → Token[] (struct array) + trivia; interned Symbols\n     │  Parser           → AST (lossless spans; error recovery)\n     ▼\n   AST ───── Name resolution   → DefIds, scopes, imports, visibility\n     │       Type collection   → struct/enum layouts (abstract), signatures\n     │       Type check        → typed HIR; kinds: Copy / Move / Scoped; unsafe ctx\n     ▼\n   HIR  (typed, desugared tree: `for`, `?`, `using`, `defer`, method calls,\n     │   auto-borrows and implicit reborrows made explicit)\n     │       Monomorphisation (M6) → concrete HIR instances\n     │       NIR build          → CFG\n     ▼\n   NIR  (place-based CFG, MIR-like; non-SSA)\n     │   1. definite-init / move check         (O2–O5)\n     │   2. borrow check (NLL regions)         (B1–B5, E1–E2, R1, R4)\n     │   3. effect check (kernel profile, C7)  (@noalloc/@nosleep/...)\n     │   4. drop elaboration                   (O4: static drops, drop flags)\n     │   5. runtime-check insertion            (bounds, overflow — explicit stmts)\n     │   6. escape / placement analysis (M5)   (optimisation only)\n     │   7. NIR optimisations (M9)             (each with preconditions + tests)\n     ▼\n   LIR  (C-shaped: C types resolved with TargetInfo, temporaries named,\n     │   evaluation order fixed, every UB-prone op replaced by a helper call)\n     │       C emitter → .c + .h per package (deterministic text)\n     ▼\n   C compiler (clang / gcc / CosmoOS cross-toolchain) → .o → link\n```\n\nSafety checking is complete before LIR. No later pass may introduce a borrow,\na move, or a drop that the checker has not seen. Optimisation passes on NIR (M9)\nmust preserve a recorded *drop schedule* and are validated by re-running the\nchecker on their output in debug builds (\"checker as validator\").\n\n## 2. Intermediate representations\n\n### 2.1 AST\nConcrete syntax with spans. Used for diagnostics, formatting (M10) and the language\nserver. Immutable class nodes in M1 (clarity first).\n\n### 2.2 HIR — typed high-level IR\n- Every expression carries a resolved `TypeId`. Every name carries a `DefId`.\n- Desugared constructs:\n  - `for x in s` becomes iterator `loop`/`match`;\n  - `e?` becomes `match` + `return`;\n  - `using`/`defer` become explicit scope-exit actions;\n  - method calls become path calls with explicit receiver mode;\n  - auto-borrow (`borrow`/`mut` arguments) becomes explicit `Borrow` nodes;\n  - implicit reborrows become explicit nodes.\n- *Why:* type checking needs tree structure and source correspondence.\n  Desugaring here means NIR construction has few constructs to handle.\n\n### 2.3 NIR — Nova IR (ownership IR)\nModelled on Rust MIR. A function body:\n\n```text\nlocals:   _0: ret, _1.._n: params, then user vars and temporaries (each with a type and a scope)\nblocks:   bb0..bbk, each: [statement]* + terminator\nstatements:\n  StorageLive(l) | StorageDead(l)\n  Assign(place, rvalue)\n  Drop(place)                    -- before elaboration: \"drop if init\"\n  CheckIndex(len_operand, idx_operand, span)   -- inserted in pass 5\n  Nop\nrvalues:\n  Use(operand) | Borrow(Shared|Mut|TwoPhaseMut, place) | BinOp(op, a, b)\n  | CheckedBinOp(op, a, b) | UnOp | Aggregate(kind, operands) | Cast(kind, operand, ty)\n  | Len(place) | Discriminant(place) | AddrOfRaw(place)\noperands: Copy(place) | Move(place) | Const(value)\nplaces:   local (. field | [index_local] | deref | downcast(variant))*\nterminators:\n  Goto(bb) | SwitchInt(operand, targets, otherwise) | Return | Unreachable\n  | Call(func, args, dest, next_bb) | Panic(kind, span)\n```\n\n*Why NIR supports ownership checking:* every move, copy, borrow, storage\nstart/end and drop is an explicit statement at a CFG point. Init analysis and\nloan liveness are ",
    8 =>
        "standard dataflow over blocks. Places make field-level\ndisjointness (B1) and partial moves (O2) exact.\n\n*Why it supports C generation:* a CFG with explicit temporaries maps 1:1 onto\nC labelled blocks and `goto`, so evaluation order is fixed by construction. Drop\nelaboration produces plain calls on edges, so cleanup on early return,\n`?` and `break` costs nothing extra in the backend.\n\nStorage representation: `Local[]`, `BasicBlock[]` and `Statement[]` stored in\nper-function arrays indexed by `int`. Dataflow bitsets are `ulong[]` per block.\n\n### 2.4 LIR — C-level IR\n- C types computed with `TargetInfo`: sizes and alignments are cross-checked\n  in the generated C with `_Static_assert(sizeof(T) == N)` and `offsetof`.\n- Nova arithmetic is mapped onto runtime helpers (`nova_add_i32_chk`, …) or onto\n  C operators only when proven UB-free (unsigned arithmetic, comparisons).\n- Each NIR block becomes a labelled C block. Locals hoist to the function top\n  with explicit initialisation where the C rules require it (`c-backend.md`).\n- Exists separately from NIR so that a future native backend or LLVM/QBE backend\n  can start from NIR while the C emitter stays dumb.\n\n## 3. Passes, inputs and outputs\n\n| # | Pass | Input → Output | Milestone | Key test corpus |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Lexer | text → tokens | M1 | `parse-pass`, `parse-fail` |\n| 2 | Parser (recursive descent + Pratt for expressions) | tokens → AST | M1 | precedence tables, recovery |\n| 3 | Name resolution | AST → resolved AST | M1 | `check-fail/name-*` |\n| 4 | Type check + HIR lowering | → HIR | M1 (prims), M2 (structs/refs), M6 (generics) | `check-pass/fail` |\n| 5 | NIR build | HIR → NIR | M1 | NIR snapshot tests (`--dump-nir`) |\n| 6 | Init/move check | NIR | M2 | `compile-fail/move-*` |\n| 7 | Borrow check | NIR | M2 | `compile-fail/borrow-*` |\n| 8 | Effect check | NIR + call graph | C7 | `compile-fail/effect-*` |\n| 9 | Drop elaboration | NIR → NIR | M2 | `run-pass/drop-order-*` |\n| 10 | Check insertion | NIR → NIR | M1 (overflow/div), M2 (bounds) | `run-fail/*` |\n| 11 | Placement analysis | NIR → annotations | M5 | `--explain-allocations` snapshots |\n| 12 | Optimisations | NIR → NIR | M9 | equivalence tests |\n| 13 | LIR lowering | NIR → LIR | M1 | — |\n| 14 | C emission | LIR → `.c/.h` | M1 | golden C + compile with `-Wall -Wextra -Werror -std=c11 -pedantic` |\n\nAll passes are deterministic. Ordered collections only. No reliance on `GetHashCode`\norder. Debug dumps (`--dump-hir`, `--dump-nir`, `--dump-lir`) are stable text\nformats, so snapshot tests are possible.\n\n## 4. Diagnostics\n\n- Error codes `N0001…`. Every code has a long explanation (`novac --explain N0123`),\n  as in rustc.\n- Borrow errors show three labelled spans: the loan, the conflicting access, and the later use.\n- Error recovery: parser recovery at statement or declaration boundaries. Type\n  errors produce an `Error` type that suppresses cascades. Borrow checking is\n  skipped for functions that fail type checking.\n- Machine-readable output `--diagnostics-format=json` for editors and CI.\n\n## 5. Command-line interface (proposed)\n\n```text\nnovac build [--profile userland|kernel|hosted] [--target x86_64-cosmoos|aarch64-cosmoos|host]\n            [--emit c|obj|exe] [-o out] [--cc <path>] [--cflags \"...\"]\nnovac check            # type + ownership only\nnovac build --explain-allocations | --allocation-report | --check-lifetimes   (M5)\nnovac test             # test runner over tests/corpus\nnovac --explain N0123\n```\n\n`--emit c` is the primary integration mode for CosmoOS: the CosmoOS build\ncompiles the generated C with its own toolchain and flags\n(`cosmoos/build-integration.md`). `--emit exe` drives the host C compiler for\nconvenience and testing.\n\n## 6. Repository structure\n\n```text\nNova/\n  Nova.slnx                    # .NET 10 solution format\n  Directory.Build.props        # warnings-as-errors, nullable, deterministic, banned-API analyzer\n  global.json                  # pinned .NET SDK\n  sr",
    9 =>
        "c/\n    Nova.Compiler/\n      Syntax/                  # Lexer, Token, Parser, AST, SourceText, Span\n      Semantics/               # Symbols, NameResolution, Types, TypeChecker, Kinds\n      Hir/                     # HIR nodes, lowering from AST\n      Nir/                     # NIR, builder, dataflow framework, printer\n      Analysis/                # InitCheck, BorrowCheck, Effects, DropElab, Checks, Placement\n      Lir/                     # LIR, lowering\n      Backend.C/               # CEmitter, Mangling, RuntimeABI\n      Target/                  # TargetInfo (x86_64/aarch64 LP64, host), Profiles\n      Diagnostics/             # Diagnostic, codes, renderers (text/json)\n    Nova.Cli/                  # `novac` entry point\n  runtime/\n    include/nova/rt.h          # freestanding core: types, checked-arith helpers, panic hook decl\n    core/                      # freestanding C (no libc; may call memcpy/memmove/memset, profiles.md §4.4)\n    hosted/                    # libc-backed: panic=abort, SystemHeap=malloc, Console\n    cosmoos/                   # CosmoOS userland glue (C1); kernel adapter (C5)\n  lib/\n    core/                      # Nova sources: Option, Result, Slice, Box, Allocator, ...  (freestanding)\n    std/                       # hosted: String, Vector, Console, IO\n  tests/\n    Nova.Compiler.Tests/       # xUnit unit tests per pass\n    corpus/\n      parse-pass/ parse-fail/ check-pass/ check-fail/\n      compile-fail/            # ownership/borrow negatives, each with `// error: N....` annotations\n      run-pass/ run-fail/      # expected stdout / expected panic kind + exit code\n    Nova.TestRunner/           # corpus runner; compiles generated C with clang AND gcc, ASan+UBSan\n  benchmarks/                  # M9: Nova vs C/C++/Rust/Zig/Goose, harness + methodology\n  examples/\n  integrations/\n    cosmoos/                   # Makefile fragment, QEMU test glue, port recipe (not in the CosmoOS tree until approved)\n  docs/\n```\n\nRationale: the compiler, runtime C and Nova libraries are versioned together\nbecause the runtime ABI between them is internal and unstable until M7. The\n`integrations/cosmoos` directory keeps every CosmoOS-specific artefact out of\nthe CosmoOS repository until the integration interface is approved\n(`prompts/cosmoos-native.md` §1).\n",
    10 =>
        "# Benchmarks — method and results\n\nStatus: **M9a baseline** (2026-10-25); `memory-copy` corrected in M9b (its blocks are permuted, suite README). Master prompt §19 (what to compare and measure) and §22 rule 3 (no performance\nclaim without a reproducible benchmark). The suite is `benchmarks/suite/` (its README defines every workload and\nbuild); `benchmarks/lz4/` and `benchmarks/fbvalid/` are the C6 component benchmarks, with their own READMEs.\n\n## 1. What is compared\n\nEach of the sixteen workloads (fifteen from M9a, and `nbody` from M11 G7d) is written once per language with the **same algorithm and data structures** (the\nsuite README's table), and prints one checksum, which `run.py` compares with the expected value before it records a\ntime. A program that computes something else is a failed run, not a fast one.\n\n| Configuration | What it is | Checks at run time |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nova` | `novac build`, its C compiled at `-O2` (M5 placement off, the default) | bounds, overflow, null unwrap, allocation failure |\n| `c` | `cc -O2 -std=c11` | none |\n| `cpp` | `c++ -O2 -std=c++20`, the standard library's containers and `unique_ptr` | none |\n| `rust` | `rustc -C opt-level=2 -C codegen-units=1` (a release build) | bounds |\n| `rust-checked` | the same with `-C overflow-checks=on` | bounds, overflow: what Nova checks |\n| `zig` | `zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast -lc`, `std.heap.c_allocator` | none |\n| `zig-safe` | `-O ReleaseSafe` | bounds, overflow, null unwrap: what Nova checks |\n| `goose` | `goose build -O2` (commit `94cc64d`) | bounds, always, with its own elimination; signed overflow wraps in a release build (Goose `docs/reference.md`, Overflow and Bounds checks) |\n\n`nova`, `c`, `cpp` and `goose` go through the same C compiler, named by `--cc`; Rust and Zig use their own LLVM.\nThat is part of what is measured: a language that compiles to C inherits that C compiler's strengths and weaknesses\n(C6b's fbvalid ran at 1.76× C's time under gcc 12 and equal to C under clang).\n\nGoose has no heap. Three workloads that free objects one at a time have no Goose version, and in three more Goose's\nnodes share one pool per list or tree (suite README). Those rows compare memory models, not allocators.\n\n## 2. What is measured\n\n| Metric | How | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wall time | `measure.c`: `posix_spawn` to `wait4`, `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`; one discarded run, then the median of `--runs` runs (default 5); the minimum and maximum are kept in the JSON | process start-up is included, under 2 ms here; every workload runs for about 0.4 s or more |\n| Peak resident memory | `ru_maxrss` from `wait4` in `measure.c` | measured from a small launcher, because Linux carries the forking process's peak across `exec` (a Python parent showed 13 MiB for every program) |\n| Allocations | `allocount.c`, preloaded (`DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES`, `LD_PRELOAD`), in a separate run: calls to `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `posix_memalign`, `aligned_alloc`; bytes requested; peak live heap in usable bytes (`malloc_size`, `malloc_usable_size`) | includes what the C library and the language runtime allocate before `main` (about 60 on macOS, 1–10 on Linux); Goose's data stacks are `mmap`'d and not counted |\n| Compile time | wall time of the build command, with each toolchain's global caches warm (one discarded build first); Zig's per-program cache emptied before each build | Nova's includes its C compile; Rust's and Zig's include linking their standard libraries |\n| Binary size | the executable's size on disk, not stripped | Rust and Zig link their standard library statically |\n\n## 3. Machines\n\n| Name | CPU | OS | C compilers | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Apple M1, 8 cores (4 performance) | macOS 27 (Darwin 27.0.0) | Apple clang 21.0.0 | development machine, shared with other work: used for checksums, not published times |\n| Ryzen | AMD Ryzen 5 PRO 3600, 6 cores, 12 threads | Debian 12, Linux 6.1 | clang 18.1.8, gcc 12.2.0 | shared server: runs under `nice`, load recorded with eac",
    11 =>
        "h run |\n| Windows VM | Apple M1 (Parallels VM on the development machine), 4 virtual processors | Windows 11 Pro (ARM64) | MSVC 19.51 (ARM64) | MSVC only (§4.1); shares the M1 with other work, so its times are compared with its own C only |\n\nToolchains: rustc 1.99.1; Zig 0.17.0 (official tarballs, sha256 checked); Goose `94cc64d` built from source.\n\n## 4. Reproducing\n\n```sh\ndotnet build src/Nova.Cli -c Release\npython3 benchmarks/suite/run.py --cc clang --runs 5 --json out/benchmarks/suite/clang.json\npython3 benchmarks/suite/run.py --cc gcc --runs 5 --json out/benchmarks/suite/gcc.json\n```\n\n`--zig`, `--goose`, `--rustc` and `--novac` name the toolchains (a missing one is skipped and reported);\n`--langs` and `--workloads` select rows. `--nova-flag=--no-bce` (repeatable) passes an option to `novac`, to measure an optimisation with its\nswitch off (`optimisations.md` §1); `benchmarks/lz4/run.py` and `benchmarks/fbvalid/run.py` take it too.\n\n### 4.1 MSVC (M9f)\n\n`run.py` needs POSIX (`measure.c` launches with `posix_spawn` and reads `wait4`'s peak memory; the allocation counter\nis an `LD_PRELOAD`/`DYLD` interposer), so MSVC has its own runner, `benchmarks/suite/run_msvc.py`, for Windows:\n\n```bat\ncall \"C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft Visual Studio\\18\\Community\\VC\\Auxiliary\\Build\\vcvarsall.bat\" arm64\ndotnet build src\\Nova.Cli -c Release\npython benchmarks\\suite\\run_msvc.py --runs 5 --json out\\benchmarks\\msvc.json\n```\n\n- Nova: `novac build --emit c`, then `cl /nologo /std:c11 /O2 /link /STACK:8388608` (the stack of `c-backend.md` §1.1).\n  C: `cl /nologo /std:c11 /O2` on the same `main.c` as elsewhere.\n- The same method otherwise: one discarded run, then the median of `--runs` wall times (Python's `perf_counter`,\n  around `subprocess.run`), and every run's output must equal the workload's checksum.\n- Not measured: peak memory, allocations and compile time. Only Nova and C are compared; the other languages'\n  toolchains are not on the Windows machine.\n- Under MSVC, `rt.h` uses no compiler builtins (`c-backend.md` §1.1): checked arithmetic is the portable code.\n  The MSVC row measures that too.\n\n## 5. Results\n\nSee `docs/milestone-9.md` (results), which records each run with its date, machine, load and the commit measured. The\nacceptance run at the end of M9 is there too (9f): the whole suite in every language, each optimisation's share\n(`benchmarks/suite/attribute.py`), and the MSVC row (§4.1).\n",
    12 =>
        "# C Backend Design (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. Applies from M1. CosmoOS-specific flags are covered in\n`cosmoos/abi.md` and `cosmoos/build-integration.md`.\n\n## 1. Target dialect\n\n- **C11** (ISO/IEC 9899:2011), compiled with `-std=c11` (or `gnu11` where the\n  host project requires it). Generated code must also compile under C17.\n- Headers used by generated code: **only** freestanding headers (`<stdint.h>`,\n  `<stddef.h>`, `<stdbool.h>`, `<stdalign.h>`, `<limits.h>`, `<stdnoreturn.h>`)\n  plus `nova/rt.h`. Everything hosted lives behind runtime functions.\n- Compiler-specific features are allowed only through macros in `nova/rt.h`\n  with a portable fallback: `__builtin_*_overflow`, `__builtin_expect`,\n  `__attribute__((noreturn, cold))`, `_Static_assert`.\n- Supported compilers: clang ≥ 15 (CosmoOS's floor; its CI uses Debian clang 19.1.7)\n  and gcc ≥ 11 for hosted builds. **CosmoOS profiles are clang/lld only**\n  (`research/cosmoos-platform-audit.md` §3). MSVC is best-effort for the hosted profile\n  (C11 mode, VS 2022), without the kernel profile.\n- The generated C must compile warning-free under\n  `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wshadow -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Werror`\n  **and** under CosmoOS's own warning set (`-Wcast-qual -Wmissing-prototypes\n  -Wstrict-prototypes -Wvla -Wundef -Wimplicit-fallthrough`, `-Wframe-larger-than=2048`\n  for the kernel; see `cosmoos/abi.md`). All non-exported functions are `static`, and every\n  exported one has a prototype in the generated header.\n- Correct both with and without `-fno-strict-aliasing`. CosmoOS compiles with it,\n  but Nova does not rely on it.\n- **No floating-point contraction or fast-math** (M11 G7, type-system.md §35.6). `rt.h` turns contraction off where\n  a pragma can (clang, MSVC) and requires `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0`, in programs that use floats (`NV_FLOATS`; a\n  program without floats compiles under `-mgeneral-regs-only`); builds pass `-ffp-contract=off` to clang and gcc\n  and never `-ffast-math` or its parts, so each float operation rounds once, as in the interpreter.\n- **No compiler-rt / libgcc.** CosmoOS links no builtins library, so generated code\n  must never need one. That rules out `__int128` division, `long double` arithmetic,\n  16-byte atomics and, in AArch64 userland, outline-atomic helpers (to be verified in C1;\n  pass `-mno-outline-atomics` if needed). The runtime header supplies portable\n  fallbacks instead of builtins when a builtin would lower to a libcall.\n- `-nostdinc` builds: freestanding headers come from `-isystem $(clang\n  -print-resource-dir)/include` (CosmoOS invariant B2). Generated code must not include libc headers\n  directly; only `nova/rt*.h` does, and only in hosted and userland profiles.\n- **Runtime per profile (M8c, `cosmoos/profiles.md` §4.2).** Hosted programs get `nova/rt.h`, the\n  core runtime `nova_rt_core.c`, and the platform layer `nova_rt_hosted.c` / `nova_rt_io.c` (C library\n  calls). A hosted program that prints a float also gets `nova_rt_float.c`, the float formatter and its table\n  (type-system.md §35.7; M11 G7b). Freestanding programs get `nova/rt.h` and `nova_rt_core.c` compiled with `NV_FREESTANDING`\n  (a `#define` at its top), which leaves out everything that needs a platform service; the\n  environment defines `nova_panic`. Freestanding builds add `-ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector\n  -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables` (the stack protector's `__stack_chk_*` symbols\n  come from the C library; unwind tables are useless with panic = abort and have no unwinder to read\n  them in a kernel).\n- Plain C `char` is **never** used for Nova data (`char` is signed on x86-64 and\n  unsigned on AArch64; CosmoOS's `limits.h` is also wrong about it on AArch64). Bytes are `uint8_t`.\n\n## 1.1 MSVC, best effort (M7e)\n\n`novac build --cc cl` uses it, and so does `novac build` on Windows when no clang or gcc is found (`tooling.md` §8).\nThe hosted profile also builds with Microsoft's C compiler: `cl /std:c11 /W3 /WX`, checked",
    13 =>
        " on\nx64 (CI) and ARM64 (a Windows 11 VM). Programs are linked with an 8 MiB stack\n(`/link /STACK:8388608`), as Linux and macOS give the main thread. Windows' default is 1 MiB, which\n`run-pass/deep-recursion.nova` overflows. This is\n**best effort**: no CosmoOS profile uses MSVC, and the strict warning set (§1) is checked with clang and\nGCC only. What differs:\n\n- **`rt.h`** uses compiler builtins only behind a test for GCC or clang, with portable code otherwise:\n  - checked arithmetic falls back to the portable overflow checks, as with `NV_NO_BUILTIN_OVERFLOW`;\n  - counting leading zeros (stable vectors) uses `nv_clz64`, a shift loop off GCC and clang;\n  - `NV_UNUSED` and `NV_COLD` are empty;\n  - `nv_max_align_t` stands for C11's `max_align_t`, which MSVC's `<stddef.h>` lacks in C mode.\n    There it is `double`. The generated alignment assertions use it.\n- **Process behaviour** (`nova_rt_hosted.c`, under `_WIN32`):\n  - standard output and standard error are switched to binary mode at the first write, so\n    `\"\\n\"` stays one byte;\n  - a panic ends with exit status 134 (`_exit(134)` after flushing), the status `abort()` gives\n    on Linux and macOS. Windows' own `abort()` would give 3.\n- **I/O** (`nova_rt_io.c`, under `_WIN32`):\n  - program arguments work as elsewhere;\n  - files use the C runtime's `_open`, `_read`, `_write`, `_close` and `_unlink` in binary mode;\n  - `Process.run` uses `_spawnvp(_P_WAIT, …)`;\n  - **TCP is not supported**: after the same address check as elsewhere, every socket call fails\n    with `ENOSYS` (`IoError.Other`).\n- **Test.** `MsvcTests` checks three things:\n  - every run-pass and run-fail program builds with MSVC and gives the corpus expectations;\n  - `nwc` reads files and standard input;\n  - a program runs `cmd /c exit 7` with `Process.run`, sees a missing program fail, and sees TCP\n    reported as unsupported.\n\n  No corpus program needs an exception: `io-errors.nova` passes only invalid addresses, which fail\n  the address check first. The `test-windows` CI job runs the tests on x64. Locally, a Windows 11\n  ARM64 VM (MSVC 19.51) runs them. On other hosts they return early.\n\n## 2. Lowering of types\n\n| Nova | C |\n|---|---|\n| `i8..i64`, `u8..u64` | `int8_t..int64_t`, `uint8_t..uint64_t` |\n| `isize`/`usize` | the fixed-width type of the target's pointer width (`int64_t`/`uint64_t` on LP64), plus `_Static_assert(sizeof(void *) == 8)`. `ptrdiff_t`/`size_t` are used at FFI boundaries (M8) |\n| `bool` | `bool` (`_Bool`) |\n| `char` | `uint32_t` |\n| `borrow fn(T…) -> R` (M11 G8b) | `const nvCK_<mangle> *`, a pointer to the first member, the code pointer `R (*)(const void *, T…)`, of an environment `nvCE_<id>` that holds a pointer per capture; the body is `nvCB_<id>`, which converts `env` back through `uintptr_t`; a call is `(*f)(f, args…)` (type-system.md §36.2) |\n| `borrow dyn I`, `borrow mut dyn I`, `Box<dyn I, A>` (M13 G2) | `nvDR_<D…>` = `struct { void *data; const nvDT_<D…> *vt; }` (16 bytes; the dyn type mangles as `D<length><interface><arguments>`, `dyn Shape` → `D5Shape0`), typedef'd before every struct, so any struct may hold one. Not a niche optional: `Box<dyn I>?` has a tag. The table type `nvDT_…` is defined after the structs: `drop` (NULL if `T` needs none), `size`, `align`, then `m0…` in the interface's method order, each taking `void *` for the receiver. Per (`T`, `I`) pair that a coercion names: `static const nvDT_… nvVT_<T>_<D…>`, with adapters `nvVT_…_<slot>` that convert the address back through `uintptr_t` and call `T`'s method, and `nvVT_…_drop` over `T`'s drop glue. A coercion is `((nvDR_…){ .data = (void *)(uintptr_t)p, .vt = &nvVT_… })`; a call through `x` is `x.vt->m<slot>(x.data, args…)`; borrowing `*x` is `x` itself. `Box<dyn I, A>`'s drop glue calls `vt->drop` and frees `data` with `A` (a user allocator's `deallocate` gets the table's size and alignment) (type-system.md §37.4). **`dyn fn`** (M13 G3, §36.5) mangles as `DF` (`DG` for `dyn mut fn`), it",
    14 =>
        "s effect list and its signature; its table's one entry `m0` is a move literal's body, whose `self` is the boxed environment `nvCE_<id>` (the code pointer, then the captured values; drop glue `nvD_CE<id>_`) |\n| `fn(T…) -> R` (M11 G8a) | a typedef `nvN_<mangle>` of a pointer to the functions' C signature; a value is a function's address, a call an indirect call (type-system.md §36.1) |\n| `f32`/`f64` | `float`/`double` (`rt.h` checks the IEEE formats; literals by their bits through `nv_f32_from_bits`/`nv_f64_from_bits`; float→integer conversions through range-checked helpers; type-system.md §35). The CosmoOS kernel forbids floats (`cosmoos/profiles.md`; `--no-float` in G7c) |\n| `struct S` | `typedef struct nvT_S nvT_S;` then `struct nvT_S { … }` with fields `f_<name>` in declaration order, structs emitted after the structs they contain. Each struct is followed by `_Static_assert`s on `sizeof`, `_Alignof` and every field's `offsetof`, against `TypeTable.LayoutOf`. An empty struct gets a `uint8_t nv_empty` member (C has no empty structs). `@repr(C)` is the default in v0; field reordering is an M9 opt-in (`@repr(auto)`) |\n| `Box<T, A>` with a user allocator (C5, type-system §30) | `T *` as below; `nvB_` calls `A`'s `allocate` method with `{ sizeof(T), _Alignof(T) }` (a `{ 0 }` value of the field-less allocator struct as `this`), and the drop glue and `BoxFree` call `nvBF_<box>`, which calls `deallocate` with the same layout |\n| `Box<T, A>` (M3b) | `T *` (a shared reference to a box is `T *const *`). Drop glue `nvD_X<T><A>` destroys `*p`, then calls `nova_free(p, NV_ALLOC_SYSTEM \\| NV_ALLOC_FAILING)`. `Box.create`/`createIn` call a per-type helper `nvB_X<T><A>(T v)`: `nova_alloc(sizeof(T), id)`; on NULL it destroys `v` and returns `Err(OutOfMemory)`, otherwise it stores `v` and returns `Ok(p)`. It asserts `_Alignof(T) <= _Alignof(max_align_t)`. **Built in place** (M9e′): when the argument is a temporary assigned by the statement just before, by a construction (an array repeat, an array, a struct or enum aggregate), and used nowhere else, and `T` needs no drop and holds no C state, the site calls `nvBA_X<T><A>()` (the allocation alone: `p` or NULL) and then writes the construction into `*p`, with no temporary and no by-value copy. The construction's operands are already evaluated, so it cannot panic or allocate, and the order of allocation and construction is unobservable; `T` has no drop, so a failed allocation leaves nothing to destroy. A stack-placed box (§2.2) is likewise built in its slot |\n| Deep boxes (M3c) | If destroying `T` ends, as its last step, in a `Box<T>` (followed through struct fields and enum variants' last payloads), the glue of `Box<T>` is a loop: destroy the node except that tail (`next = tail pointer`), `nova_free` the node, continue with `next` (type-system.md §18.5). `Unpack` becomes one assignment per target; `BoxFree` is `nova_free` |\n| `Arena` (M4a) | `nv_arena` from `nova/rt.h` (40 bytes, asserted there), created by `nova_arena_create(allocator, chunk)`, dropped by `nova_arena_drop`. A shared reference to a region is a plain `nv_arena *`: allocation mutates the arena through a shared borrow. `alloc`/`allocArray` call per-type helpers `nvAA_<T>`/`nvAR_<T>`, which bump-allocate with `nova_arena_alloc` (chunks come from `nova_alloc`, so they are counted and fault-injectable) and return the scoped result. Element names: `P`/`Q` + T for shared/mutable references, `U` + T for `[T]`, `G<len><Name>` for regions |\n| `FixedBufferAllocator`, `ScratchFrame` (M4b) | `nv_fba` (24 bytes) and `nv_scratch` (32 bytes) from `nova/rt.h`. Allocation helpers take `(void *region, uint32_t kind, …)` and call `nova_region_alloc`, which dispatches to the arena, the fixed buffer, or the runtime's scratch stack (heap chunks via `nova_alloc`; a frame's drop frees the chunks newer than its entry state). `Scratch.enter()` is `nova_scratch_enter_root(loc)`, which panics if a frame is live; `frame.enter()` is `nova_scratch_",
    15 =>
        "enter_child`. A frame's borrow-checker parts (`memory`, `allocation`) are places of the frame itself in C |\n| `Pool<T>`, `PoolBox<T>` (M4c) | `nv_pool` (48 bytes; `nova_pool_create`/`nova_pool_take`/`nova_pool_release`/`nova_pool_drop`; slots of one size, allocated at the first take, with a free list through them). `PoolBox<T>` is `nvPB_<T>` = `struct { T *ptr; nv_pool *pool; }`, and its contents are `(*b.ptr)`. Its drop glue `nvD_Y<T>` destroys `*ptr`, then releases the slot; `BoxFree` of a partially moved pool box releases only. `pool.take` calls `nvPT_<T>(pool, v)`, which destroys `v` when no slot is free |\n| Interfaces (M6b) | No runtime representation: dispatch is static. An `impl` block's methods are methods of their type, named like other members (`impl Comparable<i32> for i32` → `nvM_3i32_compare`). Calls through a bound in generic code exist only in generic versions, which are not emitted; each instantiation calls the concrete method |\n| Generic code (M6a) | Generic versions are type-, move- and borrow-checked but never emitted. Each instantiation is a separate C type or function. Struct and enum instances are `nvT_`/`nvE_` + `<len><Base><count><args>`, using the element codes (`Pair<i32>` → `nvT_4Pair1i32`; `S4Pair1i32` inside other names). Generic function instances are `nvG_<len><name><count><args>` (`id<Pair<i32>>` → `nvG_2id1S4Pair1i32`). Members of an instance are `nvM_<mangled owner>_<name>`. Every non-generic form starts with a letter or a length, so the forms cannot collide |\n| `Result<T, E>` (M3a) | like an enum, named `nvR_<T><E>` with the element codes below (`Result<Res, Code>` → `nvR_S3ResE4Code`, `void` → `v`). Its drop glue is `nvD_R<T><E>`. `e!` on `Err` calls `nova_panic(NV_PANIC_UNWRAP, loc)` |\n| `enum E { A, B(T) }` | `struct { uint32_t tag; union { T B; } u; }`. The tag width is the smallest unsigned that fits, unless `@repr(u8)` etc. A value without a payload is `((E){ .tag = n })`. A value with a payload of scalars and field-less enums is a compound literal too. A variant whose payload may hold a pointer (anything else) has a constructor `static inline nvMk_<E>_v_<variant>(payload…)`, defined after the type, which zero-initialises the value and stores the tag and each payload field; its values are built by it, in the helpers too. Clang's static analyzer loses a pointer read back from a union built by a compound literal, so a `Box`'s allocation looked leaked (M14, ADR 0025 §7). The constructors are kept off scalar payloads because gcc inlined less around them (`string-building` ran 1.8× slower with one on its cold growth path, inferred from inlining). Static initialisers (§39) keep compound literals, which C requires there |\n| `T?` (M6c) | like an enum with variants `null` (tag 0) and `Some` (tag 1, field `f__0`), named `nvO_<T>` with the element codes (`i32?` → `nvO_i32`; `O<T>` inside other names). A niche optional (type-system §22.1, M9e′) is `struct nvO_<T> { T f__0; }`: its tag is `(o.f__0 != NULL)`, `Some`'s payload is `o.f__0`, and `null` is `{ .f__0 = NULL }`; drop glue `nvD_O<T>`. `o!` on `null` calls `nova_panic(NV_PANIC_UNWRAP_NULL, loc)`; `o == null` compares the tag with 0. The niche layout for `Box` and references is future work (type-system.md §22.1) |\n| `Vector<T, A>`, `String<A>` (M6d) | `nvV_<[T] or Z><A>` = `struct { T *ptr; uint64_t len; uint64_t cap; }` (24 bytes, asserted), element code `V<U<T> or Z><A>` (`Vector<i32>` → `nvV_Ui32S10SystemHeap`). An empty vector points to the runtime's `nova_vec_empty` (a `max_align_t` object, never dereferenced) with `cap == 0`, so slices of it never do arithmetic on NULL. Growth is `nova_vec_grow` (doubling, at least 4; a first buffer is allocated, a later one resized with `nova_realloc`, which keeps the old bytes; NULL with nothing changed on overflow or allocation failure). Push and append call it only when the buffer is full, and pass the capacity through a local, so the vector's address does not escape the helper",
