        "16, and an `unsafe main` is N0219. (An `unsafe` interface method was N0216 until C5.)\n\n### Results and the heap (N0260–N0269; `type-system.md` §17–§18)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0260 | `Ok(...)` / `Err(...)` (or, since M4c, `Pool.create(...)`; since M6c, `null`) without an expected type that fixes its type arguments, or where another type is expected | `let a = Ok(1);`, `let p = Pool.create(SystemHeap {}, 4u32);`, `let n = null;` |\n| N0261 | `?` in a function that does not return a `Result` with the same error type; `?` on an optional in a function that does not return an optional (M6c) | `?` on `Result<i32, F>` in a function returning `Result<i32, E>` |\n| N0262 | `?` or `!` on something that is not a `Result` or optional value (a reference to one included) | `n?` with `n: i32` |\n| N0263 | An allocator argument that is not an allocator type (`SystemHeap`, `FailingAllocator`, or for `Box` since C5 a struct without fields implementing `Allocator`) | `Box<i32, Point>`, `Box.createIn(point, 1)` |\n| N0265 | Type arguments that cannot be inferred: a generic call, struct literal or enum value whose arguments, fields or expected type do not determine every type parameter | `let d = Maybe.Nothing;`, `let e = make();` |\n| N0266 | Generic instantiation nested more than 64 deep (polymorphic recursion) | `fn deep<T>(x: Maybe<T>) { deep(Maybe.Just(x)); }` |\n| N0267 | An `impl` that does not match its interface (receiver, parameter or return type, a missing or extra method), a duplicate `impl`, an `impl` for a generic or scoped type, an interface method without a receiver, or a method call that two bounds both provide | `impl Shape for Square { fn area(this) -> i64 … }` |\n| N0268 | A type argument that does not satisfy a bound of its type parameter (an interface it does not implement, or `Copy`) | `total(plain)` with `fn total<S: Shape>(…)` |\n| N0264 | A type a region cannot hold (R2): one that needs drop, a scoped type, or a non-Copy fill value for `allocArray` | `arena.alloc(Res { id: 1 })`, `arena.alloc(someReference)` |\n\nGeneric code (M6a) reuses codes:\n- an operator on a type parameter is N0207, a field or method on one is N0234, and a literal for\n  one is N0205;\n- a generic type without its arguments, or with the wrong number of them, is N0204;\n- a duplicate or shadowing type parameter is N0201;\n- a method with type parameters of its own is N0216;\n- a generic `main` is N0219;\n- naming something that is not an interface as a bound or in an `impl` is N0204;\n- a method on a type parameter that no bound declares is N0234;\n- an `impl` method clashing with a type's own method is N0228.\n\nRegion values reuse codes:\n- assigning a `using` binding as a whole is N0211;\n- nesting a frame that is not a `using` binding is N0248;\n- a method call on a frame that is not a local is N0247;\n- a storage that is not `[u8; N]` or `SliceMut<u8>` is N0205;\n- returning a frame or fixed-buffer allocator without sources is N0245.\n\n`?` inside a deferred statement is N0254, and `Ok`/`Err` with the wrong number of values is\nN0209. The names `Result`, `Ok`, `Err`, `AllocError`, `Box`, `SystemHeap` and `FailingAllocator`\ncannot be declared (N0201). Moving out of a box's contents is N0251 until M3c.\n\n### Build (N0300–N0399)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0300 | `novac build --emit exe` without a `main` function | a library-only file |\n\nNames across packages (N0310–N0319; `type-system.md` §2, ADR 0013):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0310 | The package named in an import, or in a struct literal `p.T { … }`, is not a dependency of the package (elsewhere an unknown `p` in `p.x` is N0200) | `import geometri;` |\n| N0311 | A dependency has no item of that name | `geometry.Pointt` |\n| N0312 | The item exists but is not `public`, or a field or method of a public struct is not | `geometry.helper()` with `fn helper` internal |\n| N0313 | A simple name is provided by two imports | `import a; import b;` both with ",
    107 =>
        "`Point`, then `Point` |\n| N0314 | A public item's signature names a type that other packages cannot see | `public fn make() -> Hidden` with `struct Hidden` internal |\n| N0315 | An item, parameter, local, loop variable or pattern binding has the name of one of the package's dependencies | `let geometry = 1;` |\n\nPackages (N0320–N0329; `tooling.md` §4, ADR 0013):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0320 | `nova.toml` or `nova.lock` is not valid in the TOML subset | `name = app` (no quotes) |\n| N0321 | A manifest field is missing, unknown or invalid, or a dependency is an executable | `kind = \"binary\"` |\n| N0322 | A path dependency's directory or manifest is missing, or its name differs from its key | `geometry = { path = \"../geo\" }` with no `../geo/nova.toml` |\n| N0323 | A git dependency cannot be fetched or resolved, or is absent with `--offline` | an unknown tag |\n| N0324 | The lock file is missing or does not match the manifest | a dependency added without `novac fetch` |\n| N0325 | A git checkout's manifest and sources do not match the lock's hash | a file edited in the cache |\n| N0326 | One package name reached through two different sources | two paths to two `geometry` packages |\n| N0327 | A dependency cycle | `a` depends on `b`, `b` on `a` |\n| N0328 | An invalid version, version requirement, or index entry (`tooling.md` §4.6) | `shapes = \"1.x\"` |\n| N0329 | No versions satisfy the requirements: the conflicting requirements and where each came from (§4.7) | `^2.1 (from geometry 1.4.0) and ^1.0 (from plot 0.3.0)` |\n| N0335 | `novac publish`: the package cannot be published as it is (§4.8) | a path dependency, or uncommitted changes |\n\nDynamic dispatch and owning closures (N0330–N0339; `type-system.md` §37, §36.5, ADR 0015):\n\n| Code | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0330 | `dyn I` where it is not behind a reference or a `Box`: a local, parameter, field, element, optional or type argument | `fn f(s: dyn Shape)`, `Vector<dyn Shape>` |\n| N0331 | `dyn I` for an interface that is not dyn-compatible: one of its methods takes `this` by value | `interface Sink { fn close(this); }` then `borrow dyn Sink` |\n| N0332 | A `move` literal captures a scoped value: a reference, view, slice, closure, or a value with region parameters (`type-system.md` §36.5) | `let v = s.view(); Box.create(move () => v.length)` |\n| N0333 | A `move` literal anywhere but as the value of `Box.create`/`createIn` with an expected `Box<dyn fn…>` | `let f = move (x: u64) => x;` |\n| N0334 | A value that is not `Send` where it must cross to another thread: a spawned closure's capture or result, a `dyn … + Send` coercion's source, a `Send` bound's argument, or, for `Thread.both`, a capture that may not be used from two threads at once or an argument that is not a closure literal or `fn()` value (`type-system.md` §38, §38.6) | `Thread.spawn(move () => view.length)` with `view: StringView` |\n| N0336 | `@effectsOf` that names no parameter, or a parameter that is not of a function or closure type, or has no names (`type-system.md` §32.5) | `@effectsOf(n)` with `n: i32`; `@effectsOf()` |\n| N0337 | A static that cannot be one: a `static` whose type is not `Share`, or a generic or local one (`type-system.md` §39) | `static C: Cell = …` with `Cell` not `Share` |\n| N0338 | Not a static initialiser: an initialiser or `@constant` body outside §39.4, or a C constant macro called elsewhere | `static N: u64 = compute();` |\n| N0339 | `offsetOf<T>(field)` whose `T` is not a struct, or has no field of that name (`type-system.md` §30.1, ADR 0030) | `offsetOf<u32>(x)`; `offsetOf<Pair>(c)` |\n| N0340 | `callerAddress()` outside the body of an `@export` function, including in a closure inside one (`type-system.md` §28.4, ADR 0030) | `fn f() -> usize => callerAddress();` |\n",
    108 =>
        "# Formatting (M10 T1)\n\n`novac fmt` rewrites Nova source into one canonical style. It has no options: two programmers who run it get the same\nspacing, indentation, blank lines, brace placement and comment alignment. Where the style leaves a choice to the author\n(§3), it is a choice of line breaks, which the formatter keeps.\n\nThe formatter changes only whitespace, and the trailing commas of §3.3. A formatted file parses to the same tree as\nthe original (the same AST, apart from spans) and keeps every comment, in order. Formatting a formatted file changes\nnothing.\n\n## 1. Command\n\n```sh\nnovac fmt file.nova...          # rewrite each file in place (only if it changes)\nnovac fmt --check file.nova...  # change nothing; list the files that would change, exit 1 if any\nnovac fmt --stdout file.nova    # print the formatted text of one file\n```\n\nA file with a syntax error is not formatted: its diagnostics are printed, the file is left as it is, and the exit\nstatus is 1. A directory argument means every `.nova` file under it.\n\n## 2. Lines and indentation\n\n- The text is UTF-8 with LF line endings; it ends with exactly one newline. No line has trailing whitespace.\n- Indentation is 4 spaces per level; there are no tabs.\n- A level is opened by a `{` that ends its line (a block, a struct, enum, interface or impl body, a match, a\n  multi-line struct or array literal) and by a broken list (§3.3). Its contents are indented one level, and its\n  closing delimiter is on its own line at the opening line's indentation.\n- **Blank lines:** at most one in a row. None directly after an opening `{` or before a closing `}`, and none at the\n  start or end of the file. Elsewhere, between two items, two members or two statements, a blank line is kept if\n  the source has one or more, and not added if it has none.\n- **Line width:** 120 columns, counting the code and not a trailing comment. A line longer than that is broken by §3.4\n  where it can be. A line that cannot be broken (a long string literal, a long name) stays long.\n\n## 3. Line breaks\n\n### 3.1 What the author decides\n\nThe formatter keeps these choices and only lays out the result:\n- whether a block, struct, enum, interface or impl body, or match is written on one line (`{ return x; }`) or across\n  lines (anywhere between its braces);\n- whether a list (arguments, parameters, struct fields, array elements) is broken, and, in a broken list, which\n  elements start a new line (so a literal can keep several fields per line);\n- whether an expression body (`=> expr`) starts on the line of the `=>` or on the next one;\n- where a long binary expression or a chain of member calls (a `.` after a call, an index, `!` or `?`) is broken;\n- whether an attribute is followed by a line break.\n\nAny other line break in the source is removed: two tokens on different lines that no rule here separates are joined\nwith their §4 spacing. In particular `else` always follows the `}` before it on the same line.\n\nA one-line form is kept only if it fits in 120 columns and holds no line comment; otherwise it is broken, as if the\nauthor had written it across lines.\n\n### 3.2 One-line forms\n\n- A one-line block has one space inside each brace: `{ x += 1; }`, `{ break; }`. An empty block is `{}`.\n- A one-line struct body is `struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }`; a one-line enum is `enum E { A, B(i32) }`.\n- A one-line match is `match (v) { 0 => \"zero\", _ => \"many\" }`, and as a statement\n  `match (o) { Some(v) => { use(v); } null => {} }`.\n- A one-line struct literal is `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`; an array literal is `[1, 2, 3]`; argument and parameter lists\n  are `f(a, b)` and `fn f(a: i32, b: i32)`. One-line lists have no trailing comma.\n- **An if/else chain is laid out as a whole:** if one of its blocks is broken, or the author started an `else` on a new\n  line, every block of the chain is broken.\n\n```nova\nif (c) { a(); } else { b(); }      // fits: kept on one line\nif (c) {                           // otherwise: every block broken\n    a();\n}",
    109 =>
        " else {\n    b();\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 Broken lists\n\nStatements, members, enum variants and match arms of a broken body are one per line. A list that is broken (by its\nauthor, or by §3.4) is laid out as a block:\n- the opening delimiter ends its line;\n- the elements are indented one level; each element that the author started on a new line starts a new line, and\n  the others follow the element before them after `, `; a list broken by §3.4 has one element per line;\n- the closing delimiter starts its own line at the opening line's indentation;\n- the last element is followed by a comma, where the grammar allows one (grammar.md §2: parameters, arguments, struct\n  field initialisers, array elements, enum variants, and the arms of a match expression, except an arm whose value is\n  a block). The commas between the arms of a match statement are left as written. Type parameters, generic arguments,\n  attribute arguments, closure parameters, function-type parameters and interface method parameters are never broken.\n\n```nova\nlet bodies = [\n    Body { x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, vx: 0.0, vy: 0.0, vz: 0.0, mass: SOLAR_MASS },\n    Body {\n        x: 4.84143144246472090e+00, y: -1.16032004402742839e+00, z: -1.03622044471123109e-01,\n        vx: 1.66007664274403694e-03 * DAYS_PER_YEAR, vy: 7.69901118419740425e-03 * DAYS_PER_YEAR,\n    },\n];\n```\n\n### 3.4 Breaking a line that is too long\n\nWhen a line does not fit, the formatter breaks one construct that starts on it and lays the file out again, until\nthe line fits or nothing on it is left to break. It takes the first kind in this order, and of that kind the\noutermost:\n1. a function's expression body (`=> e`): the expression moves to the next line, indented one level;\n2. a list or a one-line block or body (§3.3);\n3. the value of a match arm or a closure (`=> e`), moved to the next line;\n4. an initialiser or an assigned value (`= e`), moved to the next line;\n5. a binary expression: after its operator, with the right operand indented one level;\n6. a chain of member calls: before a `.`, indented one level.\n\nThe author's breaks of the same kinds (§3.1) are laid out by the same rules.\n\n## 4. Spaces\n\n- One space around binary operators (`a + b`, `x == y`, `a && b`), around `=` and the compound assignments, around\n  `->` and `=>`, after `,`, `;` (inside a one-line form) and `:` (`x: i32`, `x: 1`).\n- No space around `..` (`0..n`, `a[i..j]`, `a[..]`), `.`, inside parentheses, brackets and generic arguments\n  (`f(a)`, `[1, 2]`, `Vector<i32>`), before `(` in a call or declaration, after a unary operator (`-x`, `!b`,\n  `*p`), before `?` and `!` (`f()?`, `r!`), or before `,` and `;`.\n- Keywords: one space after `if`, `while`, `for`, `match`, `return` (with a value), `fn` (in a declaration), `let`,\n  `var`, `borrow`, `mut`, `move`, `as` and the visibility keywords; `if (c) {`, `} else {`, `} else if (d) {`.\n- One space before an opening `{` that follows code on its line.\n- Attributes are followed by one space or a line break, as written: `@context(atomic) fn f()`.\n\n## 5. Comments\n\n- Comments keep their text and order. A comment on a line of its own is indented like the code that follows it (or,\n  at the end of a block, like the code before the `}`).\n- A comment after code on the same line (a trailing comment) follows it after one space. Trailing comments on\n  consecutive lines are aligned: each starts one space after the longest code among those lines. A line without a\n  trailing comment, or a blank line, ends the group.\n- Doc comments (`///`) stay directly before their item; a blank line between a doc comment and its item is removed,\n  since it would detach the comment (grammar.md §1.2).\n- A block comment (`/* … */`) inside a line keeps one space on each side.\n\n## 6. Guarantees and their tests\n\n- **Same tree:** for every file of the repository that parses (the corpus included), the formatted text parses\n  without error to an AST equal to the original's, spans aside (`FormatterTests`).\n- **Comments:** the comments of",
    110 =>
        " the formatted text are those of the original, in order.\n- **Fixed point:** formatting the formatted text changes nothing.\n- **The repository's own code** (`lib/`, `programs/`, `benchmarks/`, `integrations/`, the README's examples) is kept\n  formatted: `novac fmt --check` runs over it in the test suite. The test corpus is not reformatted, since its\n  annotations name lines and some files test unusual layout; it is the formatter's test input.\n",
    111 =>
        "# Nova Grammar — M1 subset\n\nStatus: normative for Milestone 1 (increment 1a implements the lexer and parser for exactly\nthis grammar). Later milestones extend it. Any syntax not listed here is rejected\nby the parser, even if `language-spec.md` shows it for a later milestone.\n\nNotation: EBNF. `'x'` is a literal token, `X?` optional, `X*` zero or more, `X+` one or more.\n\n## 1. Lexical grammar\n\n### 1.1 Source text\n- UTF-8. A leading BOM is ignored. Line terminators: `\\n`, `\\r\\n`, `\\r`.\n- Columns in diagnostics are 1-based and count UTF-16 code units. Lines are 1-based.\n- Non-ASCII characters may appear only inside comments, string literals and char\n  literals.\n\n### 1.2 Whitespace and comments\n- Whitespace: space, tab, line terminators, form feed.\n- `// ...` to end of line. `/* ... */` block comments do **not** nest. An unterminated block\n  comment is error N0003.\n- Comments and whitespace are discarded by the parser, except doc comments (M8a). The lexer records every comment's span\n  for the formatter (M10 T1, formatting.md).\n- **Doc comments** (M8a): a line comment that starts with exactly `///` (not `////`). A run of doc\n  comment lines, with only whitespace between them, is attached to the declaration whose first token\n  follows the run directly. Its text is each line after `///` with leading spaces removed. Doc\n  comments elsewhere are ordinary comments. Used by the `Safety:` rule (type-system.md §27.4).\n\n### 1.3 Identifiers and keywords\n```\nIdentifier = IdentStart IdentContinue*      (not a keyword)\nIdentStart = 'A'..'Z' | 'a'..'z' | '_'\nIdentContinue = IdentStart | '0'..'9'\n```\nA lone `_` is the token `Underscore` (reserved; no meaning in M1).\n\nKeywords (all reserved, whether or not M1 uses them):\n```\nas borrow break const continue defer drop else enum extern false fn for if impl\nimport in interface internal let loop match move mut namespace null own private\npublic return Self static struct this true type unsafe using var where while\nclass async await yield macro\n```\nPrimitive type names (`i32`, `u64`, `bool`, `void`, …) are **identifiers**,\nresolved by name resolution, not keywords.\n\n### 1.4 Integer literals\n```\nIntLiteral = (Decimal | '0x' HexDigits | '0b' BinDigits | '0o' OctDigits) IntSuffix?\nIntSuffix  = 'i8'|'i16'|'i32'|'i64'|'isize'|'u8'|'u16'|'u32'|'u64'|'usize'\n```\n- `_` separators are allowed only *between* digits (`1_000`, `0xFF_FF`). A separator\n  at the start or end of the digit sequence, or doubled, is error N0010.\n- The value must fit in 64 bits (unsigned), otherwise N0004. Whether it fits the\n  *type* is checked later (type checking).\n- A digit invalid for the radix is N0008. A missing digit after a prefix is N0008. An unknown suffix is N0009.\n- Decimal literals have no leading-zero restriction, and there is no octal-by-leading-zero:\n  `010` is ten.\n\n### 1.5 Float literals (typed since M11 G7: type-system.md §35.2)\n```\nFloatLiteral = Decimal '.' Decimal Exponent? FloatSuffix? | Decimal Exponent FloatSuffix?\nExponent = ('e'|'E') ('+'|'-')? Decimal\nFloatSuffix = 'f32' | 'f64'\n```\n`1.` and `.5` are not float literals. `1..2` lexes as `1` `..` `2`.\n\n### 1.6 String and char literals\n- `\"...\"` on one line. Escapes: `\\n \\r \\t \\\\ \\\" \\' \\0 \\u{H..H}` (1–6 hex digits,\n  a Unicode scalar value). Other escapes are N0006. A newline or EOF before the closing quote is N0005.\n- `'c'`: exactly one Unicode scalar value or one escape. Otherwise N0007. Its value is the scalar value, as an\n  integer literal (`type-system.md` §4, M11); it may also be a pattern.\n\n### 1.7 Punctuation and operators\n```\n( ) { } [ ] , ; : . .. @ ? -> =>\n+ - * / % ! & | ^ ~ && || << \n= == != < <= > >=\n+= -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<=\n```\nThe lexer **never** produces `>>` or `>>=`. It emits single `>` (and `>=`) tokens.\nThe parser forms the shift operator `>>` from two *adjacent* `>` tokens, and `>>=` from `>`\nimmediately followed by `>=`. That keeps nested generic arguments\n(`A<B<C>>`) unambiguous in later milestones.\n\nAny other character is N0001 (",
    112 =>
        "unexpected character).\n\n## 2. Syntactic grammar (M1)\n\n```\nCompilationUnit = NamespaceDecl? ImportDecl* Item* EOF\nNamespaceDecl   = 'namespace' QualifiedName ';'     (* retired by ADR 0013: accepted, no effect *)\nImportDecl      = 'import' Identifier ('.' Identifier)? ';'   (* M10 T5: a dependency, or one of its items; type-system.md §2 *)\nQualifiedName   = Identifier ('.' Identifier)*\n\nItem       = Attribute* Visibility? (FunctionDecl | ConstDecl | StaticDecl | StructDecl | EnumDecl | ExternBlock)\nAttribute  = '@' Identifier ('(' AttributeArg (',' AttributeArg)* ')')?   (* M8b; type-system.md §28.1 *)\nAttributeArg = Identifier | StringLiteral | IntLiteral\nExternBlock = 'extern' StringLiteral '{' (ExternFn | ExternStruct)* '}'  (* M8b; §28.2 *)\nExternStruct = Attribute* Visibility? 'struct' Identifier ('{' ExternField* '}' | ';')   (* C6b; type-system.md §31 *)\nExternField = Attribute* FieldDecl                                           (* C9: @cname; type-system.md §31.1 *)\nExternFn   = Attribute* Visibility? 'fn' Identifier '(' (Param (',' Param)* ','?)? ')' ('->' Type)? ';'\nTypeParams = '<' TypeParam (',' TypeParam)* '>'               (* M6a; type-system.md §20 *)\nInterfaceDecl = 'interface' Identifier TypeParams? '{' MethodSig* '}'   (* M6b; §21 *)\nMethodSig  = Attribute* 'fn' Identifier '(' Receiver (',' Param)* ')' ('->' Type)? ';'   (* attributes: M13 G2, `@effects`; §37.3 *)\nImplDecl   = 'impl' Type 'for' Type '{' (Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl)* '}'   (* M6b; §21; attributes C7a *)\nTypeParam  = 'region' Identifier                               (* M6x: a region, before any type parameter; §25.1 *)\n           | Identifier (':' Type ('+' Type)*)?                (* bounds: M6b *)\n(* `region` is contextual: only `region` followed by a name declares a region. In a type, a region\n   argument is a region name or `static`, written first in the argument list (`Token<src>`,\n   `StringView<r>`, `Slice<r, T>`) or in `borrow(r) T`; type-system.md §25.2. *)\nEnumDecl   = 'enum' Identifier TypeParams? '{' (Variant (',' Variant)* ','?)? '}'        (* M2d *)\nVariant    = Identifier ('(' Type (',' Type)* ','? ')')?\nVisibility = 'public' | 'internal' | 'private'\n\nFunctionDecl = 'unsafe'? 'fn' Identifier TypeParams? '(' ParamList? ')' ('->' Type)? FunctionBody   (* 'unsafe': M8a *)\nParamList    = (Receiver | Param) (',' Param)* ','?\nReceiver     = ('borrow' 'mut'?)? 'this'   (* M2a: by value; M2b: reference receivers *)\nParam        = Identifier ':' Type\n\nStructDecl   = 'struct' Identifier TypeParams? '{' Member* '}'          (* M2a; TypeParams M6a *)\nMember       = Visibility? FieldDecl | Attribute* Visibility? FunctionDecl | DropBlock   (* method attributes: C7a, type-system.md §32 *)\nDropBlock    = 'drop' Block                (* M2c *)\nFieldDecl    = Identifier ':' Type ';'\nFunctionBody = Block | '=>' Expr ';'\n\nConstDecl = 'const' Identifier ':' Type '=' Expr ';'\nStaticDecl = 'static' 'mut'? Identifier ':' Type '=' Expr ';'   (* M14 K1; type-system.md §39 *)\n\nType = QualifiedName\n     | 'borrow' 'mut'? Sources? Type          (* M2b references; Sources only in return types *)\n     | '[' Type ';' IntLiteral ']'             (* M2d fixed array *)\n     | ('Slice' | 'SliceMut') '<' Type '>'     (* M2d slices *)\n     | 'Result' '<' Type ',' Type '>'          (* M3a; type-system.md §17 *)\n     | Type '?'                                (* M6c: optional; `borrow T?` is an optional reference *)\n     | '(' Type ')'                            (* M11 (G6): `borrow (T?)` is a reference to an optional *)\n     | Attribute* 'fn' '(' (Type (',' Type)*)? ')' ('->' Type)?   (* M11 (G8a): a function value, type-system.md §36.1;\n                                               G8b′: `@effects(…)` before `fn`, §36.3 *)\n     | Identifier '<' Type (',' Type)* '>'     (* M6a: an instantiation of a generic struct or enum; also Box, Pool, … *)\n     | '*' 'mut'? Type                         (* M8a: raw pointer; type-system.md §27 *)\n     | ",
    113 =>
        "'dyn' Type                              (* M13 G2: an interface instantiation; only behind `borrow` or in\n                                                  `Box<…>`; type-system.md §37. `dyn` is a keyword only here *)\n     | 'dyn' 'mut'? Attribute* 'fn' '(' (Type (',' Type)*)? ')' ('->' Type)?   (* M13 G3: an owning closure's type, §36.5 *)\nSources = '(' Identifier (',' Identifier)* ')'   (* only if a type follows the ')'; else '(' Type ')' *)\n\nBlock = '{' Statement* '}'\nStatement = Block\n          | LocalDecl\n          | IfStmt\n          | 'while' '(' Expr ')' Block\n          | 'loop' Block\n          | 'break' ';'\n          | 'continue' ';'\n          | 'return' Expr? ';'\n          | 'using' Identifier (':' Type)? '=' Expr ';'      (* M2c *)\n          | 'defer' Statement                               (* M2c *)\n          | MatchStmt                                       (* M2d; type-system.md §15.2 *)\n          | 'for' '(' Identifier 'in' Expr ('..' Expr)? ')' Block   (* M2d; type-system.md §16.4 *)\n          | 'unsafe' Block                                  (* M8a; type-system.md §27.4 *)\n          | Expr AssignOp Expr ';'        (* assignment: a statement, not an expression *)\n          | Expr ';'\nLocalDecl = ('let' | 'var') Identifier (':' Type)? ('=' Expr)? ';'\nIfStmt    = 'if' '(' Expr ')' Block ('else' (IfStmt | Block))?\nAssignOp  = '=' | '+=' | '-=' | '*=' | '/=' | '%=' | '&=' | '|=' | '^=' | '<<=' | '>>='\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Braces are **mandatory** after `if`, `else`, `while` and `loop`, so there is no dangling-else ambiguity.\n- Assignment is a statement, so `if (x = 1)` is a syntax error. The assignment target\n  must be a name or member access (N0104 otherwise). Whether it is *assignable*\n  (a `var`) is checked later.\n- A `LocalDecl` without an initialiser must have a type annotation (enforced by the\n  checker, not the parser).\n\n### 2.1 Expressions and precedence\n\nFrom lowest to highest binding. All binary levels are left-associative except\ncomparisons, which are **non-associative**: `a < b < c` is error N0103.\n\n| Level | Operators | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `\\|\\|` | short-circuit |\n| 2 | `&&` | short-circuit |\n| 3 | `== != < <= > >=` | non-associative |\n| 4 | `\\|` | |\n| 5 | `^` | |\n| 6 | `&` | |\n| 7 | `<< >>` | |\n| 8 | `+ -` | |\n| 9 | `* / %` | |\n| 10 | `as` | `Expr 'as' Type`, left-assoc (`x as u32 as u64`) |\n| 11 | unary `- !`, `borrow`, `borrow mut`, `move` | prefix, right-assoc (`- -x`) |\n| 12 | postfix: call `(...)`, member `.name` | |\n\nThe ordering puts bitwise operators **above** comparisons (as in Rust and Swift), so\n`x & 1 == 0` parses as `(x & 1) == 0`. This avoids a well-known C/C# pitfall.\n\n```\nPrimary = IntLiteral | FloatLiteral | StringLiteral | CharLiteral | 'true' | 'false'\n        | Identifier GenericArgs? | '(' Expr ')' | 'this'\n        | 'unsafe' '{' Expr '}'                              (* M8a: an unsafe expression *)\n        | IfExpr | MatchExpr                                 (* M11 (G5): type-system.md §15.3 *)\n        | '(' (ClosureParam (',' ClosureParam)*)? ')' '=>' (Block | Expr)   (* M11 (G8b): a closure, §36.2 *)\n        | 'move' '(' (ClosureParam (',' ClosureParam)*)? ')' '=>' (Block | Expr)   (* M13 G3: a move literal, §36.5 *)\n        | Identifier GenericArgs? '{' FieldInits? '}'       (* struct literal, M2a; GenericArgs M6a *)\n        | '[' Expr (',' Expr)* ','? ']' | '[' Expr ';' IntLiteral ']'   (* array literals, M2d *)\nClosureParam = Identifier (':' Type)?             (* a '(' … ')' followed by '=>' is a closure *)\nIfExpr    = 'if' '(' Expr ')' Branch 'else' (IfExpr | Branch)\nMatchExpr = 'match' '(' Expr ')' '{' (ValueArm (',' ValueArm)* ','?)? '}'\nValueArm  = Pattern '=>' Branch\nBranch    = Expr | Block                             (* a Block branch must not complete *)\nFieldInits = FieldInit (',' FieldInit)* ','?\nFieldInit  = Identifier ':' Expr\nGenericArgs = '<' Type (',' Type)* '>'      (* only when followed by '(', '.' or '{' — see below *)\nPostfix = Primary ( '(' A",
    114 =>
        "rgList? ')' | '.' Identifier | '[' IndexOrRange ']'\n                  | '?' | '!' )*                     (* M3a: propagate / unwrap a Result *)\nIndexOrRange = Expr | Expr? '..' Expr?               (* M2d: element or slice *)\nArgList = Arg (',' Arg)* ','?\nArg     = 'mut' Expr | Expr                (* `mut x` for a `borrow mut` parameter, M2b *)\n```\n\n**Generic arguments in expressions** (`truncate<i64>(x)`, and since M6a `id<i32>(x)`,\n`Pair<u8>.make()` and `Pair<u8> { … }`): after an identifier, `<` starts generic arguments if the\ntokens up to the matching `>` can only form types (identifiers, `.`, `,`, `;`, `*` (M8a), `?` (M9a: optional\ntypes, as in `Vector<Box<Pair>?>.create()`), integer literals, `borrow`, `mut`, and balanced `<…>` and `[…]`) and the `>` is followed by `(`, `.` or `{`, or (since\nC7d, for a generic function's instance as a value, `release<T>`, type-system §34.1) by `)`, `,`, `;`\nor `]`, which cannot follow a comparison's `>` (it needs an operand). Otherwise `<` is the less-than\noperator. Write `(f < T) > (x)` to mean the comparison.\n\n**Struct literals** (M2a): in expression position, an identifier *immediately followed by `{`* is\na struct literal. This is unambiguous in Nova because every `{` that opens a block follows a\nkeyword (`else`, `loop`), a `)` (conditions are parenthesised) or a type (`-> T {`), never an\nexpression.\n\nA `Receiver` (`this`) is only meaningful as the first parameter of a function declared inside a\n`struct`. The parser accepts it as any first parameter; the checker rejects it elsewhere (N0232).\n\n### 2.2 Nesting limit (M15 S3)\n\nNesting is limited to **1,000 levels** per file. Each of these is one level:\n- a nested block or statement;\n- a type (each `Box<…>` inside another is one level);\n- a pattern;\n- a unary operand;\n- each link of a left-associative chain, so `1 + 1 + 1` is two levels deep, as are `a.b.c` and `f()()`. The links\n  are binary operators, `as`, calls, members, indexes, `?` and `!`.\n\nEvery later pass recurses over the tree the parser builds, so its depth is what is bounded. Past the limit the parser\nreports **N0110** once, at the token where the limit was passed, and stops parsing that file. No other diagnostic in\nthe file follows it. Hand-written code stays far below the limit: none of the repository's 525 files comes near it.\nBefore M15, 100,000 levels overflowed novac's stack, ending with SIGSEGV and no message (#3). novac runs every command\non its own 256 MiB stack, so input within the limit compiles whatever the operating system's main-thread stack is\n(1 MiB on Windows; measured in milestone-15.md S3).\n\n## 3. Error recovery (normative for diagnostics tests)\n\n- Expected-token errors (N0100) are reported at the current token. When the missing\n  token is `;`, they are reported at the end of the previous token instead. The missing token is\n  *not* consumed.\n- After an error, the parser suppresses further diagnostics until it re-synchronises:\n  - in a block: at the next `;` (consumed), `}` (not consumed), or statement keyword\n    (`let var if while loop return break continue`). Balanced `{ }` groups are skipped\n    whole. An item keyword (`fn`, visibility) ends the block, which reports the missing `}`;\n  - inside a `struct` body: at the next member start (identifier followed by `:`, `fn`, or a\n    visibility keyword) or the closing `}`;\n  - at the top level: at the next declaration keyword (`fn const public internal private\n    namespace struct class enum interface impl let var static extern import using type`)\n    outside any skipped `{ }` group, or at EOF. Each misplaced declaration is therefore reported\n    once (N0101).\n- Every parse loop consumes at least one token per iteration or terminates, so\n  parsing always terminates.\n- Past the nesting limit (§2.2), N0110 ends the file's parse: no later diagnostic is reported in\n  that file.\n",
    115 =>
        "# Intermediate Representations (as implemented)\n\nStatus: describes the code as of M1 increment 1c. The design rationale is in `architecture.md` §2.\nThat document is the plan; this one is the implementation record.\n\n## 1. AST\n\n`src/Nova.Compiler/Syntax/Ast.cs`. Immutable records with spans. Printed by `novac parse --dump-ast`.\n\n## 2. HIR\n\n`src/Nova.Compiler/Hir/Hir.cs`. It is typed and fully resolved:\n\n- Constant references are replaced by their values.\n- Parentheses and expression-bodied functions are removed.\n- `else if` becomes nested `if`.\n- Assignment targets are locals.\n\nPrinted by `novac check --dump-hir`. Static semantics: `type-system.md`.\n\n## 3. NIR (Nova IR)\n\n`src/Nova.Compiler/Nir/`. A control-flow graph of basic blocks per function, modelled on Rust MIR.\nIt is not SSA. M1 subset:\n\n```text\nfunction  = locals: [local]          -- _0.._(n-1) are the parameters, in order\n            blocks: [block]          -- bb0 is the entry block\nlocal     = id, type, name?          -- name is null for compiler temporaries\nblock     = id, statements: [stmt], terminator\nplace     = local projection*                  -- projection = '.' field | Deref (M2a, M2b) | Downcast(V) | Index(operand) (M2d)\nstmt      = Assign(place, rvalue)\n          | Eval(rvalue)             -- for calls whose result is unused (void)\n          | StorageDead(local)       -- scope end; temporaries at the end of their statement (M2b)\n          | Drop(place, flag?)       -- inserted by drop elaboration (M2c); guarded by a bool flag local if present\n          | Unpack(place, V, [local?])          -- M3c: move every payload of variant V out of a consumed enum local\n          | BoxFree(place, flag?)               -- M3c: free a partially moved box without destroying its contents\n          | BoundsCheck(index, length)          -- panics unless index < length (M2d)\n          | RangeCheck(low, high, length)       -- panics unless low <= high <= length (M2d);\n                                                 -- with a `Text` place (M6c), also unless both bounds\n                                                 -- are UTF-8 character boundaries of that StringView\n          | LengthCheck(left, right)            -- panics (\"slice lengths differ\") unless left == right (M9b)\nrvalue    = Use(operand)\n          | Binary(op, operand, operand, span)     -- op semantics: type-system.md §6 (may panic)\n          | Unary(op, operand, span)\n          | Cast(operand, type)                    -- lossless `as`; also round<T> to a float (type-system.md §35.4);\n                                                 -- also a coercion of `borrow T`/`Box<T, A>` to a dyn type (M13 G2, §37.2):\n                                                 -- the pair (T, dyn I) is in the program's DynTables; loans and moves\n                                                 -- flow through it as through any cast\n          | Truncate(operand, type)\n          | FloatToInt(operand, Panic | Checked | Saturating, type, span)   -- M11 G7: truncate<T> of a float (§35.4)\n          | Aggregate(struct, [operand])           -- field values in declaration order (M2a)\n          | Ref(Shared | Mut, place)               -- creates a loan (M2b; lifetimes.md)\n          | Discriminant(place) | EnumAggregate(V, [operand])   -- M2d enums\n          | Array([operand]) | ArrayRepeat(operand)             -- M2d array values\n          | Slice(Shared | Mut, place, low, high)  -- a slice of an array or deref'd slice; a loan of the whole place\n          | Length(place)                          -- a slice's length\n          | Call(function, [operand])              -- a call; after closure-call specialisation (optimisations.md\n                                                 -- §5) it may name a variant of the function (Variant > 0), and a\n                                                 -- CallClosure through a known closure becomes a Call of its body.\n                                                 -- A dyn method (IsDynMethod, no body;",
    116 =>
        " §37.3) dispatches through\n                                                 -- the table of argument 0, a dyn reference; checked as a call of\n                                                 -- its signature, so loans and effects need no new rule\n          | Builtin(kind, [operand])               -- Console.write/writeLine; CallFunction(f, args…): a call through a\n                                                 -- function value (M11 G8a), result loans from every reference argument;\n                                                 -- CallClosure(f, args…) likewise through a closure (G8b), f passed on\n                                                 -- as the body's environment parameter\noperand   = Place(place, move?) | Const(value, type) | String(text)   -- move: non-Copy type or explicit `move` (M2c)\n                                         -- a float Const holds the float's bits (M11 G7, type-system.md §35.6)\nterminator= Goto(bb) | Branch(operand, then_bb, else_bb) | Return(operand?)\n          | Switch(operand, [(value, bb)], otherwise_bb)    -- match (M2d)\n          | Exit(operand)                          -- Process.exit\n          | Unreachable                            -- after a call of type never\n          | Panic(kind)                            -- `e!` on Err (M3a), `o!` on null (M6c); aborts\n```\n\nLowering rules (HIR → NIR, `NirBuilder`):\n\n- **Evaluation order is explicit.** Operands are evaluated left to right into operands or\n  temporaries. If the right operand of a binary operator (or a later argument) contains a call,\n  an earlier operand that reads a local is first copied into a temporary. This keeps\n  left-to-right semantics when calls can mutate state (M2+).\n- `&&` and `||` become branches into a boolean temporary. The right operand is evaluated\n  only when needed.\n- `x op= e` becomes `x = Binary(op, x, e)`; the same applies with a place such as `p.a.b`.\n- A struct literal evaluates its initialisers **in source order** into operands, then builds an\n  `Aggregate` with them in declaration order.\n- A field read of a place is a place operand (`_3.a.b`). A field read of any other value (a call\n  result) first spills the value into a temporary. A method call passes the receiver as the first\n  argument, evaluated before the explicit arguments.\n- `while` becomes `cond → body → cond`, and `loop` becomes `body → body`. `break` and `continue`\n  jump to the innermost loop's exit or head.\n- Statements after a `return`, `break`, `continue`, `Process.exit` or never-typed call are\n  unreachable and are not lowered. Blocks with no predecessors are not emitted.\n- Constants are already folded in HIR. NIR performs no optimisation in M1.\n\n- References (M2b):\n  - an explicit or implicit borrow becomes `t = Ref(k, place)`;\n  - auto-deref reads `(*r)`;\n  - a use of a `borrow mut` variable becomes `Ref(Mut, (*r))` (reborrow);\n  - a call-site borrow of a value spills it into a temporary, which gets `StorageDead` at the end of\n    the statement;\n  - for a `borrow mut this` receiver, the receiver's `Ref` is emitted after the other arguments (B5);\n  - every statement carries a source span for borrow diagnostics.\n- The **borrow checker** (`Analysis/BorrowChecker.cs`, `lifetimes.md`) runs on NIR inside\n  `Compilation.Create`, so `novac check` reports borrow errors too.\n- **Moves and destruction (M2c).**\n  - Scopes hold locals *and* deferred statements. Leaving a scope (its end, `break`/`continue`,\n    or `return`) emits, in reverse order of position, `StorageDead` for locals and a fresh\n    lowering of each deferred statement.\n  - By-value parameters form the outermost scope.\n  - `return e` computes `e` into an unscoped temporary first, then ends the statement's\n    temporaries and every scope, then returns.\n  - **Move paths** (`Analysis/MovePaths.cs`): each local is a tree, expanded field by field\n    wherever a field is assigned or moved; all siblings are expanded, so children partition their\n    parent. A forward dataflow",
    117 =>
        " over the leaves tracks maybe-init, maybe-uninit and maybe-moved.\n    `MoveChecker` uses it for N0212/N0250–N0253.\n  - **Drop elaboration** (`Analysis/DropElaborator.cs`) runs only when every check passed:\n    - it inserts `Drop` before `StorageDead` of a local that needs drop, and before an assignment\n      that overwrites one; the assignment becomes `t = rvalue; Drop(p); p = t`;\n    - what is dropped depends on the state: whole, nothing, a flagged drop, or per field for a\n      partially moved struct;\n    - one bool flag per leaf is updated after every statement that changes that leaf's state.\n\n    The C backend emits drop glue `nvD_<len><Name>(T *)`: the `drop` block, then each field that\n    needs drop, in declaration order.\n\n- **Arrays, slices and `for` (M2d).**\n  - A slice type is a reference to the unsized `[T]`; `s[i]` is the place `(*s)[i]`.\n  - An index is evaluated into a `usize` temporary (or kept as a constant), then checked with\n    `BoundsCheck` against the array's constant length or the slice's `Length`, then used as an\n    `Index` projection. Element places are never move paths: an element's init state is its\n    array's.\n  - An assignment evaluates its target's indexes (and checks them) before the value.\n  - `a[lo..hi]` evaluates the base place, then the bounds left to right, then `RangeCheck` (none\n    for `a[..]`), then `Slice`.\n  - **Bulk operations (M9b).** `d.copyFrom(s)` lowers the receiver slice, then the source slice, each into a\n    temporary, then `LengthCheck(Length(s), Length(d))`, then the built-in `SliceCopy(d, s)`, which copies\n    with `memmove` semantics. `d.fill(v)` is the built-in `SliceFill(d, v)`. Neither is two-phase.\n    `d.swap(i, j)` (M11 G8c) lowers the slice and both indices, then `BoundsCheck(i, Length(d))` and\n    `BoundsCheck(j, Length(d))`, then the built-in `SliceSwap(d, i, j)`. `v.retain(keep)` lowers the vector\n    reference and the closure, then a temporary `SliceMut` reborrowed from the vector's elements, a call of\n    lib/core's `SliceAlgorithms<T>.retain` on it and the closure, and `VectorTruncate(v, kept)` (type-system §36.4).\n  - `for` evaluates its bounds, or its sequence place and length, once into locals that live until\n    the loop exits. A `usize` counter is compared with the end at the head. The body binds the\n    variable in its own scope; `continue` jumps to the counter's increment, and `break` to the\n    exit, which ends the header's locals.\n\n- **Results (M3a).**\n  - `e?` and `e!` move `e` into a temporary, switch on its tag, and in the `Ok` block read\n    the payload `t as Ok .0`.\n  - On `Err`, `?` builds `Err(t as Err .0)` of the function's return type and returns through the\n    same path as `return`; `!` ends in `Panic`.\n  - **Optionals (M6c)** lower the same way with `Some` (tag 1) as the success variant: on `null`,\n    `?` returns the `null` of the function's return type and `!` ends in `Panic(UnwrapNull)`.\n    `o == null` (HIR `IsNull`) takes a shared borrow of `o` (or of a temporary), reads\n    `Discriminant(*r)` and compares it with 0, so `o` is not consumed.\n  - **Vectors and strings (M6d).** A vector dereferences, like a box, to its unsized elements: `v[i]`\n    is the place `v.*[i]`, with a `BoundsCheck` against `Length(v)`, and views are `Ref` of `v.*`.\n    The deref is not a move path, so moving an element out is a move out of a deref (N0251).\n    `push`/`append` lower their argument before the receiver's `Ref mut` (B5). `for (x in v)`\n    iterates over a view held in a header temporary, so the vector stays borrowed for the whole\n    loop. When the value written to a vector element contains a call, it is evaluated into a\n    temporary and the element's index is checked again just before the write (the call may have\n    shrunk the vector).\n  - **Slot maps and stable vectors (M6e)** are built-in calls on a borrow of the container\n    (`insert`, `getMut` and `remove` take `Ref mut`, with `insert` two-phase); `get`'s optional\n    reference carries t",
    118 =>
        "he map's loan like any built-in result. A stable vector dereferences like\n    a vector, so `sv[i]` is the place `sv.*[i]`; the C backend renders its elements through the\n    chunk table. Liveness treats the targets of `Unpack` as definitions (fixed in M6e: a reference\n    payload bound by a consuming match used to be live from the function's start).\n  - **Region parameters (M6x).** A loan-carrying value stored into a field or element is lowered\n    into a temporary first, so the borrow checker sees a local whose loans flow into the target.\n  - **String views (M6c).** A literal is a constant operand of type `StringView` (no loans); a place\n    is needed only when it is spilled into a temporary. `s[lo..hi]` always emits a `RangeCheck`\n    with `Text`; `s.bytes()` is a `Cast` to `Slice<u8>` that keeps the view's loans.\n  - Moving a variant payload out of a local (only these lowerings do that) uninitialises the whole\n    local in the move dataflow, so the consumed result is not destroyed again.\n  - **Open temporaries.** The builder records the temporaries of every statement still being\n    lowered, with the scope they belong to. An early exit (`return`, `break`, `continue`, `?`)\n    ends them together with that scope, before its locals. Before M3a the temporaries of an\n    enclosing statement, such as a `match` scrutinee, were never ended on such paths.\n  - An expression statement whose value needs drop assigns it to a statement temporary, which is\n    destroyed at the end of the statement (type-system.md §17.5).\n\n- **Boxes and consuming matches (M3c).**\n  - Move paths go through box derefs (`NirPlace.IsMovePath`). Once a box's contents are tracked,\n    the box node has two children: its contents (fields below), then an *allocation* leaf that is\n    initialised while the box owns its memory.\n  - Drop elaboration destroys a partially moved box as: the initialised fields of its contents,\n    then `BoxFree` of the box. `BoxFree` is flagged if the allocation is only maybe initialised.\n  - A consuming `match` moves its scrutinee into a temporary. An arm that lists payloads starts\n    with one `Unpack`, which moves every payload into its binding or into a hidden local of the\n    arm and consumes the temporary as a whole. Other arms leave the value in the temporary, which\n    is destroyed at the end of the statement.\n\n- **Generic code (M6a).** NIR is built for generic versions too (type parameters are move types\n  that may need drop), so the move and borrow checkers run on them. Instantiations skip those\n  checks, which would only repeat the generic version's diagnostics. Drop elaboration and C\n  emission skip generic versions.\n\nPrinted by `novac build --dump-nir`.\n\n## 4. LIR — deferred\n\n`architecture.md` §2.4 plans a C-shaped LIR between NIR and C. In M1 the C emitter consumes NIR\ndirectly. The C-level decisions (type names, helper selection, temporaries) are made in one place,\n`Backend.C/CEmitter.cs`, because NIR operands are already flat. LIR will be introduced when a\nsecond consumer appears or when drop elaboration makes the emitter non-trivial (M2/M7).\nThis is a recorded deviation from the plan, not an omission.\n\n## 5. The NIR reference interpreter (M7c)\n\n`novac run --interp <files> [-- <arguments>]` executes a program's NIR directly\n(`src/Nova.Compiler/Interpreter/`). It serves as an oracle for the C backend. The C backend and\nthe interpreter consume the same checked NIR, after drop elaboration. Agreement between them\ntherefore tests the C backend and its runtime, not NIR lowering.\n\n### 5.1 Observable behaviour\n\nThe interpreter must produce exactly what the native program produces:\n\n- the bytes written to standard output and standard error, in the same order: every write to\n  standard error first flushes standard output (`nova_rt_hosted.c`);\n- the exit status:\n  - `main`'s result, or `Process.exit`'s code (the operating system keeps the low 8 bits);\n  - 134 after a panic, as `abort()` gives natively;\n- the panic message (`nova ",
    119 =>
        "panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:<column>`);\n- the allocation counters: `NOVA_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and the `NOVA_ALLOC_REPORT` line. The interpreter\n  counts every attempt that `nova_alloc` would. This includes vector growth, stable-vector chunks,\n  slot-map slots, arena and scratch chunks, pool slots, and `Process.run`'s argument copies. The\n  allocation-failure sweep therefore sees the same attempts.\n\nTo get these, the runtime's algorithms are reimplemented step for step:\n- `nova_rt_core.c`: growth (doubling, at least 4), arena chunks with their 16-byte header,\n  scratch chunks of 64 KiB, the pool slot size, and the panic and report texts;\n- the emitter's helpers (`c-backend.md` §2): push, pop and clear, slot-map generations, and the\n  drop-glue order.\n\n### 5.2 Memory model\n\nMemory is **byte-addressed**. Values have the target layout of `TypeTable.LayoutOf`, which is the\nsame layout the generated C asserts. Pointers are 8-byte addresses, and null is 0. Memory is a set of\n*allocations*, each with a base address aligned to 16 (as `malloc` gives) and a size:\n\n| Kind | Created by | Ends at |\n|---|---|---|\n| local | a function's entry (every local, zero-filled, as the C locals are) | `StorageDead`, or the function's return |\n| heap | `nova_alloc` | `nova_free` |\n| static | a string literal (read-only), the empty-vector sentinel (size 0) | never |\n\n`StorageDead` ends a local's allocation. The next write to that local (a whole assignment, or a\nwrite or borrow through one of its fields) gives it a *new* allocation, so an old reference to it\nstays dead.\n\n**Interpreter faults.** Each of the following stops the program with\n`nova interp: fault: <what> at <file>:<line>:<column>` on standard error and exit status 70:\n- an access to an ended allocation, or outside an allocation's bounds;\n- a dereference of null;\n- a misaligned access through a raw pointer (M8a): the address is not a multiple of the pointee's\n  alignment;\n- a write to static memory;\n- `nova_free` of anything but the base of a live heap allocation;\n- destroying a stack-placed box whose slot has already ended (M9e), the placed counterpart of a double free; and\n  running a placed site again while its previous box is alive (`memory-model.md` §5.1);\n- a *use* of uninitialised bytes. Heap memory starts uninitialised. Each byte carries an\n  initialised bit, which copies carry along: copying a struct with padding is not a use.\n  Reading a scalar to compute with it, to branch on it, to compare it, as a tag or as an address\n  is a use.\n- a bounds check that bounds-check elimination removed, and that fails (M9c, `optimisations.md` §2.8): the\n  interpreter evaluates every check, and the compiled program would not have made this one\n  (`an eliminated bounds check fails at …`). `novac run --no-bce` turns the analysis off. Likewise an operation whose\n  overflow check was eliminated (`optimisations.md` §3) and that would overflow faults (`an eliminated overflow check\n  fails at …`); `novac run --no-oce` turns that off.\n\nNo correct program can fault. A fault means the compiler, its runtime, or its checks let an\nunsafe program through, or (since M8a) that unsafe code broke its contract (`unsafe.md` §2). This makes the interpreter a dynamic safety oracle: liveness, bounds and\ninitialisation. It does not track borrows (no Stacked Borrows), so it does not catch aliasing\nviolations that stay in bounds.\n\n### 5.3 Execution\n\n- Calls recurse on the host stack. The interpreter runs on its own thread with a 1 GiB stack.\n- Drop glue and the runtime helpers are interpreted in the same order as the emitted C. A user\n  `drop` function, for example, runs before its fields' drops.\n- Arithmetic follows `IntegerSemantics` (type-system.md §6–7), the same table the checked C helpers\n  implement.\n\n### 5.4 The host\n\n- **Output.** Standard output, standard error and standard input are streams supplied by the\n  caller. The CLI uses the process's own.\n- **Arguments.** `argv[0]` is the first source file's path",
    120 =>
        ". The arguments after `--` follow it.\n- **Configuration.** The CLI reads `NOVA_FAIL_ALLOC_AT` and `NOVA_ALLOC_REPORT` as\n  `nova_platform_config` does (leading digits).\n- **Files and sockets** use the host C library through P/Invoke (`open`, `read`, `write`,\n  `close`, `unlink`, `socket`, …) on Linux and macOS, with the same flags and errno mapping as\n  `nova_rt_io.c`. On Windows, I/O is an interpreter fault (\"unsupported\").\n- **Working directory.** An optional working directory resolves relative file paths and is\n  where child processes start. Tests use it, since they run in parallel in one process. The CLI\n  uses the process's own directory.\n- **`Process.run`** starts the program with .NET's process API, after flushing standard output.\n  When the interpreter's output streams are the process's own, the child inherits them. When the\n  caller captures output (tests), the child's output is copied into the captured streams.\n\n**C code (M8b).** The interpreter runs no C. A call to an `extern \"C\"` function is interpreted only\nfor this model of the C library, used when the declaration's parameter and result types have the\nexpected shape (integers, `bool` or pointers of the right widths):\n`strlen` (reads bytes up to a zero byte, each access checked), `puts` (writes the bytes and a\nnewline to standard output, returns 0), `abs`, `malloc` and `free` (C5: a heap allocation of the\ninterpreter's memory, not counted as Nova's), `memcmp`, `memcpy` and `memset` (checked accesses;\n`memcpy` with overlapping ranges is a fault, as it is undefined in C). Any other extern call is the\nfault `unsupported: call to C function '<name>'`. Exported functions are ordinary Nova functions.\n**User allocators (C5).** A box with a user allocator gets its memory from the allocator's\n`allocate` method and returns it through `deallocate`, run as Nova code; `this` is a one-byte\nread-only allocation per allocator type.\n**Function pointers (M8d).** The C address of an extern or exported function is a value in a range\nno allocation uses (from `0xFFFF000000000000`); a call through it runs the exported function, or\nthe C model for an extern. A call through null or any other value is a fault. Volatile accesses\nare performed like other accesses (the interpreter does every access once, in order).\n\n### 5.5a Placement and the drop trace (M5)\n\n- `InterpreterOptions.Placement` (on by default since M9e, like the C backend's) applies the same\n  `PlacementAnalysis` as the C backend. A stack-placed site stores the value in a new *local* allocation, which is\n  not counted. If the site's previous allocation is still live when it runs again, that is a fault: natively the site\n  has one slot (`memory-model.md` §5.1).\n- A `Drop` of the site's box local destroys the contents, then ends that allocation. A `BoxFree`\n  just ends it. Ending it there, where C's slot lives on until the function returns, makes a\n  late use a fault: a stronger check.\n- `InterpreterOptions.DropTrace`, when given a list, records every executed `Drop` statement as\n  `<function> <line>:<column> <type>`, in order. M5's drop-timing check compares the traces\n  with and without placement.\n\n### 5.5 Agreement tests\n\n- `InterpreterTests` run every run-pass and run-fail program. Standard output, standard error\n  and exit status must equal the corpus expectations. The native runs check the same\n  expectations under every C configuration.\n- The allocation-failure sweep runs the interpreter beside the native ASan build, for the\n  baseline and at every failure point. The outcomes must be equal.\n",
    121 =>
        "# Nova Language Specification — Initial Draft (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0. Defines the **MVP surface** (milestones 1–4) and marks\n*future proposals* explicitly. Semantics of ownership and borrowing are in\n`ownership.md` (normative); storage and allocators in `memory-model.md`.\nThe formal grammar will be written in `grammar.md` during M1. Until then this\ndocument is the reference.\n\nLegend used in every example heading:\n- **[MVP-M1]** … **[MVP-M6]**: accepted for that milestone.\n- **[FUTURE]**: proposal only; not to be implemented without an approved ADR.\n\n## 1. Lexical structure (summary)\n\n- UTF-8 source. Identifiers `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` (ASCII in v0).\n- Comments `//`, `/* */` (non-nesting), doc comments `///`.\n- Integer literals: decimal, `0x`, `0b`, `0o`, `_` separators, optional suffix\n  (`10u8`, `3i64`). An unsuffixed literal takes its type from context; with no\n  context it defaults to `i32`. A literal that doesn't fit its type is a compile error.\n- Float literals `1.0`, `1e9`, suffix `f32`/`f64` (default `f64`).\n- String literals `\"...\"` (escapes `\\n \\t \\\\ \\\" \\0 \\u{...}`) have type\n  `StringView` with static region. Char literals `'a'` are integer literals of the character's Unicode\n  scalar value, `u32` by default, ASCII in a byte (M11; `type-system.md` §4). There is no `char` type.\n- Keywords: `namespace import public private internal fn let var struct enum\n  interface impl static const if else while for in loop break continue return\n  match using defer own borrow mut move unsafe extern true false null this Self\n  as type where drop`. Reserved: `class async await yield macro`.\n\n## 2. Types (MVP)\n\n| Type | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `i8 i16 i32 i64 u8 u16 u32 u64` | Fixed-width two's complement; overflow panics (`ownership.md` §0) |\n| `isize usize` | Pointer-sized, per *target* |\n| `f32 f64` | IEEE 754; C `float`/`double` |\n| `bool` | `true`/`false`; C `_Bool` |\n| `char` | Unicode scalar value (u32 with validity invariant) |\n| `void` | Unit return type |\n| `Array<T, N>` / `[T; N]` | Fixed-size inline array (Copy if T is Copy) |\n| `Slice<T>` | Scoped: borrowed `(ptr, len)` view; `SliceMut<T>` exclusive |\n| `StringView` | Scoped: borrowed UTF-8 `(ptr, len)` |\n| `String<A>` | Owned UTF-8, allocator `A` |\n| `Box<T, A>` | Owned single heap object |\n| `Vector<T, A>` | Owned growable array |\n| `T?` | Optional. Niche-optimised for `Box` and references, otherwise `{bool, T}` |\n| `Result<T, E>` | Tagged union |\n| `borrow T`, `borrow mut T` | References (scoped). Never null |\n| `*T`, `*mut T` | Raw pointers (unsafe to dereference) |\n| `fn(A, B) -> R` | Plain function pointer (no closure environment) |\n\nDefault allocator type parameter: in the **userland** profile `A` defaults to\n`SystemHeap`. In the **kernel** profile there is no default; it must be explicit\n(`cosmoos/profiles.md`).\n\nConversions: no implicit numeric conversions except widening of an integer\n**literal**. `as` performs only lossless numeric conversions (`u8 as u32`,\n`i32 as i64`, `i32 as f64`). Lossy conversions use `truncate<T>()`,\n`checkedCast<T>()` or `f.toIntSaturating<T>()`.\n\n## 3. Declarations and statements (MVP)\n\n- `let x = e;` immutable binding. `var x = e;` mutable binding. A type annotation\n  is optional (`let x: u64 = 0;`). Bindings must be definitely initialised before\n  use (`var x: i32;` followed by assignment on all paths is allowed).\n- `fn name(p: T, ...) -> R { ... }`, `fn name(...) -> R => expr;`\n- Parameter modes: *by value* (default: copy if Copy, otherwise move),\n  `borrow T`, `borrow mut T`. `own T` is an explicit spelling of by-value for\n  move types, accepted for readability.\n- Call-site borrowing: an argument for a `borrow T` parameter is borrowed\n  implicitly. An argument for a `borrow mut T` parameter must be written\n  `mut x` (as with C# `ref`), so mutation is visible at the call site.\n  Explicit borrow expressions elsewhere: `borrow x`, `borrow mut x`.\n- `move x` explicitly moves (optional; moves of move types are implicit).\n- `if`/`e",
    122 =>
        "lse`, `while`, `loop`, `for i in a..b` (half-open integer range),\n  `for x in slice` (borrowing iteration), `break`, `continue`, `return`.\n- `match` over enums and integers, exhaustive.\n- `using x = e;`: pinned scoped binding (`ownership.md` R3).\n- `defer stmt;`: runs `stmt` at scope exit on every path (in reverse order of\n  `defer` statements). The deferred statement may not `return`/`break`/`?`.\n- Error propagation `e?`: on `Result<T,E>` yields `T` or returns `Err(e)`\n  converted by `From<E>` (M6; v0 requires identical `E`); on `T?` yields\n  `T` or returns `null`. Postfix `!` unwraps or panics.\n- Evaluation order: strictly left to right, receiver before arguments,\n  arguments before call, and right-hand side of assignment **after** the\n  left-hand side place is evaluated (as in C#). This is specified and the\n  C backend must not delegate it to C's unspecified order (`c-backend.md` §4).\n\n## 4. Example programs\n\n### Ex1 — Functions and arithmetic [MVP-M1]\n\n```nova\nnamespace Demo;\n\nfn gcd(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {\n    var x = a;\n    var y = b;\n    while (y != 0) {\n        let t = x % y;\n        x = y;\n        y = t;\n    }\n    return x;\n}\n\nfn square(x: i64) -> i64 => x * x;      // overflow panics\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    Console.writeLine(gcd(1071, 462));  // 21\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex2 — Structs and methods (Copy value type) [MVP-M2]\n\n```nova\nstruct Point {\n    public x: f64;\n    public y: f64;\n\n    public fn distance(this, other: Point) -> f64 {\n        let dx = this.x - other.x;\n        let dy = this.y - other.y;\n        return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let p = Point { x: 3.0, y: 4.0 };\n    let q = p;                            // Copy: p still usable\n    Console.writeLine(p.distance(Point { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 }));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n`this` alone = by value (copy for Copy types); `borrow this` and\n`borrow mut this` are the reference receivers.\n\n### Ex3 — Move-only resource with `drop` [MVP-M2]\n\n```nova\nstruct Fd {\n    raw: i32;\n\n    drop {                      // runs exactly once, when the owner dies\n        unsafe { Sys.close(this.raw); }\n    }\n}\n\nfn consume(fd: own Fd) -> void { }        // fd dropped at end of consume\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let fd = Fd { raw: 3 };\n    consume(fd);\n    // consume(fd);   // error[O3]: use of moved value `fd`\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nA struct with `drop` is never Copy. Fields are dropped after the `drop`\nblock, in declaration order.\n\n### Ex4 — Equivalent of classes: owned heap reference type [MVP-M3]\n\nNova v0 has **no `class` keyword** (reserved). The C#/TS \"object on the heap with\nidentity\" idiom is written as a `struct` behind `Box`:\n\n```nova\nstruct Account {\n    id: u64;\n    balance: i64;\n\n    public fn deposit(borrow mut this, amount: i64) -> void {\n        this.balance += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn open(id: u64) -> Result<Box<Account>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.create(Account { id: id, balance: 0 });   // A = SystemHeap (userland)\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var acct = open(7)!;          // panics on allocation failure\n    acct.deposit(100);            // auto-deref + implicit mut reborrow of *acct\n    return 0;\n}                                  // Box dropped: Account dropped, memory freed\n```\n\n*[FUTURE]* `class Account { ... }` as sugar for a non-Copy type that is always\n`Box`-allocated (OD-4).\n\n### Ex5 — Heap allocation with an explicit allocator [MVP-M3]\n\n```nova\nstruct User { id: u64; age: u32; }\n\nfn createUser<A: Allocator>(alloc: A) -> Result<Box<User, A>, AllocError> {\n    return Box.createIn(alloc, User { id: 100, age: 25 });\n}\n```\n\n`A` is an *allocator type* (`memory-model.md` §3). For free-standing allocators\n(`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap`) the value is a zero-sized token, so\n`Box<User, KernelHeap>` is one pointer wide.\n\n### Ex6 — Borrowing [MVP-M2]\n\n```nova\nfn inspect(buffer: borrow Buffer) -> usize {\n    return buffer.length;\n}\n\nfn modify(buffer: borrow mut Buffer) -> void {\n    buffer.clear();\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var b = Buffer",
    123 =>
        ".create()!;\n    let n = inspect(b);             // implicit shared borrow\n    modify(mut b);                  // explicit mutable borrow at call site\n    return n as i32;                // error: usize → i32 is not lossless; use checkedCast\n}\n```\n\n### Ex7 — Returning a borrow [MVP-M2]\n\n```nova\nfn first(values: Slice<i32>) -> borrow i32 {     // elided: derived from `values`\n    return borrow values[0];                     // bounds-checked\n}\n\nfn longer(a: StringView, b: StringView) -> borrow(a, b) StringView {\n    return if (a.length >= b.length) a else b;   // region ⊆ both loans\n}\n```\n\n### Ex8 — Fixed arrays and slices [MVP-M2]\n\n```nova\nfn sum(values: Slice<i64>) -> i64 {\n    var total: i64 = 0;\n    for v in values { total += v; }\n    return total;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var data: [i64; 4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n    data[2] = 10;\n    Console.writeLine(sum(data[..]));      // whole-array slice\n    Console.writeLine(sum(data[1..3]));    // checked sub-slice\n    // data[4] = 0;                        // compile error: constant index out of range\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex9 — Vectors and invalidation [MVP-M6]\n\n```nova\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var v = Vector<i32>.create();         // no allocation until first push\n    v.push(1)!;                            // push returns Result<void, AllocError>\n    v.push(v.length as i32)!;              // two-phase borrow (B5)\n\n    let r = borrow v[0];\n    // v.push(3)!;                         // error[B1]: `v` is borrowed by `r`\n    Console.writeLine(r);\n    v.push(3)!;                            // fine: `r` is dead (NLL)\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex10 — Strings [MVP-M6]\n\n```nova\nfn greet(name: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n    var s = String.withCapacity(name.length + 7)?;\n    s.append(\"Hello, \")?;\n    s.append(name)?;\n    return Ok(s);\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let msg = greet(\"Nova\")!;\n    Console.writeLine(msg.view());         // StringView borrowed from msg\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex11 — Error handling [MVP-M3]\n\n```nova\nenum ParseError { Empty, BadDigit(u8), Overflow }\n\nfn parseU32(text: StringView) -> Result<u32, ParseError> {\n    if (text.length == 0) { return Err(ParseError.Empty); }\n    var value: u32 = 0;\n    for c in text.bytes() {\n        if (c < '0' as u8 || c > '9' as u8) { return Err(ParseError.BadDigit(c)); }\n        value = value.checkedMul(10)?.checkedAdd((c - '0' as u8) as u32)\n                     .okOr(ParseError.Overflow)?;\n    }\n    return Ok(value);\n}\n```\n\n(`checkedMul` returns `u32?`; `?` on an optional inside a function returning\n`Result` is an error in v0. The example converts it with `okOr` and then uses `?`.)\n\n### Ex12 — Generics with interface constraints [MVP-M6]\n\n```nova\ninterface Comparable<T> {\n    fn compare(borrow this, other: borrow T) -> i32;\n}\n\nfn max<T: Comparable<T>>(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a, b) T {\n    return if (a.compare(b) >= 0) a else b;\n}\n```\n\nGenerics are monomorphised. Interfaces are static constraints in v0. Dynamic\ndispatch (`dyn Interface`) is *[FUTURE]* (OD-10).\n\n### Ex13 — Interfaces and implementations [MVP-M6]\n\n```nova\ninterface Shape {\n    fn area(borrow this) -> f64;\n}\n\nstruct Circle { r: f64; }\nstruct Rect { w: f64; h: f64; }\n\nimpl Shape for Circle { fn area(borrow this) -> f64 => 3.141592653589793 * this.r * this.r; }\nimpl Shape for Rect   { fn area(borrow this) -> f64 => this.w * this.h; }\n\nfn total<S: Shape>(shapes: Slice<S>) -> f64 {\n    var t = 0.0;\n    for s in shapes { t += s.area(); }\n    return t;\n}\n```\n\n### Ex14 — Recursive structure (owned tree) [MVP-M3]\n\n```nova\nenum Tree {\n    Leaf,\n    Node(Box<TreeNode>),\n}\n\nstruct TreeNode { value: i32; left: Tree; right: Tree; }\n\nfn insert(t: own Tree, v: i32) -> Result<Tree, AllocError> {\n    match (t) {\n        Tree.Leaf => return Ok(Tree.Node(Box.create(TreeNode {\n            value: v, left: Tree.Leaf, right: Tree.Leaf })?)),\n        Tree.Node(var n) => {\n            if (v < n.value) { n.left = insert(move n.left, v)?; }\n            else ",
    124 =>
        "            { n.right = insert(move n.right, v)?; }\n            return Ok(Tree.Node(n));\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nMoving out of `n.left` (a field of a uniquely owned `Box` contents whose type\nhas no `drop`) is a partial move (O2) immediately repaired by assignment.\nThe checker requires the field to be re-initialised before `n` is used whole.\nOn the `?` error path `n` is partially moved: drop elaboration drops only `n.right`\nand `n.value` (trivial), then frees the box. This is a key M3 test.\n\nDestruction of a deep tree is recursive in v0. A stack-depth concern is recorded\nin `risks.md` (R-9).\n\n### Ex15 — Scoped arena [MVP-M4]\n\n```nova\nfn process(input: Slice<u8>) -> Result<u64, AllocError> {\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap, chunkSize: 64 * 1024)?;\n\n    let counts = arena.allocArray<u32>(256)?;     // scoped to `arena`, zero-filled\n    for b in input { counts[b as usize] += 1; }\n\n    var distinct: u64 = 0;\n    for c in counts { if (c != 0) { distinct += 1; } }\n    return Ok(distinct);                          // returns a u64, not arena memory\n}                                                 // arena freed here; no live loans\n```\n\n`allocArray<T>` requires `T` without `drop` (R2).\n\n### Ex16 — Custom allocator [MVP-M4]\n\n```nova\n// A fixed-buffer allocator over caller-provided storage.\nfn parseHeaders(raw: Slice<u8>) -> Result<usize, AllocError> {\n    var storage: [u8; 4096] = [0; 4096];\n    using fba = FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage);  // scoped to `storage`\n    let fields = fba.allocArray<Slice<u8>>(32)?;          // error[E3]: arena element type is scoped\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe error is intentional: storing borrowed slices inside region memory needs\nlifetime-parameterised containers (OD-3). The accepted v0 form stores\n`(offset, length)` pairs:\n\n```nova\nstruct Span { start: u32; len: u32; }\nlet fields = fba.allocArray<Span>(32)?;\n```\n\nA user-defined allocator implements the `Allocator` interface\n(`memory-model.md` §3.2) inside `unsafe` code with a documented contract.\n\n### Ex17 — C interoperability [M8b; `type-system.md` §28]\n\n```nova\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn write(fd: i32, buf: *u8, count: usize) -> isize;\n}\n\n/// Safety: `bytes` is a valid borrowed slice; write() does not retain the pointer.\npublic fn writeAll(fd: i32, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> bool {\n    var off: usize = 0;\n    while (off < bytes.length) {\n        let rest = bytes[off..];\n        let n = unsafe { write(fd, rest.ptr, rest.length) };\n        if (n < 0) { return false; }\n        off += truncate<usize>(n); // n >= 0 here\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\n/// Safety: the caller passes `len` readable bytes at `data` (the C side's contract).\n@export(\"geo_checksum\")             // C-callable symbol; `nova_` names belong to the runtime\npublic unsafe fn checksum(data: *u8, len: usize) -> u32 {\n    let s = Slice.fromRaw(data, len);\n    var h: u32 = 2166136261;\n    for (b in s) { h = (h ^ b as u32).wrappingMul(16777619); }\n    return h;\n}\n```\n\nM8b changed this example from its M0 form: `@export` alone makes a function C-callable (no\n`extern \"C\"` on definitions), and an export that reads through a pointer is `unsafe fn`, since a\nsafe Nova caller could pass any pointer. `IoError.fromErrno()`, `checkedCast` and `wrappingMul` are\nnot built in yet. `run-pass/ffi-libc.nova` is the running form of this example, with `puts`, `strlen`\nand `memcpy` in place of `write`, which is not in every C library (MSVC calls it `_write`).\n\n### Ex18 — Unsafe systems operations: MMIO [M8d; `type-system.md` §29]\n\n```nova\n@repr(C)\nstruct UartRegs {\n    data: Volatile<u32>;\n    status: Volatile<u32>;\n}\n\nstruct Uart {\n    regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>;\n\n    /// Safety: `base` must be the mapped address of a PL011-compatible UART that no other `Uart`\n    /// value aliases.\n    public unsafe fn attach(base: usize) -> Uart => Uart { regs: MmioPtr<UartRegs>.at(base) };\n\n    public fn putByte(borrow mut this, b: u8) -> void {\n        while ((this.regs.get().status.read() & 0x20) != 0) { ",
    125 =>
        "Cpu.spinHint(); }\n        this.regs.getMut().data.write(b as u32);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`Volatile<T>`, `MmioPtr<T>`, `PhysAddr` and `VirtAddr` come from `lib/core`, Nova sources bundled\nwith the compiler (§29.2). The address types are distinct, with no implicit conversions\n(`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`); `DmaAddr` comes with the kernel profile. M8d changed\nthis example from its M0 form: `attach` takes the address as a `usize`, and register access goes\nthrough `get()` / `getMut()`, whose results borrow the `MmioPtr`. It runs as\n`run-pass/mmio.nova` and, freestanding, as `freestanding/uart.nova`.\n\n### Ex19 — Kernel-context effects [FUTURE, C7]\n\n```nova\n@context(irq)            // implies @noalloc @nosleep, transitively checked\nfn timerIrq(ctx: borrow mut IrqContext) -> void {\n    ctx.ackTimer();\n    // KernelHeap.create(...);   // error: allocation not permitted in irq context\n}\n```\n\n## 5. Packages and visibility (M10, ADR 0013)\n\n- A *package* is a directory with `nova.toml`; its sources are the `.nova` files under `src/` (`tooling.md` §4).\n- All files of a package share one scope. Another package's `public` items are named with that package's name,\n  `geometry.Point`, or brought into a file's scope with `import geometry;` or `import geometry.Point;`\n  (`type-system.md` §2).\n- `public` (visible to other packages), `internal` (default: visible within the package), `private` (visible within\n  the declaring file).\n- `namespace X;` declarations are retired: accepted, with no effect.\n\n## 6. Not in the language (v0) — deliberate omissions\n\nExceptions, implicit reference counting, tracing GC, inheritance, implicit\nnumeric conversions, null references, `async/await` (reserved; post-M9),\nclosures that capture (M6+, OD-9), operator overloading (M6+), macros.\n",
    126 =>
        "# References and the Borrow Checker (M2b)\n\nStatus: normative for increment 2b. This document turns `ownership.md` invariants B1–B5 and E1–E3\ninto an algorithm on NIR. The surface rules for references are in `type-system.md` §13.\nWhere this document is stricter than `ownership.md`, it records a v0 simplification that later\nmilestones may relax.\n\n## 1. What makes the v0 problem small\n\nThe algorithm can be simple and **exact for v0** because of four language rules (type-system §13):\n\n1. **References are values of type `borrow T` / `borrow mut T` held only in locals and temporaries.**\n   They never appear in struct fields (E3) or constants.\n2. **A reference binding is immutable and initialised at its declaration** (`let r = …;`; no\n   `var r: borrow T`). Assigning to `r` writes *through* it (`*r = v`) and never re-points it.\n   So each reference local is assigned exactly once, which makes the points-to sets below\n   flow-insensitive without losing precision.\n3. **No nested references** (`borrow borrow T` is rejected).\n4. **A use of a `borrow mut T` value is an implicit reborrow** of its referent (§4). Two usable\n   mutable references to one place can therefore never coexist.\n\nTogether with lexical scoping, (1) and (2) mean a reference cannot outlive its referent *inside*\na function, except through a **temporary** borrowed at a call site, or through **`return`**. Both\nare checked explicitly (§6, §7). Conflicting accesses while a loan is live (B1/B2) are the\nremaining work, and they need liveness (§5).\n\n## 2. NIR additions\n\n```text\nprojection = Field(f) | Deref | Downcast(V) | Index(operand)\nplace      = local projection*                 -- (*r).f is  r Deref Field(f)\nrvalue    += Ref(Shared | Mut, place)           -- creates a loan\n           | Slice(Shared | Mut, place, lo, hi) -- M2d: also a loan, of the whole array or slice viewed\nstmt      += StorageDead(local)                 -- the local's storage ends (scope exit, statement end for temps)\n```\n\nLowering facts the checker relies on:\n- Every `Ref` is assigned directly to a local or temporary: `t = Ref(k, p)`.\n- A temporary lives until the end of the source statement that created it. At that point the\n  builder emits `StorageDead(t)`. The condition of an `if` or `while` counts as its own statement\n  (M15 S2): its temporaries end once it is evaluated, before the branch, and each evaluation of a\n  `while` condition ends its own. A borrow taken in a condition therefore does not reach the body.\n- User locals get `StorageDead` at the end of their block, and on `break`/`continue` for the\n  blocks being left. (`return` ends every local; references escaping through `return` are §7's job.)\n- A use of a `borrow mut T` operand at a call argument, an initialiser or a return is lowered as\n  `Ref(Mut, (*r))`, a reborrow. A coercion of `borrow mut T` to `borrow T` is `Ref(Shared, (*r))`.\n\n## 3. Loans and points-to sets\n\n- Each `t = Ref(k, p)` at program point P creates a **loan** `L = (k, p, P)`.\n- Each reference local `r` has a set `loans(r)` of the loans it may refer to (directly or through\n  reborrows). It is the least solution of:\n\n| NIR | Constraint |\n|---|---|\n| `t = Ref(k, p)` | `L ∈ loans(t)`; and if `p` contains `Deref` of a reference local `q`, then `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)` (a reborrow keeps the original loan alive) |\n| `t = Slice(k, p, lo, hi)` | as `Ref(k, p)`: a slice is a reference to `[T]`, and its loan covers all of `p`, whatever the range |\n| `t = Use(Place(r))` with `r` of reference type | `loans(r) ⊆ loans(t)` |\n| `t = Call(f, args)` where `f` returns a reference | `loans(arg_i) ⊆ loans(t)` for every parameter `i` that `f`'s signature says the result is derived from (§7) |\n| reference **parameter** `p_i` | `Param_i ∈ loans(p_i)`, a placeholder loan for \"whatever the caller lent\" |\n\nBecause every reference local is assigned once (§1), solving these subset constraints by\nfixpoint iteration gives the same result as a flow-sensitive analysis.\n\n**Loan-holdin",
    127 =>
        "g locals (M4a).** Since M4a, every local of a *scoped* type holds loans\n(`type-system.md` §19.1), not only reference locals. The constraints generalise:\n- **Copies and moves.** When an assignment's rvalue reads a place rooted at a loan-holding local\n  `q` (`Use`, aggregates, enum values, built-in calls), then `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)`. This covers a\n  payload read such as `t = (q as Ok).0`.\n- **`Unpack`.** `Unpack(q, …)` gives each target `loans(q)`.\n- **Borrowing a loan holder.** `Ref(k, p)` where `p` is rooted at a loan-holding local `q` and does\n  not go through a deref: `loans(q) ⊆ loans(t)`. A reference to a scoped value keeps that\n  value's loans alive.\n- Liveness (§5) and the return check (§7) consider all loan-holding locals.\n\n## 4. Accesses\n\nEach NIR statement and terminator performs **accesses** to places, in evaluation order:\n\n| Construct | Access |\n|---|---|\n| operand `Place(p)` | **Read** p |\n| `Ref(Shared, p)` | **BorrowShared** p |\n| `Ref(Mut, p)` | **BorrowMut** p |\n| `Assign(p, …)` | the rvalue's accesses, then **Write** p |\n| `StorageDead(l)` | **Dead** l |\n\nTwo places **overlap** if they have the same root local and one projection list is a prefix of\nthe other. `a` overlaps `a.f`, `a.f` does not overlap `a.g`, and `(*r)` overlaps `(*r).f`.\nTwo `Index` projections always compare equal (M2d): `a[i]` overlaps `a[j]`, because v0 does no\nindex reasoning. Two mutable slices of one array therefore cannot be live at once, even if\ntheir ranges are disjoint.\nPlaces with different roots never overlap. That is correct because aliasing between different\nroots only arises through loans, and every loan is checked when it is created.\n\n**Conflicts** (B1, B2):\n\n| Live loan \\ access | Read | Write | BorrowShared | BorrowMut | Dead |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared | ok | **conflict** | ok | **conflict** | **conflict** |\n| Mut | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** | **conflict** |\n\n## 5. Liveness (NLL)\n\n- A reference local is **live** at a point if some path from that point reads it (as an operand\n  or as the root of a place) before the function ends. Reference locals are never redefined\n  (§1), so there is no kill except at the definition. This is standard backward dataflow over the\n  NIR CFG.\n- The **live loans after point P** are the union of `loans(r)` over every reference local `r` live\n  after P.\n- **Check:** for every access `a` at point P to place `q`, and every loan `L` live after P with\n  `L` not created at P, it is an error if `L.place` overlaps `q` and the table in §4 says\n  conflict. The errors are N0240 (write), N0241 (borrow), N0242 (read) and N0243 (Dead: the\n  place stops existing while borrowed).\n\nThis accepts `let r = borrow x; print(r); x = 5;`, because `r` is dead after `print`. It rejects\n`let r = borrow x; x = 5; print(r);`.\n\n**Overwriting a reference is not a use of its referent (M11, G3).** A **Write** or **Dead** of a local `r` whose\ntype is a reference does not conflict with a loan whose place goes through `*r`, such as `(*r).next`. The write\nreplaces which object `r` points to; it does not change, move or end the object it pointed to. That object stays\nprotected by the loans the new reference inherited from `r` when it was made (§3: a reborrow `Ref(k, (*r)…)` gets\n`loans(r)`). A box is not a reference: overwriting a box destroys its contents, so a loan through `*b` still conflicts.\nThis accepts a cursor walking a list, `node = cursor!; … cursor = next_of(node);`, and a temporary that is reassigned\nin each iteration of a loop while a reference made through the previous value lives on.\n\n**Writes happen after the value (M2d).** In `Assign(p, rvalue)` the rvalue is evaluated first and\n`p` is written last. The **Write** access is therefore checked against the loans live *after*\nthe statement; the rvalue's own accesses use the loans live before it. This accepts\n`a[0] = sum(a[..])`: the slice is dead once `sum` has returned, before `a[0]` is written.\nRust's MIR gets th",
    128 =>
        "e same result by writing a call's result in a separate step.\n\n**B5, two-phase borrows (as implemented).** For a method call `e.m(args)` whose receiver is\n`borrow mut this` and `e` is a place, the receiver's loan is created **after** `args` are\nevaluated, right before the call. Arguments may therefore read `e` (`v.push(v.len())`), but\nthey cannot hold a borrow of `e` across the call. Evaluating a place has no side effects, so\ncreating its loan later does not change what the program computes. The loan exists only for the\ncall, which is exactly the interval in which it must be exclusive.\n\n## 6. Temporaries\n\n`f(makeValue())` with a `borrow T` parameter borrows a temporary that dies at the end of the\nstatement (`StorageDead`). If the result of `f` is derived from that argument and is stored in a\nreference local used after the statement, the `Dead` access conflicts with the live loan: N0243\n(\"temporary dropped while still borrowed\"). The fix is to bind the value to a local first.\n\n## 7. Returning references (E1, E2)\n\n- **Signature.** A function whose return type is a reference must name the parameters the result\n  may be derived from. With exactly one reference parameter (a `borrow`/`borrow mut` receiver\n  counts), that parameter is implied (elision). Otherwise the return type must be written\n  `borrow(a, b) T` or `borrow mut(a) T`, naming reference parameters (N0245 if missing or wrong).\n  `borrow mut` results may be derived from `borrow mut` parameters only.\n- **Body check at each `Return(op)`.** For every loan `L ∈ loans(op)`:\n  - `L = Param_i`: `i` must be among the named parameters (N0245);\n  - `L` is a real loan whose place starts with `Deref` of reference parameter `p_i`: allowed if\n    `i` is named (N0245 otherwise). Examples: `borrow this.items` with `this: borrow Self`, or\n    `borrow (*a).f`;\n  - any other real loan borrows a local or temporary of this function: N0244 (\"returns a reference\n    to local data\").\n- **Callers** use the signature as the `Call` constraint in §3.\n\n## 8. What the v0 checker deliberately does not do\n\n- No references in aggregates, and no reference re-pointing — **relaxed in M6x** (OD-3,\n  `type-system.md` §25): aggregates with region parameters, scoped collections and re-pointable\n  reference fields. The checker keeps one loan set per value; writes into fields, elements and\n  through references add the written loans to the holder and to what its mutable loans point to\n  (iterated to a fixpoint); calls apply their signature flows; and a loan conflicts only at points\n  reachable from where it was created (holders such as a vector receive loans after their own\n  definition). Destroying a value whose `drop` may read its loans is a use of them.\n- Index-sensitive disjointness (`a[i]` versus `a[j]`) came in 2d with arrays, conservatively: all elements\n  overlap. Since M13 G5, two different **constant** indices are different places, so `borrow mut a[0]` and\n  `borrow mut a[2]` may be live together. That is sound because a constant index names one element whatever the\n  array's length. Any index that is not a constant still overlaps every element.\n- Moves and drops are 2c. The `StorageDead` points emitted now are where drops will go.\n\n## 9. Diagnostics\n\nEvery borrow error names the conflicting access (primary span) and carries notes for the loan's\ncreation point and for a later use of the reference that keeps it live, when one exists.\n",
    129 =>
        "# Nova Memory Model and Allocation Architecture (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. Safety rules referenced as O*/B*/E*/R*/H*/U* are\ndefined in `ownership.md`. Goose findings are in `research/goose.md` (commit\n`f605b5a`). Other prior art is in `research/comparative.md`. The CosmoOS kernel\nmapping is in `cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`.\n\n## 1. Candidate architectures\n\n### A. Ownership-based stack + heap (Rust-like)\n\nValues live on the native stack by default. Long-lived and dynamically sized data\nlive in owned heap objects (`Box`, `Vector`, `String`). Safety comes entirely\nfrom ownership and borrowing. Arenas are ordinary library types built with\n`unsafe` plus lifetime-carrying APIs.\n\n### B. Region-based + owned heap (Cyclone/Tofte–Talpin-like)\n\nEvery allocation is placed in a *region*. Regions are lexically scoped (stack frames,\ndynamic regions) or the heap region. Pointer types carry a region, and an\noutlives/effect system prevents dangling. Owned heap objects exist as unique\npointers in the heap region. Region inference reduces annotations.\n\n### C. Hybrid: ownership + explicit regions + conservative escape analysis\n\nArchitecture A's ownership/borrow core is *the only* safety mechanism. Regions\n(arenas, scratch stacks, fixed buffers) are **explicit library values whose\nallocations are loans of the region** (R1), so the borrow checker covers them\nwith no separate region type system. A conservative escape/placement analysis\n(M5) may move allocations to cheaper storage only when the move is\n**unobservable** (§5). Goose-derived techniques (data stacks for scoped\ntemporaries, construction in place, grow-only containers, typed pools, BCE)\nare adopted as **implementation strategies and library types**, not as the\nsafety argument.\n\n## 2. Evaluation\n\n| Criterion | A: Ownership | B: Regions | C: Hybrid |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Safety argument | One mechanism (affine + XOR borrows). Proven for the λRust core | Region type system + uniqueness. Proven for core calculi, but **two** interacting mechanisms in practice | Same as A (one mechanism). Regions reduce to loans. Escape analysis is outside the trusted base |\n| Kernel fit (no hidden allocation, failure values) | Good | Good if regions are explicit. Poor with inference (hidden placement) | Good: placement changes require the profile to permit them (§5), and the kernel profile forbids implicit ones |\n| Ergonomics for C#/TS users | Annotation burden for structs of references. v0 limits mitigate (E3) | Many annotations, or inference with unpredictable lifetimes (MLKit space leaks) | As A, plus `using arena = ...` for batch lifetimes. Diagnostics explain placement |\n| Implementation complexity | High (NLL borrow checker) | High (region inference + effects) **plus** uniqueness | A's complexity + an *optional* M5 pass. Region support costs nearly nothing extra |\n| Runtime cost | `malloc`/`free` per heap object unless the user picks an arena | Cheap bump allocation everywhere; risk of space leaks | Same as A by default. Arena and scratch where the user or proven-safe analysis selects them |\n| Predictability | High | Low with inference | High: placement is either explicit or reported by `--explain-allocations` |\n| Goose techniques usable | Some (in-place construction, BCE) | Data stacks map naturally to lexical regions | All the \"cheap, local\" ones (goose.md §9 key cautions) |\n| Failure mode if the analysis is wrong | Unsound borrow check → safety bug | Unsound region inference → safety bug | Unsound borrow check → safety bug. Unsound placement → **caught**, because placement output is re-validated by the checker (`architecture.md` §1) |\n\n### Recommendation: **C — Hybrid**\n\n1. It keeps **one** safety mechanism to specify, test and eventually verify. Goose's\n   own history shows the cost of a second, fragile mechanism: its lifetime checker is graded\n   \"C/D, structurally fragile\" by its own inventory, and roughly half of its\n   recent commits fixed holes (goose.md §9.4). ",
    130 =>
        "Option B would add a region type\n   system *alongside* uniqueness. Option C reduces regions to borrows.\n2. Arenas, scratch regions and fixed buffers are first-class from M4 with no\n   new type-system concept: \"an allocation borrows its region\" is checked by\n   the same B1/B2/E1 rules as `borrow x`.\n3. Escape analysis stays outside the safety argument (master prompt rules 10–11),\n   and its effects are visible and reportable.\n4. It fits CosmoOS: every allocation in the kernel profile names its allocator\n   type or region value, so nothing allocates implicitly.\n\nCost: the v0 restriction E3 (no references in user structs) is felt most\nfor arena-heavy code (e.g. AST nodes pointing to other arena nodes). The v0\nanswer is indices or `Span` offsets (Ex16), and the long-term answer is OD-3.\n\n## 3. Storage categories\n\n### 3.1 Native stack (C automatic storage)\n- Scalars, fixed-size structs/enums/arrays, references, `Box` pointers, temporaries.\n- Size limit per value: `maxStackValue` per profile (userland default 64 KiB,\n  kernel default 512 B). Larger fixed-size locals are a compile error suggesting\n  `Box` or a region. This avoids unbounded native-stack consumption.\n- No variable-length arrays (C VLAs and `alloca` are never emitted).\n- Kernel profile: per-function frame-size estimates are reported, and the deepest\n  call chain is checked against the kernel stack budget (C4/C7; see\n  `cosmoos/profiles.md`).\n\n### 3.2 Heap (owned)\n\nImplemented in C5 (`type-system.md` §30, `lib/core/alloc.nova`): `Layout` and `Allocator` with\n`allocate` and an `unsafe deallocate` (no `reallocate` yet), user allocators as field-less structs, for\n`Box` only.\n\n```nova\n// core library (sketch)\npublic struct Layout { size: usize; align: usize; }      // align: power of two\n\npublic interface Allocator {\n    // `this` is a free-standing allocator value: ZST token or a 'static handle.\n    unsafe fn allocate(borrow this, layout: Layout) -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;\n    unsafe fn deallocate(borrow this, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) -> void;\n    unsafe fn reallocate(borrow this, ptr: *mut u8, old: Layout, newSize: usize)\n        -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError>;                 // default: alloc+copy+free\n}\n```\n\n- *Free-standing allocator types* (`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap<Flags>`,\n  `SlabCache<T>`-backed allocator singletons) are zero-sized or `'static`.\n  Only these may parameterise `Box<T, A>`, `Vector<T, A>`, `String<A>` (H1).\n  This keeps `Box` one pointer wide and makes \"Box outlives allocator\"\n  trivially true.\n- *Instance allocators* (arenas, pools, fixed buffers) are values. Their\n  allocations are scoped values carrying a loan of the instance (R1). A\n  `Pool<T>` hands out `PoolBox<T>` (scoped, individually freeable). An `Arena`\n  hands out `borrow mut T` / `SliceMut<T>` (freed in bulk).\n- **Allocator lifetime**: free-standing allocators live for the whole program\n  (or kernel lifetime). Instance allocators cannot be dropped while allocations\n  are live (B2). Outstanding allocations therefore can never outlive their\n  allocator (master prompt §5.5).\n- **Ownership of allocations**: always exactly one owner (O1). Freeing with the wrong\n  allocator is impossible because the allocator is part of the type (`A`) or of the loan.\n- **Thread safety**: an allocator type declares `Sync` if it may be used\n  concurrently (`SystemHeap`, `KernelHeap`: yes; `Arena`, `FixedBuffer`: no).\n  Enforced once threads exist (M-later).\n- **Failure**: every allocating API returns `Result<_, AllocError>`.\n  Convenience `...OrPanic` / `!` usage is allowed in userland and **rejected by\n  a lint (error) in the kernel profile**.\n- **Testability**: `FailingAllocator<A>(failAfter: n)` and a fault-injection\n  hook in `SystemHeap` (env var in hosted test builds) make every failure path\n  testable (M3 acceptance).\n  - *As implemented in M3b (ADR 0003):* `FailingAllocator` is a built-in, zero-sized allocator\n    type over the platform heap, steered by `FailingAllocator.failAfter(n)` and `.",
    131 =>
        "reset()`\n    (a process-wide countdown).\n  - The runtime counts every allocation attempt. `NOVA_FAIL_ALLOC_AT=k` fails attempt *k*,\n    and `NOVA_ALLOC_REPORT=1` prints `nova alloc: attempts=<n> live=<m>` when `main` returns.\n  - The test runner's sweep uses both (`testing.md`).\n\n### 3.3 Arenas (regions)\n\n- `Arena.create(parent: A, chunkSize)`, an instance allocator over a free-standing\n  parent. Bump allocation in chunks, with all chunks released at drop.\n- v0 contents must have no `drop` (R2), so bulk release equals destruction.\n  Arena-with-drop-list is OD-8.\n- `reset(borrow mut this)` requires no live loans (B2), so it is safe by\n  construction.\n- An arena is **not** the native stack: it is heap-backed (master prompt rule 9).\n\n### 3.4 Scratch regions (Goose-style data stacks)\n\nFrom goose.md §9.1/§9.2: a per-thread, compiler-known **scratch stack** used\nfor dynamically sized temporaries with nested lifetimes:\n\n```nova\nfn render(n: usize) -> Result<void, AllocError> {\n    using frame = Scratch.enter();                 // records watermark\n    let buf = frame.allocArray<u8>(n)?;            // bump; scoped to `frame`\n    ...\n}                                                  // watermark restored: one store\n```\n\n- Semantically an arena with LIFO discipline. Safety follows from R1 + R3\n  (`using` frames cannot be moved, and their allocations cannot escape).\n- Storage: hosted/userland uses a reserved virtual region with guard pages,\n  plus an explicit capacity check. Exhaustion returns `AllocError`\n  **unless** the frame was created with `Scratch.enterOrHeap()`, in which case it falls back\n  to the parent heap. Kernel: a fixed per-thread buffer of profile-defined size\n  with explicit checks. No reliance on page faults.\n- Nested frames must be strictly LIFO. *Corrected by ADR 0004:* `using` alone does not ensure\n  this. Nested frames come from `parent.enter()`, which mutably borrows the parent, and only\n  one root frame per thread may be live (a second `Scratch.enter()` panics).\n  Goose's \"one growable on top\" rule is **not** adopted in v0. The scratch region\n  hands out fixed-size allocations only. Growable scratch arrays are a future\n  extension (OD-12).\n\n### 3.5 Fixed-buffer and pool allocators\n- `FixedBufferAllocator.over(mut storage)` borrows its backing storage mutably\n  for its whole lifetime, so the backing array cannot be touched directly while it is used.\n- *As implemented in M4c (ADR 0004):* `Pool<T>` is a fixed-capacity pool whose `take(v)` returns a\n  scoped `PoolBox<T>`: a box that holds a loan of the pool and returns its slot when destroyed\n  (`type-system.md` §19.6). `SlotMap` with generation-checked handles is still M6.\n- `Pool<T>` / `SlotMap<T>`: typed slots with generation counters (goose.md §9.8\n  plus Vale). Handles are free-standing `Handle<T>` values (index + generation)\n  validated on access (`get(h) -> borrow T?`).\n\n### 3.6 Static data\nString literals and `static let` constants (immutable). `static var` is\n`unsafe` to access in v0 (data races); safe statics with synchronisation come with\nconcurrency.\n\n## 4. Collections and invalidation\n\n| Type | Ownership | Growth | Element references | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `[T; N]` | value | — | loans of the array | Copy iff T Copy |\n| `Slice<T>` / `SliceMut<T>` | scoped borrow | — | reborrows | bounds-checked |\n| `Vector<T, A>` (M6d, type-system §23.1) | owns buffer | reallocates (amortised ×2) | loans of the vector. Growth requires `borrow mut` → **rejected while element loans are live** | contiguous; **no address stability promise** |\n| `StableVector<T, A>` (M6e, type-system §24.2) | owns chunks | appends a chunk, never moves | loans with *shared* access permitted during `push` (grow-only: goose.md §9.3) | push takes `borrow this` with interior bump. Not `Sync` |\n| `SlotMap<T, A>` (M6e, type-system §24.1) | owns slots | may reallocate | `Handle<T>` (not a loan) validated by generation | stable *identity*, not stable addresses |\n| `String<A>` ",
    132 =>
        "/ `StringView` | owns / scoped | as Vector | as Vector | UTF-8 invariant |\n| `Box<T, A>` | owns one object | — | loans of the box | `T?` niche |\n\nIterator invalidation is impossible in safe code: iterators are scoped values\nholding a loan of the collection.\n\n## 5. Placement analysis (M5; on by default since M9e) — rules\n\nThe analysis may change *where* a value lives only under these conditions:\n\n1. **Box → native stack / scratch**: allowed iff (a) the Box is created and\n   dropped in the same function on all paths, (b) it is never moved into a\n   callee or returned, (c) its address is not exposed to `unsafe`/FFI, (d)\n   the profile marks `A` as *placement-transparent* (`SystemHeap` in userland: yes;\n   **`KernelHeap`: no**, because kernel allocation is observable through\n   accounting, and failure injection must remain meaningful), and (e) the size is\n   below the profile's stack limit. Failure behaviour: the original program could fail\n   with `AllocError`, and the optimised one cannot. This is permitted only because the\n   placement-transparent contract declares allocation failure of that allocator\n   \"may spuriously succeed\".\n2. **Never** the reverse: a scoped/borrowed value is never promoted to the heap to\n   make a program type-check (master prompt §7 and rule 11). There is no such\n   code path, because placement runs after the borrow check on accepted programs only.\n3. Destruction order and timing are preserved (master prompt rule 12): drops of\n   demoted Boxes run at exactly the same NIR points.\n4. Every decision is reported by `--explain-allocations`:\n   `src/a.nova:12:9: Box<Node> placed on stack (does not escape; SystemHeap is placement-transparent)`\n   `src/a.nova:20:9: Box<Node> stays on heap: moved into call to 'insert' at 21:5`\n\n### 5.1 The M5 implementation (`PlacementAnalysis`)\n\nThe analysis works per function on NIR after drop elaboration. It is deliberately simple and\nconservative: a box it cannot follow stays on the heap. Rule 1's conditions become the\nfollowing checks.\n\n- **Sites.** A site is `R = Box.create(v)` or `Box.createIn(a, v)`, with `R` a whole local. The\n  site is **eligible** when:\n  - the allocator is `SystemHeap`, the only placement-transparent allocator so far (rule 1d).\n    `FailingAllocator` is not transparent: its failures are its purpose;\n  - `T` is at most 1 KiB, and the function's stack-placed boxes total at most 4 KiB (rule 1e);\n  - each site has at most one box alive at a time, so one storage slot per site suffices. A site in\n    no loop of the function's graph has this by construction. A site in a loop has it when every\n    local of its flow set `F` (below) ends, by its `StorageDead`, on every path from the site back to\n    itself (M9e): no local can then hold one execution's box when the site runs again, and since the box\n    does not escape, it has been dropped by then. A local that is not ended inside the loop keeps the\n    box on the heap (\"may outlive its loop iteration\"). The interpreter checks this rule: a site whose\n    previous box is still alive when it runs again is an interpreter fault.\n- **The flow set** `F` of a site is the set of locals that may hold its box: `R` itself, and every\n  whole local assigned (moved or copied) from a place rooted in `F` whose type is the box type or\n  `R`'s result type. That includes the unpacked `Ok` payload and copies of the result. The\n  analysis iterates until `F` stops growing.\n- **Escapes.** The box stays on the heap, with the first reason found, if:\n  1. a local in `F` has more than one definition. Its drops could then see a box from elsewhere;\n  2. a value of the box type or of `R`'s type, read from a place rooted in `F`, flows anywhere\n     but into a local of `F`: a call or built-in argument, an aggregate, a return, or a\n     non-local place (rule 1b);\n  3. a place rooted in `F` of the box type is borrowed **mutably** (`borrow mut Box<T>`). The\n     borrower could replace the box, and drop ours with the heap's drop glue. Borr",
    133 =>
        "owing the box\n     shared, or borrowing its contents either way, is fine: the borrow checker ends those\n     borrows before the box's drop;\n  4. a `Drop` destroys a place rooted in `F` that is neither a whole local of the box type nor\n     inside its contents. Destroying contents in place is fine: drop elaboration does that to\n     the fields left after a move out. A result holding the box, though, would be destroyed by\n     the heap's drop glue. A result *may* be destroyed where it cannot hold the box: in a block\n     dominated by a non-`Ok` target of a switch on that result's own discriminant, where the target\n     is entered only from the switch. That is the error path of `?` and `!`, whose drop glue sees\n     an `Err`.\n\n  Reads through the box (`b.f`, `b.f = x`, moving a field out) and `discriminant` of the result\n  are not escapes. Rule 1c (unsafe and FFI), from M8a: a function that makes a raw pointer from a reference, or a\n  reference or slice from a raw pointer, keeps all its boxes on the heap (reason \"address exposed\";\n  `type-system.md` §27.5). Rule 1a (created and\n  dropped in one function) follows from rules 2 and 4.\n- **Demotion.** A stack-placed site writes `v` into a per-site slot of the function's frame and\n  makes `Ok(&slot)` without allocating; that allocation cannot fail.\n  - A `Drop` of a box local in `F` destroys the contents only, and does not free.\n  - A `BoxFree` of one does nothing.\n  - Drops therefore run at the same NIR points with the same contents (rule 3).\n\n## 6. No-GC baseline and cycles\n\nNo tracing GC, no implicit RC. Ownership is a forest (H3). Graph-shaped data\nuses one of: `SlotMap` handles, arena + indices, or (future, OD-13) an explicit\n`Rc<T>`/`Weak<T>` library type with documented cycle-leak behaviour. In\nthe kernel, explicit refcounted *kernel objects* wrap CosmoOS's own refcount\nprotocol (`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`). That is a different\ntype (`KRef<T>`) with type-specific release, not a generic `free`.\n\n## 7. Goose technique adoption summary\n\n| Goose technique (goose.md §9) | Nova adoption | Milestone |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data stacks for scoped temporaries | `Scratch` regions (§3.4), with heap fallback and a no-MMU kernel mode | M4/M5 |\n| One-growable-on-top non-moving growth | Not adopted; `StableVector` instead | M6 |\n| Grow-only vs grow-shrink split | `StableVector`, monotone lengths feed BCE | M6/M9 |\n| Root inference by monomorphisation | **Rejected** (whole-program, fragile). NLL + E1–E4 instead | — |\n| Construction into destination / guaranteed NRVO | NIR destination places + guaranteed RVO for single-named-result functions | M2 (RVO), M7 |\n| Inline variable-size layouts | Future opt-in `@flat` types (OD-14) | post-M9 |\n| Relative references + verifier | Future library feature for zero-copy formats | post-M9 |\n| Type-safe pools | `Pool<T>`/`SlotMap<T>` with generations | M6 |\n| BCE with index types, 2^48 size cap, `checkfree` | Adopt the size cap in the spec now (all `len ≤ 2^48`), BCE + `@checkfree` in M9 | M0 (spec) / M9 |\n| `return … from` | Not adopted; `Result` + `?` | — |\n| Share-nothing workers | Possible library model later | post-M9 |\n\n**Spec rule adopted now (from Goose BCE):** every object, slice and collection\nlength is `≤ 2^48 − 1` elements. Allocators reject larger requests with `AllocError`. This makes\n`len * sizeof(T)` overflow-free for `sizeof(T) < 2^15` and lets index arithmetic\non `usize` be proven non-overflowing.\n",
    134 =>
        "# Milestone 1 — Smallest End-to-End Compiler (specification)\n\nStatus: M0 proposal (master prompt §23.L). Starts after OD-1/2/5/6/11 are approved.\n\n## 1. Language subset (\"Nova-M1\")\n\n- **Types:** all fixed-width integers (`i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`, `isize`, `usize`), `bool`, `void`.\n  No floats in M1 (keeps the kernel subset in reach and avoids float formatting). The full\n  static semantics are in `type-system.md`.\n- **Declarations:** one file or a few files in one package. `namespace`, top-level\n  `fn` (including `public`/`internal`), `let`, `var`, optional type annotations,\n  `const` integer constants.\n- **Statements:** blocks, `if`/`else`, `while`, `loop`, `break`, `continue`, `return`,\n  expression statements, assignment and compound assignment (`+= -= *= /= %=`).\n- **Expressions:** literals, names, calls (including recursion), unary `- !`, binary\n  `+ - * / % == != < <= > >= && ||` (short-circuit), `& | ^ << >>` on\n  unsigned types, lossless `as`, `truncate<T>(x)`, parentheses.\n- **Built-ins:** `Console.write(x)` / `Console.writeLine(x)` for integer, `bool` and\n  **string literals only**. `Process.exit(code: i32)`.\n- **Semantics:** overflow, division and shift checks panic (OD-6). Panic = message +\n  abort (OD-5). Definite initialisation of `var` (the M2 init analysis restricted to\n  scalars). Strict left-to-right evaluation. `main() -> i32` or `main() -> void`.\n- **Excluded:** structs, arrays, references, heap, strings as values, generics,\n  imports across packages.\n\n## 2. Pipeline delivered\n\nLexer → Parser (with recovery) → AST → name resolution → type check → HIR → NIR\n(CFG, with `CheckedBinOp` and `Panic` terminators) → LIR → C emitter\n(labelled blocks) → host C compiler → executable. The full IR stack exists from M1,\nso M2 adds analyses, not a new pipeline.\n\nRuntime: `runtime/include/nova/rt.h` (types, checked arithmetic, `nova_panic`\ndeclaration), `runtime/hosted/rt_hosted.c` (stdio + abort),\n`runtime/cosmoos/user/rt_cosmoos_user.c` (`write(1, …)`, `abort`).\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria\n\n1. **Corpus:** ≥ 150 tests in total: ≥ 40 parse-pass/fail (including the full precedence\n   table and recovery cases), ≥ 40 check-fail (types, names, definite init,\n   lossy `as`, literal out of range), ≥ 50 run-pass (including `gcd`, recursive `fib`,\n   Collatz, integer `isqrt`, an FNV-1a hash of constant input, nested loops with\n   `break`/`continue`), ≥ 15 run-fail (each overflow, division, shift and `MIN/-1` case with the\n   expected exit status and message).\n2. **C quality:** all generated C compiles with\n   `-std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wshadow -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion\n   -Wmissing-prototypes -Wstrict-prototypes -Wvla -Wcast-qual -Wundef -Werror`\n   on clang and gcc.\n3. **Differential:** every run-pass test gives identical output under {clang, gcc}\n   × {-O0, -O2} × {no sanitizer, ASan+UBSan} on the macOS (arm64) dev host. Linux x86-64 runs in CI.\n4. **Cross-compile:** the corpus compiles for `x86_64-unknown-none-elf` and\n   `aarch64-unknown-none-elf` with CosmoOS `USER_CFLAGS` (compile only; linking is C1).\n5. **Determinism:** compiling the corpus twice gives byte-identical C.\n   Output is independent of the host path.\n6. **Diagnostics:** every check-fail test asserts the error code and primary span.\n7. **Docs:** `grammar.md` (complete for Nova-M1), `intermediate-representation.md`\n   (HIR/NIR/LIR as implemented), `diagnostics.md`, `testing.md` updated. Limitations listed.\n\nC1 then reuses the M1 compiler unchanged: `hello.nova` prints `NOVATEST: PASS`\ninside CosmoOS on both arches (`roadmap.md`).\n\n## 4. The exact first implementation increment (1a) — **done 2026-10-22**\n\nDelivered: `Nova.slnx` (the .NET 10 solution format) with `Nova.Compiler`, `Nova.Cli` (`novac`)\nand `Nova.Compiler.Tests`; `docs/grammar.md`; `docs/diagnostics.md`; 151 passing tests\n(see `testing.md` §4). Deviation from the plan: the corpus runner lives inside the xUnit\nproject for now. A separate `Nova.TestR",
    135 =>
        "unner` is deferred to 1c, when tests need to invoke C\ncompilers. Trivia (comments) is not retained yet; it is needed only for the M10 formatter.\n\nOriginal plan:\n\n1. `Nova.sln` with `Nova.Compiler`, `Nova.Cli`, `Nova.Compiler.Tests`,\n   `Nova.TestRunner`. `Directory.Build.props`: nullable enabled, warnings as errors,\n   `<Deterministic>true</Deterministic>`. `global.json` pinned to SDK 10.0.401.\n2. `SourceText`, `Span`, `Diagnostic` and a text renderer.\n3. Lexer for the full M1 token set: struct tokens, interned identifiers,\n   literal validation, and keyword table with reserved words.\n4. Parser: recursive descent for declarations and statements, Pratt parsing for expressions,\n   recovery at `;`/`}`/declaration keywords. AST plus a `--dump-ast` printer.\n5. Corpus runner for `parse-pass`/`parse-fail` with `// error: N0xxx` annotations.\n   The first ~40 parser tests.\n\nExit criterion for 1a: parser tests green, AST dumps stable.\n\n**Increment 1b — done 2026-10-22.** `type-system.md` (spec first), then:\n- the type checker with bidirectional literal typing;\n- constant folding with cycle detection (`IntegerSemantics` defines the exact operator semantics\n  that 1c's C runtime must match);\n- definite assignment and missing-return analysis on structured HIR;\n- the HIR printer and `novac check [--dump-hir]`.\n\nSemantic analysis runs only on files that parsed cleanly. Two simplifications, both documented:\nthe flow analysis runs on HIR, not NIR (no moves exist yet, so no fixpoint is needed), and all\nfiles of a compilation share one item scope.\n\n**Increment 1c — done 2026-10-22.**\n- `intermediate-representation.md` (spec first).\n- NIR (CFG) and the HIR→NIR lowering, with explicit evaluation order and short-circuit branches.\n- The C emitter: labelled blocks, checked helpers, zero-initialised locals, deterministic output.\n- The runtime: `nova/rt.h` (freestanding helpers), `nova_rt_core.c` (formatting, panics) and\n  `nova_rt_hosted.c` (stdio). They are embedded in the compiler and written next to the generated C.\n- `novac build [--emit c|exe] [--dump-nir]`.\n- Tests: run-pass/run-fail corpora, the runtime agreement harness, determinism, C snapshots and\n  freestanding cross-compiles.\n\nDeviation: no separate LIR yet (`intermediate-representation.md` §4).\n\n**Acceptance status (§3), 2026-10-22:**\n\n| # | Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Corpus ≥ 40 parse, ≥ 40 check-fail, ≥ 50 run-pass, ≥ 15 run-fail | met: 38 files / 72 cases, 19 files / 99 cases, 51, 19 |\n| 2 | Generated C warning-free under the strict set on clang **and gcc** | met: Apple clang 21 locally, plus clang 18 and GCC 13.3 on Linux CI (run 39792099920: every run test passed under GCC) |\n| 3 | Differential: {clang, gcc} × {-O0, -O2} × {plain, ASan+UBSan} agree | met, with one difference: ASan+UBSan runs with clang only. The GCC configurations are `-O0`, `-O2` and portable-overflow |\n| 4 | Corpus compiles for both CosmoOS triples with CosmoOS `USER_CFLAGS` | met, and exceeded: all 70 run programs compile with the exact flags, link against CosmoOS libc, **and run correctly inside CosmoOS** on x86-64 and AArch64 (`cosmoos/c1-report.md`) |\n| 5 | Determinism | met (byte-identical C, no host paths) |\n| 6 | Diagnostics tests assert code and position | met (line, and column where annotated) |\n| 7 | Docs | met: grammar, type-system, IR, diagnostics, testing |\n\n**Increment 1d — done 2026-10-22.**\n- C1 on both architectures, plus the whole run corpus inside CosmoOS (`cosmoos/c1-report.md`).\n- Linux CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) with GCC 13 and clang 18.\n- GCC's `-Wclobbered` fired on the runtime harness's setjmp use; it is now disabled for that\n  harness only.\n\n**Milestone 1 is complete** (all criteria in §3 met). 1b (resolution + types + HIR),\n1c (NIR + LIR + C + runtime + run tests) and 1d (C1 script) follow.\n",
    136 =>
        "# Milestone 10 — Developer tooling (plan and report)\n\nStatus: **complete** (2026-10-26; the acceptance is the last section). The owner put M11 (language gaps) before this milestone. On 2026-10-26\nthe owner chose the order (tools first, modules and packages last), git dependencies as well as path ones, a VS Code\nextension, and a formatter with one canonical style and no options.\n\n## Why\n\nThe master prompt's Milestone 10 asks for a formatter, a package manager, a language server, debugging integration,\na documentation generator and build-system improvements. Today:\n- **Formatting:** none. The lexer discards comments (`Lexer.SkipWhitespaceAndComments`), and no document states a\n  style. The repository's 465 `.nova` files (14,902 lines) are written in one style: 4-space indents, no tabs, braces\n  on the line that opens them, `} else` on one line; 33 lines are longer than 120 columns.\n- **Editors:** nothing. The parser recovers after an error (grammar.md §3), and diagnostics have spans, so a\n  language server can report them as the user types; the AST has spans for every node, and the HIR has types.\n- **Packages:** none. A compilation is one package, every file in one item scope; `namespace` is parsed and recorded\n  but does not qualify names, and `import` is reserved but not implemented (type-system.md §1, language-spec.md §5).\n  OD-11 proposes `nova.toml` with path dependencies.\n- **Debugging:** `-g` and `--line-directives` map the generated C back to Nova lines (M7a), so gdb and lldb step\n  through Nova source. Names in the debugger are the C names (`nvF_…`).\n- **Documentation:** `///` doc comments are part of the grammar (§1.2) and attach to items, but nothing reads them.\n- **Building:** `novac build` takes files on the command line; there is no project file.\n\n## Plan\n\n| # | Increment | Spec | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **T1** | Formatter: `novac fmt` (rewrite files, or `--check`), one canonical style. The lexer keeps comments; the formatter prints the source's tokens and decides the whitespace between them, with a 120-column limit. Every parseable file in the repository must format to the same AST, keep every comment, and be a fixed point | `formatting.md` (new) | done |\n| **T2** | Documentation generator: `novac doc` writes a Markdown page of a package's `public` items, their signatures as `novac fmt` prints them and their `///` comments; `--missing` lists the undocumented ones. `docs/api/core.md` is generated from `lib/core` | `tooling.md` (new) §1 | done |\n| **T3** | Language server: `novac lsp` over stdio (LSP 3.17, JSON-RPC by hand, no new dependency). Diagnostics as you type, hover with types, go to definition, document symbols, formatting through T1. A VS Code extension in `editors/vscode`: the TextMate grammar and the client that starts `novac lsp` | `tooling.md` §2 | done |\n| **T4** | Debugging integration: a debugging guide for lldb and gdb (how Nova names and lines appear), commands that show locals and the backtrace by their Nova names (`editors/debug`), and a test that runs a real debugger on a `-g` build | `tooling.md` §3 | done |\n| **T5** | Names across packages: a dependency's public items as `geometry.Point`, `import geometry;` / `import geometry.Point;`, `public`/`internal` enforced between packages, package-prefixed C names | ADR 0013 (accepted 2026-10-26), type-system §2 | done |\n| **T6** | Packages and building: `nova.toml`, path and git dependencies (a commit pinned in `nova.lock`), `novac new`, `novac fetch`, and `build`/`check`/`run`/`doc`/`fmt` from a manifest; the language server checks a document with its package | ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4 | done |\n\nT5 and T6 change the language and the build model; they got a design and an ADR for the owner before code, as G7 and\nG8 did: ADR 0013, accepted on 2026-10-26 with every recommendation (package-level names, `namespace` retired, `src/` by\ndefault, git `rev`/`tag`/`branch` pinned in the lock, dependencies' C names prefixed).\n\nEach increment: ",
    137 =>
        "spec first, positive and negative tests, the full test suite, the docs, then commit and CI.\n\n## Results\n\n### T1: the formatter\n\n- `novac fmt file.nova…` rewrites files (or every `.nova` file under a directory), `--check` lists the files that\n  would change and exits 1, `--stdout` prints one. A file with a syntax error is reported and left alone.\n- **Design** (`src/Nova.Compiler/Formatting/Formatter.cs`): the lexer now records every comment's span\n  (`LexedSource.Comments`). The formatter prints the file's own tokens in order and decides only the whitespace between\n  them, and the trailing commas of `formatting.md` §3.3. A walk over the AST marks:\n  - spacing roles: a call's `(`, generic `<` `>`, unary and postfix operators, pointer `*`;\n  - containers that are laid out on one line or broken: blocks, bodies and match arms one per line, lists with the\n    author's line breaks;\n  - continuations: the places after `=>`, `=` or a binary operator, and before a chained `.`, where a long line may\n    break.\n\n  It renders, breaks the first construct of the first line over 120 columns, and renders again until every line fits\n  or nothing is left to break.\n- Printing the source's tokens is what makes the result safe. The formatter cannot misspell a literal or drop a\n  parenthesis, and it checks its own output anyway. It re-parses the result and refuses to return text whose tree,\n  tokens (commas aside) or comments differ from the original's: an internal error, never a changed program.\n- Decisions made while implementing, now in the spec:\n  - the blocks of an if/else chain break together;\n  - attributes keep the author's line break;\n  - trailing comments align over consecutive lines only;\n  - a trailing comment does not count toward the width;\n  - the order of the kinds to break (§3.4).\n- **The repository:**\n  - Of its 465 `.nova` files, 27 are parse-fail tests, which the formatter refuses as it should. Of the other 438,\n    253 were already in canonical form.\n  - The 11 files outside the test corpus that were not, and the README's examples (the ten `readme-*` files), are now formatted, and the\n    README's copies with them. The changes are long lines broken, `else` joined to its `}`, `{ }` written `{}`, and\n    hand-aligned trailing comments.\n  - The corpus is not reformatted: its annotations name lines and columns.\n- Tests: `FormatterTests`, 26 style cases (each also a fixed point), CRLF and final-newline handling, a syntax error,\n  every parseable repository file formatted twice (697 at first, including git-ignored fuzz findings; then the tracked\n  source directories only), and the repository's own code already formatted.\n\n### T2: the documentation generator\n\n- `novac doc path… [-o file] [--title text]` writes one Markdown page for the files' public items (tooling.md §1),\n  made from the syntax alone, so code that does not type-check yet can be documented. `--missing` lists the public\n  items and methods without a doc comment, `path:line: kind name`, and exits 1 if there is any.\n- **Design** (`src/Nova.Compiler/Documentation/DocGenerator.cs`):\n  - Each file is parsed and also formatted. The two token streams are the same apart from commas, so each item's\n    signature is cut from the formatted text: a long signature is broken as `novac fmt` breaks it.\n  - Doc comments are read from the comments before an item's first token, attributes included, keeping the\n    indentation after `/// `. The lexer's own doc text strips every leading space, which would flatten nested\n    Markdown lists.\n- `docs/api/core.md` is generated from `lib/core`. Seven public methods there had no doc comment\n  (`PhysAddr`/`VirtAddr` `of`, `bits`, `offset`; `MmioPtr.address`); they have one now, and `lib/core` passes\n  `--missing`.\n- Tests: `DocTests`:\n  - a package with every kind of item, public and not: the expected page, which keeps a nested list and shows a\n    signature broken by the formatter;\n  - the `--missing` lines;\n  - a syntax error;\n  - repeated",
    138 =>
        " headings getting distinct anchors;\n  - `docs/api/core.md` up to date, with nothing missing in `lib/core`.\n\n### T3: the language server and the VS Code extension\n\n- `novac lsp` serves LSP 3.17 over standard input and output (tooling.md §2). JSON-RPC and its framing are written\n  by hand on `System.Text.Json`, so `novac` gained no dependency (`src/Nova.Compiler/Tooling/LanguageServer.cs`).\n- **Checking:** each open document is checked as its own package with `lib/core`, from the editor's text, on every\n  change. Its diagnostics are published with their codes, and notes become related information.\n- **Requests:**\n  - hover: a local's `let`/`var` and type, a function's signature, a field's type, any expression's type;\n  - definition: locals, parameters, functions and methods, fields, and struct, enum and interface names;\n  - document symbols;\n  - formatting, through T1.\n- **The index** (`SemanticIndex.cs`) answers hover and definition from the typed tree (HIR) and the syntax tree. It\n  takes the innermost thing at the position. The HIR has 57 node kinds, so it is visited through its records'\n  properties, by reflection, rather than by a switch that would have to follow every new kind. A document that does\n  not parse has no typed tree: hover and definition then answer null, and the diagnostics say why.\n- `editors/vscode`: the TextMate grammar, the language configuration, and a client that starts `novac lsp` through\n  `vscode-languageclient` (the `nova.server.path` setting). It is built with Vite+ (`vp`, at the owner's suggestion),\n  outside the .NET build. `vp pack` bundles the client into `dist/extension.cjs`: `vsce` could not package pnpm's\n  `node_modules` layout, and one file is smaller anyway. `vp run package` makes the `.vsix`: 7 files, 119 KB.\n  - `vp test`, run 2026-10-26: 19 passed. `grammar.test.mjs` (16) tokenizes Nova lines with `vscode-textmate`, as\n    VS Code does, and checks each token's scope. `server.test.mjs` (3) drives the built `novac lsp` through\n    `vscode-jsonrpc`, the library under VS Code's own client, so the hand-written framing is known to work with it.\n  - `vp lint` is clean, and `vp fmt` keeps the JavaScript and JSON in its default style.\n  - The extension has not been installed in a running VS Code.\n- Tests: `LspTests`, through in-memory streams:\n  - initialize;\n  - diagnostics on open, re-checked on change and cleared on close;\n  - hover and definition at the positions above, and null where nothing is;\n  - symbols and formatting;\n  - a document that does not parse;\n  - an unknown method, and exit without shutdown;\n  - the grammar's keywords equal to the compiler's.\n\n  Also, by hand, the real `novac lsp` process over pipes: initialize, diagnostics, hover, shutdown, exit status 0.\n\n### T4: debugging\n\n- Nothing needed to change in the compiler. `-g` already writes `#line` directives and compiles with `-g -O0` (M7a),\n  so lldb and gdb stop and step at Nova lines, and a breakpoint on `nova_panic` stops at a failed check (tooling.md\n  §3.1). What was missing was knowing what the C names are, and seeing Nova names.\n- `tooling.md` §3 is the guide: the commands for both debuggers, and the C name of each Nova name (`c-backend.md`\n  §2.1).\n- `editors/debug/nova_lldb.py` and `nova_gdb.py` add two commands, with the naming rules shared in\n  `nova_names.py`:\n  - `nova-locals` prints the frame's locals and parameters by their Nova names (`p = { x: 3, y: 4 }`), without the\n    compiler's temporaries;\n  - `nova-bt` prints the backtrace as `Counter.bump at demo.nova:6`.\n- Tests: `DebuggerTests`.\n  - It builds a program with `-g` and runs lldb (macOS) or gdb (elsewhere) in batch mode: breakpoints at two Nova\n    lines, a method and a function, and then `nova-locals` and `nova-bt`. Also a breakpoint on `nova_panic`, whose\n    backtrace must reach the overflowing function and its caller at their Nova lines. The demangling rules are\n    checked through `nova_names.py` itself.\n  - Run 2026-10-26 under lldb on macOS:",
    139 =>
        " passed.\n  - gdb is in neither the Mac nor the Debian box. The Linux CI job installs it: the first run (`898d0b8`, gdb 15.1 on\n    Ubuntu 24.04) passed both debugger tests through `nova_gdb.py`.\n\n### T5: names across packages (ADR 0013)\n\n- `Compilation.Create(PackageInfo root, dependencies, …)` compiles a package with its dependencies, as one program\n  (ADR 0013 D4). The old entry point is a package with no dependencies, so every existing program is unchanged.\n- **Qualified names** (`PackageNames.cs`): before type checking, `geometry.X` with `geometry` a dependency becomes one\n  dotted name, in types, expressions, patterns, and struct literals (which the parser joins, `p.T { … }`). A\n  dependency's name cannot name anything else in the package (N0315, a rule added in the spec), so the rewrite needs\n  no scopes, and every place in the checker that dispatches on the shape of a callee works unchanged.\n- **The checker** keeps one item table per package (and one for `lib/core` and the built-in items) instead of one\n  for everything. A lookup tries the package's own items, then a dotted name's dependency, then the file's imports,\n  then `lib/core`.\n  - A dependency's items are named with their package (`geometry.Point`) in messages and in the HIR.\n  - The C backend writes the `.` as `_` (`nvT_geometry_Point`, `nvF_geometry_area`), so two packages may declare the\n    same name. Imported C structs keep their C names.\n- **Checks:**\n  - N0310–N0313 are reported syntactically, before any declaration. The checker's own \"cannot find\" for the same\n    name is left out, so each mistake is reported once.\n  - N0312 also covers fields and methods.\n  - N0314 runs once types are resolved.\n  - N0109 is an `import` after an item.\n- Tests: `PackageTests`.\n  - A program using a dependency through qualified names, an import, a generic struct and function of the\n    dependency, an interface bound, an enum in a pattern, and the same names declared in both packages. It is\n    compiled with clang under the strict warnings and run: the right output, and both `nvT_Point` and\n    `nvT_geometry_Point` in the C.\n  - Nine negative cases, one per check.\n  - N0313 with two imports.\n  - N0314.\n  - An import bringing public names only.\n  - A dependency that cannot see its dependent.\n\n### T6: packages, the lock file, the commands\n\n- `src/Nova.Compiler/Packages/`:\n  - `Toml.cs`, the TOML subset of manifests and locks, with N0320 at the place of the mistake;\n  - `Manifest.cs`, `nova.toml` and its checks (N0321);\n  - `Workspace.cs`, resolution, the lock, and the git cache.\n  Nothing new is a dependency of `novac`: git is run as the `git` executable.\n- **Resolution** (tooling.md §4.2–§4.3):\n  - Dependencies are found breadth-first from the package being built. A dependency is known by its own manifest's\n    name (N0322), and must be a library (N0321).\n  - One name through two sources is N0326, naming both dependents; cycles are N0327.\n  - `novac fetch` resolves tags and branches to commits in a bare clone of each repository\n    (`$NOVA_HOME/git/db`), checks each commit out once (`$NOVA_HOME/git/checkouts`), and writes `nova.lock`:\n    sorted by name, git entries with their commit and a SHA-256 of the checkout's manifest and sources.\n  - Every other command resolves through the lock. A manifest the lock does not match is N0324, and an edited\n    checkout N0325. A missing checkout is made again from the cached repository, even with `--offline`; with no\n    cache, `--offline` is N0323.\n- **Commands:**\n  - `novac new <name> [--lib]`;\n  - `novac fetch [--offline]`;\n  - `parse`, `check`, `build` and `run` without files use the nearest manifest's package with its dependencies. The\n    manifest's profile, `float`, kind and C files are defaults the command line can override, and an executable goes\n    to `out/<name>`;\n  - `doc` and `fmt` without files take the package's own sources.\n- **The language server** checks a document of a package with that package and its lock",
    140 =>
        "ed dependencies, never\n  fetching. Other documents are checked alone, as before.\n- Run by hand, 2026-10-26:\n  - `novac new app` and `novac new geometry --lib`;\n  - a build before `fetch` (N0324);\n  - `fetch`, then a build that prints 84 (a path dependency);\n  - the same with `geometry` from a local git repository by tag;\n  - a checkout edited in the cache (N0325);\n  - `--offline` with the checkout deleted (rebuilt from the cache), and with no cache (N0323).\n- Tests:\n  - `PackageManagerTests`, 29:\n    - every manifest field, and 13 bad manifests, each with its one code;\n    - a path dependency locked (the lock's exact text) and compiled;\n    - a dependency added after `fetch`;\n    - a conflict, a diamond reaching one package, two cycles, a missing, a misnamed and an executable dependency;\n    - the hash;\n    - git by tag, by branch and by commit, where the lock keeps the commit when the branch moves;\n    - an unknown tag, an edited checkout, and offline with and without the cache.\n\n    The git cases use a repository the test makes, so the suite needs no network.\n  - `LspTests` gains a document checked with its package: no diagnostics, and hover on the dependency's function,\n    where the same text alone is an error.\n- Risk R-12 (fetched dependencies) is new in `risks.md`.\n\n### Acceptance (2026-10-26)\n\nThe criterion (`roadmap.md`): each tool has its spec, its tests, and the repository's own code kept passing it. Each\nincrement above records its spec and tests; the close-out checked the whole.\n\n| # | Spec | Tests | The repository |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 `novac fmt` | `formatting.md` | `FormatterTests`: 27 style cases, every parseable file formatted twice | its own code and the README's examples are formatted, which the suite checks |\n| T2 `novac doc` | `tooling.md` §1 | `DocTests` | `docs/api/core.md` generated from `lib/core`, current, nothing missing |\n| T3 `novac lsp`, `editors/vscode` | `tooling.md` §2 | `LspTests` (22); `vp test` (19: grammar, and `novac lsp` through `vscode-jsonrpc`) | the grammar's keywords are the compiler's |\n| T4 debugging | `tooling.md` §3 | `DebuggerTests`, under lldb locally and gdb in CI | — |\n| T5 names across packages | ADR 0013, type-system §2 | `PackageTests` | — |\n| T6 packages | ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4 | `PackageManagerTests` (30) | `programs/nwc` is a package (`nova.toml`, `sources = [\".\"]`): `novac build` there writes `out/nwc`, and a test resolves and checks it |\n\n**Nothing else moved.**\n- **Generated C.** Every run-pass, run-fail and emit-c corpus program, the benchmark programs and `nwc` (222 programs)\n  were compiled to C with M11's compiler (`b8b0907`) and with M10's. 221 are byte-identical. One differs by a single\n  source location: `run-pass/mmio.nova` reaches `PhysAddr.offset`, which moved from line 10 to line 15 of\n  `lib/core/address.nova` when T2 documented it.\n  - T5 rewrote the type checker's name lookup, so this was the risk. Since M11's acceptance measured every\n    benchmark, the identical C means its performance figures still hold, without rerunning them.\n- **CosmoOS**, no boots: `c4.py` on both arches, `c6.py`, `c7.py --no-boot`, `c8.py --no-boot`: every check passes,\n  and CosmoOS's tree is unchanged.\n- **Fuzzing:** 400 programs locally (seeds 9400000–9400399): no findings.\n- **CI:** every M10 commit passed on Linux and Windows. Windows ran the git tests with Windows paths; Linux ran the\n  debugger tests under gdb.\n\n**Not done, and recorded:**\n- The VS Code extension was built (a `.vsix`), its client library tested, and installed in the owner's VS Code 1.143.1 (`code --install-extension`, 2026-10-26). Its behaviour in the editor has not been observed by the agent.\n- `namespace` declarations are accepted with no effect; making them an error is left for later (ADR 0013 D2).\n- Builds stay whole-program, with no cache (ADR 0013 D4): to be decided after measuring build times.\n- Monomorphised closures, owning closures and effect-polymorphic functions remain open (O",
    141 => "D-9a, OD-9b, OD-9c).\n\n**Result:** M10 is complete.\n",
    142 =>
        "# Milestone 11 — Language gaps (plan and report)\n\nStatus: **complete** (2026-10-25 to 2026-10-26). G1–G6, G7a–G7d (floats, ADR 0011) and G8a–G8c (closures, ADR 0012)\nare done; the acceptance is the last section. The owner chose these gaps before M10 (tooling), which is next.\n\n## Why\n\nM9's suite and the README's tour were written in Nova next to the same programs in C, Rust and the others. Each\nplace where Nova needed a workaround, or could not say something at all, is recorded in `milestone-9.md` (findings\nF1–F8) and in the README (\"What Nova does not have yet\"). This milestone takes them in order of use and cost. Each\ngap was first reproduced against the compiler of M9 (2026-10-25); the probe is quoted.\n\n## Plan\n\n| # | Gap | Probe on M9's compiler | Spec | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **G1** | Nested patterns: `Ok(E.A(x))`, `Some(3)`, `Err(IoError.NotFound)` | `expected ')', found '.'` (N0100) | type-system §15.2, §18.4 | done |\n| **G2** | `for` over an array, slice or vector of a non-`Copy` element: the variable is a reference | `cannot move a 'S' out of the value 'shapes' refers to` (N0251) | §16.4 | done |\n| **G3** | Borrow-checker precision: a reference local reassigned while a loan of its *referent* is live (a cursor walking a list), and a temporary reassigned in a loop while its earlier value is still borrowed (F1) | `cannot assign to 'node' while 'node.next' is borrowed` (N0240); `cannot assign to a temporary value while a temporary value is borrowed` (N0240) | `ownership.md` B-rules, `lifetimes.md` | done |\n| **G4** | Small conveniences: byte and character literals (`'k'` as `u8`), `wrapping*`, `checked*` and `saturating*` arithmetic (promised by OD-6), number formatting into a `String` | `'char' values are not supported in M1` (N0216); `type 'u8' has no methods` (N0234); `'String' has no method 'appendNumber'` | §4, §6, §23.2 | done |\n| **G5** | `if` and `match` as expressions | `expected expression, found 'if'` (N0102) | §5, §9, §15.3 | done |\n| **G6** | A reference to an optional as a type (F2: `borrow T?` is an optional reference) | — (no syntax) | §13.5, §22.1, grammar.md §2 | done |\n| **G7** | Floating-point types (OD-23) | `unknown type 'f64'` (N0204) | a design and an ADR first | [ADR 0011](decisions/0011-m11-floating-point.md) accepted; G7a–G7d done |\n| **G8** | Capturing closures (OD-9) | `expected ')', found ':'` (N0100) | a design and an ADR first | [ADR 0012](decisions/0012-m11-closures.md) accepted; G8a, G8b, G8b′, G8c done |\n\nNot a gap any more: F3 (`Some(b)` with `b: borrow Box<T>` as a `borrow T?`) is accepted and runs (probe, 2026-10-25).\nThe `contents` helper in `benchmarks/suite/linked-list/main.nova` is no longer needed.\n\nG7 and G8 change the language beyond its v0 design: each got a design and an ADR for the owner before code (both accepted\n2026-10-25). Their increments are G7a–G7c then the first math functions (ADR 0011 §5 Q5, §6), and G8a–G8c (ADR 0012\n§6).\n\n## Results\n\nRun results are in `testing.md`'s run log; this section says what each increment changed.\n\n### G1: nested patterns (`1b949a7`)\n\n- A variant pattern's payload holds sub-patterns: `_`, a binding, an integer or `bool` literal, or another variant\n  pattern, to any depth (type-system §15.2, §18.4). The HIR keeps the pattern tree (`HirPattern`).\n- Exhaustiveness and unreachable arms use Maranget's usefulness algorithm (`Semantics/PatternUsefulness.cs`), and a\n  missing case is reported with a witness, e.g. `Ok(Shape.Square(_))`.\n- Lowering tests and binds through the tree (`NirBuilder.LowerNestedMatch`); flat matches lower as before.\n- Restriction: a sub-pattern below a reference payload is N0205 for now.\n- The fuzzer generates nested `Err` patterns and literal sub-patterns.\n\n### G2: `for` over non-`Copy` elements (`8e2c65b`)\n\n- Over a sequence of a non-`Copy` element, the loop variable is a `borrow T` to the element in place, the rule of a\n  `match` on a reference; the sequence cannot change w",
    143 =>
        "hile it lives. `Copy` elements are still copied (§16.4).\n- Fixed on the way: a shared slice of pointers is declared `T *const *` in C, not `const T **`.\n\n### G3: borrow-checker precision (`b5b359f`)\n\n- A Write or Dead of a reference local does not conflict with a loan whose place goes through it, such as\n  `(*r).next`: overwriting a reference changes what it points to, not the object (`lifetimes.md`, \"Overwriting a\n  reference is not a use of its referent\"). This accepts a cursor walking a list and a temporary reassigned in each\n  loop iteration (F1). A box is not a reference: a loan through `*b` still conflicts when `b` is overwritten.\n\n### G4a: character literals (`4704317`)\n\n- `'k'`, `'\\n'` and `'\\u{1F600}'` are integer literals of their Unicode scalar value: `u32` by default, any integer\n  type that holds the value, ASCII only in 8-bit types (N0206), and usable as patterns (§4).\n\n### G4b: explicit overflow behaviour\n\n- `wrappingAdd/Sub/Mul`, `checkedAdd/Sub/Mul` (returning `T?`) and `saturatingAdd/Sub/Mul` on every integer type;\n  none of them panics (§6). The names are reserved on integer types: an interface method of that name cannot be\n  implemented for one (N0228).\n- C: `rt.h` helpers `nv_wrapping_*`, `nv_try_*` (for the checked methods) and `nv_saturating_*`, each in both of\n  `rt.h`'s variants: the compiler builtins and the portable fallback MSVC uses. Signed wrapping goes through the\n  unsigned twin and `nv_as_iN`, so it has no implementation-defined conversion. The checked operators' helpers did\n  not change.\n- The interpreter computes the exact result in `Int128` and then wraps, tests or clamps it.\n- Tests: `run-pass/overflow-methods.nova` (every width, at and past both bounds, compiled and interpreted) and\n  `check-fail/overflow-methods.nova`. The fuzzer now generates `wrapping*` and `saturating*` calls.\n\n### G4c: numbers into a `String`\n\n- `s.appendNumber(n)` appends any integer in decimal (§23.2): `Result<void, AllocError>`, two-phase and unchanged on\n  failure, like `append`. A non-integer argument is N0207.\n- C: `rt.h`'s `nv_format_i64`/`nv_format_u64` write the digits into a 20-byte compound literal, which lives to the\n  end of the enclosing block, and the string's existing `nvVA_` helper appends them. `INT64_MIN` is negated in\n  unsigned arithmetic. The interpreter shares one append routine (`AppendText`) between `append` and `appendNumber`.\n- Tests: `run-pass/append-number.nova` (every type at its bounds, a reference, a literal, a loop with `?`; also run by\n  the fault-injection sweep) and `check-fail/append-number.nova`.\n- The suite's `parsing` and `string-building` workloads keep their hand-written digit loops: they mirror the other\n  languages' versions, and M9's figures were measured on them.\n\n### G5: `if` and `match` as expressions\n\n- `if (c) a else b` and `match (e) { p => v, ... }` in expression position (type-system §15.3, grammar.md). A\n  branch is an expression, or a block that cannot complete (`{ return; }`, `{ continue; }`); a block that can\n  complete has no value (N0235, new). An `if` or `match` that begins a statement is still the statement form, and\n  an `if` expression needs its `else`.\n- **Design choices** (for the owner to revisit): branches are expressions, as in Kotlin, rather than blocks that end\n  in a value, as in Rust. This keeps C#'s and TypeScript's statement blocks unchanged and needs no new rule for\n  \"the last expression of a block\"; a branch that needs statements is a statement `if` assigning a `var`. `?:` is\n  not available, since `?` is already postfix (`Result` propagation). Without an expected type, the first branch\n  that is neither flexible nor `null` gives the type, so `x + if (c) 1 else 2` with `x: u8` is `u8` arithmetic.\n- HIR: an `HirStmtValue` holds the statement form, whose value branches end in an `HirYield`; NIR lowering assigns\n  each yield to one temporary of the enclosing statement, so moves, drop flags, loans and temporaries follow from\n  the existing N",
    144 =>
        "IR analyses. A branch's temporaries end with the branch (a reference to one is N0243). The\n  missing-return analysis looks into branches, so a `break` in a branch counts as an exit of its loop.\n- Tests: `run-pass/branch-expressions.nova` (strings, references with two sources, a consuming match, nested\n  patterns, generics, drops of the branch taken only, `continue` and `break` from a branch, evaluation order;\n  compiled and interpreted), `parse-pass` and `parse-fail/branch-expressions.nova`, `check-fail/branch-expressions.nova`\n  and `check-fail/branch-expression-ownership.nova`. The fuzzer generates `if` and `match` expressions.\n\n### G6: references to optionals (F2)\n\n- A type may be parenthesised (grammar.md §2), so a reference to an optional is `borrow (T?)` or\n  `borrow mut (Box<Node>?)`; `borrow T?` is still an optional reference, and `(borrow T)?` spells it out\n  (type-system §22.1). After `borrow`, a `(` begins a source list only when it holds names and a type follows the\n  `)` (§13.5): `borrow(a) (T?)` names a source.\n- Diagnostics print the two as written, `borrow i32?` and `borrow (i32?)`; before, both printed as `borrow i32?`.\n- Nothing else changed: the type existed (a borrow of an optional place had it) and behaves as any reference, so a\n  list can be appended at its tail through a `borrow mut (Box<Node>?)` slot. Parenthesised types are not part of\n  the generic-argument lookahead in expressions: `g(a < (b), c > (d))` stays two comparisons.\n- Fixed on the way: an assignment whose target fails (N0211) no longer adds N0260 for a `null` value.\n- Tests: `run-pass/optional-references.nova` (compiled and interpreted), `check-fail/optional-references.nova`,\n  `parse-pass/parenthesised-types.nova` (new `.ast` snapshot, reviewed).\n\n### G7a: floating-point types (ADR 0011)\n\n- `f32` and `f64` (type-system §35):\n  - literals rounded once in their own type, and flexible: an unsuffixed literal takes an expected `f32`;\n  - `+ - * /` and comparisons by IEEE, never panicking;\n  - lossless `as`, `round<T>`, and `truncate<T>` with its `checked` and `saturating` forms;\n  - `bits()` and `fromBits`;\n  - constants folded with the run-time rounding.\n\n  No new diagnostic codes.\n- C: literals by their bits (`nv_f64_from_bits`), plain operators, and `rt.h` helpers.\n  - The helpers keep conversions free of C's undefined behaviour. `nv_ftoi_*` compares with exact exclusive bounds\n    first. `nv_f64_to_f32` rounds finite values beyond `FLT_MAX` itself: to `FLT_MAX` below the midpoint\n    2^128 − 2^103, to infinity from it.\n  - `rt.h` checks the IEEE formats and `FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0`, and turns contraction off by pragma (clang, MSVC).\n    novac passes `-ffp-contract=off` (`Toolchain.SemanticFlags`).\n  - A new panic kind, `NV_PANIC_FLOAT_RANGE` (\"float out of range\").\n- Interpreter and constant folding share `FloatSemantics`:\n  - each operation in .NET's `float` or `double`;\n  - integer-to-float conversion rounded in one step from the exact integer, so a `u64` to `f32` is never rounded\n    twice through a double.\n- Two emitter shortcuts excluded floats: folding `x == x` to `true` (false for NaN), and comparing two constants as\n  integers.\n- Tests:\n  - `run-pass/floats.nova`: literals, arithmetic, infinities, NaN, signed zeros, every conversion at its\n    boundaries, bits, constants, structs, generics and branch expressions. The output is identical compiled and\n    interpreted, and every value was checked against Python's IEEE arithmetic.\n  - `run-fail/float-truncate-nan.nova` and `float-truncate-range.nova`;\n  - `check-fail/floats.nova`;\n  - `emit-c/floats.nova` (snapshot);\n  - `rt.h`'s helpers in a C program under `-Wconversion -Werror`, with UBSan's `float-cast-overflow` check.\n- Fix (`d112351`). C4 on x86-64 showed that `rt.h`'s `FLT_EVAL_METHOD` check broke every kernel object: CosmoOS's\n  `-mgeneral-regs-only` leaves no SSE there. The float parts of `rt.h` now apply only under `NV_FLOATS`, which a\n  program with floats defines. `Frees",
    145 =>
        "tandingTests` builds its cross objects with that flag too.\n\n### G7b: printing floats\n\n- `Console.write`, `writeLine` and `String.appendNumber` print `f32` and `f64` (type-system §35.7). The text is the\n  shortest decimal that reads back as the value, the nearest on a tie of length, laid out as ECMAScript's\n  Number::toString (`1e+21`, `1.5e-7`, `0.000001`).\n- C: Schubfach (`runtime/hosted/nova_rt_float.c`).\n  - It uses integers only, and a 617-entry table of 126-bit powers of ten, generated exactly by a Python script.\n    The script also checked the three floor-log approximations over every input they get.\n  - Unlike the paper's Java code, it allows one-digit results (`5e-324`).\n  - The file is linked only into programs that print a float.\n- Interpreter: an exact search over rationals (`FloatSemantics.ShortestDigits`).\n  - Finding: .NET 10's own shortest formatting is wrong at some powers of two. 2^-25 prints as\n    `2.980232238769531E-08`, which reads back as a different double.\n  - So .NET serves as neither formatter nor oracle. Python's `repr` and Schubfach agree with each other, and with\n    the search.\n- ADR 0011 gained a §5a with these deviations, and the exponent sign `+`.\n- Tests:\n  - `FloatFormatTests`: C against the interpreter on 75,000 values in every C configuration, plus ECMAScript layout\n    cases;\n  - `run-pass/float-printing.nova`: the expected text from Python and numpy, not from either implementation;\n  - offline: Python and numpy on 412,042 values. On the shared box, every `f32`: 4,278,190,082 values, all but the\n    NaNs, each reading back and none with a shorter decimal next to it. Also 5,010,501 doubles against Python\n    `repr`. 0 failures.\n\n### G7c: `--no-float`, math methods, floats in the fuzzer\n\n- `--no-float` (`build` and `check`; type-system §35.9) makes every float type, literal and `fromBits` N0297, a new\n  code. `c4.py` to `c8.py` pass it, and the corpus directive `// option: no-float` tests it.\n- Math methods (§35.8): `sqrt`, `abs`, `floor`, `ceil`, `min` and `max`, by IEEE.\n  - None calls libm. ADR 0011 Q5 expected single instructions, but baseline x86-64 has none for `floor` and `ceil`\n    (`roundsd` needs SSE4.1).\n  - So `floor`, `ceil`, `abs`, `min` and `max` are exact portable C in `rt.h`. `sqrt` is the hardware instruction:\n    `__builtin_elementwise_sqrt` with clang, and `__builtin_sqrt` under `-fno-math-errno` with gcc\n    (`Toolchain.SemanticFlags` now passes it).\n- The fuzzer generates float expressions:\n  - literals at the edges, arithmetic, the math methods, conversions both ways, and branch expressions;\n  - in print statements, in comparisons, and in integer expressions through `saturatingTruncate`, and rarely\n    through `truncate`, which may panic.\n- Its first run found a bug: `if` and `match` expressions whose branches are all flexible float literals typed as\n  `i32` (G5's default, from before float literals were flexible). They now default to `f64` (§15.3).\n- Tests: `run-pass/float-math.nova` (signed zeros, halves, 2^52 + 1, NaN, f32; compiled and interpreted, the same),\n  `check-fail/no-float.nova`, new cases in `check-fail/floats.nova`, and the regression in\n  `run-pass/float-printing.nova`.\n- Not done here: a float workload in `benchmarks/suite`, in every language, run on the Ryzen box (G7d). MSVC builds\n  every float corpus program in CI's Windows job.\n\n### G8a: function values (ADR 0012)\n\n- `fn(T…) -> R` types (grammar.md §2, type-system §36.1): a new type kind, `Function`. It is Copy, holds no loans\n  and is not FFI-safe, separate from C's `extern \"C\" fn`.\n- Values:\n  - a package function's name is a value of its type, and so is a generic instance named with its type arguments;\n  - not values (N0298, new): `extern` and `unsafe` functions, since calls through values are safe; functions with\n    region parameters, whose signatures can store loans through an argument; `main`; and functions whose\n    reference result has several possible sources.\n- Calls through a l",
    146 =>
        "ocal, a parameter or a struct field are safe.\n  - They lower to a builtin. The borrow checker's existing rule for builtins gives the result the loans of every\n    reference argument, which is sound because the function held cannot have region parameters.\n  - Effects were `sleep` and `alloc` until G8b′'s effect lists.\n- C: a typedef `nvN_…` per type, the function's address as the value, and an indirect call. The interpreter\n  reuses its function addresses.\n- Fixed on the way, older than G8: an array of a struct with an unknown field type crashed the checker (`HasLayout`\n  ignored error types). It now reports the unknown type.\n- Tests:\n  - `run-pass/function-values.nova`: arguments, branch expressions, generic instances, struct fields, a\n    higher-order helper, scoped parameters, `borrow mut`, no result; compiled and interpreted, the same;\n  - `parse-pass/function-types.nova` (new snapshot, reviewed);\n  - check-fail `function-values`, `function-value-loans`, `function-value-effects` and `array-of-broken-struct`.\n  - Two older tests changed with the rules: `let fnValue = two;` is now valid (`calls.nova`), and `match (f)` on\n    a function is N0207 (`enums.nova`).\n\n### G8b: closures (ADR 0012, amended in its §5a)\n\n- Literals `(x: i32, y) => expression` or `=> { block }` (grammar.md §2, type-system §36.2).\n  - A capture-free literal is a plain function value.\n  - One that captures is a `borrow fn(…) -> R`, or a `borrow mut fn(…) -> R` if it changes what it captures.\n- Representation: one pointer. The closure points at its environment, a hidden local of the enclosing block whose\n  first member is the code pointer, followed by a reference to each capture (C11 6.7.2.1p15). So a closure type is an\n  ordinary reference type, and the existing borrow checker enforces every capture rule. G8b adds two `IsScoped` cases\n  (the environment and the callable object) and no checker rule.\n- The body is a synthetic function. It is checked where the literal is, with the outer scopes visible. Captures are\n  recorded at name lookup (shared), and become mutable at a mutable borrow or an assignment rooted at them.\n  - A reflection-based scan of the body's syntax decides beforehand whether the literal names an outer local; locals\n    cannot be shadowed, so the answer is exact.\n  - NIR maps each captured local to its place through the environment parameter.\n- Conversions: a function value becomes a closure through an adapter (an environment holding a copy of it), and a\n  `borrow fn` becomes a `borrow mut fn`. The reverse is N0299 (new), and so is a capturing closure where a plain `fn`\n  is expected. An uninferable parameter or result is N0236 (new).\n- C: `nvCE_<id>` environments, `nvCB_<id>` bodies taking `const void *env`, `nvCK_…` code-pointer typedefs, and a call\n  `(*f)(f, args…)`.\n- Found on the way: G6's source-list lookahead did not count `fn` as a type start, so `borrow(r) fn(…)` misparsed.\n  Fixed.\n- Tests:\n  - `run-pass/closures.nova`: shared and mutable captures, capture-free literals, an adapter, nested closures,\n    generics, `this`, a closure in a struct with a region parameter, a loop, a reference parameter, a `String`;\n    compiled and interpreted, the same;\n  - `parse-pass/closures.nova` (new snapshot, reviewed);\n  - check-fail `closures`, `closure-loans` (N0240, N0242, N0244, N0251 from the existing rules) and\n    `closure-effects`.\n\n### G8b′: effect lists on function and closure types (ADR 0012 §3.7, amended in §5a)\n\n- `@effects(…)` before `fn` in a type (grammar.md §2, type-system §36.3): `@effects() fn(i32) -> bool`,\n  `borrow @effects() fn(i32)`. The list is part of the type; no list means both effects. A call through a value has\n  its type's effects, so `@context(atomic)` and `@context(irq)` code can call closures and function values.\n- How values are held to a list:\n  - a named function where a type with a list is expected takes that type. After effect inference, the effect checker\n    compares the functio",
    147 =>
        "n's effects with the list (N0295 where it is named, with the call chain);\n  - a closure literal, or an adapter closure, gets the list as its body's `@effects` bound;\n  - any other value converts only to a type whose list contains its own (N0299 otherwise).\n- Inference does not narrow types: `let f = isEven;` is a `fn(i32) -> bool`. That keeps `var` and branch expressions\n  as they were, at the cost of writing the type where a list is wanted.\n- C: names gain `e<bits>` after `N`/`Q` only for types with a list, so every existing name is unchanged. Typedefs\n  differing only in the list name the same C type.\n- Found on the way (older than G8b′, not fixed): an integer literal fixes a type parameter before a function argument\n  is considered, so `apply(square, 7)` with `square: fn(i64) -> i64` reports `fn(i32) -> i32` expected (N0205).\n- Tests:\n  - `run-pass/effect-lists.nova`: atomic and irq functions calling closures and function values, a generic\n    higher-order function, an `@effects()` function calling through a value, adapters, a field, conversions to wider\n    lists; compiled and interpreted, the same;\n  - `parse-pass/effect-lists.nova` (new snapshot, reviewed), `parse-fail/effect-list-placement.nova`;\n  - check-fail `effect-lists` (N0290, N0299) and `effect-list-violations` (N0295: named functions directly and\n    through a call, an adapter, a literal's body, an assignment, a field, calls in irq and atomic contexts).\n\n### G8c: library methods taking closures (ADR 0012 §3.9, amended in §5a)\n\n- `sortBy`, `forEach`, `any`, `all` on slices, arrays (`a[..]`) and vectors, and `retain` on vectors\n  (type-system §36.4). They are written in Nova, in `lib/core/slices.nova`, as static functions of\n  `SliceAlgorithms<T>`; the method calls name them. Each closure is a `borrow mut fn` over `borrow T`, so elements\n  need not be Copy.\n- `sortBy` is an unstable introsort: median-of-three quicksort, insertion sort below 16 elements, heapsort past a\n  depth of 2·log₂ n. It allocates nothing, and with an inconsistent comparator still leaves a permutation.\n- Two new primitives the library needs, also public:\n  - `s.swap(i, j)` (§16.5): both indices checked, any element type;\n  - `v.truncate(n)` (§23.1).\n  `retain` lowers to the library's retain over the elements, then a truncation, so the vector is evaluated once.\n- Effects: a call through a closure without an effect list, so not in atomic or irq code; effect-polymorphic\n  functions are OD-9c. Found on the way: `clear` had no effects in the effect checker although it runs the\n  elements' drops (§32.1). It and `truncate` now have their drops' effects.\n- Also found and fixed:\n  - **G8b:** a closure capturing a reference parameter emitted `const const T * *`, which clang rejects. Found by the\n    fuzzer. C now writes `T *const *`, and `run-pass/closures.nova` covers it;\n  - **older than G8:** an array literal of string literals, `[\"kiwi\", \"fig\"]`, was typed `[str; 2]` and crashed NIR\n    lowering. Array elements are no longer read through references to unsized values, so it is N0246, like any\n    array of string views;\n  - **G8b:** a rejected call with an untyped closure argument also reported N0236 for each parameter. Such closures\n    are now checked quietly.\n- The fuzzer generates closures: local ones, shared and mutating, called once; function values; and `forEach`,\n  `any`, `all`, `sortBy` (a key comparison, so equal keys test the sort's determinism) and `retain` on its arrays and\n  vectors.\n- Tests:\n  - `run-pass/closure-methods.nova`: every method on arrays, sub-slices and vectors; Copy, `String` and boxed\n    elements; empty and one-element slices; random, duplicate, all-equal (heapsort), sorted and reversed inputs;\n    an inconsistent comparator; `swap` and `truncate`. Compiled and interpreted, the same;\n  - `run-fail/slice-swap-bounds.nova`;\n  - check-fail `closure-methods`, `closure-method-loans` (N0241) and `closure-method-effects` (N0295, including\n    `clear` and `truncate`",
    148 =>
        ").\n- The sort benchmark, `benchmarks/sort/` (README there), sorts 10 × 1,000,000 values on the Ryzen. Sort times in\n  seconds (median of 9, minus the generate-only baseline; run after the campaign, on an otherwise quiet box):\n\n  | Compiler | Nova `sortBy` | C `qsort` | the same introsort in C | the same, comparator opaque |\n  |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n  | clang 18.1.8 | 1.07 | 1.17 | 0.63 | 0.91 |\n  | gcc 12.2.0 | 1.13 | 1.20 | 0.67 | 0.94 |\n\n  - `sortBy` is 6–9% faster than `qsort` with both compilers.\n  - Against the same algorithm in C, the gap has two parts:\n    - about 0.28 s is the indirect call: C slows from 0.63 s to 0.91 s when its comparator is opaque, as Nova's is;\n    - the remaining 0.16–0.19 s (Nova over `same-opaque`, about 1.2×) is Nova's checks and closure environment.\n      `--no-bce` changes nothing (1.08 s and 1.10 s in the first run, against 1.10 s and 1.11 s with elimination),\n      so it is not the index checks that elimination can remove. Not analysed further.\n  - Inlining the comparator, as C can when it sees the function, would need monomorphised closures (OD-9a).\n- The G8c campaign on the shared Debian box: 60 minutes, seeds from 9200000, 10 jobs, closures generated.\n  - 8,517 programs, 1 finding (seed 9202817), older than G8.\n  - Under a condition that is never true (`if (1u16 < 0u16)`), the overflow-check analysis's facts contradict. So it\n    removed the check of a constant `1i32 + 2147483647i32` there, and clang `-Werror` rejected the overflowing\n    constant expression in the C.\n  - Fixed: operations on constants are decided by their exact result (optimisations.md §3.3), with\n    `run-pass/dead-constant-overflow.nova` (reduced from the finding by line deletion).\n\n### G7d: a float workload in the suite (ADR 0011)\n\n- `benchmarks/suite/nbody/`: the Benchmarks Game's n-body, the sixteenth workload, in Nova, C, C++, Rust, Zig and Goose\n  (suite README).\n  - The same algorithm everywhere: an array of five `f64` structs and 10,000,000 steps.\n  - The checksum combines the energy before and after, to 10⁻⁹. All eight builds print 169075164169077842, with and\n    without FMA contraction.\n- C's build gains `-lm` (for `sqrt` under glibc); nothing else in the suite's builds changed.\n- Ryzen, median of 5 (ms):\n\n  | C compiler | nova | c | cpp | rust | rust-checked | zig | zig-safe | goose |\n  |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n  | clang 18.1.8 | 624 | 614 | 619 | 818 | 824 | 361 | 412 | 612 |\n  | gcc 12.2.0 | 593 | 970 | 977 | 800 | 831 | 348 | 412 | 710 |\n\n  - **Nova and C** are within 2% under clang.\n  - **gcc:** C and C++ take 1.6× Nova's time because of `errno`. Nova's C is compiled with `-fno-math-errno`\n    (§35.8), and C's is not, so gcc keeps a call to libm's `sqrt` for the errno case. Checked on the same box:\n    - gcc's C goes from 0.98–1.04 s to 0.58–0.59 s with `-fno-math-errno`;\n    - clang's changes little (0.63–0.68 s to 0.61 s).\n  - **Rust** takes 1.3× C's time under clang. Not investigated.\n  - **Zig** takes 0.58× C's time. Not investigated (its LLVM, not `--cc`, compiles it).\n  - **Checks:** Nova's bounds and overflow checks cost nothing visible here. The only integer operations are loop\n    counters over constant bounds, which elimination removes.\n\n### Acceptance (2026-10-26)\n\nThe criterion (`roadmap.md`): each gap has its spec, positive and negative tests, and the probe that showed it now\npassing. Each increment above records its spec and tests. The close-out added a re-run of the probes, the README's\nown examples of the new features, and a regression pass against M9's acceptance.\n\n**The probes.** Each gap's probe, written again against the close-out compiler (`fc` below), built with clang and\nrun under `--interp`:\n\n| # | Probe | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| G1 | `match` on `Result<E, IoError>` with `Ok(E.A(x))`, `Err(IoError.NotFound)`; `Some(3)` | runs, same output; **found a backend bug** (below) |\n| G2 | `for (s in shapes)` over a `Vector` of a struct holding",
    149 =>
        " a `String` | runs |\n| G3 | a cursor walking a boxed list, reassigned while a reference made through it lives | runs |\n| G4 | `'k'` as a `u8`, `wrappingAdd`, `saturatingAdd`, `checkedAdd`, `String.appendNumber` | runs |\n| G5 | `let parity = if (…) \"even\" else \"odd\"`, a `match` expression | runs |\n| G6 | a parameter of type `borrow (i32?)` | runs |\n| G7 | `f64`, `sqrt`, printing | runs |\n| G8 | `retain` with a capturing closure, `sortBy`, a mutating `forEach` | runs |\n\n**Found by the probes:** a program's own `Result<S, IoError>`, with `S` a struct or an enum, did not compile to C.\nThe backend wrote its I/O conversion helper (`nvIOR_…`, c-backend.md §2) for every `Result<_, IoError>`, casting\nthe runtime's integer to `S`, which clang rejects. It is older than M11 (C3); the interpreter was not affected.\nFixed: the helpers are written only for the results I/O built-ins return (`void`, an integer or a descriptor), with\n`run-pass/io-error-results.nova`.\n\n**Where the gaps showed.**\n- F1 (`arena`) and F3 (`linked-list`): fixed in G3 and earlier; the suite keeps its helper functions so that\n  timings stay comparable (commented there).\n- F5: `parsing` now writes `'k'`, `'='`, `'\\n'` and `'0'` instead of their numbers; its C is unchanged apart from\n  one source column. `string-building` still appends each digit as a one-byte view, `digitText[d..d + 1]`: there is\n  no method that appends a single character to a `String`. Not one of this milestone's gaps; recorded here.\n- The README gained a feature entry for floats and closures and a tour section, \"Closures and floats\"\n  (`run-pass/readme-closures.nova`).\n\n**Regression.**\n- Local suite on macOS (clang): 2,401 passed.\n- CosmoOS, no boots: `c4.py` on both arches, `c6.py`, `c7.py --no-boot`: every check passes; CosmoOS's tree is\n  unchanged.\n- The whole suite in every language on the Ryzen (as 9f's A; `m11.sh`), sixteen workloads. Nova/C, median of five:\n\n  | Compiler | Nova/C (sixteen workloads) | median | 9f (fifteen) |\n  |---|---|---:|---|\n  | clang 18.1.8 | 0.89 (`large-objects`) to 2.45 (`string-building`) | 1.19 | 0.93 to 2.44, median 1.17 |\n  | gcc 12.2.0 | 0.61 (`nbody`) to 3.50 (`string-building`) | 1.46 | 1.02 to 3.54, median 1.40 |\n\n  The gcc row leaves out `recursion` (0.03 of C), as 9f's did. Without `nbody`, gcc's range is 0.96 to 3.50.\n  Two Nova rows moved by more than 9f's 15% noise bound against 9f's single run: `numeric` under clang (+16%) and\n  `binary-trees` under gcc (+21%). Their `main.c` is byte-identical to 9f's compiler's (`7510bd2`), and the runtime\n  only gained code they do not call. Re-run interleaved on the same box (`m11-ab.sh`: 9f's compiler, then the close-out's, three rounds\n  each, clang and gcc, Nova and C): the fastest of each round's five runs is the same for both compilers\n  (`binary-trees` under gcc 545–549 ms against 545–548 ms; `numeric` under clang 332–342 ms against 333–365 ms). Only\n  the medians spread, and C's spread as much between the two trees with identical code (`numeric` under gcc 223 ms\n  against 267–284 ms). The two rows are this shared machine's noise, not a change.\n- LZ4 and fbvalid, once per compiler, against their C originals:\n\n  | | clang | gcc |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | LZ4 compress, Nova/C speed (text, random, records) | 0.76, 0.53, 0.59 | 0.69, 0.60, 0.56 |\n  | LZ4 decompress, Nova/C speed | 0.99, 0.96, 0.76 | 0.84, 0.91, 0.70 |\n  | fbvalid, Nova/C time | 1.00 | 1.06 |\n\n  Random-data compression under clang, at 0.53, is below the README's \"compresses at 0.59–0.75\". That range was\n  wrong already at M9: 9f's own runs measured 142–144 MB/s against C's 261–272 (0.53–0.55), as `benchmarks/lz4`'s\n  earlier tables do (0.54–0.57). Interleaving 9f's compiler with the close-out's (`m11-ab2.sh`, three rounds per\n  compiler) gives the same speeds (random compression 141–144 MB/s against 135–144), and the generated C of both\n  ports is byte-identical to 9f's. The README now gives the medians of four clang runs per d",
    150 =>
        "ata set: compression\n  0.52–0.76 of C, decompression 0.77–1.02.\n- Fuzz campaign on the shared Debian box at the close-out code: 60 minutes, seeds from 9300000, 10 jobs. 8,502\n  programs, **0 findings** (1,748 panicked, 5,570 other exit statuses, as generated).\n\n**Result.** Every gap of the plan is closed, each with its spec, tests and passing probe; the probes found one older\nbackend bug, fixed. Nothing regressed against M9's acceptance. M11 is complete; M10 (tooling) is next.\n",
    151 =>
        "# M12 — Tooling for AI agents (plan and report)\n\nStatus: **complete 2026-10-26** (results below). The owner asked for \"MCP or any better feature AI agents can depend on\" and chose\nall three layers below. Each is specified in `tooling.md` before it is built, and tested as M10's tools were.\n\n## Why\n\nAgents write and fix code in a loop: write, compile, read the errors, change, compile again. Nova's compiler\nalready knows most of what an agent needs. But it says so only as text for people, its spec lives in a repository\nan agent may not have, and nothing tells a model which mistakes Nova programs most often contain. The layers below\nmake that knowledge available in the forms agents use, in order of how widely each one helps:\n\n1. **Machine-readable diagnostics with fixes** work in any agent harness, MCP or not, and in CI.\n2. **An MCP server** (`novac mcp`) gives an agent's host Nova's checker, formatter, interpreter, C generator, spec\n   and library documentation as tools and resources, with no repository checkout.\n3. **`llms.txt` and an agent guide** put the language in a model's context, and list the mistakes models make.\n\n## A1 — JSON diagnostics and fixes (`tooling.md` §5)\n\n- `--diagnostics-format=json` (the name `diagnostics.md` planned) on `check`, `build`, `run` and `fmt --check`: one JSON document on standard output (the\n  program's own output, for `run`, goes to standard output only when there are no errors). Each diagnostic has its\n  code, severity, file, a 1-based start and end line and column, the message, its notes with their spans, the\n  `docs/diagnostics.md` row's text, and its **fixes**.\n- **Fixes** are edits the compiler is sure of: a text range and its replacement, with a title. A fix is offered only\n  when applying it removes that diagnostic and introduces no other at that place, which the tests check for every\n  fix kind on every corpus case that offers it. The first set: `mut` before an argument for a `borrow mut`\n  parameter (N0249), `let` to `var` for a variable that is assigned or mutably borrowed (N0211, N0248),\n  `as` to `truncate<T>(…)` for a lossy cast (N0208), and a missing `;` (N0100 where the parser knows the token).\n- `novac fix FILES` applies every fix, re-checks, and repeats until none applies (at most 8 rounds), then prints\n  what remains. `--dry-run` prints the edits instead.\n- `novac explain N0249` prints a code's row of `diagnostics.md` and the spec sections it cites (planned in\n  `diagnostics.md` since M1).\n- The language server sends the same fixes as code actions (`textDocument/codeAction`).\n\n## A2 — `novac mcp` (`tooling.md` §6)\n\n- The Model Context Protocol over standard input and output: JSON-RPC 2.0, one message per line. Written in the\n  compiler with no new dependencies, as `novac lsp` is.\n- **Tools.** Every tool takes source text (one file or several, by name) or paths, and returns structured content\n  with a schema as well as text:\n  - `nova_check`: A1's diagnostics, with fixes;\n  - `nova_fix`: the source with every fix applied, and what remains;\n  - `nova_format`: the formatted source, or the error that prevents it;\n  - `nova_run`: run in the interpreter, **sandboxed**: no files, sockets, processes or environment; a step\n    budget and an output cap; standard input as text; the exit status, output and any panic with its location;\n  - `nova_emit_c`: the generated C, optionally with `--explain-allocations`, `--explain-bounds-checks` or\n    `--explain-overflow-checks` output, for either profile;\n  - `nova_explain`: a diagnostic code's row and the spec sections it cites;\n  - `nova_spec_search` and `nova_spec_section`: search the specification, and read one section;\n  - `nova_api`: the `lib/core` and built-in API for a name;\n  - `nova_symbol`: the type and definition at a position, from the language server's index.\n- **Resources.** The specification (`docs/type-system.md`, `grammar.md`, `diagnostics.md`, `unsafe.md`, and the rest of\n  the normative set), `docs/api/core.md`, and",
    152 =>
        " the guide, embedded in `novac` at build time so the server needs no\n  checkout and always matches the compiler it runs.\n- **Prompts.** `write-nova` (the agent guide, A3) and `port-c-to-nova` (the method of C6 and C9).\n\n## A3 — `llms.txt` and the agent guide\n\n- `site/public/llms.txt` (the llms.txt convention): what Nova is and links to the guide and spec pages as Markdown,\n  and `llms-full.txt`, the guide and the normative spec in one file, generated at site build time.\n- `site/guide/for-agents.md`: how to set up `novac mcp`, the edit-check loop with `--diagnostics-format=json` and\n  `novac fix`, and **common mistakes**, each a wrong program and its correction, compiled by `SiteTests` like every guide\n  example, so the page cannot drift from the compiler.\n\n## A4 — agent instructions in new packages (`tooling.md` §7; added 2026-10-26)\n\nThe owner asked for `novac new` to write an agent instructions file, as Vite+'s `vp create` does. `novac new` writes\n`AGENTS.md` with a Nova section between `<!--NOVA START-->` and `<!--NOVA END-->` (from `templates/AGENTS.md`, embedded\nin `novac`); `--agent claude|gemini|copilot|cursor` adds that agent's file (an import of `AGENTS.md` where the agent\nsupports one, a copy otherwise) and its MCP configuration for `novac mcp`; `--no-agent` writes none. `novac agents`\nrefreshes the section in an existing package and never changes text outside the markers or an existing MCP\nconfiguration. Tests: `AgentFilesTests` (choice of agents, every file's content, MCP configurations parse, refresh\nkeeps the authors' text, appends without markers, idempotent, every command the section names exists) and\n`CliTests` (`new` with and without agents, usage errors, `agents` from a subdirectory).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Each layer: spec, positive and negative tests; the repository's own tests run against it.\n- A1: every fix kind applied to every corpus case that offers it removes the diagnostic and adds none; the JSON\n  schema is checked against a snapshot.\n- A2: an MCP client test drives `novac mcp` through initialisation and every tool, including sandbox refusals and the\n  step budget, and checks responses against the protocol's schema for the version negotiated.\n- A3: `llms.txt` links resolve on the built site; the agent guide's examples pass `SiteTests`.\n\n## Results (run 2026-10-26)\n\n- **A1.** Fixes are attached where the checker and parser report (`Diagnostic.Fixes`): `mut` before an argument\n  (with `let` → `var` in the same fix when the place is a `let` local), a stray `mut` removed, `let` → `var` for an\n  assigned or mutably borrowed local, `truncate<T>(…)` for a lossy integer `as`, and a missing `;` after a declaration,\n  assignment, `return` or call. Each is offered only where the tests show it resolves the diagnostic; two rules were\n  narrowed by them: `mut` is not offered for a place behind a shared reference (it would be N0248), and `;` is not\n  offered after a non-call expression statement (`{ -1 }` would become N0225) or after an earlier error.\n  `--diagnostics-format=json`, `novac fix [--dry-run]`, `novac explain`, and quick fixes in the language server.\n  The specification and guide are embedded in `novac` (`Documentation/EmbeddedDocs.cs`).\n  Tests: `FixTests` (every corpus case that offers a fix, 34 fixes of all six kinds; targeted cases; cases where no\n  fix may be offered; the JSON shape; a catalogue row for every code), `CliTests` (the commands, in process), and\n  `LspTests` (code actions).\n- **A2.** `novac mcp` (`Tooling/McpServer.cs`): versions `2025-06-18`, `2025-03-26` and `2024-11-05`; nine tools, 75\n  resources, two prompts. The interpreter gained a sandbox (`InterpreterOptions.Sandboxed`, `StepLimit`,\n  `MemoryLimit`): files, sockets and processes are refused, the step budget and memory budget end the run with a\n  fault, and the heap reports out of memory at the memory budget. Tests: `McpTests` (negotiation, every tool, the\n  sandbox's refusals and limits, resources, prompts, protocol er",
    153 =>
        "rors).\n- **A3.** `site/guide/for-agents.md` (set-up, the loop, ten common mistakes, each compiled by `SiteTests`); `llms.txt`,\n  `llms-full.txt` and each page's Markdown, generated by `site/scripts/prepare.mjs`; all 39 links of `llms.txt`\n  resolve in the built site.\n\n| Criterion | Result |\n|---|---|\n| A1: every fix kind resolves its diagnostic on every corpus case that offers it | met (`FixTests`, 34 fixes) |\n| A1: the JSON schema | met (`FixTests.Json_document_has_the_documented_shape`, `CliTests`) |\n| A2: an MCP client test through initialisation and every tool, with the sandbox and the step budget | met (`McpTests`, 15 tests) |\n| A3: `llms.txt` links resolve; the agent guide's examples pass `SiteTests` | met |\n\nNot done: `nova_check` and the other tools take files, not a package directory (`nova.toml`); an agent checks a\npackage's files by listing them. The sandbox bounds what a program can reach and how long it runs; it runs in the\nserver's process, so it is not a boundary against a compiler or interpreter bug (risks.md).\n",
    154 =>
        "# Milestone 13 — The documented gaps (plan and report)\n\nStatus: **complete** (2026-10-28). G1–G6 and their follow-ups are done; the acceptance is the last section. The owner\nasked, on 2026-10-28, for Nova's remaining known gaps to be taken up while CosmoOS's CI is slow.\n\n## Why\n\nThe README and the earlier milestone reports name what Nova still lacks:\n\n- no userland threads and no data-race guarantee for them;\n- no dynamic dispatch (`dyn`, OD-10);\n- no owning, escaping closures (OD-9b);\n- no package registry or version solver: path and git dependencies only (ADR 0013);\n- vector and string growth 2–3.5× slower than C (`milestone-9.md` 9f);\n- a borrow checker that rejects some valid programs whose lifetimes cross functions.\n\nEach gap below was reproduced against the compiler at `b9fa451` (2026-10-28) before any change. The probe is\nquoted.\n\n## Plan\n\n| # | Gap | Probe at `b9fa451` | Spec | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **G1** | Vector and string growth: allocate, copy, free on every growth; an out-of-line call on every `push` and `append`; the vector's address escapes into that call, so its length lives in memory | `vector-growth` 1.79× C, `string-building` 2.72×, `parsing` 1.31×; peak heap 384 MiB against C's 256 (Mac, below) | `c-backend.md` §2 (vectors), §6 (`nova_realloc`) | done; measured on the Mac and the Ryzen box |\n| **G2** | Dynamic dispatch: `dyn Interface` behind a pointer (`Box<dyn Shape>`, `borrow dyn Shape`) | `expected '>', found identifier` (N0100) at `Box<dyn Shape>` | [ADR 0015](decisions/0015-m13-dynamic-dispatch-and-owning-closures.md) accepted 2026-10-28; type-system §21, §37 | done (G2a, G2b) |\n| **G3** | Owning closures: returnable and storable, owning their captures (OD-9b; ADR 0012 deferred it to come with `dyn`) | `expected the plain function type 'fn(u64) -> u64', but this closure captures 'n', so it is a 'borrow fn'` (N0299) | ADR 0015 §3.6; type-system §36.5 | done |\n| **G4** | Userland threads with a data-race guarantee: spawn and join, values that may cross threads, shared state only through locks or atomics | `cannot find 'Thread' in this scope` (N0200) | [ADR 0016](decisions/0016-m13-userland-threads.md) accepted 2026-10-28; type-system §38 | done (G4a–G4d) |\n| **G5** | Checker generality: valid programs the borrow checker rejects | — (a corpus to collect first) | `lifetimes.md` §8, type-system §16.5, §36.2 | done |\n| **G6** | A package registry and a version solver | — (ADR 0013 chose path and git only, as the owner asked) | [ADR 0017](decisions/0017-m13-package-registry.md) accepted 2026-10-28; tooling.md §4.6–§4.8 | done; index at github.com/vkuttyp/nova-index |\n\nThe order is G1 (no language change), then G2–G4, which build on each other, then G5 and G6. G2, G4 and G6 change\nthe language or the toolchain beyond their accepted designs: each gets a design and an ADR for the owner before code,\nas G7 and G8 did in M11.\n\n## G1: vector and string growth (done, 2026-10-28)\n\nThree changes, none to the language:\n\n1. **Growth by `realloc`.** `nova_vec_grow` allocates a first buffer and resizes a later one with the new\n   `nova_realloc` (`runtime/core/nova_rt_core.c`), over the new platform hook `nova_platform_realloc`\n   (`runtime/hosted/nova_rt_hosted.c`: `realloc`). A reallocation is one counted attempt, so the fault-injection sweep\n   sees the same attempts as before, and on failure the old block is unchanged and still live. The old and new\n   buffers no longer coexist, so the peak heap is C's.\n2. **A fast path.** The generated `push` and `append` (`CEmitter.EmitVectorHelpers`) call `nova_vec_grow` only when\n   the buffer is full, and `append` copies with `nova_mem_copy` instead of a byte loop.\n3. **No escaping address.** They pass the capacity through a local, not `&v->cap`. Before, the vector's address\n   escaped into the out-of-line call, so clang kept its length in memory and stored and reloaded it on every\n   `push`.\n\nThe interpreter's `VectorGrow` (`Interpreter",
    155 =>
        "/Runtime.cs`) needs no change: it already counts one attempt per growth\nand leaves the vector unchanged on failure.\n\n### Measurements (run 2026-10-28, Mac M-series, Apple clang 21, `benchmarks/suite/run.py --runs 5 --langs nova,c`)\n\n| Workload | Before: Nova / C ms (ratio) | After: Nova / C ms (ratio) | Peak heap before → after (C) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `vector-growth` | 630.0 / 351.7 (1.79) | 370.1 / 390.0 (0.95) | 384.0 → 256.0 MiB (256.0) |\n| `string-building` | 595.0 / 219.1 (2.72) | 410.6 / 218.4 (1.88) | 12.0 → 8.0 MiB (8.0) |\n| `parsing` | 181.8 / 138.9 (1.31) | 156.0 / 138.7 (1.12) | 48.0 → 32.1 MiB (32.0) |\n\nThe C times vary between runs on this shared Mac (321.6 to 390.0 ms for `vector-growth`).\n\n### Measurements of record (run 2026-10-28, the Ryzen box, `docs/benchmarks.md`)\n\nThe trees at `b9fa451` (before) and `587e38f` (after) were each built and run with `benchmarks/suite/run.py --runs 5\n--langs nova,c` three times, before and after interleaved, under `nice`, with a load average of about 2 (a shared\nserver). The time is the median of the three repetitions' medians (ms), Nova / C:\n\n| Workload | clang 18 before | clang 18 after | gcc 12 before | gcc 12 after | Peak heap before → after (C) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `vector-growth` | 1150 / 553 (2.08) | 603 / 584 (1.03) | 1174 / 580 (2.02) | 570 / 577 (0.99) | 384.0 → 256.0 MiB (256.0) |\n| `string-building` | 841 / 348 (2.42) | 555 / 346 (1.61) | 1094 / 310 (3.53) | 817 / 309 (2.64) | 12.0 → 8.0 MiB (8.0) |\n| `parsing` | 203 / 137 (1.49) | 172 / 138 (1.25) | 263 / 182 (1.45) | 206 / 191 (1.07) | 48.0 → 32.0 MiB (32.0) |\n\nAllocation counts are unchanged (`vector-growth` 241, `string-building` 661, `parsing` 25 under both). Raw results:\n`/root/nova-ci/m13/out` on that machine. The other thirteen workloads were not rerun: the change touches only vector,\nstring and slot-map growth.\n\nSteps, measured on the generated C (2026-10-28): the `realloc` and fast path alone moved `vector-growth` from 630 to\n662 ms (within noise) and `parsing` to 151 ms. The local capacity moved `vector-growth` to 370 ms. In `vector-growth`,\nthe overflow check on the final summing loop costs about 5% (0.63 against 0.60 s, the loop with and without\n`nv_add_u64`). That check is Nova's semantics (OD-6), not overhead to remove.\n\n### What is left in `string-building`\n\nThe workload appends `digitText[d..d + 1]`. Each append checks the index and both ends of the slice for a UTF-8\nboundary: two loads from the text and their compares per byte, where C stores a byte. Two ways to close it, neither\ndone:\n\n- **An optimisation:** a slice of a constant string whose bytes are all ASCII needs no boundary check. That is a\n  check elimination (`optimisations.md`), with the same equivalence tests as BCE.\n- **A library member:** a way to append one ASCII byte (`'0' + d`) without a view, which the spec does not have\n  (type-system §23.2: text enters a string only through `append` of a `StringView` or `appendNumber`).\n\n**Since done** (2026-10-28, after M13): the optimisation (`optimisations.md` §2.9: no boundary check on a slice of\nan ASCII literal), and a cause the analysis above missed, gcc calling `memcpy` for every one-byte append, which the\nappend helper now copies with a byte loop up to 16 bytes (c-backend §2). On the Ryzen box `string-building` went from\n548.7 to 436.8 ms under clang (1.58× to 1.26× C) and from 809.7 to 434.9 ms under gcc (2.58× to 1.40×); `parsing`\nand `vector-growth` did not move. The library member was not needed.\n\n## G2: dynamic dispatch (done, 2026-10-28; ADR 0015)\n\nSpec: type-system.md §37 (and §21.1, effect lists on interface methods), grammar.md, c-backend.md §2,\nintermediate-representation.md §3, diagnostics N0330–N0331. The guide's generics page has a checked example.\n\n- **Types.** `dyn` is contextual in types (`Parser.ParseTypeCore`, `DynType`). `TypeKind.Dyn` (`TypeTable.DynOf`)\n  names an interface instantiation. The checker allows it only as a referen",
    156 =>
        "ce's pointee or a box's contents\n  (`ResolvePointee`, N0330), and only for interfaces whose methods all take a reference receiver (N0331).\n- **Coercions** (`TypeChecker.CoerceToDyn`) are casts, so moves and loans flow through them as through any cast.\n  Each records its (`T`, `dyn I`) table. An argument for a `borrow dyn I` parameter may be a `T`, a box, or a\n  temporary box: a temporary `Box<dyn I>` is lent, never copied out. `Box.create` takes no hint from an expected\n  `Box<dyn I>`.\n- **Calls.** Each interface method is a bodiless `HirFunction` of the `dyn` type (`IsDynMethod`, `DynSlot`), so a\n  call is an ordinary `NirCall`. The borrow checker summarises it by its signature, and the effect checker uses its\n  declared effects, or `sleep` and `alloc` (§37.3). An interface method's `@effects` is inherited by `impl` methods\n  without their own list, and bounds those with one (`InheritEffects`).\n- **C and the interpreter** dispatch through the table (c-backend.md §2). The interpreter gives tables addresses\n  from `0xFFFE…`, below its function addresses.\n- **Not done** (when G3 was written; both done the same day, \"The remaining items\" below): the random program\n  generator (`tests/Nova.Fuzz`) does not generate `dyn` values or owning closures yet (ADR 0015 §6 planned it with\n  G3). No `dyn` benchmark yet: ADR 0015 §4 asks for one before any performance claim.\n\nTests (run 2026-10-28):\n\n- `run-pass/dyn.nova`:\n  - a vector of boxes of three types;\n  - mutation through `borrow mut dyn` and through a vector of boxes;\n  - a generic interface;\n  - a box in a field and in an optional, a function returning a box, and a `Result` of one;\n  - a temporary box as an argument;\n  - drop through the table;\n  - an `@effects()` interface method called from an `@effects()` function.\n- `run-pass/dyn-allocators.nova`: a `Box<dyn I, A>` with a user allocator, freed with the implementation's layout\n  (16 bytes, not the pair's).\n- Both are compiled under every C configuration with `-Werror`, and interpreted; the outputs agree.\n- `check-fail/dyn.nova`:\n  - N0330 four ways;\n  - N0331;\n  - N0268, a type that does not implement the interface;\n  - N0248, a mutating method through a shared reference;\n  - N0234, a method the interface lacks;\n  - N0295, an `impl` list wider than its interface's.\n- `check-fail/dyn-effects.nova`: N0295 for a `dyn` call without a list in an atomic context, and for an inheriting\n  `impl` that sleeps. A call with `@effects()` in that context is accepted.\n\n## G3: owning closures (done, 2026-10-28; ADR 0015 §3.6)\n\nSpec: type-system.md §36.5, grammar.md (`move` literals, `dyn fn` types), c-backend.md §2, diagnostics N0332–N0333.\nThe guide's closures page has a checked example.\n\n- **Types.** `dyn fn(…) -> R` and `dyn mut fn(…) -> R` (`TypeTable.DynFunctionOf`) are `dyn` types without an\n  interface. Their one method, `call`, is a synthetic bodiless dyn method (`DynFunctionMethod`) with the type's\n  effect list.\n- **Literals.** `move (…) => …` (`ClosureExpr.IsMove`) is checked as a closure literal. Its expected type is a\n  `Box<dyn fn…>`, reaching `Box.create` through `!` and `?`. Its captures become `CaptureMode.Move`, and its\n  environment (`IsMoveEnvironment`, not scoped) holds the captured values. `NirBuilder.LowerMoveLiteral` moves (or\n  copies) each captured local in.\n- **Boxes and calls.** `Box.create` boxes the environment. Coercing it to `Box<dyn fn…>` records the table, whose\n  only entry is the body. `f(args)` on a place that reaches a `dyn fn` borrows it, mutably for `dyn mut fn`, and\n  calls `call`, so it is an ordinary dyn call. N0248 is reported when that access is not mutable.\n- **Drop.** The environment's drop glue destroys the captured values, through the table.\n- **Parser.** A type argument list in an expression (`Vector<Box<dyn fn(u64) -> u64>>.create()`) may now contain\n  parentheses and `->` after `fn`.\n\nTests (run 2026-10-28):\n\n- `run-pass/move-closures.nova`:\n  - a returned adder;\n  - a cou",
    157 =>
        "nter (`dyn mut fn`), called directly and through `borrow mut Box<…>`;\n  - a closure owning a vector;\n  - a vector of closures;\n  - a closure in a struct field;\n  - `@effects()` on a `dyn fn` type, called from an `@effects()` function;\n  - a closure owning a value with a drop, destroyed with the box.\n- It is compiled under every C configuration and interpreted.\n- `check-fail/move-closures.nova`: N0330, N0333, N0332, N0299 and N0248.\n- `check-fail/move-closures-moves.nova`: use after the capture's move (N0250), and a capture moved out of the body\n  (N0251).\n\n## G4: threads (G4a–G4c done, 2026-10-28; ADR 0016)\n\nSpec: type-system.md §38, ownership.md T1–T3, c-backend.md §6, N0334. The guide has a new page, Threads, whose\nexamples are checked.\n\n- **G4a, `Send` and `Share`** (`TypeTable.IsSend`/`IsShare`). They are inferred structurally, used as bounds like\n  `Copy`, and checked at instantiation (N0334). `@threadSafe`, accepted only in library files, marks `Arc`, `Mutex`\n  and the atomics.\n- **G4b, spawn and join.**\n  - `Thread.spawn(move () => …)` is checked as a move literal whose result type comes from an expected `Thread<T>` or\n    from the body: an expression, or the type of every `return`.\n  - Its captures and result must be `Send`.\n  - It becomes the `ThreadSpawn` builtin with the environment, and the (environment, `dyn mut fn() -> T`) table is\n    recorded. `t.join()` is `ThreadJoin`. `Thread<T>` is `TypeKind.Thread`, a move type whose drop joins.\n  - Effects: spawning has `sleep` and `alloc`; joining and dropping have `sleep`.\n- **G4c, shared state.**\n  - `lib/std/sync.nova`: `AtomicU64`, `AtomicU32`, `AtomicUSize`, `AtomicBool`, `Mutex<T>` with `MutexGuard`,\n    `Arc<T>`, and `SpawnError`.\n  - It is Nova code over the runtime's `nova_mutex_*` and `nova_atomic_*`, in the style of C7's locks.\n  - `lib/std` is new: the hosted library, compiled into hosted packages only (`CoreLibrary.HostedSources`). The\n    emitter now also emits library `drop` functions that only drop glue reaches.\n- **The interpreter** (`Interpreter/Threads.cs`):\n  - A host thread per Nova thread, with a baton, switching only when a thread blocks (a join, a held mutex) or ends,\n    to the first runnable thread in spawn order.\n  - Every thread waiting is a deadlock fault.\n  - A panic, fault or exit in a thread ends the run from the main thread.\n  - It makes the C runtime's three allocations per spawn, so fault-injection counts agree.\n- **Found on the way:** the emitter printed any statement-position builtin it did not list as a console write. A\n  bare `t.join();` printed the handle. The thread builtins are now listed.\n\nTests (run 2026-10-28):\n\n- `run-pass/threads.nova`:\n  - `u64`, `String` and `void` results;\n  - four threads × 1000 increments under an `Arc<Mutex<…>>`;\n  - two threads on an `Arc<AtomicU64>`;\n  - `AtomicBool`;\n  - a value moved into a thread, destroyed at join;\n  - an unjoined thread joined by its drop.\n- It is compiled under every C configuration and interpreted. One scratch copy of its worker part was also built with\n  `-fsanitize=thread` and run three times: no report.\n- `check-fail/threads.nova`: a capture and a result that are not `Send`, a spawn without a move literal, a `Send`\n  bound, and `@threadSafe` in user code.\n- `check-fail/threads-freestanding.nova`: N0293.\n- **G4d, CosmoOS userland.** `nova_rt_thread.c` has a third branch over `cosmo_thread_*` and `cosmo_mutex_*`, which\n  `integrations/cosmoos/c1.py` selects with `-DNV_COSMOOS`. `c2.py --arch aarch64 --ssh root@10.211.55.15` (run\n  2026-10-28, against CosmoOS v1.0.14 on the Parallels VM) passed:\n  - 185 corpus programs in the guest, `threads`, `dyn` and `move-closures` among them;\n  - the guest fault-injection sweep, 42 programs and 386 runs;\n  - the CosmoOS tree left unchanged.\n- To make that possible, emitted C no longer carries what lib/std alone asks for. Composites requested only while\n  checking lib/std (`TypeTable.InHostedLibrary`), and built-in items only ",
    158 =>
        "library code names (`IsLibraryUse`), are\n  emitted only where emitted code uses them, and library externs only when called. Every emit-C snapshot is\n  unchanged.\n- `c2.py --arch x86_64` (QEMU, run 2026-10-28) passed the same: 185 programs, the guest sweep (42 programs, 386 runs),\n  a clean shutdown (QEMU status 33). The corpus had outgrown CosmoOS's 64 MiB boot partition (`scripts/mkimage.sh`:\n  \"Disk full\"), so `c1.make_image` now puts the programs in the image without debug information\n  (`llvm-objcopy --strip-debug`; `nv-threads` 301 → 72 KB). The built ELFs keep it.\n- **Not done** (when G4 was written; the status of each since, in \"The remaining items\" below):\n  - the parallel benchmark on the Ryzen box (done: `benchmarks/parallel`);\n  - ThreadSanitizer as a standing C configuration of the corpus (still open: the acceptance below ran it once, on the worker test);\n  - `dyn … + Send` (type-system §38.4) is specified but not implemented, so `Thread.spawn` takes only a move literal\n    (done);\n  - the random program generator does not generate threads (done).\n\n## G5: checker generality (first increment, 2026-10-28)\n\nFifteen probes of programs that borrow checkers commonly reject, run against `ee9b159` (scratch, 2026-10-28):\n\n| Probe | Result | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| two-phase `v.push(v.length)` | accepted | |\n| conditional return of a borrow, then mutate (Rust's NLL problem case 3) | accepted | |\n| a loop that returns a borrow or pushes and returns another | accepted | |\n| `borrow mut p.a` and `borrow mut p.b` | accepted | |\n| a slice of an array, then a write to the array after its last use | accepted | |\n| a field of a struct while iterating another field | accepted | |\n| a move out of a local and back, in a branch | accepted | |\n| a function returning one of two views (`StringView<r>`) | accepted | |\n| an optional reference returned, then the vector grown | accepted | |\n| a closure reading `s.b` while `s.a` is written | **rejected**, now accepted | field-precise captures |\n| `borrow mut v[0]` and `borrow mut v[2]` | **rejected**, now accepted | constant indices |\n| a method returning `borrow this.field`, then another field written | rejected | as in Rust: a method's signature borrows all of `this` |\n| `borrow mut v[i]` and `borrow mut v[j]` | rejected | as in Rust; a `splitAtMut` is the remedy, not yet in the library |\n| a reference variable re-pointed (`var cur = n`) | rejected | by design: assigning to a reference writes through it (N0246) |\n\nChanges:\n\n- **Constant indices** (`BorrowChecker.Overlap`). Elements at different constant indices do not overlap. Messages\n  name a constant index (`values[0]`, not `values[_]`), so three quoted messages in the README and guide changed.\n- **Field-precise captures** (`TypeChecker.FieldPrecise`, `HirCapture.Field`, `NirBuilder._fieldCaptures`).\n  - A captured struct local that the body reaches only through its fields is captured as one reference per field.\n    That covers expressions, assignment targets and borrowed places, including their index expressions.\n  - The closure's loans are then on those fields.\n  - Nested closures and whole uses keep the whole capture.\n\nTests (run 2026-10-28):\n\n- `run-pass/precise-borrows.nova`: reads and writes through field-precise closures, and two mutable loans of\n  constant elements. Compiled under every C configuration, and interpreted.\n- `check-fail/precise-borrows.nova`: a captured field written while the closure lives, a whole-local capture, one\n  element borrowed twice, and a variable index.\n\nSecond increment (2026-10-28):\n\n- **Field-precise captures through references.** A reference to a struct (`this` in a method) that a closure reaches\n  only as `this.x` is captured as references to `(*this).x`, so a method's closure that reads `this.width` leaves\n  `this.count` to the method (`HirCapture.ThroughReference`).\n- **`splitAt` and `splitAtMut`** (type-system §16.5) on slices and vectors, in lib/core (`SliceSplitting<T>`,\n  `SliceHalves<T>`, ",
    159 =>
        "`SliceHalvesMut<T>`). Two disjoint mutable parts of one slice at a split known only at run time:\n  the remedy for `borrow mut v[i]` and `borrow mut v[j]`. The halves borrow the slice while they live (N0240 for a\n  write to it meanwhile). A split past the end panics, at lib/core's line.\n- Tests: `precise-borrows` gains a method's closures over `this`, a run-time split of an array, and a vector's\n  `splitAt`; its check-fail twin gains a write to `this.width` under a closure that reads it, and a write to an array\n  while its halves live.\n\nWhat stays rejected, as in Rust: a method returning `borrow this.field` borrows all of `this` (its signature says\nso), and a reference variable cannot be re-pointed (by design).\n\n## G6: a package registry (2026-10-28; ADR 0017)\n\nSpec: tooling.md §4.6 (the registry and its index), §4.7 (version solving), §4.8 (publishing), diagnostics N0328,\nN0329 and N0335, and an amendment to ADR 0013 D6.\n\n- **Versions and requirements** (`Packages/Versions.cs`): `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. Caret requirements are the default\n  (`\"1.4\"`), with `=`, `>=`, `>`, `<`, `<=`, commas and `*`.\n- **The index** (`Packages/Registry.cs`, `PackageIndex`): a git repository fetched through the git cache. It holds\n  `packages/<name>.toml` with `[[version]]` entries (version, git, commit, hash, dependencies, yanked).\n- **The solver** (`VersionSolver`) chooses the newest version that fits, preferring a locked one, and backtracks. On\n  failure it names the requirements on the package it could not choose and where each came from.\n- **`novac fetch`** (`Workspace`):\n  - resolves path and git dependencies first, collecting their registry requirements;\n  - solves per index (`--update [name]` ignores the lock);\n  - checks out each chosen commit, verifying the index's hash and the manifest's name and version;\n  - writes `registry+<index>` entries with version and repository.\n  Other commands read them from the lock (N0324 when a requirement no longer fits). A registry package's own\n  dependencies come from its index.\n- **`novac publish`** (`Packages/Publishing.cs`) checks the package: a library with a version, registry dependencies\n  only, type-checking, a clean tree at a commit on a remote. It then prints the entry. **`novac index-check`** is the\n  index's CI: each entry's commit, name, version, hash and dependencies, and increasing versions.\n- **The index repository's files** are in `packaging/nova-index/`: a README with the publishing steps, `packages/`,\n  and a `check` workflow that builds novac from this repository and runs `index-check`. The repository\n  `https://github.com/vkuttyp/nova-index` was created from them on 2026-10-28, at the owner's request.\n\nTests (run 2026-10-28, `PackageManagerTests`):\n\n- requirements, valid and invalid;\n- the solver: newest, yanked, backtracking, a stale lock given up, a kept lock, explained conflicts;\n- end to end with git repositories the test makes (no network):\n  - a registry dependency solved, locked, read through the lock offline, and compiled;\n  - a newer release taken only with `--update`;\n  - a requirement the lock no longer meets (N0324), and a conflict (N0329);\n- `publish` on a clean clone, and refused after an edit (N0335); `index-check` passing, and finding a wrong hash\n  (N0328).\n\n## The remaining items (2026-10-28)\n\n### `dyn … + Send` (type-system §38.4)\n\n`dyn I + Send` and `dyn fn(…) -> R + Send` (`DynType.IsSend`, `NovaType.IsSendDyn`) are `dyn` types whose boxes are\n`Send`. Only a `Send` value coerces to one (N0334), and one converts to its plain form, never back.\n\n- Both forms share a C type and tables. The emitter emits each composite once by its mangled name, and a cast\n  between equal C types is the value itself.\n- `Thread.spawn` takes a `Box<dyn mut fn() -> T + Send>` too (`ThreadSpawnBoxed`, `nvTSB_`), so a closure made in\n  one place runs on a thread started in another.\n- A move literal's environment is `Send` when its captures are; its code pointer is an address.\n- Tests: ",
    160 =>
        "`threads.nova` spawns a boxed closure made by a function, and moves a `Box<dyn Job + Send>` into a thread.\n  `check-fail/threads.nova` coerces a type that is not `Send` to a `+ Send` type, and moves a plain `Box<dyn Job>`\n  into a thread.\n- Found on the way: deduplicating composites by C name merged a `Box<T, A>` of a user allocator with the system\n  heap's, which have one C type but different drop glue. They are deduplicated by mangled name.\n\n### The random program generator\n\n`tests/Nova.Fuzz` now generates three more kinds of statement:\n- dynamic dispatch: a vector of boxes of two `Op` implementations, folded over, then changed through `bump()`;\n- owning closures: a `Box<dyn fn(T) -> T>` called once, and a `dyn mut fn` counter called twice;\n- threads: a computation spawned and joined at once.\n\nMove bodies capture only Copy values that hold no loans (`InMoveClosure`). All of the first 1,000 seeds type-check\n(`--accept 1000`, 2026-10-28).\n\nThe oracle on seeds 1–150 found 16 programs whose C did not compile under `-Werror`. These were two bugs, both fixed:\n- dyn tables and thread helpers whose only use was in unreachable code were unused statics (now `NV_UNUSED`);\n- a `Result<Thread<T>, SpawnError>` left by such code named lib/std's `SpawnError`, which was not emitted. The\n  emitter now treats every emitted composite's parts as used.\n\nWith both fixed, seeds 1–300 run with no finding (2026-10-28). Each program is checked under five clang\nconfigurations and the interpreter. Of the 300, 66 panic and 204 exit with another status, the same in every\nconfiguration.\n\n### Benchmarks (run 2026-10-28, the Ryzen box)\n\n`benchmarks/dispatch` and `benchmarks/parallel` (each with a README and a `run.py`), on the AMD Ryzen 5 PRO 3600 (6\ncores, 12 threads), Debian 12, load average about 2 (a shared server), under `nice`. The figures are medians; every\nprogram printed the same checksum.\n\n**Dispatch** (9 interleaved runs; a million boxed objects of two types, 100 passes):\n\n| Program | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nova `dyn` (`Vector<Box<dyn Shape>>`) | 290.2 ms (1.03 × C's table) | 311.5 ms (1.05) |\n| Nova `enum` and `match` | 271.1 ms (0.96) | 293.4 ms (0.99) |\n| C function-pointer table | 283.1 ms (1.00) | 297.4 ms (1.00) |\n| C tagged switch | 248.6 ms (0.88) | 258.0 ms (0.87) |\n\nA `dyn` call costs what C's hand-written table costs, within 5%. Where the set of types is closed, an enum is a little\nfaster, as in C.\n\n**Parallel** (5 interleaved runs; 40 million values over N threads). Nova's time, its speed-up over one thread, and\nNova's time over C's (pthreads):\n\n| Threads | clang 18 | gcc 12 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 631.7 ms (1.00×), 1.02 | 735.1 ms (1.00×), 0.99 |\n| 2 | 315.0 ms (2.01×), 1.00 | 370.8 ms (1.98×), 1.00 |\n| 4 | 161.8 ms (3.90×), 1.00 | 191.0 ms (3.85×), 1.00 |\n| 6 | 109.8 ms (5.76×), 0.96 | 128.8 ms (5.71×), 1.01 |\n| 12 | 90.9 ms (6.95×), 0.98 | 99.4 ms (7.39×), 1.05 |\n\nNova threads scale as pthreads do: nearly linearly to the six cores, and a little further with their second\nhardware threads. The spawn, the closure and `join` add nothing measurable.\n\n## Acceptance (2026-10-28)\n\nThe roadmap's criterion: for each gap, the probe that showed it now passes, with a spec, positive and negative tests;\nG2, G4 and G6 after an ADR each; performance measured on the suite.\n\n| Gap | Probe at `b9fa451` | Now | Spec and ADR | Tests | Measured |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| G1 growth | `vector-growth` 2.08×, `string-building` 2.42× C (Ryzen, clang) | 1.03×, 1.61× | c-backend §2, §6 | `emit-c/vectors.c`; the whole corpus | Ryzen, clang and gcc |\n| G2 `dyn` | `Box<dyn Shape>` is N0100 | runs, compiled and interpreted, also in CosmoOS | ADR 0015; type-system §37 | `run-pass/dyn`, `dyn-allocators`; `check-fail/dyn`, `dyn-effects` | `benchmarks/dispatch`: within 5% of C's table |\n| G3 owning closures | returning a capturing closure is N0299 | `Box<dyn fn…>` returned, stored, called | ADR 0015 §3.6; type-system §36.5 | `run-",
    161 =>
        "pass/move-closures`; `check-fail/move-closures`, `move-closures-moves` | — (an allocation per closure, as specified) |\n| G4 threads | `Thread` is N0200 | spawn, join, `Arc`, `Mutex`, atomics, `+ Send`; deterministic in the interpreter; in CosmoOS on both arches | ADR 0016; type-system §38; ownership T1–T3 | `run-pass/threads`; `check-fail/threads`, `threads-freestanding` | `benchmarks/parallel`: 5.76× on 6 cores, as pthreads |\n| G5 checker generality | 4 of 15 probes rejected that need not be | 2 of the 4 accepted, and `splitAtMut` for a third; 2 rejected by design, as in Rust | lifetimes §8; type-system §16.5, §36.2 | `run-pass/precise-borrows`; `check-fail/precise-borrows`, `slice-borrows` | — |\n| G6 registry | — (path and git only) | an index, solving, the lock, publishing; github.com/vkuttyp/nova-index with its CI | ADR 0017; tooling §4.6–§4.8 | `PackageManagerTests` (requirements, solver, end to end with local repositories) | — |\n\nEvery run below was on 2026-10-28.\n\n- **The full suite** (`dotnet test Nova.slnx`) passed at each commit. The last complete run had 3,388 tests, before\n  the final emitter fix.\n  - That fix then passed the suite's fuzz, corpus and interpreter tests (409), and every emit-C snapshot.\n  - **Nova CI** passed on each push, the last run being 40669583703 on `3bceb32`.\n- **CosmoOS** (`integrations/cosmoos/c2.py`, after G4): 185 corpus programs and the guest fault-injection sweep on\n  aarch64 (Parallels VM) and x86_64 (QEMU).\n- **The fuzzer**, extended to `dyn`, owning closures and threads: seeds 1–300 under five clang configurations and the\n  interpreter, no finding.\n- **ThreadSanitizer**: the worker test, three runs, no report.\n\nWhat M13 leaves open, as recorded open decisions or reports:\n- several versions of one package in a build (OD-26);\n- weaker atomic orderings (OD-24) and scoped threads (OD-25);\n- scoped `dyn(r) I` and downcasting (OD-10a, OD-10b);\n- monomorphised closures (OD-9a);\n- ThreadSanitizer as a standing C configuration of the corpus.\n\nThe suite-wide performance figures in the README are from M11, rerun only for G1's three workloads.\n",
    162 =>
        "# Milestone 14 — Nova for a kernel written in Nova (plan)\n\nStatus: **in progress** (started 2026-10-29 at the owner's word). K1, K2a and K3 are done and accepted (booted in CosmoOS), K4 decided, K5's inline atomics and K7's generator work done (novac 0.3.1). Open: K2's separate compilation, which waits for converted subsystems' build times; K5's check eliminations, which wait for PF1; K6, the outside audit, which the owner engages. Each item that changes the language or the toolchain\ngets a design and an ADR for the owner before code, as in M11 and M13.\n\n## Why\n\nCosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 (proposed, CosmoOS `docs/roadmap-2.0.md`, merged as #483) makes \"the kernel in Nova\" its\ngoal. All new kernel code would be Nova, all existing C would be converted, and only a named floor of assembly and C\nwrappers would remain. Its SA track assumes Nova can write whole subsystems: allocation and object lifetimes (SA2),\nlocks, waits and quiescence (SA3), drivers (SA4), then the network stack, storage and the core. Today's four ports\n(`gpt`, `cputime`, `nice`, `fair`; 982 lines) are leaf functions whose state lives in C. Reviewing the roadmap\nagainst the compiler (below) found what Nova still lacks for the rest. This milestone is that list, ordered by what\nthe roadmap needs first.\n\nThe roadmap names these Nova gates (its \"Nova prerequisites\" section, proposed with this plan):\n\n| CosmoOS milestone | Needs from Nova |\n|---|---|\n| SA1 (gate, build from Nova) | K4 decided (how a self-hosted CosmoOS builds its kernel), K2a (C split into translation units) |\n| SA2 (allocation, objects), SA3 (locks, waits) | K1 (global and per-CPU state), K3 (the kernel library) |\n| SA6 onward (whole subsystems) | K2 (Nova-to-Nova across compilation units, or a measured whole-program build), K6 (an outside audit) |\n| PF1, PF2 (performance) | K5 as measured |\n| SA5 (parsers) | nothing new: parsers are leaf code, as GPT was |\n\n## Plan\n\n| # | Gap | Probe (2026-10-29, `a032dc6`) | Needs | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **K1** | Global state: a kernel has lock-protected tables, singletons and per-CPU data; Nova has only compile-time `const` (type-system §10) | `static var count: u64 = 0;` and `var count: u64 = 0;` at top level: N0101 \"expected 'fn', 'const', 'struct' or 'enum'\" | ADR (OD-28) | **done** (2026-10-29, [ADR 0024](decisions/0024-m14-k1-static-items.md), type-system §39): `static`, `static mut`, `@constant`, C constant macros. Accepted: CosmoOS's `nova-kcore` keeps a table under a static spin lock and a per-CPU counter in every debug boot (CosmoOS #489, #490); the negative cases are check-fail `kernel-static-writes.nova`, `kernel-percpu.nova`, `kernel-percpu-guard.nova` and `kernel-library.nova` |\n| **K2** | Nova-to-Nova calls with Nova's types across compilation units. Today each kernel `.nova` file compiles alone and they meet only through `@export` C functions, so references, regions, effects and `Send`/`Share` stop at every file boundary. ADR 0013 D4 keeps builds whole-program, with separate compilation deferred until build times were measured | Measured below: a whole-program build of 202,000 lines is 13.1 s in novac and 33.8 s in clang for one 1,076,000-line C file, on every change | ADR (OD-29); K2a first | **K2a done** (2026-10-29, [ADR 0022](decisions/0022-m14-k2a-generated-c-in-units.md)): `--split` builds it in 14.0 s on 10 cores, 2.4 s after a one-file change; separate compilation open |\n| **K3** | A kernel library: `KernelHeap` (C5), `KRef<T>`, `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>`, `Mutex<T>` and `Quiesce` (C7) exist as demonstrations in `integrations/cosmoos/c5` and `c7`, not as a package a kernel depends on; `ObjectCache<T>`, which roadmap 2.0 lists as built, does not exist | `grep -r ObjectCache` in Nova's repository: no match | ADR (OD-30): where it lives and who owns it | **in Nova** (2026-10-29, [ADR 0025](decisions/0025-m14-k3-kernel-library.md)): `lib/kernel`, checked against CosmoOS's headers by `k3.py`; `ObjectCache<T>` and a static `Mutex` s",
    163 =>
        "ince [ADR 0027](decisions/0027-m14-static-mutex-and-object-cache.md). **Done and accepted** (2026-10-29): CosmoOS's self-test `nova-kcore` passes in debug boots on both architectures (CosmoOS #489; #490 with novac 0.3.1 adds the static mutex, checked against `mutex_init`, and the object cache) |\n| **K4** | novac where CosmoOS builds itself: roadmap 2.0's rule 7 stops committing generated C, so every kernel build runs novac, a .NET NativeAOT binary for macOS, Linux and Windows that does not run on CosmoOS (SW0, self-hosting) | — | a decision (OD-31), recorded in both roadmaps | **decided** ([ADR 0023](decisions/0023-m14-k4-self-hosted-builds.md), 2026-10-29): source releases carry the generated C; CosmoOS's `make dist` is its side |\n| **K5** | Kernel hot paths within roadmap 2.0's 5%: inline atomics (OD-27; ADR 0021 measured a further 1.8×), and the check elimination that converted code needs | per workload, with PF1's benchmarks | OD-27's ADR; others as measured | **inline atomics done** (2026-10-29, [ADR 0026](decisions/0026-m14-k5-inline-atomics.md)): static inline in `rt.h`, the atomic types in `lib/core` for kernel code; the ordered ring within 1.24–1.34× of inline C (was 2.3–2.8×); check eliminations wait for PF1 |\n| **K6** | An outside audit of the checker before the compiler carries whole subsystems | `safety-audit.md` §4: the 2026-10-29 review, by the same model that wrote the compiler, found two holes, one in a release (0.1.1) | the owner engages a reviewer; Nova provides the pack | proposed |\n| **K7** | The compiler as part of a kernel's trusted base: kernel-style code in the random program generator (statics, guards, the freestanding profile), and a pin-bump checklist (the nightly campaign green, the corpus under the interpreter) | — | none (tests) | **started** (2026-10-29): the generator writes statics (`testing.md` §5), which found an unused static's library type left out of the C (fixed, `run-pass/statics-unused.nova`); the checklist is in `release.md`; since 2026-10-29 kernel-style programs built freestanding and checked against their hosted twin (`--freestanding`, testing.md §5), in the nightly campaign. Guards need CosmoOS's C, so `k3.py` covers them |\n\n### Measurements for K2 (run 2026-10-29, Mac M-series, Apple clang 21, novac 0.2.0-dev Release)\n\nA synthetic program of structs and functions with loops, vectors, matches and `?`: 400 structs and 2,000 functions\nmake 46,404 lines. Four renamed copies, with a `main` that calls every function so the C compiler cannot drop them,\nmake 202,004 lines. Indicative only: real kernel code has more generics and fewer, larger functions (inferred).\n\n| Nova lines | `novac check` | `novac build --emit c` | generated C | `clang -O2 -c` of it |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 46,404 | 1.79 s, 152 MB | 4.25 s, 213 MB | 220,934 lines | — |\n| 185,604 (functions unreachable) | 4.79 s, 409 MB | 11.65 s, 607 MB | 883,334 lines | — (clang dropped the unused functions) |\n| 202,004 (all reachable) | — | 13.10 s, 721 MB | 1,076,433 lines | 33.84 s, 1.25 GB, one core |\n\nnovac scales about linearly. The cost is the rebuild: every change recompiles everything, and the C is one\ntranslation unit that a parallel build cannot spread over cores. CosmoOS's C kernel compiles each file in parallel.\n\n## K1: global and per-CPU state (design sketch for the ADR)\n\n- `static NAME: T = e;` at package level, with `e` a constant expression or a constant constructor of `T`. A static is\n  never written directly. Its mutable state is reached only through a type that is `Share` (§38.1): a spin lock or\n  mutex guard type, an atomic, or a library type declared `@threadSafe`. That makes `static` the global counterpart of\n  `Arc<T>`, needing no new borrow rule: `borrow STATIC` is a `borrow T` with the static region.\n- **Constant construction.** A kernel lock is C state that may point to itself once initialised (R-11). It needs an\n  initialiser that C can evaluate statically (CosmoOS's `SPINLOCK_INIT(n",
    164 =>
        "ame)`), so the ADR must say how an imported C\n  type's static initialiser is named (`@cinit(\"SPINLOCK_INIT\")`, or a constant constructor in the binding).\n- **Per-CPU data.** `@percpu static NAME: T`, accessed only where the CPU cannot change: in `@context(atomic)` or under\n  a guard that disables preemption. The effect checker already proves contexts (§32), so this is a new obligation on a\n  checked fact, not a new analysis.\n- **Interrupts.** Data shared with an interrupt handler needs an irq-saving lock. That is a property of the lock type\n  (C7's `IrqSpinLock<T>`), so the static needs nothing of its own.\n- **Hosted programs** get the same `static` for `Mutex<T>` and atomics (lib/std): a `Mutex` initialised statically\n  (`PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER`, `SRWLOCK_INIT`).\n- **C.** A static is a C object with internal linkage, or external with `@export`, and its initialiser is a C constant\n  expression. No constructor runs at startup.\n\n## K2: typed calls across compilation units (options for the ADR)\n\n1. **K2a, cheap and first: the C in several translation units.** novac stays whole-program but emits one `.c` per\n   source file, or per N functions, plus a shared header. The C compiles in parallel and only changed files recompile.\n   novac's own time stays about 13 s at 200,000 lines.\n2. **Separate compilation per package.** Each package is checked against its dependencies' *interface files*: their\n   public signatures with regions, effects, `Send`/`Share` and `@effectsOf`, and the bodies of their generic code,\n   because generics are monomorphised (ADR 0013's stated precondition). A kernel would be a few dozen packages, so a\n   change rebuilds one package and those whose interface it changes.\n3. **Stay whole-program**, accepting K2a's numbers.\n\nThe recommendation will come from measuring K2a on the converted subsystems first. Option 2 is a large change, and\nit is the one that makes a ~200,000-line kernel practical to develop. In both cases, calls between Nova packages keep\nNova's types, which `@export`-only boundaries do not.\n\n## K3: the kernel library\n\nC5 and C7 built most of the kernel abstractions roadmap 2.0's rule 4 names: `KernelHeap` (`c5/kernel.nova`),\n`KRef<T>` (`c7/kobject.nova`), `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `Mutex<T>` with their guards (`c7/sync.nova`), and\n`Quiesce` (`c7/quiesce.nova`). They live as demonstrations with their own C glue. `ObjectCache<T>`, a typed slab cache\nover CosmoOS's `kmem_cache`, is not built: the roadmap's list is ahead of the code there, and K3 writes it. The ADR\ndecides:\n\n- **Where they live.** The proposal is a Nova package in CosmoOS's tree (`kernel/nova-core/`, a `nova.toml` package\n  the kernel's Nova packages depend on by path). The abstractions bind CosmoOS's own C API, so CosmoOS owns them. Nova\n  keeps the language features they need and the tests that pin them (`integrations/cosmoos/c7`).\n- **Its API**, written once in Nova with `/// Safety:` contracts at the floor.\n- **Its tests**, the C5 and C7 self-tests and stress tests, moved with it.\n\n## K4: novac and a self-hosted CosmoOS\n\nroadmap 2.0's rule 7 (generated C not committed) and SW0 (CosmoOS builds itself) conflict, because novac does not run\non CosmoOS. Options:\n\n1. **Ship the generated C in source releases.** The repository holds only `.nova`; a release tarball, or a `make dist`\n   tree, holds the generated C. A build from the tarball needs no novac, so a self-hosted build works, but changing a\n   `.nova` file needs a host with novac. Recommended.\n2. **novac on CosmoOS.** NativeAOT has no CosmoOS target, and porting the .NET runtime is far larger than this milestone.\n3. **A Nova compiler written in Nova.** A long-term direction, not a 2.0 one.\n\n## K5–K7\n\n- **K5** starts with OD-27 (inline atomics: atomics as built-in operations emitted as `__atomic_*`), whose ADR is the\n  first item. Then PF1's numbers on converted code decide which check eliminations to add, each with\n  `optimisations.md`'s written preconditions.\n- **K6*",
    165 =>
        "* is outside Nova's repository to perform. Nova keeps `safety-audit.md` current and turns each finding into a\n  test.\n- **K7** adds generator features as the language gains them (K1's statics first). The pin-bump checklist goes in\n  `docs/release.md` and CosmoOS's `docs/build/nova.md`.\n\n## Order and release\n\nK4's decision and K2a come first, because SA1 needs them. Then K1 and K3, which gate SA2 and SA3; then K2's ADR,\ninformed by K2a; K5, K6 and K7 run alongside. K1 and K2a change the language and the compiler's output, so they ship in\n**Nova 0.2.0**, with the work since 0.1.2 (M13, ADR 0018–0021), and CosmoOS moves its pin to it in one PR.\n0.2.0 was released on 2026-10-29 with K1, K2a and K3. 0.3.0, the same day, adds K5's inline atomics, ADR 0027 and K7's freestanding programs.\n[ADR 0029](decisions/0029-m14-exported-structs-with-c-state.md) (2026-10-30), for SA2's handle table: `@repr(C)` structs that\nhold C state and arrays, which C embeds by value; released in 0.5.0 (2026-10-30).\n[ADR 0030](decisions/0030-m14-sa3-kernel-interop.md) (2026-10-30), for SA3's ports: generated C includes its exports header\ninstead of redefining `@repr(C)` structs (`--exports-header`), `offsetOf<T>(field)`, `callerAddress()` in exports, and\nlib/kernel's `List`; it ships in 0.6.0. Point\nreleases stay for fixes (`release.md`).\n\n## Acceptance (proposed)\n\n- K1: a CosmoOS kernel object table written in Nova with a `static` spin-lock-guarded table and a per-CPU counter;\n  negative tests for a static written outside a guard, a per-CPU access that may migrate, and a sleep under a static's\n  spin lock.\n- K2a: `novac build --emit c` of a multi-file package writes one translation unit per file, byte-identical between\n  runs; a one-file change recompiles one `.c`.\n- K3: the kernel library builds in CosmoOS's tree, and its self-tests pass in a debug boot on both architectures.\n- K4: the decision recorded in both roadmaps; for option 1, a source release built without novac.\n- K5, K6, K7: as their items say, each with a dated result.\n",
    166 =>
        "# Milestone 15 — Prerequisites for a compiler written in Nova (plan)\n\nStatus: **implemented** (2026-10-30, at the owner's word). S1–S7 are on `main` with their spec text and tests\n(S7's ADR 0028 was accepted 2026-10-30). Open: a release carrying them, and the emitter gap noted under S7. Bug fixes\n(S1, S2, S4, S5) follow the usual rule: a corpus regression test with each fix. Items that change the language, the\nlibrary or a diagnostic (S3, S6, S7) get spec text, and an ADR where they add API.\n\n## Why\n\nOD-33 (recorded by [ADR 0023](decisions/0023-m14-k4-self-hosted-builds.md) as option C) is a Nova compiler written in\nNova. It is the long-term way for a toolchain to run where .NET does not, CosmoOS included. The owner started that\nport on 2026-10-29 as a separate project named **CeeSafe**, outside this repository. It ports novac's 36,559 lines of\nC# pass by pass, against this repository's corpus and specs.\n\nIts first phase (P1, 2026-10-30) wrote probes in Nova for what a compiler needs:\n- ID-indexed trees;\n- owning enums in vectors;\n- a generic hash map;\n- a lexer and interner run over all 525 `.nova` files here;\n- error layers, dataflow bitsets and processes.\n\nIt also wrote a support library checked against independent oracles. The probes found that the language core is\nenough: maps, generic `impl`s, `From` and borrow precision all have accepted patterns. What they did find is:\n\n- **five novac defects**, filed as [#1](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/1)–[#5](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/5).\n  Two of them, a skipped drop and a compiler crash, matter beyond the port.\n- **two library gaps** that block it: turning bytes read from a file into text, and the host services `novac build`\n  itself uses (captured process output, environment variables, directories, temporary directories).\n\nThis milestone is that list. Every item also benefits Nova programs that are not compilers.\n\n## Plan\n\n| # | Gap | Evidence (novac 0.3.1 and `main` 07d561f, 2026-10-30) | Needs | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **S1** | **A compiler crash.** A generic `match` that rebuilds `Ok(v)` from a `Result<T, E>` crashes novac when instantiated with `T = void`: \"cannot lower HirError; the program has errors\" (`NirBuilder.LowerRvalueCore`), with no diagnostic. `check`, `build` and `run --interp` all crash. | [#2](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/2), an 8-line reproducer | a fix and a run-pass regression | **done** (2026-10-30): the generic body is valid for every type argument except `void`, so an instance that does not check is never lowered, and is **N0222** at its use, naming the type arguments and the first error, when no other error explains it (an unmet bound or a move of C state usually does; type-system §20.4). Before, it crashed. Making `void` payloads uniform is left open. `check-fail/generic-void-instance.nova` |\n| **S2** | **A missed drop.** A temporary that needs drop, made in an `if` or `while` condition, is never destroyed. A `while` condition evaluated twice destroys one temporary. Native code and the interpreter agree, so the fault is in lowering. Nova's run corpus does not catch it: none of its 199 programs leaks, and the corpus never checks for leaks on its own. | [#1](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/1). A heap-owning case ends at `live=1`, and LeakSanitizer reports a direct leak. This breaks type-system §14.5 (O4: \"destroyed exactly once\"). | spec clarification (below), a fix, a regression, and a leak check in `RunCorpusTests` | **done** (2026-10-30): a condition's temporaries end once it is evaluated, before the branch, on every evaluation (lifetimes.md §2, type-system §14.5; see below). `run-pass/condition-temporaries.nova`. `RunCorpusTests` now requires `live=0` at a normal exit for every program in every configuration; 194/194 pass, and with the old lowering the regression fails |\n| **S3** | **No nesting limit.** 100,000 nested parentheses crash novac with SIGSEGV (exit 139), with no message; 10,000 are ",
    167 =>
        "accepted. The parser, checker and NIR builder recurse with no depth check. | [#3](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/3) | spec text (a limit and a diagnostic code; grammar.md, diagnostics.md) | **done** (2026-10-30): grammar.md §2.2 sets a limit of 1,000 levels, with **N0110**. Every command runs on a 256 MiB thread. Check elimination gets a 128-fact budget (optimisations.md §2.5). Details below. `parse-fail/nesting-too-deep.nova`; `CliTests.Nesting_up_to_the_limit_runs_and_past_it_is_one_diagnostic` builds 998 levels and a 999-term chain through `Program.Main` |\n| **S4** | **`truncate` is not two-phase.** §23.1 says the arguments of `push`, `pop`, `clear` and `truncate` are evaluated before the loan starts. `v.push(…v.length…)` is accepted, but `v.truncate(v.length - 1)` is N0242. | [#4](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/4) | a fix (the spec is clear) and a check-pass case | **done** (2026-10-30): `VectorTruncate` joins the two-phase builtins in `NirBuilder`. `run-pass/truncate-two-phase.nova`; `check-fail/truncate-invalidation.nova` keeps truncation under a live element borrow rejected |\n| **S5** | **`borrow(this)` ignored.** A result declared `-> borrow(this) T?` is treated as also borrowing another reference parameter whose type is a type parameter (`key: borrow K`), so a temporary key is N0243. With a concrete key type, it is accepted. | [#5](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/5) | a fix and a check-pass case | **done** (2026-10-30): a named source contributes its regions to the result, not its type parameters' flow names (`CollectFlowNames`). `run-pass/borrow-this-generic-key.nova`; `check-fail/borrow-this-generic-key.nova` keeps the real conflict rejected. Still imprecise, and sound: for `-> borrow(this) K`, the pointee `K` itself names `key` as a source, in caller and callee alike |\n| **S6** | **Bytes to text.** `File.read` and `Console.read` give bytes, and nothing turns valid UTF-8 bytes into a `StringView` or appends them to a `String` (§22.2, §23.2). A compiler cannot quote a source name in a message it builds, nor build JSON, fixes or protocol replies that contain one. | the port's `text-gaps` probe: N0200 on `StringView.fromUtf8`, `String.appendBytes` | spec (§22.2), and an ADR if the owner wants one | **done** (2026-10-30): `StringView.fromUtf8`, type-system §22.2. Native code uses `nova_utf8_valid`; the interpreter uses .NET's `Utf8.IsValid`, so the run-pass test is a differential test of the two. Checked freestanding (an object with no undefined symbols, called from C). `run-pass/string-view-from-utf8.nova`, `check-fail/string-view-from-utf8*.nova` |\n| **S7** | **Host services that novac itself uses.** All of them are missing from §26 and `runtime/hosted`:<br>• a process with captured stdout and stderr, and stdin input (`CBackend.cs:257-259`);<br>• environment variables (`CC`, `PATH`, `NOVA_HOME`, `NOVA_INDEX`, `INCLUDE`);<br>• creating and listing directories;<br>• whether a file exists;<br>• a temporary directory (`Program.cs:614`);<br>• the working directory. | the port's `host-process` probe; a source audit of novac's `System.IO` and `Process` use | ADR (API, the interpreter sandbox, CosmoOS's spawn API) | **done** (2026-10-30): [ADR 0028](decisions/0028-m15-host-services.md) accepted, all as recommended. `File.kind` replaces `Path.kind`, since lib/std's names cannot be redeclared and the corpus has a `Path`. Spec in type-system §26.7–§26.10. The code is `lib/std/host.nova` over `nova_host_*` in `nova_rt_io.c` (POSIX, CosmoOS, Windows), with the interpreter's models in `Interpreter/HostServices.cs`. Tests: `run-pass/host-services.nova`, `check-fail/host-services.nova`, `HostServicesTests`, and `c3-host` (ProgramTests, and `c3.py` in a CosmoOS guest). Found on the way: library-only types an instance of lib/core's generic sort would need were pruned while its function-pointer typedefs were still emitted, which broke every hosted build. lib/std avoids the instance; the",
    168 =>
        " emitter gap stays open (see below) |\n\nFound while implementing S7, not yet fixed: an instance of a generic lib/core function made only by lib/std code (for\nexample `SliceAlgorithms<String>.sortBy`) has its types pruned as library-only (`IsUsedOutsideHostedLibrary`), while\nits function-pointer typedefs are still emitted. The generated C then fails to compile in every hosted program.\nlib/std's code avoids such instances; the emitter should prune the two consistently.\n\nFound after 0.4.0 (the CeeSafe port's re-pin, 2026-10-30): lib/std's private helpers in `host.nova` (`textOf`,\n`pathIn`, `fromAlloc` and others) were top-level functions, and lib/std's names cannot be redeclared in a hosted package\n(§38.5), so a program with its own `pathIn` or `fromAlloc` failed with N0201. Fixed by making them static functions of\n`HostServices` (`main` `24106b0`; released in 0.4.1); `run-pass/names-beside-lib-std.nova` declares every one of the\nold names.\n\nSmaller findings, not milestone items:\n- `type-system.md:702` still names N0258, which was retired in M3c.\n- N0245 is reported twice at one position in one larger program; every reduction tried reports it once.\n- When a `match` scrutinee fails to check (N0248), its arms' bindings are also reported as undefined (N0200).\n- The formatter wraps chained `if` expressions inside `match` arms awkwardly.\n- There is no explicit panic for internal-error assertions.\n\nFix the doc line with S1. Record the others in `risks.md` or as issues when they are reproduced.\n\nNot needed, despite first appearances (the port's probes):\n- a library hash map: one is written in Nova, insertion-ordered, `K: Key<K>`;\n- generic `impl<T>` and `Self`: one `impl` per instantiation works;\n- `From` for `?`: a generic lifting function works;\n- more index precision in the borrow checker: `splitAtMut`, a copy-out, or separate tables work;\n- a line-reading stdin: LSP and MCP framing can be buffered over `Console.read`.\n\n## S2: when a condition's temporary dies (decided 2026-10-30)\n\nlifetimes.md says a temporary lives \"until the end of the source statement that created it\". For `let` and `match`,\nnovac already destroyed it at the end of the statement: for a `match` scrutinee, after the arm. For a condition the\nsentence needed a precise reading.\n\nThe plan recommended the end of the `if` statement. Implementing it showed a cost: a borrow taken in the condition\nwould then reach the body, so `if (v.view().length > 0) { v.push(1); }` could become an error. Code accepted today\ncould have broken. So the rule chosen is the one C++ and Rust use for `if`:\n- **a condition counts as its own statement**: its temporaries end once it is evaluated, before the branch;\n- each evaluation of a `while` condition ends its own;\n- within a condition they end in reverse order of creation, as any statement's do.\n\nNo borrow is extended, and resources are freed sooner. The text is in lifetimes.md §2 and type-system §14.5.\n\nThe leak check: `RunCorpusTests` runs every program with `NOVA_ALLOC_REPORT=1` and requires `live=0` at a normal\nexit. A panic skips destructors by design, and `Process.exit` prints no report.\n\n## S3: a nesting limit, the compiler's stack, and a fact budget\n\n- **The limit.** grammar.md §2.2 sets 1,000 levels per file. A level is a nested block, statement, type, pattern or\n  unary operand, or a link of a left-associative chain (operators, `as`, calls, members, indexes, `?`, `!`): a long\n  chain is a deep tree, and the crash in #3 also came from `1 + 1 + …` with 100,000 terms. Past the limit, the parser\n  reports N0110 once and stops parsing the file. All 525 `.nova` files in the repository stay far below it.\n- **The stack.** Measured on 2026-10-30 (Release build, macOS, `ulimit -s 1024`), a 1 MiB main thread held only:\n\n  | Shape | Deepest accepted |\n  |---|---:|\n  | Parentheses | 426 |\n  | Blocks | 990 |\n  | Operator-chain links | 264 |\n\n  So the limit could not rest on the operating system's main thread, which is 1 MiB on Windows. Every ",
    169 =>
        "command now\n  runs on a dedicated thread with a 256 MiB stack, reserved address space committed only as used\n  (`CompilerThread`, as Goose's compiler does: `research/goose.md`). `Parser.ParseText` and `Compilation.Create` use\n  it too, so the language and MCP servers and the tests get the same guarantee. The deepest accepted inputs (998\n  parentheses, a 999-term chain) then pass `check`, `build` and `run --interp` with the OS stack at 1 MiB.\n- **The fact budget.** The deepest inputs exposed a pre-existing slowdown, present in 0.3.1 too: check elimination\n  saturated a closure over every fact at each checked operation, roughly cubic in a straight-line function's length.\n  A 300-term sum took 17 s to build and 25 s to interpret. A proof now considers at most 128 facts (optimisations.md\n  §2.5), and past that the check stays. The most any program in this repository or CosmoOS's Nova sources needs is 44,\n  and their generated C is byte-identical with and without the budget (216 hosted programs, 4 CosmoOS ports, 9 kernel\n  integrations). A 999-term sum now builds in 1.9 s.\n\n## S6: bytes to text (design sketch for the spec change)\n\n- `StringView.fromUtf8(bytes: Slice<u8>) -> StringView?`: a view of the same bytes if they are well-formed UTF-8\n  (Unicode Table 3-7), otherwise `null`. The view borrows `bytes` (scoped like the slice, §16.1), so it holds that\n  slice's loans. This keeps §22.2's invariant: every `StringView` is UTF-8.\n- `String.append(view)` then works on it. A separate `appendBytes` is not needed.\n- A runtime helper in `rt.h`/core (freestanding too), and the interpreter's equivalent. Differential tests against\n  the C helper cover every well-formed and ill-formed class: overlong forms, surrogates, values above U+10FFFF, and\n  truncation.\n- Not included: lossy decoding (U+FFFD) and character iteration. Each can follow on demand.\n\n## S7: host services (design sketch for the ADR)\n\n- `Process.capture(program, arguments, input: Slice<u8>?) -> Result<ProcessOutput, IoError>`, where `ProcessOutput`\n  holds `status: i32`, `stdout: Vector<u8>` and `stderr: Vector<u8>`. Hosted `posix_spawnp` with pipes. CosmoOS's spawn\n  API differs (§26.6), so `c3.py` covers it there. On Windows it uses the same layer MSVC builds already use.\n- `Environment.variable(name) -> Result<String?, AllocError>`, then `Directory.create`, `Directory.list` (sorted\n  by name, so programs stay deterministic), `File.exists`, `Directory.temporary()` (a fresh directory, removed by its\n  owner), and `Directory.current()`.\n- The interpreter's sandbox (`novac mcp`, `nova_run`) refuses all of them, as it refuses processes today.\n- Effects (§32): these operations are hosted-only and blocking, like the existing I/O.\n\n## Order and release\n\n1. **S1, S2, S4, S5** are fixes. They go on `main` with regressions, and are backported to `release-0.3` as\n   **novac 0.3.2** (`release.md`), so the port can re-pin without taking 0.4's other changes. S2's spec text is\n   decided first.\n2. **S3 and S6** change the language surface (a diagnostic; library API), so they ship in **0.4.0**. The port needs S6\n   before its P2 (the frontend), and S3 before P2's robustness gate.\n3. **S7**, after its ADR, ships in 0.4.0 or 0.5.0. The port needs it before its P5 (`build`).\n\n## Acceptance (proposed)\n\n- S1: #2's reproducer ends with N0222 at the call, not a crash, and `T = u32` still compiles and runs.\n- S2: the spec text accepted. The drop-order regression passes. `RunCorpusTests` checks `live=0`, and the whole corpus\n  passes it. #1's reproducer prints one `drop 1` and two `drop 4`s.\n- S3: N0110 documented with a parse-fail case at the limit plus one. The limit's measurement is recorded, and\n  100,000-deep input ends with N0110, not a crash.\n- S4, S5: #4's and #5's reproducers accepted as check-pass cases. The mutation suite and the corpus are unchanged.\n- S6: the spec section; run-pass cases with valid and invalid input, freestanding included; the differential helper\n  test.\n- S7: the ",
    170 =>
        "ADR accepted; run-pass and io tests on macOS, Linux and Windows; `c3.py` in CosmoOS for the spawn path;\n  sandbox refusals in `McpTests`.\n- For the milestone: CeeSafe's probes and support tests re-pinned to the release that carries each item, with their\n  workarounds removed.\n",
    171 =>
        "# Milestone 2 — Ownership and Borrowing (plan and acceptance)\n\nStatus: started 2026-10-22. Normative semantics: `ownership.md` (invariants O*/B*/E*/R*),\n`type-system.md`, `grammar.md`, and `lifetimes.md` (to be written in 2b). As in M1, each increment\nwrites its spec section first, then the code and tests.\n\n## Increments\n\n| # | Content | Spec sections | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **2a** | **Structs as plain-data value types**: declarations, struct literals, field read and write, nested structs, methods with a by-value `this`, static functions (`Point.origin()`), target layout with C static asserts | grammar §2, type-system §12 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 2b | **References**: `borrow T`, `borrow mut T`, borrow expressions, implicit call-site borrows and `mut x`, `borrow this` / `borrow mut this` receivers, auto-deref of field access. NIR places and loans. **NLL borrow checker** (B1–B5), return elision (E1–E2), no scoped fields (E3) | lifetimes.md, type-system §13 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 2c | **Move types and destruction**: `drop { }` blocks, implicit `Copy` (OD-7) versus move types, moves and `move x`, partial moves, init/move analysis on NIR (O2–O5, replacing the HIR flow analysis), drop elaboration with drop flags (O4), `using` (R3), `defer` | ownership.md §2–3, type-system §14 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 2d | **Enums and arrays**: `enum` with payloads, exhaustive `match`, fixed arrays `[T; N]`, `Slice<T>` / `SliceMut<T>` from arrays, bounds checks, `for` over arrays, slices and ranges; the mutation suite | grammar, type-system §15–§16 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n\n**M2 is complete** (2026-10-22): every acceptance criterion below is met, as recorded in the\nstatus notes after the criteria.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (whole milestone; from `roadmap.md`)\n\n1. Each invariant O1–O5, B1–B5 and E1–E4 has ≥ 2 compile-fail tests and ≥ 1 \"near-miss\"\n   compile-pass test (≥ 120 tests in total for these).\n2. Run tests for drop order (locals in reverse declaration order; fields in declaration order),\n   drops on early return, `break` and `continue`, and partial moves.\n3. Mutation suite: automated mutants of compile-pass programs (reordered statements, removed\n   scopes, duplicated moves) whose verdicts are computed independently of the checker.\n4. All run tests stay clean under ASan+UBSan and GCC/clang (CI), and still run inside CosmoOS\n   (`integrations/cosmoos/c1.py --corpus`) on both architectures.\n5. Docs: `lifetimes.md` (the checker algorithm as implemented), plus updated `ownership.md`,\n   `type-system.md`, `grammar.md`, `intermediate-representation.md` and `diagnostics.md`.\n\nStatus at the end of 2d:\n1. **Met.** 133 must-fail annotations for the ownership and borrowing diagnostics (N0212,\n   N0240–N0259) in `tests/corpus/check-fail/`, plus 26 near-miss functions in\n   `check-pass/{borrows,moves,slice-borrows}-accepted.nova`. The table below maps them to\n   invariants.\n2. **Met.** `run-pass/drop-*.nova`, `drops-*.nova`, `using-and-defer.nova`, `enum-drops.nova` and\n   `for-drops.nova`.\n3. **Met.** `tests/Nova.Compiler.Tests/MutationTests.cs`. Five seed programs of straight-line\n   ownership code are mutated with swaps, moves and removals of statements, added and removed\n   scopes, and duplicated moves. Each mutant's diagnostics, as (code, line) pairs, are predicted\n   by an independent oracle (scope-based name resolution, a move-state simulation, and\n   last-use liveness for loans). The compiler must report exactly those diagnostics. Each run\n   checks 1,152 distinct mutants. 600 of them resolve their names; 325 of those are rejected\n   by move or borrow errors and 275 are accepted. Injected checker bugs (shared loans\n   conflicting with nothing, use-only liveness, piecewise re-initialisation of a drop type) are\n   caught. The suite found one real gap: writing the only field of a moved drop-type struct was\n   accepted (fixed; `type-system.md` §14.4).\n4. **Met.** Local suite under clang `-O0`, `-O2`, portable ",
    172 =>
        "overflow and ASan+UBSan; CI with GCC\n   13 and clang 18; `c1.py --corpus` runs 99 programs inside CosmoOS on both arches.\n5. **Met.** `type-system.md` §15–§16, `grammar.md`, `lifetimes.md` (slices, index overlap,\n   write after value), `intermediate-representation.md`, `c-backend.md`, `diagnostics.md`.\n\n## 2a scope details\n\nIn scope:\n- `struct` declarations at the top level;\n- fields of any M1 type or another struct (no recursion by value);\n- literals `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`;\n- `p.x` reads and `p.x = e` writes on `var` locals;\n- methods `fn len(this) -> u64`, called as `v.len()`;\n- static functions in a struct, called as `Point.origin()`;\n- structs as parameters, return values and locals, all by value.\n\nEvery 2a struct is **Copy** because all fields are Copy and `drop` does not exist yet (OD-7).\n\nNot in 2a:\n- `==` on structs;\n- printing structs;\n- struct constants;\n- field shorthand `{ x }`;\n- generic structs;\n- `borrow this` (2b);\n- `drop` (2c).\n\n## Invariant coverage (criterion 1), as of 2d\n\nMust-fail tests are in `tests/corpus/check-fail/`. Near-misses (programs that must be accepted)\nare in `tests/corpus/check-pass/borrows-accepted.nova`.\n\n| Invariant | Must-fail tests | Near-miss passes |\n|---|---|---|\n| B1 XOR aliasing | borrow-conflicts: `twoMutableBorrows`, `sharedThenMutable`, `mutableThenShared`; borrow-two-phase: 2 | `disjointFields`, `manySharedBorrows` |\n| B2 loan validity | borrow-conflicts: `writeWhileShared`, `compoundWriteWhileShared`, `readWhileMut`, `passWhileMut`, `wholeWhileFieldBorrowed`, `fieldWhileWholeBorrowed`; borrow-temporaries: 2 | `mutableThenSharedAfterUse`, `temporaryUsedWithinStatement` |\n| B3 liveness (NLL) | borrow-conflicts: `inLoop` (loan live across iterations), `writeWhileShared` | `borrowEndsBeforeWrite`, `borrowInLoopBody` |\n| B4 reborrow | borrow-reborrow: 3 | `reborrowEndsThenOriginalUsable` |\n| B5 two-phase | borrow-two-phase: 2 | `twoPhaseMethodCall` |\n| E1 no return of local data | borrow-returns: 3 | `returnsFieldOfParameter` |\n| E2 return sources | borrow-returns: 2; reference-types: 5 | `returnsListedParameter` |\n| E3 no scoped fields | reference-types: struct field, constant | n/a (a type rule) |\n| O1 unique owner / O2 move de-initialises | moves: `useAfterMove`, `useAfterMoveIntoLocal`, `maybeMoved`, `movedInLoop`, `explicitMoveOfCopy`, `fieldThroughReference`, `wholeThroughReference`, `fieldOfDropType`, `fieldOfDropTypeTemporary` | moves-accepted: `reassignAfterMove`, `moveOnOnePathThenReassign`, `returnsMovesOut`, `borrowEndsThenMove` |\n| O3 definite initialisation | definite-assignment (5), field-of-unassigned, moves: `wholeAfterPartial` | moves-accepted: `deferReadsLaterAssignment`, `copyStructIsNotMoved` |\n| O4 exactly-once destruction | run tests check every drop's order and count: drop-order, drop-flags, drops-early-exit, drops-moves-and-returns, using-and-defer; also run under ASan | — |\n| O5 partial initialisation | moves: `wholeAfterPartial`; field-of-unassigned | moves-accepted: `reinitializeMovedField`, `movedFieldOtherFieldStillUsable` |\n| R3 using | moves: `fromUsing`, `fieldFromUsing` | moves-accepted: `usingIsBorrowable` |\n| Moves vs borrows (B2) | moves: `moveWhileBorrowed`, `moveFieldWhileWholeBorrowed` | moves-accepted: `borrowEndsThenMove` |\n| E4 no scoped globals | reference-types: constant; scoped-types: `EMPTY` | n/a (a type rule) |\n| 2d: enums | enums, moves: payload moves (N0251, N0258), `movedInArm`, `assignedInSomeArms`, `payloadThroughReference` | enums (check-pass), run-pass/enum-* |\n| 2d: arrays and slices (B1–B4, E1, E3) | slice-borrows: 6; slice-returns: 4; arrays: 31 type errors; scoped-types: 8; ownership-more: `sliceThenMutElement`, `loanAcrossIterations`, `elementReborrow`, `returnsLocalElement` | slice-borrows-accepted: 2 functions (5 near-miss patterns); check-pass/arrays |\n\n`check-fail/ownership-more.nova` adds 24 cases across O2, O3, O5, R3, B1–B4, E1 and temporaries.\n",
    173 =>
        "# Milestone 3 — Safe heap (plan and acceptance)\n\nStatus: started 2026-10-22. Normative semantics: `ownership.md` (H1–H3, O1–O5),\n`memory-model.md` §3.2, `type-system.md` §17–§18 (written increment by increment), and\nADR 0003 (built-in `Result`, `Box` and allocators ahead of M6 generics). As before, each\nincrement writes its spec first, then the code and tests.\n\n## Increments\n\n| # | Content | Spec sections | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **3a** | **Results and error propagation**: built-in `Result<T, E>` with `Ok`/`Err`, matching on results, `e?` (propagate) and `e!` (unwrap or panic), built-in `AllocError`. Also fixes discarded call results of drop types, which were never destroyed (an O4 bug from M2c) | type-system §17 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 3b | **Box and allocators**: `Box<T, A>` (owned, one pointer wide), `Box.create` / `Box.createIn`, auto-deref through boxes, recursive types through `Box`, `SystemHeap`, `FailingAllocator`. The runtime allocation API, with allocation accounting and a fault-injection hook (`NOVA_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`). Ex4 and Ex5 (concrete allocator). The fault-injection sweep in the test runner. Allocation in CosmoOS's libc for C2 | type-system §18, memory-model §3.2, c-backend | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 3c | **Moves out of boxes and deep structures**: moving fields out of a box's contents and re-initialising them (Ex14), moving payloads out of a `match` on an owned value, drop elaboration of partially moved boxes, and iterative destruction for deep recursive structures (R-9) | type-system §18.3–§18.5 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n\n## Acceptance criteria (from `roadmap.md`, adjusted by ADR 0003)\n\n1. **Fault-injection sweep.** For every run test that allocates *n* times, run it with\n   allocation *k* failing, for each *k ≤ n*. Every run must end in the expected way: a clean\n   exit with no live allocations, or the expected panic. Runs are clean under ASan+UBSan, with\n   no double frees.\n2. **Examples.** Ex4 and Ex14 as written, apart from syntax that has since changed. Ex5 with a\n   concrete allocator type, since generic functions are M6. Ex11 adapted: no `StringView`,\n   `checkedMul` or `okOr`, which are M6 library features.\n3. **Tests.** Positive and negative tests for every new rule, plus run tests for drop order\n   with boxes, on error paths (`?`), and for deep structures.\n4. All run tests stay clean under GCC/clang, ASan+UBSan and inside CosmoOS on both arches\n   (heap programs included, as groundwork for C2).\n5. Docs: `type-system.md`, `grammar.md`, `memory-model.md`, `c-backend.md`,\n   `intermediate-representation.md`, `diagnostics.md`, `risks.md` (R-7, R-9).\n\n**M3 is complete** (2026-10-22). Status of the criteria:\n1. **Met.** `FaultInjectionTests` sweeps every allocating run-pass program (6 programs: boxes, deep-structures, ex4, ex5, ex14, heap-error-paths) under\n   ASan+UBSan. Each failure point must end with `live=0` or the unwrap panic. Programs with more\n   than 200 attempts (`deep-structures.nova`, 600,000) are sampled at 200 points.\n2. **Met.** `run-pass/ex4-account.nova` and `ex14-tree.nova` run as written, apart from Ex14's\n   arm syntax and `own` parameter. `ex5-allocator.nova` uses a concrete allocator type, and\n   `ex11-errors.nova` is the adapted Ex11.\n3. **Met.** Check-fail: `results`, `boxes`, `box-borrows`, `box-moves`. Run tests:\n   `result-propagation`, `temporaries-on-early-exit`, `boxes`, `heap-error-paths`,\n   `consuming-match`, `deep-structures`.\n4. **Met.** Local suite (clang -O0/-O2/portable/ASan+UBSan); CI with GCC 13 and clang 18;\n   `c1.py --corpus` inside CosmoOS on both arches, heap programs included.\n5. **Met.** `type-system.md` §17–§18, `grammar.md`, `memory-model.md` §3.2, `c-backend.md`,\n   `intermediate-representation.md`, `diagnostics.md`, `risks.md` (R-7, R-9).\n\nFound and fixed during M3:\n- discarded call results of drop types were never destroyed (O4, from M2c);\n- statement temporaries were not ended on early exits from inside a statement (O4).\n\n##",
    174 =>
        " Deferred to M6 (ADR 0003)\n\n- user generic types and functions (Ex5 as written);\n- the `Allocator` interface for user allocators;\n- `T?`, `null`, and `?` on optionals;\n- `StringView`, `checkedMul` and `okOr` (Ex11 as written);\n- `From<E>` conversion in `?`. v0 requires the identical error type.\n",
    175 =>
        "# Milestone 4 — Regions (plan and acceptance)\n\nStatus: **complete 2026-10-22** (criteria below). Normative semantics: `ownership.md` R1–R4, `memory-model.md` §3.3–3.5,\n`type-system.md` §19 (written increment by increment), ADR 0004. Each increment writes its\nspec first, then the code and tests.\n\n## Increments\n\n| # | Content | Spec | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **4a** | **Scoped results and `Arena`**. Results holding references or slices, with loans tracked through any loan-holding local. `Arena.create(allocator, chunkSize)`, `arena.alloc(v)`, `arena.allocArray(n, fill)`, `arena.reset()`, bulk release on drop. R1 and R2 | type-system §19.1–19.3, lifetimes §3 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 4b | **`FixedBufferAllocator` and `Scratch`**: allocators over caller storage (scoped to it), and LIFO scratch frames (ADR 0004) | type-system §19.4–19.5, §14.6 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 4c | **`Pool<T>` and `PoolBox<T>`**: a typed pool of fixed capacity, with individually freeable, scoped boxes | type-system §19.6, §19.1 (`var`) | **done 2026-10-22** |\n\n## Acceptance criteria (`roadmap.md`)\n\n1. **Compile-fail tests for every escape form.** An allocation must not:\n   - be returned;\n   - be stored in a `Box` or another owned value;\n   - outlive its region through `reset`, drop, move or a scope end.\n   Each form gets a test for each region type.\n2. **Run tests for bulk release.** An arena with many allocations frees everything at reset and\n   drop (`live=0`), also under the fault-injection sweep.\n3. **R2 enforced.** No region accepts a type with `drop` or a scoped type (N02xx).\n4. Ex15 and Ex16 in adapted forms (ADR 0004): `allocArray(n, fill)`, and positional arguments.\n5. Clean under GCC/clang, ASan+UBSan, the fault-injection sweep, and inside CosmoOS\n   (`c2.py`) on both arches.\n6. Docs: `type-system.md`, `lifetimes.md`, `memory-model.md`, `c-backend.md`, `diagnostics.md`,\n   `risks.md`.\n\n## Status at completion\n\n1. **Met.** Escape forms by region type:\n\n   | Escape | Arena | FixedBufferAllocator | ScratchFrame | PoolBox |\n   |---|---|---|---|---|\n   | returned | `arena-escapes` (2) | `region-escapes` | `region-escapes` | `pool-borrows` |\n   | stored in a box, struct or other owned value | `arena-types` | `region-types` | — (a frame can't be stored either: `region-types`) | `pool-escapes` |\n   | region reset, moved or dropped while live | `arena-escapes` (reset, move) | storage access: `region-escapes` | `region-escapes` (move; parent allocates while child live) | `pool-borrows` (move) |\n   | temporary region | `arena-escapes` | `region-escapes` | `region-types` (N0247) | `pool-borrows` |\n\n2. **Met.** `arena-bulk-release` (5 reset rounds, then drop, `live=0`), `ex15-arena`,\n   `fixed-buffer-and-scratch` and `pools`, under the host and guest fault-injection sweeps.\n3. **Met.** N0264: `arena-types`, `region-types`, `pool-escapes`. Pools hold values with\n   `drop`: R2 is about bulk release, and pool boxes are destroyed one by one.\n4. **Met.** `run-pass/ex15-arena.nova`, `run-pass/ex16-fixed-buffer.nova`.\n5. **Met.** Local suite (clang -O0/-O2/portable/ASan+UBSan), CI (GCC 13, clang 18), and\n   `c2.py` inside CosmoOS on both arches.\n6. **Met.** `type-system.md` §14.6, §19; `lifetimes.md` §3; `memory-model.md` §3.4–3.5;\n   `c-backend.md`; `diagnostics.md`; ADR 0004.\n\nDesign corrections made during M4, all recorded in ADR 0004 and the spec:\n- `memory-model.md` §3.4 claimed that `using` alone kept scratch frames LIFO. It does not.\n  Nesting now borrows the parent's allocation right, and roots are unique.\n- A scratch frame's two borrow parts let a parent's existing allocations stay usable while a\n  child frame is live.\n- `using` bindings are pinned, not immutable.\n- Scoped values other than references and slices may be `var` (M4c).\n",
    176 =>
        "# Milestone 5 — Escape and placement analysis (plan and acceptance)\n\nStatus: **complete 2026-10-23** (run in parallel with M7's 24-hour campaign). Normative:\n`memory-model.md` §5 (rules, with the M5 implementation rules in §5.1), `c-backend.md` §2.2 (emission),\n`intermediate-representation.md` §5.5a (the interpreter). Spec first, then code and tests.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Analysis** (`src/Nova.Compiler/Analysis/PlacementAnalysis.cs`). It runs on checked NIR after\n  drop elaboration, so it never decides whether a program is accepted. For each `Box.create`\n  site it decides between:\n  - **stack**: the box does not escape, and its allocator is placement-transparent;\n  - **heap**: with the first reason it must stay there.\n- **Demotion.** A stack-placed box's value lives in a local of the function. The C backend and\n  the interpreter carry this out with `--placement` / `CEmitOptions.Placement`. It is **off by\n  default**: it removes allocation attempts, which the allocation report and the fault-injection\n  sweep observe. Making it the default is an M9 decision, taken with benchmarks. (M9e made it the default, with\n  `--no-placement` to turn it off: `milestone-9.md`, `optimisations.md` §4.)\n- **Report.** `novac build --explain-allocations` prints one line per allocation site: every\n  `Box.create`, and the collection and region sites that M5 does not analyse.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (`roadmap.md`)\n\n1. Optimised and unoptimised outputs are equal on the whole run corpus: standard output, exit\n   status and panics. A `clang -O2 placement` configuration runs every program.\n2. Drop timing is identical, checked by instrumentation: the interpreter records every executed\n   drop, and the trace with placement equals the trace without it, for every run program.\n3. Report snapshots: `tests/corpus/placement/*.nova` with reviewed `.alloc` files. They cover\n   each reason a box stays on the heap, and each placement.\n4. No program is accepted only because of placement. The analysis runs after checking, so\n   diagnostics cannot depend on it. A test checks that it leaves the NIR unchanged.\n5. The interpreter with placement agrees with the native placement build, allocation report\n   included. With placement, a program makes at most as many allocation attempts as without.\n6. Docs: `memory-model.md` §5, `c-backend.md`, `intermediate-representation.md`, `roadmap.md`,\n   `risks.md` (R-8), `testing.md`.\n\n## Acceptance results (2026-10-23)\n\n| # | Criterion | Evidence | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Equal outputs | Run-corpus configuration `clang -O2 placement`: all 137 run-pass and run-fail programs give the corpus expectations. `PlacementTests.Placement_is_unobservable`: in the interpreter, standard output, exit status and standard error (without the allocation line) are equal with and without placement, for every run program and `placement/reasons.nova`. The fuzz oracle gained a `clang -O2 placement` configuration: 500 programs (seeds 20000–20499) and the 32 `FuzzTests` seeds, 0 findings | met |\n| 2 | Drop timing identical (instrumented) | The same test compares the interpreter's drop traces (every executed `Drop`: function, position, type, in order): equal for every program | met |\n| 3 | Report snapshots | `placement/reasons.nova` / `.alloc` (reviewed): placements (field reads and writes, a shared borrow of the box, a field moved out, `?`) and each heap reason (not transparent, size, loop, assigned twice, moved into a call, borrowed mutably, stored outside the locals, an aggregate, a built-in, a result destroyed holding the box, returned), plus the unanalysed vector storage | met |\n| 4 | Not accepted because of placement | The analysis runs on checked NIR in the backend; `Analysis_does_not_change_the_program`: the NIR prints the same before and after it, for every run program | met |\n| 5 | Interpreter agrees with native placement; attempts never increase | `Native_placement_agrees_with_the_interpreter`: ASan+UBSan build with placement against the ",
    177 =>
        "interpreter with placement, allocation report included, for every run program; `Placement_is_unobservable` checks attempts with ≤ without | met |\n| 6 | Docs | `memory-model.md` §5.1, `c-backend.md` §2.2, `intermediate-representation.md` §5.5a, this file, `roadmap.md`, `risks.md` R-8, `testing.md` | met |\n\nOver the run corpus, 6 of 29 `Box.create` sites are placed on the stack. The rest escape by design\n(lists, trees, results stored in structures, failure-injection programs), are created in loops, or\nuse `FailingAllocator`. In generated fuzz programs, about 70% of sites are placed. Found while\nbuilding it, and fixed before acceptance: the first version of rule 4 sent every `Box.create(…)?`\nto the heap, because the error path destroys the result, which holds an `Err` there.\n\nNot done (recorded):\n- boxes created in loops (one slot per site; a per-iteration slot would need the box dead before\n  the back edge);\n- vector, string and collection storage;\n- `KernelHeap` (no kernel profile yet);\n- placement is opt-in until M9's benchmarks decide the default.\n",
    178 =>
        "# Milestone 6 — Generics and collections (plan and acceptance)\n\nStatus: **complete 2026-10-23** (started 2026-10-22). Normative semantics: `type-system.md` §20 onwards (written increment by\nincrement), `ownership.md` H2, `memory-model.md` §4, ADR 0005. Each increment writes its spec\nfirst, then the code and tests.\n\n## Increments\n\n| # | Content | Spec | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **6a** | **Generics**: type parameters on functions, structs and enums; explicit and inferred type arguments; checking generic bodies once with opaque type parameters (types, moves, borrows); monomorphisation | type-system §20 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 6b | **Interfaces**: `interface` declarations (generic too), `impl I for T`, bounds `<T: I>`, interface methods called on type parameters, `Copy` as a bound. Ex12, Ex13 (adapted) | type-system §21 | **done 2026-10-22** |\n| 6c | **Optionals and string views**: `T?`, `null`, `?` and `!` on optionals; `StringView` with string literals, `length`, indexing, slicing, comparison, printing | type-system §22 | **done 2026-10-23** |\n| 6d | **`Vector<T, A>` and `String<A>`**: growth with `Result`, indexing, views as slices, `for` over a vector, invalidation through borrows (H2, B5). Ex9 (adapted: `truncate<i32>` for `as i32`), Ex10 | type-system §23 | **done 2026-10-23** |\n| 6e | **`SlotMap<T>` / `Handle<T>` and `StableVector<T>`** | type-system §24 | **done 2026-10-23** |\n| 6f | **Model-based property tests** of the collections; invalidation compile-fail suite; the **OD-3 design** (lifetime-parameterised aggregates) | `od3-design.md`, testing.md §3 | **done 2026-10-23** |\n\n## Acceptance criteria (`roadmap.md`, adjusted by ADR 0005)\n\n1. Invalidation compile-fail tests: every operation that can move a collection's storage\n   (growth, removal, clear, drop, move) is rejected while an element borrow or view is live.\n2. Property tests: random operation sequences on each collection agree with a model, in the\n   test runner, under ASan+UBSan and the fault-injection sweep.\n3. Ex9, Ex10 and Ex12 as written apart from syntax that has since changed; Ex13 with integer\n   geometry (ADR 0005).\n4. Generic code is checked once: an error in a generic body is reported at the definition, even\n   if the body is never instantiated.\n5. Clean under GCC/clang, ASan+UBSan, and inside CosmoOS (`c2.py`) on both arches.\n6. Docs: `type-system.md`, `grammar.md`, `c-backend.md`, `diagnostics.md`, `memory-model.md`,\n   the OD-3 design, and `risks.md`.\n\n## Acceptance results (2026-10-23)\n\n| # | Criterion | Evidence | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Invalidation compile-fail suite | `check-fail/vector-invalidation.nova` (11 cases: push, pop, clear, element write, move, `viewMut`, growth through a call, push in `for`, escape, `append` of a self-view, element move-out), `check-fail/slot-map-invalidation.nova` (insert, remove, two `getMut`, move; stable-vector push under a mutable borrow), `check-fail/collection-invalidation.nova` (destruction while borrowed for all four collections; String append, clear and move; stable-vector write and move; vector reassignment). Near-misses accepted in the same files (handles across insert/remove/move; stable-vector push under a shared borrow) | met |\n| 2 | Model-based property tests | `CollectionPropertyTests`: 4 collections × 6 seeds × 80 operations. Each program matches its model under clang/GCC O0/O2/portable-overflow and ASan+UBSan, and passes the fault-injection sweep (48 cases). Planted bugs are caught: a different minimum capacity fails 8 cases, and a slot map that does not bump generations on removal fails 4 | met |\n| 3 | Ex9, Ex10, Ex12, Ex13 | `run-pass/ex9-vectors.nova` (adapted: `truncate<i32>` for a lossy `as`), `ex10-strings.nova` (as written), `ex12-ex13-interfaces.nova` (Ex13 with integer geometry, ADR 0005) | met |\n| 4 | Generic code checked once | `check-fail/generics.nova`: errors in never-instantiated generic bodies (`add<T>`, `field<T>`, `literal<T>`, …) are reported at the de",
    179 =>
