        "# Milestone 9 — Performance engineering (plan and report)\n\nStatus: **complete** (2026-10-25): 9a–9f done; the acceptance results are in 9f below. Roadmap acceptance: *optimisations with preconditions; a benchmark suite vs\nC/C++/Rust/Zig/Goose; each optimisation: equivalence tests + measured gain; published methodology; no claims without\ndata* (`roadmap.md`). Master prompt: §16 (optimisation strategy: each optimisation needs safety preconditions, tests,\ncorrectness checks, debugging support and performance measurements, against a correct baseline), §19 (the benchmark\nlist, the comparisons and the metrics), §21 Milestone 9 (\"implement optimization passes only after their correctness\nfoundations exist; measure each improvement\"), §22 rule 3 (no unproven performance claims).\n\n## Where the time goes today (inputs from earlier milestones)\n\n| Evidence | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| C6a LZ4 (`benchmarks/lz4/`) | compression 0.48–0.78 of C; decompression of literal runs 0.02–0.03 of C on x86-64, because a byte loop with two checked indexes per byte stands where C has `memcpy`; with `memcpy` (scratch experiment) Nova reached 0.94 of C |\n| C6b fbvalid (`benchmarks/fbvalid/`) | equal to C with clang, 1.76× C's time with gcc 12, cause not established |\n| risk P-7 | checked Nova too slow for kernel hot paths would push code back to C or to `unsafe` |\n| M5 | placement (non-escaping boxes on the stack) is opt-in, \"until M9's benchmarks decide the default\" |\n\nThe C compiler already does what most of §16's list asks for on Nova's C (constant folding, dead code and dead stores,\ncopy propagation, CSE, inlining, loop optimisation, scalar replacement), so Nova's own passes go where it cannot\nprove what Nova knows. That means the checks Nova inserts (bounds, overflow), where values live (placement), what\ndoes not alias (exclusive borrows), and operations that C expresses in bulk.\n\n## Plan\n\n| Increment | Content |\n|---|---|\n| **9a** the suite and its method | `benchmarks/suite/`: the 15 workloads of §19, each written in Nova, C, C++ and Rust with the same algorithm and data structures (and in Zig and Goose where the toolchain is available and the workload expressible). A runner that measures wall time (median of repeated runs), peak resident memory, allocation count and bytes (one counting `malloc` interposer for every language, since all of them allocate through the C library here), compile time and binary size, on the M1 and the Ryzen box. `docs/benchmarks.md` publishes the method; the baseline results are recorded before any optimisation. |\n| **9b** checked bulk operations | `copyFrom` and `fill` on `SliceMut` (spec first): one range check, then `memcpy`/`memset`; the LZ4 port and the suite's copy workload use them. **Done** (below) |\n| **9c** bounds-check elimination | a NIR pass that removes an index check another check, or a loop's range, already proves (a slice's length cannot change while it is borrowed). Preconditions written down; `--no-bce` to compare; equivalence tests: corpus, fuzz campaign, interpreter agreement. **Done** (below) |\n| **9d** overflow checks and the gcc gap | value-range elimination of overflow checks that cannot fail (loop counters bounded by a length); the cause of fbvalid's gcc gap, measured, then fixed or recorded. **Done** (below) |\n| **9e** placement by default | M5's placement analysis made the default if the suite shows a gain and every equivalence test passes; `--no-placement` stays. **Done** (below) |\n| **9e′** candidates from 9a | the niche layout for `Box<T>?` and `borrow T?` (F4: one pointer, null for `null`; spec first, type-system §22.1), and building `Box.create`'s argument in the heap rather than on the stack (F6); each taken only if the baseline shows it matters. **Done** (below) |\n| **9f** acceptance | the suite rerun after 9b–9e, each optimisation's gain attributed with its switch off and on, and the results published. **Done** (below) |\n\nNot planned: `restrict` from exclusive borrow",
