        "s (raw pointers and C code may alias what Nova thinks is exclusive; it\nneeds its own analysis of unsafe code first), and devirtualisation (Nova's generics are monomorphised; no dynamic\ndispatch exists yet).\n\n## Results\n\n### 9a — what writing the suite found in Nova (2026-10-25)\n\nWriting the fifteen workloads in Nova, next to the same programs in C, C++, Rust, Zig and Goose, showed these gaps.\nThey are recorded here, not fixed in 9a. Each one either cost a workaround in the suite's Nova code, which is\ncommented where it occurs, or made a Nova row slower or larger.\n\n| # | Finding | Where it shows | Kind |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | `last = Some(arena.alloc(Node { … })!)` inside a loop is rejected with N0240 (\"cannot assign to a temporary value while a temporary value is borrowed\"). The same expression moved into a helper function that returns `borrow(r) Node<r>` is accepted | `arena/main.nova` (`push`) | borrow checker false positive (inferred: the temporary `Result` is still considered borrowed at the assignment) |\n| F2 | `borrow T?` means an optional reference. A reference to an optional cannot be written, and both forms print identically in diagnostics | — | syntax and diagnostics; closed in M11 (G6: `borrow (T?)`) |\n| F3 | `Some(b)`, with `b: borrow Box<T>`, does not coerce to `borrow T?`; a helper `fn contents(b: borrow Box<Node>) -> borrow Node => b;` does | `linked-list/main.nova` | coercion gap |\n| F4 | `Box<T>?` and `borrow T?` take a tag word plus the pointer, 16 bytes rather than 8: the niche layout of `language-spec.md` §2 and `memory-model.md` is still future work (`type-system.md` §22.1, `c-backend.md` §2). A linked-list node is 24 bytes rather than 16 | `linked-list` allocates 48 MB where the others allocate 32 MB; on macOS, whose allocator rounds 24 bytes up to 32, its peak heap is twice C's | layout; **fixed in 9e′** (the niche layout) |\n| F5 | No byte or character literals (`'k'`); no number formatting into a `String`; no `if` expressions; no wrapping arithmetic; no floating point | `parsing`, `string-building` (`\"0123456789\"[d..d+1]`) | expressiveness; all but floating point closed in M11 (G4, G5; `milestone-11.md`) |\n| F6 | `Box.create([0u8; 65536])` zeroes a 64 KiB stack temporary with a byte loop, passes it by value to the box helper (`nvB_…(nvA65536_u8 v)`), and copies it into the heap: two 64 KiB copies where C has one `memset` | `large-objects` (generated C) | code generation; **fixed in 9e′** (built in place) |\n| F7 | The emitter wrote type names containing `??` (as in `Box<Res>??`, the result of `Vector<Box<Res>?>.pop()`) into `_Static_assert` messages unescaped. `??)` is a C trigraph, so clang warned and `-Werror` builds failed | **fixed** in 9a: the messages go through `CMessageLiteral`, which writes `?` as `\\?` (not `CStringLiteral`'s octal escapes, which clang rejects in an unevaluated string such as a `_Static_assert` message); `run-pass/vectors.nova` covers it | backend bug |\n| F8 | The expression parser did not accept `?` inside generic arguments (`Vector<Box<Pair>?>.create()`) | **fixed** in 9a (`grammar.md`, generic arguments in expressions) | parser |\n\n### 9a — the baseline (run 2026-10-25)\n\nRyzen 5 PRO 3600, Debian 12, Linux 6.1; the Nova tree at the 9a change (on top of `227bec9`); `run.py --runs 5`\nunder `nice`, once with clang 18.1.8 and once with gcc 12.2.0 as the C compiler (`nova`, `c`, `cpp` and `goose` use\nit; Rust and Zig use their own LLVM). Load average 0.8 before, 2.1–2.3 during and after: the machine is shared, and\nthe median spread (maximum − minimum over the median) of the five runs is 9%, with a few rows up to 55%. Differences\nunder about 15% are within that noise. All 234 rows printed the expected checksum. Reproduce with\n`benchmarks/suite/run.py` and `report.py` (`benchmarks.md` §4). The memory, allocation and compile tables are from\nthe clang run; with gcc they differ only in the C-compiled rows, by under 1%.\n\n**Wall time, ms, and relative to C (x86_64 ",
