    193 =>
        "ector-growth` | 2610 (4.41) | 592 (1.00) | 1051 (1.77) | 553 (0.93) | 621 (1.05) | 621 (1.05) | 608 (1.03) | 304 (0.51) |\n| `string-building` | 1216 (3.94) | 308 (1.00) | 527 (1.71) | 431 (1.40) | 483 (1.57) | 359 (1.16) | 489 (1.58) | 363 (1.18) |\n| `recursion` | 449 (3.04) | 148 (1.00) | 146 (0.99) | 313 (2.12) | 493 (3.34) | 287 (1.95) | 436 (2.95) | 176 (1.19) |\n| `linked-list` | 495 (1.18) | 419 (1.00) | 454 (1.08) | 471 (1.12) | 467 (1.12) | 389 (0.93) | 378 (0.90) | 60 (0.14) |\n| `binary-trees` | 630 (1.32) | 477 (1.00) | 571 (1.20) | 552 (1.16) | 583 (1.22) | 494 (1.04) | 557 (1.17) | 69 (0.15) |\n| `hash-map` | 872 (1.57) | 554 (1.00) | 458 (0.83) | 505 (0.91) | 493 (0.89) | 390 (0.70) | 499 (0.90) | 399 (0.72) |\n| `parsing` | 355 (1.96) | 181 (1.00) | 154 (0.85) | 186 (1.03) | 248 (1.37) | 139 (0.77) | 205 (1.13) | 143 (0.79) |\n| `memory-copy` | 4544 (12.96) | 351 (1.00) | 367 (1.05) | 266 (0.76) | 249 (0.71) | 533 (1.52) | 600 (1.71) | 1900 (5.42) |\n| `numeric` | 369 (1.56) | 236 (1.00) | 214 (0.91) | 231 (0.98) | 230 (0.97) | 231 (0.98) | 246 (1.04) | 233 (0.98) |\n| `data-oriented` | 254 (2.19) | 116 (1.00) | 138 (1.19) | 161 (1.39) | 136 (1.17) | 212 (1.83) | 137 (1.19) | 117 (1.01) |\n\n**What the baseline says about Nova** (ratios are Nova's time over C's, clang / gcc):\n\n| Gap | Cause | Increment |\n|---|---|---|\n| `memory-copy` 17.3 / 13.0 | the block copy is a byte loop with two checked indexes per byte, where C has `memcpy`; and C's time is understated: clang merged its block copies into one (found in 9b, below) | 9b (bulk copy) |\n| `vector-growth` 1.87 / 4.41, `string-building` 2.37 / 3.94, `parsing` 1.56 / 1.96 | each `push`/`append` is a checked call through the `Result` path; the vector grows by allocate, copy byte by byte, free (`nova_vec_grow`, `runtime/core/nova_rt_core.c:131-146`), not `realloc`, so its peak heap is 1.5× C's (384 against 256 MiB, 48 against 32 MiB in `parsing`) | 9b (the copy as `memcpy`), 9c/9d (checks in the loop); growth through the allocator's `realloc` is a candidate |\n| `recursion` 1.63 / 3.04 | the overflow-checked add; `rust-checked` (1.51 / 3.34) and `zig-safe` (1.38 / 2.95) pay the same | 9d (value ranges cannot remove it here: fib's values are unbounded; recorded as the cost of the check) |\n| `large-objects` 0.98 / 3.02 | F6: two 64 KiB copies per box, which clang removes and gcc does not | 9e′ |\n| `binary-trees` 1.43 / 1.32, `arena` 1.55 / 2.27, `linked-list` 1.12 / 1.18 | F4: nodes holding `Box<T>?` or `borrow T?` are larger (binary-trees' peak heap 20 against 12 MiB, arena's chunks 29,601 against 19,601) | 9e′ (niche layout) |\n| `stack-alloc` 1.17 / 1.60, `numeric` 1.52 / 1.56, `data-oriented` 0.86 / 2.19 | index and overflow checks in tight loops; under gcc more than under clang, as in C6b's fbvalid | 9c, 9d |\n| `small-objects` 1.22 / 1.24, `heap-alloc` 1.20 / 1.82 | the allocation path (`nova_alloc`'s fault-injection counters, `nova_rt_core.c:232-248`) and `Box.create`'s `Result` (inferred) | a leaner allocation path |\n| `hash-map` 1.02 / 1.57 | probing loop checks; equal to C under clang | 9c |\n\nNova never failed a check here; its binaries are 32 KiB (C's 16), and it allocates nothing before `main` beyond what C does. Its compile time is\n0.5–0.6 s per program, most of it the .NET start-up of `novac` (inferred: the C compile is 0.05 s).\n\nOther languages, for context and not as Nova claims: Goose's pools make its rows with many small nodes 4–7× faster\nthan C's allocator (`linked-list`, `binary-trees`, `arena`); Zig's ReleaseFast `heap-alloc` makes one allocation in\nall (LLVM removed the allocation, which C at -O2 did not); Rust's checked overflow costs it 1.0–1.6× its unchecked build.\n### 9b — checked bulk operations (2026-10-25)\n\nWhat was built:\n- `d.copyFrom(s)` and `d.fill(v)` on a `SliceMut<T>` (type-system §16.5; spec first). `copyFrom` checks\n  that the lengths are equal (a new panic, \"slice lengths differ\", `NV_PANIC_LENGTH`), then makes one copy with",
    194 =>
        " `memmove` semantics.\n  `fill` is a `memset` for one-byte elements, and otherwise a loop in the generated C. The safety argument\n  is unchanged: the receiver's loan is live while the source is evaluated, so in safe code the source cannot overlap the\n  destination (`check-fail/slice-bulk-loans.nova`). If `unsafe` code makes them overlap, the result is still defined\n  (`run-pass/slice-bulk.nova` copies through overlapping `fromRaw` slices in both directions, under ASan+UBSan too).\n  In the IR, a `LengthCheck` statement is followed by a `SliceCopy` or `SliceFill` built-in (`intermediate-representation.md`).\n  The C runtime has `nova_mem_copy/move/set` (`__builtin_mem*`, `<string.h>` under MSVC) and\n  guards the zero-length case, since a slice from raw parts may be null when empty.\n- `nova_vec_grow` copies with `nova_mem_copy` instead of a byte loop.\n- The LZ4 port's literal copy and table clear use the new operations (`integrations/cosmoos/c6/lz4.nova`). `c6.py\n  --component lz4` passes: the host test, 20,000 fuzz runs, and the differential test (20,000 cases, 0 mismatches).\n  The aarch64 module object now has `memmove` undefined, which CosmoOS exports, and still meets C4's checks.\n- Tests:\n  - run: `run-pass/slice-bulk.nova` (every receiver form; u8, i8, bool, u32, u64, struct and enum elements; generic\n    elements; empty and null slices; evaluation order), `run-fail/slice-copy-length.nova`;\n  - check: `check-fail/slice-bulk.nova` and `slice-bulk-loans.nova`;\n  - emit: the C snapshot `emit-c/slice-bulk.c`;\n  - freestanding: `demo_blocks` in `freestanding/demo.nova`, on the host and both CosmoOS triples, with the symbol\n    allowlist unchanged.\n  - 1,995 tests pass.\n\n**A flaw in the 9a `memory-copy` workload.** In the baseline the blocks were copied in address order, and clang turned C's\n4,096 adjacent `memcpy` calls of 4 KiB into one 16 MiB `memcpy` (the disassembly passes `0x1000000` to `memcpy`). C's\nbaseline time (252 ms) therefore measured one large copy, not the workload. Rust's 290 ms suggests the same of LLVM\n(inferred, not disassembled). The workload now copies block i × 2053 mod 4096 in step i: each block once, in an order no\ncompiler can merge. The checksum is unchanged, and the change was made in all six languages (`benchmarks/suite/README.md`).\nThe M9a `memory-copy` row above stays as it was measured, but it is not comparable with the rows below.\n\nResults (Ryzen, as for 9a; load 0.6–2.5; `run.py --runs 5`; the tree at the 9b change). `memory-copy` with the permuted\nblocks, all languages:\n\n| C compiler | nova | c | cpp | rust | rust-checked | zig | zig-safe | goose |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| clang 18.1.8 | 686 (1.06) | 646 (1.00) | 686 (1.06) | 705 (1.09) | 638 (0.99) | 608 (0.94) | 661 (1.02) | 636 (0.99) |\n| gcc 12.2.0 | 834 (1.67) | 499 (1.00) | 425 (0.85) | 631 (1.27) | 629 (1.26) | 632 (1.27) | 590 (1.18) | 2080 (4.17) |\n\nTimes are ms, with the ratio to C in brackets.\n- `copyFrom`'s own gain was measured against the 9a element loop, with the same permuted blocks. That variant is the\n  workload with the call replaced by `for (k in 0..BLOCK) { to[block + k] = from[block + k]; }`, run through\n  `measure.c` six times; the medians below are of the last five runs. It took 2,552 ms (clang) and 3,220 ms (gcc), so\n  `copyFrom` is 3.7× and 3.9× faster.\n- Under clang Nova is now level with C, C++, Rust and Zig.\n- Under gcc the remaining gap has a visible cause: gcc compiles C's constant-size `memcpy(…, 4096)` inline as\n  `rep movsq`, whereas Nova's length is a value at run time and goes to glibc's `memmove`. That is for 9d (the gcc gap).\n\nThe rest of the suite, Nova and C only (`run.py --langs nova,c`), against the baseline:\n- **Vector growth under gcc** went from 2,610 to 1,217 ms (4.41× → 2.11× C), from the `memcpy` in `nova_vec_grow`.\n  Under clang it is unchanged (1.87× → 1.86×), presumably because clang already made the byte loop a `memcpy`\n  (inferred). The peak heap is still 1.5",
    195 =>
        "× C's: growth is still allocate, copy, free.\n- Every other Nova/C ratio moved by less than the 15% noise band, in both directions: string-building 2.37 → 2.57 under\n  clang, binary-trees 1.43 → 1.23, numeric 1.56 → 1.77 under gcc. The C times moved by as much (C's binary-trees went\n  469 → 513 ms, with nothing changed), so these are noise, not effects of 9b.\n\nLZ4 (`benchmarks/lz4/run.py`, same machine, MB/s, Nova/C; baseline in `benchmarks/lz4/README.md`):\n\n| Compiler | Set | compress | decompress before → after |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| clang | text | 0.73 | 1.12 → 0.94 |\n| | random | 0.57 | **0.03 → 0.97** |\n| | records | 0.57 | 0.18 → 0.22 |\n| gcc | text | 0.63 | 0.65 → 0.69 |\n| | random | 0.55 | **0.02 → 0.89** |\n| | records | 0.48 | 0.31 → 0.39 |\n\nLiteral copies (`random`) now run at about C's speed, as the C6 scratch experiment predicted (0.94). Compression and\nshort matches (`records`) are unchanged: they are byte loops with checked indexes in both codecs, which is 9c's work.\n`text` decompression, whose literals are short, measured 545, 586 and 514 MB/s in three clang runs (0.89–1.02 of C),\nagainst 657.7 in the single baseline run (1.12). Each literal is now a call to `memmove` where the loop was inline, so a\nsmall cost for short copies is possible. It is not established beyond the run-to-run variation, and is a 9c question:\nwhen the range checks are proven, a short copy could stay inline.\n\n### 9c — bounds-check elimination (2026-10-25)\n\nWhat was built (spec first: `optimisations.md`, normative; ADR 0010):\n- `Analysis/BoundsCheckElimination.cs` (renamed `CheckElimination.cs` in 9d): a forward dataflow over each function's NIR. It removes a `BoundsCheck` when\n  facts holding before it prove the index is below the length (§2.4 lists the facts and how they are derived).\n  The C backend omits proved checks. `--no-bce` keeps them; `--explain-bounds-checks` lists every check as removed\n  or kept.\n- The owner chose to add a rule to the unsafe contract (ADR 0010, `unsafe.md` §2). A pointer made from a reference\n  may write its target only until the target is next used directly. That lets the analysis treat any local,\n  including a vector's length, as fixed between two mutable borrows of it. The repository's unsafe code was audited\n  against the rule (casts of references to pointers in `lib/core`, `integrations/cosmoos/**`, `tests/corpus/**`). Every\n  such pointer targets a struct, a field or array elements, or (`emit-c/volatile.nova`) is written before the local\n  is used again: no violation.\n- Equivalence checks:\n  - the interpreter evaluates every check and faults if a removed one would fail; every interpreter run in the suite\n    checks this, and `BoundsCheckEliminationTests` shows the fault on a forced removal;\n  - a `clang -O2 no-bce` configuration in the run corpus and the fuzzer's oracle;\n  - `tests/corpus/bounds-checks/proved.nova` and `kept.nova`, with reviewed `.bce` snapshots: 14 checks proved, one per\n    loop or guard shape, and 8 kept. Seven of the kept are near misses: a vector popped in the loop, a borrowed index,\n    another slice's length, `<=`, `||`, a stable vector and an unguarded offset. The eighth is a known miss, a vector\n    behind a reference.\n- Two emit-C snapshots lost a check (`arrays.c`: index 2 of a 3-element array through a local; `vectors.c`: a second\n  `v[0]` after the first one passed). 2,000 tests pass. `c6.py --component all` passes: both host tests, the fuzz\n  targets and the differential tests, 0 mismatches.\n\nWhat it removes. With `--explain-bounds-checks`:\n- the LZ4 port: 14 of 21 checks, all of the decompressor's hot ones (literal and match copies, token and offset\n  reads) and the compressor's match extension. The 7 kept are in `load32`, which indexes through its parameter;\n  `table[h]` (a function's result); `out[token]`; and `readLength` (through `*ip`). All of these need facts from\n  another function;\n- the suite: `data-oriented` 20 of 26, `stack-alloc` and",
    196 =>
        " `heap-alloc` all; `hash-map`, `numeric`, `string-building` and\n  `memory-copy` none. Their indexes relate to a vector's length only through the number of `push` calls made earlier\n  (`composite[j]` with `j ≤ LIMIT`), or through a constant mask, which no rule of §2.4 covers.\n\nMeasurements (Ryzen, as before; load 1.2–2.5; `run.py --runs 5`, and the same with `--nova-flag=--no-bce`):\n- **Under clang, nothing changes.** clang's binary for `data-oriented` is byte-identical with and without the analysis,\n  although the generated C differs by 20 checks: clang's own optimiser had already removed them, so 9c cannot gain\n  there (verified with `cmp`). LZ4 under clang: equal within noise.\n- **Under gcc, LZ4 gains**: decompression 1.32× for `text` (423 → 557 MB/s) and 1.33× for `records` (1310 → 1748 MB/s),\n  compression 1.13× for `text`. Nova/C for `records` decompression is now 0.53 (0.39 before 9c).\n- **The suite** shows no change beyond its noise in either compiler. An interleaved control (`hash-map`, whose\n  binaries are identical with and without the analysis) varied by up to 20% between runs on this shared machine,\n  so suite differences under that are not evidence. `data-oriented` under gcc, measured interleaved: equal.\n- Compile time: unchanged within noise (0.51–0.74 s either way).\n\nSo 9c's value is in what gcc does not prove itself, and in kernel code whose loops have their tests spelled out (LZ4).\nIt does not close the suite's gaps; most of those are vector growth, overflow checks and allocation (9d, 9e′).\n\nFuzz campaign (2026-10-25, Ryzen, 10 jobs, 60 minutes, seeds 2000000–2011301): 11,302 programs, 0 findings\n(1,447 panicked, 8,065 other exit statuses). The oracle compared every C configuration, `clang -O2 no-bce` among them,\nand the interpreter, which faults if a removed check would fail.\n\n### 9d — overflow checks and the gcc gap (2026-10-25)\n\nWhat was built. The spec came first: `optimisations.md` §3 (normative), and `c-backend.md` §5 and §6.\n- **Overflow-check elimination.**\n  - `Analysis/CheckElimination.cs` (9c's analysis, renamed) also decides each checked `+`, `-`, `*` and negation.\n  - It uses the same facts at the same program points. Each term gets bounds: its type's range, narrowed by what it\n    is known to be below or above.\n  - An operation is proved when its result fits by interval arithmetic, when an unsigned subtraction has `b ≤ a`, or\n    when an unsigned sum is bounded through the facts (`to + i < L`).\n  - Two new kinds of fact feed it: the exact result of arithmetic on constants, and the range of a widening cast of\n    a value that is not a term (a field).\n  - Proved operations are emitted as plain C, `(T)(a + b)`.\n  - `--no-oce` keeps every check; `--explain-overflow-checks` lists every decision.\n  - The interpreter evaluates every operation exactly, and faults if a removed check would have overflowed.\n- **Location constants.**\n  - The checked helpers in `rt.h` now take `const nv_loc *`, pointing to a per-site constant `nvL_<n>` that is read\n    only on failure.\n  - The emitter writes the program twice: a trial emission collects the locations, so that the table can come\n    before the code.\n  - `nova_panic` still takes its location by value, so environments that define it are unchanged.\n  - C4's checks pass with the table on both architectures: its relocations are `R_X86_64_64` and `R_AARCH64_ABS64`,\n    which CosmoOS's module loader applies.\n- **Zero-initialisation only where it is needed.**\n  - An aggregate local keeps `{0}` only when part of it can be written without assigning it whole (a field, an\n    element, a borrow, an `Unpack` target), or when one of its variant payloads is read.\n  - Every other aggregate is assigned whole before each read.\n- **Equivalence checks.**\n  - The `clang -O2 no-bce` configuration of the run corpus and the fuzzer became `clang -O2 no-elim`, with both\n    eliminations off.\n  - `tests/corpus/overflow-checks/proved.nova` and `kept.nova`, with rev",
    197 =>
        "iewed `.oce` snapshots: 13 proved and 1 kept\n    in `proved` (the kept one is `out.length - to`, which nothing tests first); 7 kept and 1 proved in `kept`.\n  - `CheckEliminationTests` shows the interpreter's fault on a forced removal.\n\nWhat it found:\n- **The cause of fbvalid's gcc gap (C6b, 1.76×).** It was the zero-initialisation.\n  - Every aggregate local was declared `= {0}`. `refusal` has 32 such locals: optional `StringView` temporaries, one\n    per early return.\n  - gcc 12 does not remove those stores. It reserves 424 bytes of stack and zeroes 144 of them on every call, before\n    the first check.\n  - Removing the initialisers from a copy of the generated C took the port from 12.9 to 7.8 ns/call, against C's 7.3.\n    Removing only the scalar initialisers changed nothing.\n- **Locations in the hot path.** Passed by value, each check's location (a pointer and two 32-bit numbers) was loaded\n  into registers on the path where the check passes. A copy of `data-oriented`'s C with constant locations went from\n  254 to 203 ms under gcc; clang was unchanged.\n- **A gcc warning the fuzzer found.** The first fuzz campaign found 4 programs, all one case, that gcc `-O0 -Werror`\n  rejected after the zero-initialisation change.\n  - An enum local that only ever holds `V2` is matched against `V0`, whose payload sits in bytes `V2` does not\n    write.\n  - The arm never runs, but `-Wmaybe-uninitialized` cannot relate the payload to the tag.\n  - Locals whose payload is read keep `{0}` (`c-backend.md` §5), and `run-pass/payload-reads.nova` keeps it fixed.\n- **Code placement moves LZ4 under gcc by up to 25%.**\n  - Restoring every `{0}` in LZ4's generated C made its decompressor faster at default alignment (`records` 1816 →\n    2290 MB/s).\n  - With `-falign-loops=32` the order reversed: 2359–2383 without the initialisers, 2219–2255 with them.\n  - The two decompressors execute the same instructions in a different register order. LZ4 differences under gcc of\n    that size are placement, not cost.\n- **The location change alone slows LZ4's decompressor under gcc by 6–15%,** at either alignment.\n  - The assembly shows why: `ip` lives on the stack because `readLength` borrows it, and with registers freed gcc\n    now reloads it there at every sequence.\n  - With overflow elimination as well, the decompressor is faster than before (below).\n- **`recursion` under gcc runs in 3 ms (C: 166).**\n  - Once `n - 1` and `n - 2` are unchecked, gcc inlines several levels of `fib` and, treating it as a pure function,\n    merges the repeated calls. The exponential recursion collapses.\n  - gcc does not do this for the C program at `-O2`. The checksum is right, so it is a legitimate result, but it is\n    gcc's transformation, not evidence about Nova.\n  - From here on the workload is uninformative under gcc.\n\nWhat it removes, with `--explain-overflow-checks`:\n- LZ4: 35 of 50.\n- fbvalid: all 4: two products of 32-bit fields widened to `u64`, a sum of two widened `u8`s, and `U64_MAX - x`.\n- The suite: 91 of 151 (`numeric` 16 of 25, `hash-map` 10 of 17, `parsing` 10 of 13, `data-oriented` 8 of 23).\n- What stays is mostly arithmetic on values read from memory (`x[i] += vx[i]`) and accumulators (`t += …`), which no\n  fact bounds.\n\nMeasurements (Ryzen; three rounds alternating 9c, this tree with `--no-oce`, and this tree; medians of the rounds'\nmedians; `benchmarks/suite/run.py --runs 5`, `benchmarks/lz4/run.py`, `benchmarks/fbvalid/run.py`):\n\n| gcc 12 | 9c | 9d `--no-oce` | 9d | 9d / 9c |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| fbvalid (ns/call; C 7.2–7.5) | 12.95 | 7.67 | 7.86 | 0.61 |\n| LZ4 `text` decompress (MB/s; C 669–693) | 536 | 465 | 575 | 1.07× faster |\n| LZ4 `records` decompress (MB/s; C 3000–3437) | 1801 | 1641 | 2229 | 1.24× faster |\n| LZ4 `text` compress (MB/s; C 512–534) | 335 | 360 | 364 | 1.09× faster |\n| `data-oriented` (ms; C 117) | 266 | 182 | 202 | 0.76 |\n| `parsing` (ms; C 198) | 311 | 272 | 262 | 0.84 |\n| `hash-map` (ms; C 533) | 873 | 680 | 75",
    198 =>
        "8 | 0.87 |\n\n| clang 18 | 9c | 9d `--no-oce` | 9d | 9d / 9c |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| fbvalid (ns/call; C 6.4–6.6) | 6.62 | 6.76 | 6.90 | 1.04 (rounds 6.60–7.09) |\n| LZ4 `records` decompress (MB/s; C 7700–9340) | 2072 | 2093 | 7137 | 3.44× faster |\n| LZ4 `text` decompress (MB/s; C 566–605) | 588 | 572 | 600 | equal |\n| LZ4 `text` compress (MB/s; C 501–508) | 364 | 362 | 384 | 1.05× faster |\n| `parsing` (ms; C 136) | 243 | 232 | 202 | 0.83 |\n| `numeric` (ms; C 233) | 325 | 340 | 371 | 1.14 (not reproduced, below) |\n\n- **LZ4 under clang.** With `op + i` and `from + i` unchecked, clang vectorises the decompressor's match copy (16-byte\n  `movups`, two per iteration), which it did not do in 9c. `records`, whose matches are long, is now 0.77 of C.\n- **`numeric` under clang** was 1.13× (the first run) and 1.14× (this run) slower with overflow elimination. Built and\n  timed outside the suite runner, the two builds take the same time (334–339 ms each, five runs). The cause is not\n  established; it is recorded for 9f's attribution.\n\nElsewhere in the suite the differences are within the rounds' spread (1–13%), except gcc's `recursion` (above).\nCompile time is unchanged (0.5–0.7 s; the second, trial emission is a few milliseconds).\n\nRemaining gcc gaps:\n- `memory-copy`, 1.77× C: C's constant-size `memcpy` is inlined as `rep movsq`, while Nova's length is a run-time\n  value.\n- `large-objects` (3.2×) and `string-building` (3.5×): F6 and vector growth (9e′).\n- These are recorded, not fixed in 9d.\n\nFuzz campaign (2026-10-25, Ryzen, 10 jobs, 60 minutes, seeds 3000000–3011580): 11,581 programs, 0 findings\n(1,430 panicked, 8,312 other exit statuses). The first campaign on the same seeds, before the payload rule, found\nthe gcc warning above in its first 400 programs.\n\n### 9e — placement by default (2026-10-25)\n\nWhat it found first: as M5 left it, placement changed nothing in the suite.\n- `--explain-allocations` over the fifteen workloads placed no box at all. Two sites are returned (`binary-trees`),\n  two are stored in aggregates (`small-objects`, `linked-list`), and one is over the 1 KiB limit (`large-objects`).\n- The fifth, `heap-alloc`, was rejected only because it is in a loop: M5 refused every loop site, so that each site\n  needs one slot.\n- Making placement the default as it was would have been a switch with nothing to measure.\n\nWhat was built. The spec came first: `memory-model.md` §5.1 and `optimisations.md` §4, plus `c-backend.md` §2.2 and\n`intermediate-representation.md` §5.5a.\n- **Loop sites.**\n  - A box created in a loop is placed when every local that may hold it ends, by its `StorageDead`, on every path\n    from the site back to itself.\n  - The box of one iteration is then gone before the next, so one slot per site still suffices.\n  - A local declared outside the loop and assigned in it keeps the box on the heap (\"may outlive its loop\n    iteration\").\n- **Interpreter checks.**\n  - Running a placed site again while its previous box is alive is a fault, since natively the slot would be\n    overwritten.\n  - So is destroying a placed box twice. Before, the second destruction was silent; natively it runs the contents'\n    drop glue twice. A mutation test shows each fault.\n  - The interpreter now keeps one slot allocation per site instead of one per execution.\n- **The default.**\n  - `CEmitOptions.Placement` and `InterpreterOptions.Placement` default to on. `novac --no-placement` keeps every box\n    on the heap, and `--placement` is still accepted.\n  - The run corpus's and the fuzzer's oracle configuration is now `clang -O2 no-placement`.\n  - `optimisations.md` §1's rule that an optimisation keeps allocation counts now names placement as its one\n    exception (§4.2). The allocation report and fault injection see fewer `SystemHeap` attempts, which `SystemHeap`'s\n    placement-transparent contract allows. `FailingAllocator`, `KernelHeap` and user allocators are never placed.\n- **Tests.**\n  - `placement/lo",
    199 =>
        "ops.nova` (`.alloc` snapshot):\n    - five loop sites placed: per-iteration use, `continue` and `break`, nested loops, and a box whose field is moved\n      out (ended by `BoxFree`);\n    - one near miss kept on the heap.\n  - `reasons.nova`'s loop site is now placed.\n  - `emit-c/placed-boxes.nova` shows the slot reused in a loop.\n  - Two interpreter mutation tests: a duplicated drop of a heap box, and of a placed one.\n  - 2,117 tests pass.\n\nMeasurements:\n- Only `heap-alloc`'s generated C changes; the other fourteen workloads' C is byte-identical with and without\n  placement.\n- `heap-alloc` (Ryzen, five alternated rounds, `run.py --workloads heap-alloc`):\n\n| `heap-alloc` | without placement (ms) | with placement (ms) | gain | C | Rust |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| gcc 12 | 804–845 (median 821) | 699–743 (median 708) | 1.16× | 457 | 567 |\n| clang 18 | 379–427 (median 381) | 287–309 (median 293) | 1.30× | 318 | 548 |\n\n- Allocations drop from 10,000,001 to 1.\n- Under clang Nova is now 0.92 of C. Under gcc it is still 1.55× C.\n- The generated C zeroes a 256-byte temporary each iteration and copies it into the slot before the loop writes the\n  real values; C writes the values directly. That is F6's shape, the likely cause of what remains under gcc\n  (inferred; 9e′).\n\nNot done: placement of boxes that escape into aggregates (`small-objects`, `linked-list`) or are returned\n(`binary-trees`). Those need ownership to move with the value, a different analysis.\n\nFuzz campaign (2026-10-25, Ryzen, 10 jobs, 60 minutes, seeds 4000000–4011613): 11,614 programs, 0 findings\n(1,453 panicked, 8,384 other exit statuses). Placement is on in every configuration but the `clang -O2 no-placement`\noracle, and in the interpreter.\n\n### 9e′ — the niche layout and `Box.create` in place (2026-10-25)\n\nBoth 9a findings showed in the baseline:\n- F4 made nodes holding `Box<T>?` or `borrow T?` 24 bytes instead of 16.\n- F6 made `large-objects` 2.9× C under gcc.\n\nSo both were taken. Each spec came first.\n- **The niche layout** (type-system §22.1, `c-backend.md` §2).\n  - `Box<T, A>?` and `borrow T?` / `borrow mut T?` are their pointer alone, null for `null`. Pool boxes, slices and\n    string views keep the tag.\n  - A box or reference is never null: allocation returns a pointer or fails, `nova_alloc` gives zero-sized objects\n    one byte, a placed box points to its slot, and U2 requires a reference made from a raw pointer to be non-null.\n  - Optionals do not cross the C boundary (§28.3), so no C code sees the layout.\n  - `TypeTable.EnumLayout` gives these optionals the pointer's size, no tag and the payload at offset 0.\n  - In the emitter, three helpers carry every tag read, payload access and construction: `Tag`, `Payload` and\n    `OptionValue`. They cover the drop glue, `Unpack`, the discriminant, enum aggregates, and the vector and\n    slot-map helpers.\n  - In the interpreter, `VariantIndex` reads a niche optional's pointer, and `EnumValue` writes it.\n- **`Box.create` in place** (`c-backend.md` §2). When the argument is a temporary built by the statement just before\n  (array repeat, array, struct or enum aggregate) and used nowhere else, and its type has no drop and no C state:\n  - the site calls `nvBA_<box>()`, the allocation alone, and writes the construction into `*p`;\n  - a placed box is built in its slot the same way;\n  - the construction's operands are already evaluated, so it can neither panic nor allocate, and the order is\n    unobservable;\n  - a type with a drop goes through `nvB_` as before, which destroys the value if allocation fails.\n- **Tests.**\n  - `run-pass/niche-optionals.nova`: a linked list through `Box<Node>?`, `borrow Node?` walks, `?` and `!` on\n    optional references, a consuming match out of `Some(box)`, `Box<T>??` from `Vector.pop`, and drops of\n    `Some(box)`. It runs in every configuration and in the interpreter.\n  - `NicheLayoutTests` checks the sizes: 8 bytes for the three niche kinds, and a tag for views, sli",
    200 =>
        "ces, nested\n    optionals and integers. A `Node` holding a `Box<Node>?` is now 16 bytes.\n  - `emit-c/boxes-in-place.nova`: a heap box built in place, and one that must not be, since its type has a drop.\n  - The `slot-maps` and `placed-boxes` snapshots were re-reviewed.\n  - 2,130 tests pass. `c2.py --ssh` on the CosmoOS VM: 160 programs and the guest sweep (30 programs, 251 runs)\n    PASS. `c4.py` on both arches: PASS.\n- One emitter detail: the structured printer re-indented every statement line from scratch, which flattened the\n  in-place block. Lines deeper than one level now keep their extra indent.\n\nMeasurements:\n- Only six workloads' generated C changes. LZ4, fbvalid and the other nine workloads are byte-identical.\n- Ryzen, three alternated rounds of 9e and 9e′, medians in ms, with Nova/C in brackets:\n\n| Workload | gcc 12: 9e | gcc 12: 9e′ | gain | clang 18: 9e | clang 18: 9e′ | gain | memory |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| `large-objects` | 971 (2.92) | 278 (0.84) | 3.49× | 292 (1.03) | 286 (1.01) | 1.02× | — |\n| `arena` | 1681 (2.11) | 950 (1.20) | 1.77× | 1290 (1.53) | 930 (1.10) | 1.39× | peak 4.6 → 3.1 MiB (C 3.1); chunks 1850 → 1225 MiB |\n| `heap-alloc` | 721 (1.63) | 551 (1.25) | 1.31× | 289 (0.91) | 290 (0.91) | equal | — |\n| `linked-list` | 508 (1.30) | 415 (1.06) | 1.22× | 430 (1.06) | 417 (1.02) | 1.03× | 458 → 305 MiB allocated (C 305) |\n| `binary-trees` | 626 (1.28) | 587 (1.20) | 1.07× | 664 (1.32) | 568 (1.13) | 1.17× | peak 20 → 12 MiB (C 12); 915 → 457 MiB allocated |\n| `small-objects` | 388 (1.29) | 351 (1.17) | 1.11× | 366 (1.24) | 369 (1.25) | equal | — |\n\n- F6 is most of the gcc gains: clang had already removed the temporary and the copies, so its rows barely move.\n- F4 is the memory column, and `arena`'s and `binary-trees`' gains under both compilers. Nodes are now C's size, and\n  so are the peak heaps.\n\n- MSVC: the first CI run failed. With in-place construction the temporary was no longer used, and MSVC's C4101\n  (\"unreferenced local\", an error with `/WX`) rejected its declaration; `NV_UNUSED` does not silence MSVC. Such\n  temporaries are no longer declared.\n\nFuzz campaign (2026-10-25, Ryzen, 10 jobs, 60 minutes, seeds 5000000–5011602): 11,603 programs, 0 findings\n(1,468 panicked, 8,377 other exit statuses).\n\n### 9f — acceptance (2026-10-25)\n\nRun on the Ryzen (`m9f.sh`: `benchmarks/suite/run.py` and `benchmarks/suite/attribute.py`) and on the Windows VM\n(`benchmarks/suite/run_msvc.py`, `docs/benchmarks.md` §4.1):\n- **A.** The whole suite in every language, as 9a's baseline was run: one `run.py --runs 5` per compiler, with Zig\n  0.17.0 and Goose.\n- **B.** Nova by default, with each switch off (`--no-bce`, `--no-oce`, `--no-placement`), and with all three off:\n  three alternated rounds per compiler, on the suite, LZ4 and fbvalid.\n- **MSVC.** Nova and C, three rounds.\n\n**Where Nova stands.** Nova/C, with Nova's time the median of B's three default rounds and C's from A:\n\n| Compiler | Nova/C across the fifteen workloads | median | 9a's baseline |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| clang 18 | 0.93 (`heap-alloc`, `data-oriented`) to 2.44 (`string-building`) | 1.17 | 0.86 to 17.33 (`memory-copy`) |\n| gcc 12 | 1.02 (`large-objects`) to 3.54 (`string-building`) | 1.40 | 1.18 to 12.96 (`memory-copy`) |\n| MSVC 19.51, ARM64 | 0.99 (`large-objects`, `recursion`) to 2.27 (`string-building`) | 1.18 | — |\n\nThe gcc row leaves out `recursion`, which gcc rewrites algorithmically (9d): 0.02 of C. Every number keeps every check\nthat could fail.\n\n**Against 9a's baseline** (Nova/C in A's single run against 9a's; days apart, so read changes under about 15% as\nnoise):\n\n| Workload | clang 9a → 9f | gcc 9a → 9f | what changed it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `memory-copy` | 17.33 → 0.96 | 12.96 → 1.56 | 9b `copyFrom` (and 9a's C was understated, 9b) |\n| `vector-growth` | 1.87 → 2.03 | 4.41 → 2.12 | 9b, `memcpy` in vector growth |\n| `large-objects` | 0.98 → 1.00 | 3.02 → 1.02 | 9e",
