        "′ `Box.create` in place |\n| `arena` | 1.55 → 1.02 | 2.27 → 1.22 | 9e′ one-pointer optionals |\n| `heap-alloc` | 1.20 → 0.91 | 1.82 → 1.28 | 9e placement of loop boxes, 9e′ in place |\n| `binary-trees` | 1.43 → 1.28 | 1.32 → 1.22 | 9e′ one-pointer optionals |\n| `parsing` | 1.56 → 1.44 | 1.96 → 1.61 | 9c/9d, 9d locations |\n| `data-oriented` | 0.86 → 0.93 | 2.19 → 1.93 (B: 1.72) | 9d locations |\n| `hash-map` | 1.02 → 1.23 | 1.57 → 1.27 | (clang: within this machine's noise; B's median: 0.96) |\n| `string-building` | 2.37 → 2.51 | 3.94 → 3.55 | — (growth and appends, not yet addressed) |\n\n**Each switch's share.** The time with the optimisation off, over the time with it on (B's medians). `numeric` and\n`linked-list` have no box placement could place, so their C is identical with and without `--no-placement`. Yet\nthey read 1.13 and 1.15 under clang: differences under about 15% between variants are this machine's noise, not\nevidence.\n\n| Program | `--no-bce` | `--no-oce` | `--no-placement` | all off |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| `heap-alloc`, gcc / clang | 1.00 / 1.03 | 0.99 / 0.97 | **1.16 / 1.29** | **1.21 / 1.29** |\n| `parsing`, clang | 1.00 | 1.17 | 0.99 | 1.15 |\n| LZ4 `records` decompress, clang (MB/s 7149 on) | **3.30** | **3.44** | 1.00 | **3.43** |\n| LZ4 `records` decompress, gcc (MB/s 2398 on) | 1.13 | **1.46** | 0.98 | **1.73** |\n| LZ4 `text` decompress, gcc (MB/s 575 on) | 1.02 | **1.22** | 0.99 | **1.24** |\n| LZ4 `text` compress, gcc / clang | 1.02 / 1.05 | 1.00 / 1.06 | 1.02 / 0.99 | 1.08 / 1.09 |\n| `recursion`, gcc | 1.00 | 186 | 1.53 | 197 (gcc's call merging, 9d) |\n| every other suite workload | within noise | within noise, except `data-oriented` gcc 0.91 and `hash-map` gcc 0.83 (11% spread) | within noise | — |\n| fbvalid, gcc / clang | 1.01 / 1.01 | 0.99 / 0.99 | 1.00 / 1.00 | 0.99 / 1.00 |\n\n- LZ4's decompressor under clang needs both eliminations: with either off, the match copy is not vectorised. Under\n  gcc, overflow elimination gains the most and both together 1.73×.\n- fbvalid's gain (12.9 → 7.8 ns under gcc, 9d) came from the zero-initialisation rule, which has no switch.\n- So did 9b's bulk copies and 9e′'s layout and in-place construction, which the table against 9a shows.\n- `data-oriented` under gcc is 9% faster with overflow elimination off, as in 9d. The code is placement-sensitive\n  under gcc (9d), and the cause is not established. It stays recorded.\n\n**MSVC** (Windows VM, ARM64, three rounds; Nova/C per round, then the median): `small-objects` 1.12, `large-objects`\n0.99, `stack-alloc` 1.73, `heap-alloc` 1.01, `arena` 1.07, `vector-growth` 1.33, `string-building` 2.27, `recursion`\n0.99, `linked-list` 1.08, `binary-trees` 1.18, `hash-map` 1.31, `parsing` 1.79, `memory-copy` 1.07, `numeric` 1.38,\n`data-oriented` 1.43. Under MSVC, `rt.h` uses the portable overflow checks, not compiler builtins (`c-backend.md`\n§1.1).\n\n**Acceptance** (`roadmap.md`, M9):\n- *Optimisations with preconditions:* `optimisations.md` §2–§4 (normative), with a switch each.\n- *A benchmark suite against C/C++/Rust/Zig/Goose:* fifteen workloads in eight languages and modes (A, below).\n- *Each optimisation: equivalence tests and a measured gain:*\n  - the interpreter checks every removed check and every placed box;\n  - the corpus and the fuzzer build without each optimisation;\n  - a 60-minute campaign after each increment: 9c–9e′, 0 findings at the end of each, with 9d's first campaign\n    finding a gcc `-Werror` issue that was fixed;\n  - gains measured in 9b–9e′ and attributed above.\n- *Published methodology:* `benchmarks.md`.\n- *No claims without data:* each figure here names its run.\n\nM9 is complete.\n\nWhat remains slow, for later work:\n- `string-building` (2.4–3.5× C) and `vector-growth` (about 2×): growth is allocate, copy, free (no `realloc`), and\n  every `push`/`append` goes through the `Result` path;\n- `parsing` and `numeric` (about 1.45×);\n- `stack-alloc` under gcc (1.43×) an",
