    214 =>
        "# Optimisations — preconditions and checks\n\nStatus: **normative** from M9c (2026-10-25); §3 from M9d; §4 from M9e. Master prompt §16: an optimisation needs safety preconditions, tests,\ncorrectness checks, debugging support and measurements, against a correct baseline. This document states each\noptimisation's preconditions, which must hold for every program the checker accepts, and how the toolchain checks\nthat the optimisation changes nothing a program can observe. Results are in `milestone-9.md`.\n\n## 1. Rules for every optimisation\n\n- It must not change what a program does: its output, exit status, panics (which one, and where), and its\n  allocations as counted by the runtime. A removed check must be one that cannot fail. The one exception is\n  placement (§4), which removes allocations of a placement-transparent allocator by design.\n- It has a switch that turns it off (`--no-…`), so that the unoptimised build stays available as an oracle and for\n  measuring the gain.\n- It is decided from facts the compiler already proves (types, loans, checked arithmetic), never from heuristics\n  about what a program \"probably\" does, and never by weakening a check of `ownership.md`.\n- The interpreter checks it on every program it runs, where it can (below), and the run corpus and the fuzzer run\n  with it both on and off.\n- Its output is deterministic, like the rest of the compiler.\n\n## 2. Bounds-check elimination (M9c)\n\n### 2.1 What is removed\nA `BoundsCheck(i < L)` (`intermediate-representation.md`; the index check of `type-system.md` §16.3) whose condition\nholds every time the statement is reached. The C backend then emits no `nv_check_index` for it. Nothing else is\nremoved: range checks (`a[lo..hi]`), length checks (`copyFrom`), overflow checks and every other panic stay. Because a\nremoved check never fails, which panic a program reaches, and where, does not change.\n\nThe analysis is `Analysis/CheckElimination.cs`, which also decides overflow checks (§3). It runs on each function's final NIR (after drop\nelaboration), and its result is the set of checks it proved. The C backend and the interpreter consult that set.\nThe NIR itself is not changed.\n\n### 2.2 Terms and facts\nA **term** is an integer the analysis can name at a program point:\n- the current value of a tracked local (§2.3);\n- an integer constant;\n- the length of a tracked slice, string view, vector or string local.\n\nA **fact** relates two terms: `a = b`, `a ≤ b`, `a < b`. Terms are mathematical integers and facts are integer\nrelations. That is sound because Nova's arithmetic is checked: `+`, `-`, `*` panic rather than wrap (§6), and `as`\nis lossless (§7). A **definition** `x = a op b` records how a local was computed, for `op` among `+`, `-`, `%`, `&`\nand `>>` on unsigned integers. A **condition** `c = a cmp b` records the comparison that produced a `bool` local.\n\n### 2.3 Tracked locals\nA fact may mention only a tracked local:\n- an integer, `bool`, slice or `StringView` local, for its value (and a slice's or view's length);\n- a `Vector` or `String` local, for its length.\n\nA tracked local changes only through an assignment to it, which the analysis sees, or through a mutable loan of its\nown storage: `borrow mut i`, or `push`, `pop`, `clear` and `append`, which take `borrow mut` of a vector. While such a\nloan is live, every other use of the local is rejected (B1–B2), so no new fact about it can be made. After the loan,\nnothing may write the local through a pointer made from it (U2, ADR 0010). A shared loan cannot change it. So facts\nabout a local end at each mutable `Ref` or `Slice` of a place rooted at it without a dereference, and a value or\nlength read after that is a new fact.\n\nA tracked local's facts end at:\n- an assignment to a place rooted at the local without a dereference;\n- a mutable borrow of such a place, as above;\n- being a target of `Unpack`;\n- its `StorageDead`.\n\nA local that is moved out is uninitialised until it is assigned again, and that assignment e",
    215 =>
        "nds its facts.\n\nA fact that ends with a local but also holds of a term known to be equal to it is kept, rewritten to that term.\nSo `t = length(s); m = t - 1; StorageDead(t)` still knows `m < len(s)`.\n\n### 2.4 Where facts come from\n- **Copies**: `x = y` and `x = y as T` (lossless) give `x = y`. `x = length(s)` gives `x = len(s)`. `x = c` gives\n  `x = c`, and `x = c op d` for constants and `+`, `-`, `*` gives `x` its exact value when that is in `x`'s range (M9d).\n  `x = e as T` where `e` is not a term (a field, an element) gives `min(E) ≤ x ≤ max(E)` for `e`'s type `E` (M9d).\n- **Definitions**: `x = a op b` with unsigned operands, unless `x` is itself one of `a`, `b` (as in `i = i + 1`, where\n  the old value is gone). If a definition `y = a op b` of the same terms already holds, `x = y` holds after the\n  assignment, even when `a` or `b` is `x`'s old value: `i = i + 1` after a test of `i + 1 < n` gives `i < n`.\n- **Comparisons**: a `bool` local `c = a cmp b` comparing integers (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) gives two implications:\n  if `c` is true, the relation holds; if false, its negation (`a < b` false is `b ≤ a`). A copy of `c` or `!c`\n  carries them, and a `Branch` on `c` adds the relation of the edge it takes. A `bool` local assigned a constant is\n  known to hold it.\n- **Passed checks**: after `BoundsCheck(i < L)`, `i < L`; after `RangeCheck(lo, hi, L)`, `lo ≤ hi` and `hi ≤ L`;\n  after `LengthCheck(a, b)`, `a = b`.\n\nDerived facts:\n- a < b implies a ≤ b;\n- ≤ and < are transitive (and a ≤ b < c gives a < c);\n- constants compare as integers;\n- equal definitions (the same operation on equal terms) have equal results;\n- x = a − b gives x ≤ a and b ≤ a (the subtraction did not panic), and x < a when b is a constant ≥ 1;\n- t = L − o with a constant c ≥ 1 and c ≤ t gives o < L (some length remains after o);\n- x = a % n gives x < n;\n- x = a & m gives x ≤ m and x ≤ a;\n- x = a >> k gives x ≤ a;\n- x = a + b gives a ≤ x and b ≤ x, and a < x when b is a constant ≥ 1;\n- x = o + i, with i < t (or i ≤ t) and t = L − o for the same `o`, gives x < L (or x ≤ L): an offset plus an index\n  below the remaining length stays below the length.\n- x = a + m and y = b + m (the same `m`) with a ≤ b give x ≤ y, and with a < b, x < y.\n\nA check `BoundsCheck(i < L)` is removed when `i < L` is derived from the facts that hold before it.\n\n### 2.5 The analysis\nForward dataflow over the control-flow graph. The facts at a block's entry are those holding on every incoming edge;\na block not yet reached holds everything. At a join of two paths the analysis keeps:\n- the facts of both paths;\n- an implication \"if `c` is `w`, then F\" of one path, if the other path knows `c` is not `w` (it holds vacuously\n  there) or knows F itself;\n- a comparison or definition F of one path as \"if `c` is not `w`, then F\", if the other path knows `c` is `w`.\n\n`a && b` and `a || b` lower to joins of a path that computed `b` and a path that assigned a constant, so\n`while (i < s.length && s[i] != 0)` and `if (k == 0 || n - k < 4) { return; }` keep what their tests showed.\n\nEvery rule keeps only facts that hold on both paths, so each fact in a block's entry holds on every execution that\nreaches it. Loops are iterated until no entry changes. The join is not a plain intersection, so the iteration is\nbounded (64 visits per block on average). A function that reaches the bound has nothing removed. Implications use a\nlength rather than a compiler temporary known to equal it, since the temporary may be gone where the implication is\nused. Derived facts are computed by saturation, which does not depend on the order facts were found, so the result\nis deterministic.\n\nA proof considers at most **128 facts** (M15 S3); where more hold, the check stays. The cost of saturation grows with\nthe number of facts, cubically, and a long straight-line function adds one at each step, so a sum of 300 terms took\n17 s to compile before the budget. That covers both this secti",
    216 =>
        "on's index checks and §3's overflow checks. The most\nfacts any proof needs in the repository's programs, benchmarks and libraries, or in CosmoOS's Nova sources, is 44\n(measured 2026-10-30). Their generated C was byte-identical with and without the budget.\n\n### 2.6 Unsafe code\nThe rule of ADR 0010 is the only assumption about `unsafe` code. The unsafe code in this repository (`lib/core`,\n`integrations/cosmoos/**`, `tests/corpus/**`, `programs/`, `benchmarks/`) was audited for writes through a pointer\nmade from a reference after its target was used directly (2026-10-25): none, as recorded in `milestone-9.md` (9c).\n\n### 2.7 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `novac build --no-bce` and `novac run --no-bce` keep every check (`CEmitOptions.BoundsCheckElimination`,\n  `InterpreterOptions.BoundsCheckElimination`).\n- `novac build --explain-bounds-checks` prints each index check of the package, in source order, with\n  `removed` or `kept`.\n\n### 2.8 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** evaluates every bounds check, removed or not. If a removed one would fail, it stops with an\n  interpreter fault (exit status 70, \"an eliminated bounds check fails\"): a compiler bug. Every interpreter run checks\n  this: the corpus agreement tests, the fault-injection sweep, the property tests and the fuzzer's oracle.\n- **The run corpus** builds every program with the analysis (every configuration) and without it (`clang -O2 no-elim`),\n  so output and panics are compared both ways. So does the fuzzer's oracle.\n- **`CheckEliminationTests`** and `tests/corpus/bounds-checks/*.nova`: snapshots of `--explain-bounds-checks`\n  (`.bce`) for loops that must be proved, and for near misses that must keep their check (a reassigned index, a\n  mutated vector, a borrowed index, `<=` bounds, a loop bounded by another slice's length, a stable vector, whose\n`push` takes only `borrow this`).\n\nStable vectors (`type-system.md` §24.2) are not tracked: their length grows through a shared borrow.\n\n### 2.9 Character boundaries in ASCII text (after M13)\nA slice of a `StringView` (`v[lo..hi]`, type-system §22.2) checks the range and that `lo` and `hi` are character\nboundaries. In text whose bytes are all ASCII (each below 0x80), every index is a boundary, so the boundary check\ncannot fail. When the text is a local every assignment of which is a string literal of ASCII bytes, or a copy of\nsuch a local, the C backend checks the range only (`nv_check_range` for `nv_check_str_range`). The range check stays.\n- The analysis is per function and flow-insensitive, like §5.1's; it is part of `CheckElimination` and runs under the\n  same switch (`--no-bce` keeps the boundary check too). `--explain-bounds-checks` lists each slice of a view with\n  `boundary removed` or `boundary kept`.\n- The interpreter checks boundaries on every slice; if one whose check was removed is not a boundary, it stops with\n  an interpreter fault (\"an eliminated boundary check fails\"), as for §2.8.\n- The run corpus's and the fuzzer's `clang -O2 no-elim` configuration builds without it.\n\n## 3. Overflow-check elimination (M9d)\n\n### 3.1 What is removed\nThe overflow check of an integer `+`, `-` or `*` (`type-system.md` §6) or of a negation, when the exact result is in\nthe range of its type every time the operation runs. The C backend then writes the operation without a check\n(`(T)(a + b)`, with no undefined behaviour, since the exact result fits). Nothing else is removed: division and\nremainder keep their zero-divisor and `MIN / -1` checks, shifts their amount checks, and conversions their range\nchecks. A removed check never fails, so which panic a program reaches, and where, does not change.\n\nIt is part of the same analysis as §2 (`Analysis/CheckElimination.cs`), with the same facts at the same program\npoints, and it adds no new kind of fact. §2's facts treat checked arithmetic as exact; that stays true, since an\noperation whose check is removed was proved not to overflow.\n\n### 3.2 Bounds of a term\nEvery term ha",
    217 =>
        "s a range that holds whatever the facts say: a local's is its type's (`i32`: −2³¹ to 2³¹ − 1), a\nlength's is that of `usize`, and a constant's is itself. From the facts before the operation, a term `a` has:\n- an **upper bound**, the least of `max(u)` over the terms `u` with `a ≤ u` derived (§2.4), and of `max(u) − 1`\n  over those with `a < u` derived;\n- a **lower bound**, the greatest of `min(l)` over the terms `l` with `l ≤ a`, and of `min(l) + 1` over those with\n  `l < a`.\n\nAn operand that is not a term (an element read from memory, a field) has its type's range.\n\n### 3.3 When an operation cannot overflow\nWith the operands' bounds `[lo(a), hi(a)]` and `[lo(b), hi(b)]` and the result type's range `[MIN, MAX]`, the check is\nremoved when one of these holds:\n- **by interval**: every combination of the bounds stays in `[MIN, MAX]`: for `a + b`, `lo(a) + lo(b) ≥ MIN` and\n  `hi(a) + hi(b) ≤ MAX`; for `a - b`, `lo(a) − hi(b) ≥ MIN` and `hi(a) − lo(b) ≤ MAX`; for `a * b`, the four products of\n  the bounds; for `-a`, `−hi(a) ≥ MIN` and `−lo(a) ≤ MAX`;\n- **an unsigned subtraction** `a - b` with `b ≤ a` derived;\n- **an unsigned addition** `a + b` whose sum `s`, added to the facts as the definition `s = a + b` (exact: §2.4's\n  rules for `+` hold of the mathematical sum), has an upper bound of at most `MAX`. That covers an offset plus an\n  index below the remaining length (`to + i < L`, §2.4), which the interval rule does not.\n\nAn operation whose operands are all constants is decided by its exact result alone, whatever the facts (M11, found by\nthe G8c fuzz campaign). Under a condition that is never true, such as `if (1u16 < 0u16)`, the facts contradict and\nevery rule above would hold vacuously. The check would then be removed from code that never runs, which is sound,\nbut the C would hold an overflowing constant expression such as `1 + 2147483647`, which C compilers reject under\n`-Werror`.\n\nThe bound rule is what makes a counter safe: in `for (i in 0..n)` the increment `i + 1` runs after `i < n`, so\n`hi(i) ≤ max(n) − 1` and `i + 1` does not overflow. A loop over a bound held in a constant (`for (s in 0..100)`) is\nthe same.\n\n### 3.4 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `novac build --no-oce` and `novac run --no-oce` keep every overflow check\n  (`CEmitOptions.OverflowCheckElimination`, `InterpreterOptions.OverflowCheckElimination`). `--no-bce` and `--no-oce`\n  are independent.\n- `novac build --explain-overflow-checks` prints each checked `+`, `-`, `*` and negation of the package, in source\n  order, with `removed` or `kept`.\n\n### 3.5 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** evaluates every operation exactly, removed or not. If a removed one would overflow, it stops\n  with an interpreter fault (exit status 70, \"an eliminated overflow check fails\"). As for §2, every interpreter run\n  checks this.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program without either elimination (`clang -O2 no-elim`) as well as\n  with both, and compare.\n- **`CheckEliminationTests`** and `tests/corpus/overflow-checks/*.nova`: snapshots of `--explain-overflow-checks`\n  (`.oce`) for operations that must be proved (counters, constant bounds, `b ≤ a` subtractions, offsets below a\n  length, widened operands) and near misses that must keep their check (a counter bounded by `<=` the type's\n  maximum, a sum of two unbounded values, a subtraction tested the wrong way, a signed negation that may be `MIN`).\n\n## 4. Placement (M5; on by default from M9e)\n\n### 4.1 What changes\nA `Box.create` site that `PlacementAnalysis` places (`memory-model.md` §5) keeps its value in a slot of the\nfunction's frame instead of the heap. The rules and preconditions are `memory-model.md` §5 and §5.1; they are not\nrepeated here. Since M9e a site in a loop may be placed when its box ends within the iteration (§5.1).\n\n### 4.2 What a program can observe\nOutput, exit status, panics and drop timing are unchanged. Allocation counts are not. A placed ",
    218 =>
        "box is no allocation\nattempt, so the allocation report (`NOVA_ALLOC_REPORT`) shows fewer attempts, and fault injection\n(`NOVA_FAIL_ALLOC_AT`) cannot make a placed box fail. That is allowed only for an allocator whose contract says\nallocation \"may spuriously succeed\": `SystemHeap` in userland (memory-model.md §5, rule 1d). `FailingAllocator`,\n`KernelHeap` and user-defined allocators are never placed, so counting and failure injection stay exact for them.\nA test that needs every `SystemHeap` attempt counted builds with `--no-placement`.\n\n### 4.3 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default (M9e). `novac build --no-placement` and `novac run --no-placement` keep every box on the heap\n  (`CEmitOptions.Placement`, `InterpreterOptions.Placement`). `--placement` is still accepted.\n- `novac build --explain-allocations` prints every allocation site of the package with its decision and reason. The\n  libraries' sites are left out, as in §2.7's listings, since the libraries are part of every package.\n\n### 4.4 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** applies the same decisions. A placed box lives in its own allocation, which ends at the box's\n  drop, so a use after it is a fault. A site whose previous box is still alive when it runs again is a fault (\"a\n  stack-placed box's slot is reused while its previous box is alive\"), since natively the site has one slot.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program with placement (every configuration) and without it\n  (`clang -O2 no-placement`), and compare.\n- **`PlacementTests`**: the same output, panics and drop trace with and without placement, never more allocation\n  attempts, and native code that agrees with the interpreter on everything, the allocation report included.\n  `tests/corpus/placement/*.alloc` are reviewed snapshots of `--explain-allocations`.\n\n## 5. Closure-call specialisation (OD-9a; ADR 0019)\n\n### 5.1 What changes\nA call through a closure (`CallClosure`, type-system §36.2) whose closure is *known* becomes a direct call to the\nclosure's body function, which the C compiler may inline. A call that passes a known closure to a function's\nclosure-typed parameter goes to a **variant** of that function: a copy in which that parameter is bound to the\nclosure's body, so the calls through it inside are direct too.\n\n- **Known.** In a function, a local holds a known closure with body `K` when every assignment to it is one of:\n  a reference to field 0 of an environment local every assignment of which is an aggregate whose field 0 is\n  `K`'s address (a closure literal, or an `fn` value's adapter, made in this function); a copy, cast or reborrow of a\n  local holding a known closure with body `K`; or, in a variant, the parameter bound to `K`. Any other assignment\n  makes the local unknown. The analysis is per function, flow-insensitive, and iterated to a fixed point.\n- **Variants.** One per (function, bindings), where the bindings map each bound parameter index to a body. They are\n  made from a worklist in the order the program's functions and their calls are visited, so the result is\n  deterministic. A recursive call with the same known argument goes to the same variant. Generic functions are\n  specialised as their instances (§20.4); a function with no NIR (an `extern`) is never specialised.\n- **Not specialised:** a closure read from a field, an element or a `dyn fn` (§37), a `Box<dyn fn…>`, a closure whose\n  value depends on a branch, and a `Thread.both` argument (called through the runtime, c-backend §2).\n- A variant is the same NIR with call targets fixed: the same statements, checks and frame. Borrow and effect\n  checking run before the pass and are not repeated.\n\n### 5.2 What a program can observe\nNothing: output, exit status, panics and allocations are unchanged. Only the C function a call goes to changes, and the\ngenerated C has one more function per variant (`nvXS<n>_<name>`, c-backend §2.1).\n\n### 5.3 Switch and diagnostics\n- On by default. `novac build --no-closure-specialisat",
    219 =>
        "ion` and `novac run --no-closure-specialisation` keep every\n  closure call indirect (`CEmitOptions.ClosureSpecialisation`, `InterpreterOptions.ClosureSpecialisation`).\n- `novac build --explain-closure-calls` prints each call through a closure with its function and line, and whether it\n  became direct (and to which body) or why not, then each variant made.\n\n### 5.4 How it is checked\n- **The interpreter** runs the specialised NIR when the pass is on, so the corpus's comparison of interpreter and\n  native output covers it.\n- **The run corpus and the fuzzer** build every program with the pass (every configuration) and without it\n  (`clang -O2 no-closure-specialisation`), and compare.\n- **`ClosureSpecialisationTests`**: the C of `benchmarks/sort` has no indirect comparator call left, and the run corpus\n  gives the same output with and without the pass. `tests/corpus/closure-calls/*.calls` are reviewed snapshots of\n  `--explain-closure-calls`.\n",
    220 =>
        "# Ownership, Borrowing and Lifetimes — Formal Safety Design (v0)\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 proposal. This document is normative for Milestone 2, unless\nan item is marked *future*. **B1–B5 and E1–E3 are implemented as specified in `lifetimes.md` (M2b),**\nwhich records the v0 simplifications: immutable, once-initialised reference bindings; and B5\nimplemented as \"the receiver's loan starts after the arguments\". Related: `memory-model.md`, `language-spec.md`,\n`intermediate-representation.md`, `unsafe.md` (M8a).\n\n## 0. Scope of the guarantee\n\n**Memory safety** for Nova means: no execution of a well-typed program that\ncontains no `unsafe` code (and links only correctly-specified `unsafe` libraries)\nperforms any of:\n\n1. use-after-free; 2. double free; 3. dereference of a dangling reference;\n4. out-of-bounds access; 5. read of uninitialised memory; 6. type-confused\naccess; 7. invalid ownership transfer; 8. unsynchronised concurrent access where\nat least one access is a write (data race; only once threads exist, M-later).\n\n**Not** included in memory safety, but each with defined behaviour:\n\n| Condition | Defined behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| Integer overflow (`+ - *`, negation, `abs`) | **Panic** in all profiles (OD-6). `wrapping*`, `saturating*`, `checked*` methods available |\n| Division / remainder by zero, `MIN / -1` | Panic |\n| Shift by ≥ bit width | Panic (`wrappingShl` masks) |\n| Out-of-bounds index | Panic (`get(i)` returns `T?`) |\n| Narrowing numeric cast | `as` is **only** allowed where lossless; `truncate<T>(x)`, `checkedCast<T>(x)` explicit |\n| `T?` unwrap of `null` | Panic (`!` postfix operator) |\n| Allocation failure | **Value** (`Result<_, AllocError>`); panicking convenience APIs exist only in the userland profile |\n| Stack overflow | Profile-defined: userland relies on guard page → process termination; kernel profile uses static stack-depth checking (see `cosmoos/profiles.md`) |\n| Resource exhaustion, deadlock, logic errors, leaks via `forget` | Not prevented |\n| Panic | **Abort**, no unwinding (OD-5). Destructors do not run on panic. Userland: print + `abort()`; kernel: CosmoOS `panic()` |\n\nLeaks are memory-safe (as in Rust), but the safe standard library must not leak\nin ordinary use, and no Nova feature may use leaking as a substitute for\nlifetime soundness (master prompt §5.6).\n\n## 1. Vocabulary\n\n- **Place**: a storage location expression: a local, a field of a place\n  (`p.f`), an element of a place (`p[i]`), or the target of a reference\n  (`*r`, written implicitly in surface syntax).\n- **Copy type**: a type whose values may be duplicated bitwise: primitives,\n  `borrow T` (shared), raw pointers, and `struct`/`enum` types all of whose\n  fields are Copy and which declare no `drop` (and are not marked `@noncopy`).\n  Everything else is a **move type** (affine).\n- **Loan**: the creation of a reference to a place. Kinds: `shared`\n  (`borrow T`) and `mut` (`borrow mut T`).\n- **Region**: a set of points in a function's control-flow graph (NIR)\n  during which a loan must remain valid.\n- **Scoped type**: any type that contains a reference (`borrow T`,\n  `borrow mut T`, `Slice<T>`, `StringView`, arena handles, guard-protected\n  handles), transitively, including through generic arguments. A non-scoped type is\n  **free-standing**: it may outlive any particular stack frame.\n- **Owner**: the place that holds a value of a move type. Destroying the owner\n  destroys the value.\n\n## 2. Invariants\n\nThe checker establishes these properties on the NIR (control-flow IR) of every\nfunction. \"At point p\" means on entry to the NIR statement at p.\n\n### Ownership\n\n- **O1 — Unique owner.** Every live value of a move type is held by exactly one\n  initialised place.\n- **O2 — Move de-initialises.** Moving from a place makes it *uninitialised*.\n  A move out of a place is permitted only if the place is a local, or a field\n  path rooted at a local, whose type has no `drop` (partial move). It is\n  never permitted through a reference, an index, or ou",
    221 =>
        "t of a `drop`-implementing\n  struct.\n- **O3 — Definite initialisation.** A place may be read, borrowed, or have a\n  field assigned only if it is *definitely initialised* on every path reaching\n  that point. (Forward must-dataflow over NIR. The same analysis implements\n  use-after-move detection.)\n- **O4 — Exactly-once destruction.** At every point where a place goes out of\n  scope (`StorageDead`), and before every assignment that overwrites an\n  initialised place, the compiler inserts `drop(place)` if the place is\n  definitely initialised, or a guarded drop if it is *maybe* initialised (a drop\n  flag). A definitely uninitialised place gets no drop. Drop order: locals\n  in reverse declaration order; struct fields in declaration order; vector\n  elements in index order.\n- **O5 — Partial initialisation.** Struct literals initialise all fields\n  atomically from the program's point of view. Their C lowering constructs\n  in place, and no partially built struct is observable. A partially *moved*\n  struct (O2) drops only its remaining initialised fields.\n\n### Borrowing\n\n- **B1 — XOR aliasing.** At any point, for any place P, the live loans that\n  overlap P are either all shared, or exactly one is `mut`. Overlap is\n  path-prefix based: `a` overlaps `a.f`; `a.f` does not overlap `a.g`;\n  `a[i]` overlaps `a[j]` (no index disjointness in v0).\n- **B2 — Loan validity.** While a loan L of P is live (inside its region),\n  P must not be moved, dropped, go out of scope, or be assigned. If L is\n  shared, P must also not be mutated or mutably borrowed. If L is `mut`,\n  P must not be accessed at all except through L.\n- **B3 — Liveness regions (NLL).** A loan's region is the smallest set of\n  CFG points containing every point at which some reference derived from the\n  loan (transitively, through reborrows and copies of shared refs) may still be\n  used. It is computed by backward liveness over NIR, then by solving outlives\n  constraints. A shared reference copied into another variable propagates the loan.\n- **B4 — Reborrowing.** `borrow mut T` passed to a `borrow mut T` parameter\n  is implicitly reborrowed. The original is unusable until the reborrow dies\n  and becomes usable again afterwards (no move).\n- **B5 — Two-phase borrows.** For method calls `recv.m(args)` with a\n  `borrow mut this` receiver, the mutable loan of `recv` is *reserved* while\n  `args` are evaluated (permitting shared reads) and *activated* at the call.\n  Required for `v.push(v.length)`. This is the only two-phase site in v0.\n\n### Escape (lifetime) rules\n\n- **E1 — No escape beyond the owner.** A value of scoped type may flow only to:\n  (a) locals of the same function; (b) callee arguments; (c) the function's\n  return value, if the elision rule E2 links it to a parameter; (d) fields of\n  scoped *local* aggregates (tuples/anonymous temporaries), which then become\n  scoped.\n- **E2 — Return elision.** A function returning a scoped type must have exactly one\n  `borrow`/`borrow mut` parameter (including `this`), or must annotate the\n  source: `fn pick(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) -> borrow(a) T`. The returned\n  value's region is constrained to be within the named parameter's loan at the call\n  site. A function with no borrow parameters cannot return a scoped type, except\n  `static` data (string literals, `static let`), which have the `static` region.\n- **E3 — Scoped fields only with region parameters.** In v0, user `struct`/`enum`\n  declarations could not contain scoped types. Since M6x (OD-3, ADR 0006) a struct\n  or enum may hold scoped fields if it declares region parameters\n  (`struct Parser<region src> { input: StringView<src> }`), and generic instances\n  with scoped type arguments are scoped themselves (`type-system.md` §25.3–§25.4).\n  Region values and pool boxes still stay in locals.\n- **E4 — No scoped globals or heap.** Scoped values may not be stored in\n  `static`s, constants, `Box`, pools, or arrays. Since M6x, `Vector`,\n  `StableVector`, `SlotMap` and",
    222 =>
        " region memory may hold scoped values; the container\n  is then scoped and carries their loans (`type-system.md` §25.4, §25.7).\n\nE1–E4 deliberately trade expressiveness for an implementation of tractable size\n(see \"Why not full Rust lifetimes\" below).\n\n### Regions (arenas) and guards\n\n- **R1 — Allocation borrows the region.** `arena.alloc(...)` takes `borrow this`\n  and returns a scoped value carrying a *shared loan of the arena*. Therefore:\n  - the arena cannot be dropped, moved, reset, or mutably borrowed while any\n    allocation from it is live (B2);\n  - arena allocations cannot escape the arena's scope (E1–E4).\n- **R2 — Trivially-destructible contents (v0).** Arena allocation is\n  permitted only for types with no `drop` (transitively). Bulk release is\n  then semantically equivalent to individual destruction. Arena-with-drop-list is\n  *future* (OD-8).\n- **R3 — Pinned scoped bindings.** `using x = e;` declares a binding that cannot\n  be moved, reassigned, or returned; it is dropped exactly at the end of the\n  enclosing block. Arenas, lock guards and quiescence guards are normally bound\n  with `using`. (Types may require `using` by declaration: `@scopedOnly`.)\n- **R4 — Guard-protected borrows.** An API may return values scoped to a\n  guard (`table.lookup(borrow guard, key) -> Obj?` where `Obj` is scoped to\n  `guard`). By B2/E1 these cannot outlive the guard. This is the mechanism for\n  epoch/quiescence-protected kernel lookups (`cosmoos/kernel-memory-and-lifetimes.md`).\n\n### Heap\n\n- **H1 — Box ownership.** `Box<T, A>` is a move type that owns exactly one\n  allocation of `T` obtained from allocator `A`. Its drop runs `T`'s drop, then\n  `A.free`. `A` must be a free-standing *allocator type* (§3 of\n  `memory-model.md`). Allocations from non-free-standing allocators are scoped\n  values (R1).\n- **H2 — Collections.** `Vector<T, A>` owns its buffer. Element access returns\n  loans of the vector (`borrow this`/`borrow mut this`), so growth (which takes\n  `borrow mut this`) is rejected while element borrows are live (B1/B2).\n  Stable references into collections use `SlotMap<T>`/`Handle<T>` with\n  generation validation (library type, M6).\n- **H3 — No owning cycles.** Only `Box` and owning collections own, and their\n  ownership graph is a forest by construction (O1). Back-references and\n  graphs use indices, handles, or arenas.\n\n### Threads (M13 G4; ADR 0016, type-system.md §38)\n\n- **T1 — Only `Send` values cross.** A value reaches another thread only by being moved into a spawned closure, or\n  returned from one through `join`. Each is checked `Send` (N0334). A `Send` value holds no loan and no raw pointer,\n  so after the move the spawner reaches nothing the new thread owns.\n- **T2 — Sharing is through `Arc` of `Share` types.** The only safe way for two threads to reach the same memory is\n  an `Arc<T>` with `T: Share`, which lends only `borrow T`. A v1 type can be changed through a shared reference only\n  if it is one of the library's `@threadSafe` types: `Mutex<T>` (whose guard lends `borrow mut T` to one thread at a\n  time) and the atomics (whose operations are atomic). So two threads can write the same memory only through a\n  mutex or an atomic: there is no data race in safe code.\n- **T3 — The library's `@threadSafe` types** keep T2 in their `unsafe` code. `Mutex.lock` takes the platform lock\n  before lending. The guard's drop releases it, after the guard's loan has ended (B-rules). `Arc`'s count changes\n  only by atomic operations, and only the owner that drops it to zero destroys the value. A spawned thread is\n  joined before `main` returns, so no thread outlives the values `main` dropped (O2). `@threadSafe` is accepted only\n  in `lib/std`, so user code cannot claim it (N0290).\n- **T4 — Fork-join borrows end with the call** (`Thread.both`, §38.6; ADR 0018). A closure passed to `Thread.both`\n  may reach the caller's values through references, because it runs only during the call and the call returns only",
    223 =>
        "\n  after the thread running it has ended. Both closures' loans are live for the whole call, so the B-rules keep them\n  from reaching one value in conflicting ways. A value reached from both threads is reached shared by both and is\n  `Share`; one reached mutably is reached by one thread only and is `Send`. So T2's conclusion holds: there is no data\n  race in safe code.\n\n### Unsafe\n\n- **U1 — Capabilities.** Raw pointer dereference, pointer arithmetic, calls to\n  `extern` or `unsafe fn`, `transmute`, access to `static mut`, MMIO and inline\n  assembly are allowed only inside `unsafe { }` or an `unsafe fn`.\n- **U2 — Contract.** Every `unsafe` block must preserve O1–O5, B1–B5, E1–E4,\n  R1–R4, H1–H3 and T1–T3 *as observed by safe code*. Every `unsafe fn` and every safe\n  function containing `unsafe` must state its contract in a `/// Safety:` doc\n  comment. A lint enforces that the comment exists.\n- **U3 — Raw pointers are inert.** Raw pointers (`*T`, `*mut T`) are Copy,\n  carry no loan, and are ignored by the borrow checker. Converting a raw pointer\n  to a reference (`unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(p) }`; `type-system.md` §27.3) creates a reference with an\n  *unconstrained* region, which must be immediately bounded by a safe wrapper's\n  signature (as with Rust's unbounded lifetimes; this is the main hazard in\n  unsafe code).\n\n## 3. How the compiler rejects invalid programs\n\nPass order (see `architecture.md`):\n\n1. **Type check (HIR)**: kinds (Copy / move / scoped), E2 annotations,\n   E3/E4 structural checks, `unsafe` context (U1), `using` restrictions (R3),\n   arena element constraint (R2).\n2. **NIR construction**: explicit `StorageLive/StorageDead`, `Move`, `Copy`,\n   `Borrow(kind, place)`, two-phase reservation markers, and the `drop` sites\n   still unelaborated.\n3. **Definite-initialisation / move analysis** (O2–O3): forward dataflow, with a\n   per-place bitset of {init, uninit, maybe}.\n4. **Borrow check** (B1–B5, E1–E2, R1, R4):\n   a. compute liveness of every reference-typed local;\n   b. generate loans at `Borrow` statements and region variables for every\n      reference type occurrence;\n   c. generate outlives constraints from assignments, calls (signature\n      instantiation), and returns (E2);\n   d. solve regions as sets of points (NLL-style fixpoint);\n   e. for every access, report a conflict if any live loan in the solved region at\n      that point conflicts (B1/B2).\n5. **Drop elaboration** (O4): inserts static drops and drop flags. Runs after the\n   borrow check, so drops are checked as accesses in step 4 using the\n   *unelaborated* drop sites.\n\nDiagnostics name the loan's creation point, the conflicting access, and the\nlater use that keeps the loan alive, as three labelled spans. This is the\nsingle most important usability feature of the checker (see `diagnostics.md`).\n\nExample compile-fail cases (each becomes a test in `tests/corpus/compile-fail/`):\n\n```nova\nfn dangling() -> borrow i32 {         // error E2: no borrow parameter to derive from\n    let x = 5;\n    return borrow x;\n}\n\nfn useAfterMove(b: Box<Node>) -> void {\n    consume(b);\n    print(b.value);                    // error O3: `b` used after move at line 2\n}\n\nfn invalidate(v: borrow mut Vector<i32>) -> void {\n    let first = v[0];                  // Copy of i32: fine\n    let r = borrow v[0];               // shared loan of *v\n    v.push(1);                         // error B1: mutable loan while `r` live\n    print(r);                          //   ... `r` used here\n}\n\nfn arenaEscape() -> Slice<u8> {        // error E2: no borrow parameter\n    using arena = Arena.create(SystemHeap)!;\n    return arena.allocArray<u8>(16)!;\n}\n```\n\n## 4. Why not full Rust lifetimes in v0\n\nNamed lifetime parameters on aggregates, higher-ranked bounds and variance are\nwhere most of Rust's borrow-checker complexity and annotation burden live.\nE1–E4 keep the analysis entirely **intraprocedural** (signatures summarise\ncallees completely), with no lifetime parameters on ",
    224 =>
        "types. That is enough for:\nborrowing parameters, slices, string views, iterators over a borrowed collection\n(as a local), arena-scoped computations, and guard-scoped kernel lookups. It is\nnot enough for: long-lived parsers holding `StringView`s, structs of\nreferences, or zero-copy data structures. Those are OD-3, designed in M6f\n([`od3-design.md`](od3-design.md)) so that v0 programs remain valid.\n\n## 5. Behaviours requiring runtime checks\n\n| Check | Elidable when | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| Array/slice bounds | Index proven `< len` by range analysis (M9) | Lowering emits `nova_check_index` |\n| Integer overflow | Value ranges proven (M9) | `nova_add_i32` etc. |\n| Division by zero / `MIN/-1` | Divisor is a non-zero, non-minus-one constant | — |\n| Shift width | Constant shift amount | — |\n| Optional unwrap `!` | Flow-typing after `!= null` | — |\n| Drop flags | Static init state known (most cases) | Drop elaboration |\n| Allocation failure | Never elided (it is a value) | — |\n| Handle generation check (`SlotMap`) | Never in v0 | Built-in (M6e; library in M8) |\n| Kernel: stack depth | Static call-graph bound (kernel profile) | Link-time report |\n\n## 6. Testing obligations (M2 acceptance)\n\n- Every invariant above has at least two compile-fail tests and one compile-pass\n  test that is \"close\" to failing (for example NLL cases where a loan ends\n  before the conflicting access).\n- Mutation suite: for each compile-pass test, automated mutants (swap statement\n  order, remove a scope, duplicate a move) whose expected verdict is computed\n  independently, by hand-labelling or a reference interpreter, not by the\n  checker itself.\n- Generated C for compile-pass tests is run under ASan + UBSan in CI\n  (supporting evidence only).\n",
    225 =>
        "# Releasing novac\n\nUsers install `novac` without cloning the repository: from a prebuilt binary attached to a GitHub release, usually\nthrough the install script. This document is how a release is made, and what it contains.\n\n## What a release contains\n\nFor each platform, `novac` compiled with NativeAOT: one native executable of about 9 MB that needs no .NET (and\nstarts in milliseconds), with `COPYING` and `COPYING.RUNTIME`:\n\n| Asset | Platform |\n|---|---|\n| `novac-linux-x64.tar.gz`, `novac-linux-arm64.tar.gz` | Linux with glibc 2.35 or newer (built on Ubuntu 22.04) |\n| `novac-osx-arm64.tar.gz`, `novac-osx-x64.tar.gz` | macOS, signed with the project's Developer ID and notarized |\n| `novac-win-x64.zip`, `novac-win-arm64.zip` | Windows |\n| `SHA256SUMS` | the SHA-256 of every archive |\n\nThe names carry no version, so `https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/releases/latest/download/<asset>` is always the latest\nrelease; `releases/download/v0.1.0/<asset>` is a given one. `novac --version` names the version.\n\n`novac build` still needs a C compiler on the user's machine (clang, gcc or MSVC); `check`, `run --interp`, `fmt`,\n`fix`, `lsp` and `mcp` do not.\n\n## Installing\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsSL https://vkuttyp.github.io/Nova/install.sh | sh          # macOS and Linux\nirm https://vkuttyp.github.io/Nova/install.ps1 | iex                # Windows (PowerShell)\n```\n\nThe scripts live in `site/public/` and are published with the documentation site. They pick the archive for the\nmachine, check it against `SHA256SUMS`, and put `novac` in `~/.nova/bin` (`%USERPROFILE%\\.nova\\bin`). The shell\nscript then says how to add that to `PATH`; the PowerShell one adds it to the user's `PATH`. `NOVA_VERSION=v0.1.0` installs a given release; `NOVA_INSTALL=<dir>` another directory.\n`InstallScriptTests` runs `install.sh` against a local copy of a release.\n\n## Making a release\n\n1. On `main`, with CI green: set `NovaVersion.Text` (`src/Nova.Compiler/NovaVersion.cs`) to the version, `0.1.0`.\n   It is a constant, not build metadata, because generated C names it and output must be reproducible: re-bless the\n   snapshots that contain it (`NOVA_BLESS=1 dotnet test Nova.slnx`) and commit `release: 0.1.0`.\n2. Tag that commit `v0.1.0` and push the tag. `.github/workflows/release.yml` checks the tag against\n   `NovaVersion.Text`, builds each binary on its own platform (NativeAOT does not cross-compile between operating\n   systems: Ubuntu 22.04 x64 and ARM, Windows x64 and ARM, macOS for both Mac architectures), runs each one it can\n   (`--version`, a program in the interpreter, the MCP server), signs and notarizes the macOS ones, and publishes the\n   release with generated notes. The Linux x64 job first runs `packaging/aot-check.py` (below).\n3. Set `NovaVersion.Text` to the next development version (`0.2.0-dev`), re-bless, and commit.\n\n## Point releases\n\nA fix that users of a release need before the next one, a soundness fix above all, goes out as a point release from a\nmaintenance branch, not from `main`, whose new language features belong in the next minor version. The branch\n`release-0.1` starts at `v0.1.1`. The fix is backported to it, with only what that version's code needs. Then the\nversion is set, the snapshots are re-blessed, the full suite runs, and the commit `release: 0.1.2` is tagged `v0.1.2`\nas in step 2. `main` keeps its development version. 0.1.2 (2026-10-29) is the first: the shared-path soundness fix\n(`safety-audit.md` §4), whose program 0.1.1 accepts and runs into a use after free. 0.3.1 (2026-10-29), from `release-0.3`, is the\nsecond: lib/kernel's `ObjectCache` asked CosmoOS for an alignment below its minimum, found by CosmoOS's `nova-kcore`\nself-test with 0.3.0. 0.4.1 (2026-10-30), from `release-0.4`, is the third: 0.4.0's\nlib/std declared its host helpers at top level, so a program could not use names such as `pathIn` (N0201), found by\nthe CeeSafe port's re-pin.\n\n## Moving CosmoOS to a new release\n\nCosmoOS's `make nova` accepts only its pinned novac (`scripts/no",
    226 =>
        "va-gen.py`, CosmoOS `docs/build/nova.md`). A pin bump\nis its own CosmoOS PR, opened after the release is published and merged only on the owner's word. Its checklist\n(M14 K7):\n\n1. Nova, at the release's commit: CI green (the full suite runs the run corpus compiled and in the interpreter), the\n   latest nightly fuzz campaign (`fuzz.yml`) green with no finding open, and the release workflow's `aot-check`\n   passed.\n2. CosmoOS, on a branch: `NOVAC_VERSION`, the install lines in `docs/build/nova.md` and `design.md`, and CI's\n   `NOVAC_SHA256`, the release's `novac-linux-x64.tar.gz` line in `SHA256SUMS`.\n3. `NOVAC=~/.nova/bin/novac gmake nova` with the release installed, and every difference in the generated C besides\n   its version line explained in the PR.\n4. `gmake ARCH=aarch64`, `gmake ARCH=x86_64` and `gmake test` on both (a debug boot, with lockdep), then\n   `make nova-check`.\n5. Once CosmoOS uses `lib/kernel`: `integrations/cosmoos/k3.py` against the branch.\n\nThe first bump under it was 0.1.2 → 0.2.0 (CosmoOS #488).\n\n## NativeAOT\n\nNativeAOT removes what it cannot see used. novac walks its syntax and typed trees through their properties\n(reflection: `SemanticIndex`, and two scans in the checker), so its NativeAOT build roots `Nova.Compiler` for the\ntrimmer (`Nova.Cli.csproj`), and those sites carry a suppression that says so. `packaging/aot-check.py` shows the\nresult is the same program: it runs the NativeAOT novac and the JIT build side by side over the whole corpus and\ncompares `check --diagnostics-format=json`, `run --interp`, `build --emit c` and `fmt --stdout`, plus `nova_symbol` at\nevery identifier of every run-pass program and the language server's symbols and hovers. On 2026-10-26 (macOS arm64):\n802 of 802 comparisons identical, 20,458 `nova_symbol` positions. The release fails if any differs.\n\n```sh\ndotnet publish src/Nova.Cli -c Release -r osx-arm64 -p:PublishAot=true -o out/aot\npython3 packaging/aot-check.py --aot out/aot/novac\n```\n\n## macOS signing\n\nThe macOS job signs `novac` with the hardened runtime and a secure timestamp (NativeAOT code needs no JIT\nentitlement), and submits it to Apple's notary service; a release with an unsigned or rejected macOS binary fails. A bare executable cannot have its ticket stapled, so Gatekeeper checks it online the first time.\nThe job needs these repository secrets, which only the owner sets:\n\n| Secret | What |\n|---|---|\n| `APPLE_CERTIFICATE_P12` | the Developer ID Application certificate and its private key, exported from Keychain Access as `.p12`, base64 |\n| `APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD` | the password chosen for that export |\n| `ASC_P8` | an App Store Connect API key (role Developer or higher), the downloaded `AuthKey_<id>.p8`, base64 |\n| `ASC_KEY_ID` | that key's ID |\n| `ASC_ISSUER` | the issuer ID shown above the keys list in App Store Connect |\n\nThe `ASC_*` names are the ones the owner's App Store tooling uses (`ASC_KEY_ID`, `ASC_ISSUER`, `ASC_P8`), so an\nexisting key can serve: its `.p8` is usually in `~/.appstoreconnect/private_keys/`.\n\n```sh\nbase64 -i DeveloperID.p12 | gh secret set APPLE_CERTIFICATE_P12\ngh secret set APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD\nbase64 -i ~/.appstoreconnect/private_keys/AuthKey_<id>.p8 | gh secret set ASC_P8\ngh secret set ASC_KEY_ID\ngh secret set ASC_ISSUER\n```\n\nTo check the secrets without releasing, run the workflow by hand: `gh workflow run release.yml`. It builds, checks,\nsigns and notarizes as a release does, skips the tag check, and publishes nothing.\n\nWindows binaries are not signed: SmartScreen may warn on a browser download until there is a code-signing certificate.\n\n## Later\n\nHomebrew, Scoop and winget packages can point at the same assets. Replacing the reflection-based walkers with\nexplicit ones would let the trimmer drop more of `Nova.Compiler` and remove the suppressions.\n",
