        "# Technical Risk Register\n\nStatus: Milestone 0 / C0 (master prompt §23.J and CosmoOS deliverable 9). Ranked by\n(likelihood × impact on safety). Reviewed at the end of every milestone.\n\n| ID | Risk | L | I | Mitigation | Detection | Owner milestone |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **R-1** | **Borrow-checker unsoundness.** NLL region solving, two-phase borrows, reborrows and partial moves are subtle. One hole silently breaks the safety guarantee (Goose's own lifetime checker had about half of recent commits fixing holes: `research/goose.md` §9.4) | High | Critical | Keep v0 intraprocedural and small (E1–E4). Specify every rule in `ownership.md` before implementing it. Use a separate init check and borrow check. The checker runs once, before optimisation; each optimisation keeps to written preconditions (R-8) | Compile-fail corpus (≥ 2 tests per rule; M6d adds a vector-invalidation suite, and closes two growable-buffer hazards in lowering: `for` over a vector holds a view loan, and an element write after a call re-checks its index; M6e fixed a liveness hole that made the checker over-strict, not unsound: `Unpack` targets were not definitions; M6x adds writes into aggregates and through references, signature flows and a drop check, each with compile-fail tests and a model-based property test), mutation-generated negatives with independently computed verdicts, ASan/UBSan on all run-pass output, the random program generator (M7d; its first runs found a false N0272: a Copy value read through a `borrow mut` parameter was treated as carrying the parameter's loans), the NIR reference interpreter (M7c) as a dynamic oracle for liveness, bounds and initialisation on every run program and sweep point; borrow tracking (Stacked-Borrows-style) is not done. M8a adds `unsafe`: raw pointers carry no loans (U3), and references made from them are unconstrained, so a wrong `Safety:` argument is unsound without any diagnostic. Containment: only the U1 operations need `unsafe` (N0280, compile-fail corpus), every unsafe function states its contract (N0283), safe code is checked exactly as before, and the interpreter faults on invalid accesses through pointers (`UnsafeTests`). An independent review of M13's closures and threads (2026-10-29) found two holes, both closed with compile-fail tests (`check-fail/shared-path-mutation.nova`): a `borrow mut` or `SliceMut` stored where a shared reference leads gave mutable access (in 0.1.1; a use after free under ASan, and with `Thread.both` a data race under TSan), and so did the field reborrow added the day before. A shared dereference now makes the rest of the path immutable (type-system §13.2) | M2, M7, M8 |\n| **R-2** | **Unsafe/FFI contract errors.** C libraries and kernel APIs do not follow Nova ownership. Unbounded regions from raw→ref conversions (U3). Wrong effect declarations in binding files | High | Critical | All `extern` calls are `unsafe`. Safe wrappers carry `/// Safety:` contracts. Binding files are reviewed and default to the most conservative effects. Raw→ref conversion is only possible through APIs that bound the region by signature | Lint for missing contracts. Wrapper unit tests under ASan. Kernel: lockdep and `might_sleep` stay on in debug. **C3:** the I/O runtime (`nova_rt_io.c`), the first binding with OS semantics, runs against CosmoOS's own libc in the guest (`c3.py`), including its non-POSIX `sockaddr`, spawn API and wait status; known gap: CosmoOS has no `MSG_NOSIGNAL`/`setsockopt`, so a socket write to a closed peer may raise `SIGPIPE` there. **M8a:** the `Safety:` lint is N0283, and the interpreter faults on invalid accesses through pointers (`UnsafeTests`). **M8b:** C prototypes use C's own types, `@repr` enum values from C are checked, and `FfiTests` runs C→Nova and Nova→C under ASan+UBSan. **M8c:** a freestanding object's undefined symbols are an allowlist, so nothing hosted leaks into a kernel build (`FreestandingTests`). **C4/C5:** Nova objects meet the module l",
