system §33.1): Nova computes no layout for them at all, and the interpreter faults instead of guessing one. Nova never uses C `char` | Static asserts fail the C build. Cross-compile tests for both CosmoOS triples in M1 | M1 |\n| **R-5** | **Kernel context-effect unsoundness.** Atomic/sleepable is a runtime property in CosmoOS. Indirect calls, callbacks and wrong binding annotations can let sleeping code run under a spinlock or quiesce guard | Medium | Critical (deadlock, RCU violation) | Conservative defaults (`sleep+alloc` for externs without `@effects`, for C function-pointer calls, and for calls through Nova function and closure types without an effect list; M11 G8b′, type-system §36.3). Effects inferred over the package, drops included, and checked in `@context` functions and wherever an `@context(atomic)` value (a spin guard) may be live (C7a, type-system §32). CosmoOS's own `might_sleep()` in every sleeping primitive stays the runtime backstop (Nova emits none: its debug half is not exported to modules). Never claim effects prove kernel concurrency properties | Kernel debug builds with lockdep. Negative effect tests (`check-fail/effects.nova`, 13 cases, C7a; `c7/negative/`, sleeping under CosmoOS's own spin guards, C7c). QEMU stress tests: **C7e** ran Nova locks, kernel objects and grace periods on 4 CPUs in CosmoOS's debug build, lockdep-clean, on both arches; lockdep still catches what Nova does not check (lock order) | C7 |\n| **R-6** | **Kernel object lifetime and quiescence misuse.** `kobject_put` with a blocking release under a spinlock (unchecked in CosmoOS today). Guard-scoped borrows escaping. Callbacks outliving module unload. No `call_quiesce` barrier | Medium | Critical | `KRef` drop has the `sleep` effect. Guard-scoped borrows (R4). Registrations are owned values torn down by generated `shutdown()`. `deferDrop` forbidden in modules until KQ-3 | Unload stress tests in QEMU (C8). Zombie-module detection. Lockdep  **C7d:** `KRef<T>` drop is `kobject_p",
