order or which code runs, allocator observability or bounds behaviour | Medium | High | Placement rules in `memory-model.md` §5 (allocator must be placement-transparent; KernelHeap never is). Each optimisation has written preconditions. Check elimination and placement change no NIR, only what the backend emits for it; closure-call specialisation does change it, and is not re-checked: it fixes only a call's target, to the body the analysis proves the closure has (optimisations.md §5.1), so the checked program's statements, loans and effects are unchanged, and the interpreter runs the specialised NIR | Optimisation on/off equivalence tests, fuzz-differential on BCE (as Goose does), `--explain-allocations` snapshots. M5 (done): placement off/on gives equal outputs and identical drop traces on every run program, native and interpreter agree with placement, and a fuzz configuration builds with placement. ADR 0019: a `clang -O2 no-closure-specialisation` configuration of the run corpus and the fuzzer, `ClosureSpecialisationTests` | M5, M9 |\n| **R-9** | **Stack exhaustion.** Recursive drop of deep `Box` trees and lists. 16 KiB kernel stacks shared with IRQs. Large by-value structs | Medium | High (kernel: corruption/panic; user: crash) | Per-value size limits. Kernel frame (2 KiB) and chain checks. Recursion requires `@unboundedStack` in the kernel. Library `drop` for recursive types uses iterative destruction (explicit worklist). **Status (M3c):** compiler drop glue iterates over a box's *tail* (type-system.md §18.5), so lists of any length are destroyed in constant stack. `deep-structures.nova` (3 × 200,000 nodes) crashes with recursive glue at `-O0` and passes with the loop. Trees still recurse along non-tail children: balanced trees are fine, left-degenerate ones are not (open for the kernel profile, C4) | `-fstack-usage` reports. Deep-structure run-pass tests. **The compiler itself (2026-10-30, [#3](https://github.com/vkuttyp/Nova/issues/3)):** 100,000 nested parentheses ove",
