        "rflow novac's stack (SIGSEGV), with no limit or diagnostic. Fixed in M15 S3 (2026-10-30): a 1,000-level limit (N0110, grammar.md §2.2), and the passes run on the compiler's own 256 MiB stack (`CompilerThread`) | M3, C4, M15 |\n| **R-10** | **Expressiveness gap drives `unsafe` use.** v0 forbids references in structs (E3), so users write `unsafe`, which erodes the guarantee | High | Medium | Ship safe alternatives early: `Span` indices, `SlotMap` (M6e: handles can be stored in structs and collections), arenas. OD-3 is implemented (M6x, `type-system.md` §25): structs, enums and collections may hold references with region parameters, so zero-copy parsers and arena graphs need no `unsafe`. Track `unsafe` density per package. M8a: `unsafe` exists, and every use needs a `Safety:` contract (N0283), which keeps it visible in review. M11 closed the gaps that M9's suite and the README's tour found in safe code (`milestone-11.md`), among them a cursor walking a list without `unsafe` (G3), wrapping arithmetic for hashes (G4b), floats (G7) and closures that borrow what they capture (G8), so callbacks need no C function pointers. Closures cannot outlive their block (owning closures are OD-9b). M13 added owning closures (G3), `dyn` (G2) and `splitAtMut` (G5), which remove more reasons to reach for `unsafe` | `unsafe` audit report in `novac` | M6, M8, M11 |\n| **R-11** | **Moved or uninitialised C state.** Kernel C state can point into itself once initialised (an empty list head; a mutex's wait queue), so a bitwise move corrupts it; C state used before its init runs misbehaves (a zeroed spinlock's owner is CPU 0) | Medium | Critical | C-laid-out values are made only in place, by a literal given to `Box.create`/`createIn`, never moved (every move is N0294), and `@placed` methods run the C initialisers at the final address before any code can see the value (C7b, type-system §33) | `check-fail/c-state-moves.nova`; `ffi/embedded.nova` checks in C that self-pointing list heads survive in their boxes | C7 |\n| **R-12** | **Fetched dependencies** (M10 T6, ADR 0013). A git dependency is code the build compiles and trusts; a changed tag or branch, a rewritten repository, or a tampered cache could change what is built without the package changing | Medium | High | A dependency is pinned to a commit in `nova.lock`, and builds use the lock, never the manifest's tag or branch (`tooling.md` §4.3). Each checkout's manifest and sources are hashed into the lock and verified on every build (N0325). No build scripts: nothing runs but `git` and the C compiler. `--offline` builds never touch the network. Review of what a dependency contains stays the user's | `PackageManagerTests` (pinning, a moved branch, an edited checkout, offline) | M10 |\n| **R-13** | **Data races through threads** (M13 G4, ADR 0016). Safe code's race freedom rests on `Send`/`Share` inference and on lib/std's `@threadSafe` types keeping T1–T3 in their `unsafe` code; a wrong inference rule or a library bug would let two threads write the same memory. The interpreter runs threads one at a time, so it cannot find races | Low | Critical | `Send` is structural and conservative (references, raw pointers, C types, user allocators and closures are not); `@threadSafe` is accepted only in library files (N0290); the library's `unsafe` states its contract (N0283); every thread is joined before `main` returns; `Thread.both` (ADR 0018) lets two threads borrow only for the call, which returns after the join (ownership T4), and extends `Send`/`Share` to references there only (`TypeTable.IsForkSafe`); atomic orderings (ADR 0021) are opt-in `…With` methods whose every access is still atomic, so a weak ordering is a logic error, not a race, and is found by neither the interpreter nor TSan | `check-fail/threads.nova` (each kind that is not `Send`); `check-fail/thread-both.nova` and `thread-both-borrows.nova` (each refused capture, each conflict); `check-fail/shared-path-mutation.nova` (two closures writing t",
