res before safety foundations | Milestone gates (`roadmap.md`). The master prompt's rule 15 is enforced at review |\n| P-6 | Docs/code drift inside CosmoOS (≈18 disagreements found in the audits) | Nova designs cite *code*, not docs. Re-audit the touched interfaces at the start of each C-milestone |\n| P-7 | Checked Nova is too slow for kernel hot paths, so components stay in C or move to `unsafe` | Measured, not assumed: C6a's LZ4 port runs at 0.48–0.78 of C for compression and 0.02–1.12 for decompression (`benchmarks/lz4/README.md`), mostly in byte loops that C does with `memcpy`. C6b's fbvalid port costs the same as C with clang (0.98–1.03) but 1.76× with gcc 12, cause not established (`benchmarks/fbvalid/README.md`). C9a's GPT port, a parser of untrusted disk input, runs at 0.98–1.03 of C's time under clang and is faster under gcc (`benchmarks/gpt/README.md`). M9a's suite (`milestone-9.md`, 2026-10-25) confirms it across fifteen workloads: Nova is 0.86–1.63× C on 12 of the 15 with clang, 13–17× on block copies, 1.9–4.4× on vector and string growth, and further behind under gcc. M9b's checked bulk copy (`copyFrom`, 2026-10-25) brought the block copy to 1.06× C under clang (1.67× under gcc) and LZ4's literal decompression to 0.89–0.97 of C, keeping every check. M9c's bounds-check elimination removes the index checks that loop and guard tests prove (14 of LZ4's 21), keeping every check that could fail; it gains under gcc (LZ4 decompression 1.32×) and nothing under clang, which already removed them. M9d removes the overflow checks that cannot fail (35 of LZ4's 50) and found fbvalid's gcc gap (zero-initialised locals): LZ4 `records` decompression is now 0.77 of C under clang (3.44× faster), and fbvalid is 1.06× C under gcc. The suite is at 0.81–2.37× C under clang and 1.18–3.48× under gcc. M9e makes placement the default and extends it to boxes that die within a loop iteration (`heap-alloc` 0.92 of C under clang). M9e′ makes optional boxes one pointer a",
