    233 =>
        "# Roadmap — Language Milestones (M) and CosmoOS Integration Milestones (C)\n\nStatus: written at the end of M0/C0 (2026-10-22), kept current since (last 2026-10-28). Rule: no milestone is complete because a\ndemo runs. Each one requires its acceptance criteria, positive and negative tests,\nupdated docs, and an updated risk register (master prompt §22).\n\n## Ordering\n\n```text\nM0/C0 ─► M1 ─► C1 ─► M2 ─► M3 ─► C2 ─► M4 ─► M6 ─► C3\n                                   │            └─► M7 ─► M8 ─► C4 ─► C5 ─► C6 ─► C7 ─► C8 ─► C9 ─► A5\n                                   └─► M5 (after M4)      M9 (after M7 + correctness baseline) ─► M11 ─► M10 ─► M12 ─► M13\n```\n\nM11 came before M10 at the owner's order (2026-10-26). A5 is CosmoOS's own roadmap unit, written in Nova inside\nCosmoOS once C9b had put Nova sources in its tree.\n\n## Milestones and acceptance criteria\n\n| # | Scope | Acceptance (measurable) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **M0/C0** ✅ 2026-10-22 | Research, architecture, specs, audits | This document set. All decisions accepted (ADR 0002) |\n| **M1** ✅ 2026-10-22 Minimal compiler | Lexer, parser, AST, primitives, locals, functions, arithmetic, conditions, loops, semantic checks, HIR, NIR, LIR, C emission, executable | See `milestone-1.md`: ≥ 150 corpus tests, generated C clean under strict warnings, clang + gcc host runs agree, both CosmoOS triples compile, determinism check |\n| **C1** ✅ 2026-10-22 Native Hello World | M1 output linked against CosmoOS libc and run in QEMU (S0, out of tree) | `NOVATEST: PASS` printed by the Nova program inside CosmoOS on **x86-64 and AArch64**. `.note.cosmo` present. `llvm-nm -u` shows only libc symbols. Exact flags recorded. No CosmoOS file modified (`git status` clean) |\n| **M2** ✅ 2026-10-22 Ownership & borrowing ([milestone-2.md](milestone-2.md)) | Structs, enums, references, moves, init check, NLL borrow check, drop elaboration, `drop`, `using`, `defer`, slices of arrays | Each of O1–O5, B1–B5, E1–E4 has ≥ 2 compile-fail and ≥ 1 near-miss compile-pass test (≥ 120 total). Drop-order run tests. Mutation suite. ASan/UBSan clean |\n| **M3** ✅ 2026-10-22 Safe heap (ADR 0003: built-in `Result`/`Box`/allocators) | `Allocator`, `SystemHeap`, `Box`, `AllocError`, `Result`/`?`, `FailingAllocator`, recursive types, iterative drops | Fault-injection sweep: for every test that allocates *n* times, run with failure at each *k ≤ n*; no leaks/double frees under ASan. Ex4, Ex5, Ex11, Ex14 pass |\n| **C2** ✅ 2026-10-22 Safe allocation in CosmoOS ([report](cosmoos/c2-report.md)) | M2+M3 features in Profile A | Allocation, destruction and fault-injection tests pass inside QEMU on both arches |\n| **M4** ✅ 2026-10-22 Regions (ADR 0004) | `Arena`, `Scratch`, `FixedBufferAllocator`, `Pool` | Compile-fail: every arena-escape form (return, store in Box, outlive via `reset`/drop). Run tests for bulk release. R2 enforced |\n| **M5** ✅ 2026-10-23 Escape/placement analysis ([milestone-5.md](milestone-5.md)) | CFG analysis, classification, `--explain-allocations`, Box→stack demotion | Optimised vs unoptimised outputs equal on the whole run-pass corpus. Drop timing identical (instrumented). Report snapshots. No program accepted only because of placement |\n| **M6** ✅ 2026-10-23 Generics & collections (ADR 0005) | Monomorphisation, interfaces, `Vector`, `String`, `StringView`, `StableVector`, `SlotMap`, iterators. OD-3 design | Invalidation compile-fail tests. Property tests of collections against a model. Ex9, Ex10, Ex12, Ex13 |\n| **C3** ✅ 2026-10-23 Collections & syscalls ([report](cosmoos/c3-report.md)) | Files, sockets, processes in Profile A; a useful utility (candidate: a `wc`/`sha256sum`-class tool or a hexdump) | The utility runs in QEMU on both arches, with output compared to the C/BusyBox equivalent on a test corpus |\n| **M7** ✅ 2026-10-24 Production C backend ([plan](milestone-7.md)) | Source maps, s",
    234 =>
        "tructured C, Csmith-style fuzzer, NIR reference interpreter, MSVC best-effort | 24 h fuzz campaign with no miscompile across clang/gcc × O0/O2. Interpreter agrees with native output |\n| **M8** ✅ 2026-10-24 Systems/freestanding ([plan](milestone-8.md)) | core library split, FFI, raw pointers, MMIO types, custom entry points, `freestanding` profile | Freestanding object with no libc: verified by `llvm-nm -u` allowlist |\n| **C4** ✅ 2026-10-24 Freestanding in CosmoOS ([report](cosmoos/c4-report.md)) | Nova kernel-profile object built with kernel flags | Symbols, relocations and sections checked against the module loader allowlist. Frame sizes ≤ 2 KiB. No FP registers (disassembly check) |\n| **C5** ✅ 2026-10-24 Kernel memory adapter ([report](cosmoos/c5-report.md)) | `KernelHeap`, `ObjectCache<T>` bindings; isolated test component | In-kernel selftest: allocation, exhaustion (fault-injected), ownership transfer, cleanup. No allocator modifications |\n| **C6** ✅ 2026-10-24 Controlled kernel component ([report](cosmoos/c6-report.md): LZ4, fbvalid) | One small, non-critical component reimplemented beside its C original (candidate chosen with the owner) | Same test suite passes for both. Performance compared (measured, reproducible) |\n| **C7** ✅ 2026-10-25 Kernel concurrency ([report](cosmoos/c7-report.md)) | Effects, `SpinLock<T>`/`Mutex<T>` guards, `KRef`, `QuiesceGuard`, `PerCpu`, deferred reclamation | Negative effect tests. Lockdep clean under QEMU stress. Real concurrency rules exercised |\n| **C8** ✅ 2026-10-28 Experimental module (C8a 2026-10-25; C8b against CosmoOS itself once its hook was merged, #457; [report](cosmoos/c8-report.md)) | Nova `.ko` with generated `shutdown()` | Load/unload loops (≥ 1000) with no zombie modules, leaks or lockdep reports, on both arches. Signing via `modsign.py` |\n| **M9** ✅ 2026-10-25 Performance ([report](milestone-9.md)) | Optimisations with preconditions; benchmark suite vs C/C++/Rust/Zig/Goose | Each optimisation: equivalence tests + measured gain. Published methodology. **No claims without data** |\n| **M11** ✅ 2026-10-26 Language gaps ([plan and report](milestone-11.md)) | The gaps M9's suite and the README's tour ran into: nested patterns, `for` over non-`Copy` elements, borrow-checker precision, small conveniences, `if`/`match` expressions, references to optionals; floats and closures after an ADR each | Each gap: spec, positive and negative tests, the probe that showed it now passing |\n| **M10** ✅ 2026-10-26 Tooling ([plan and report](milestone-10.md)) | Formatter, docs generator, language server and VS Code extension, debugging integration; then modules and packages (path and git dependencies) after an ADR | Each tool: spec, tests, and the repository's own code kept passing it (`novac fmt --check`) |\n| **C9** Gradual adoption (C9a, C9b ✅ 2026-10-26: GPT in Nova, merged into CosmoOS; [report](cosmoos/c9-report.md)) | Evaluate further components | Per-component ADR |\n| **A5** ✅ 2026-10-28 CosmoOS features in Nova: CPU time and nice values (#461, #476, #477 merged; [report](cosmoos/a5-report.md)) | CosmoOS roadmap 1.2 A5's kernel lines (LX-13, LX-14) written in Nova: CPU-time timers, then nice values, then a weighted share | Tests written first; CosmoOS's matrix on both arches; merged by the owner |\n| **M13** ✅ 2026-10-28 The documented gaps ([plan and report](milestone-13.md)) | Vector and string growth; dynamic dispatch (OD-10) and owning closures (OD-9b); userland threads with a data-race guarantee; checker generality; a package registry and version solver | Each gap: the probe that showed it now passing, spec, positive and negative tests; G2, G4 and G6 after an ADR each; performance on the suite (`benchmarks/`) |\n| **M14** proposed 2026-10-29 Nova for a kernel written in Nova ([plan](milestone-14.md)) | What CosmoOS's roadmap 2.0 (proposed) needs: global and per-CPU state (K1), typed calls across compilation units (K2), a kernel library (K3), novac for a self",
    235 =>
        "-hosted build (K4), hot-path performance (K5), an outside audit (K6), the compiler as trusted base (K7) | Each item's acceptance in `milestone-14.md`; K1 and K2a ship in Nova 0.2.0 |\n| **M15** implemented 2026-10-30 (S1–S7) Prerequisites for a compiler written in Nova ([plan](milestone-15.md)) | For OD-33's self-hosting port (CeeSafe, outside this repository):<br>• fixes for #1–#5 (a missed drop, a compiler crash, a nesting crash, two false rejections);<br>• bytes to text (S6);<br>• the host services `novac build` uses (S7) | Each item's acceptance in `milestone-15.md`; the fixes in novac 0.3.2 |\n| **M12** ✅ 2026-10-26 Tooling for AI agents ([plan and report](milestone-12.md)) | JSON diagnostics with fixes, `novac fix`/`explain`; `novac mcp`; `llms.txt` and the agent guide | Each fix resolves its diagnostic on the corpus; an MCP client test covers every tool and the sandbox; the guide's examples compile |\n\n## Next highest-priority task\n\nEvery milestone above is complete (2026-10-28), C8 last: its 1,000-cycle test passed against CosmoOS itself on both\narches once CosmoOS had merged the hook (`cosmoos/c8-report.md`). C9 stays open by design: each further component is\nits own decision with the owner. Next, at the owner's choice:\n\n- **M14, if CosmoOS adopts its roadmap 2.0** ([plan](milestone-14.md), proposed 2026-10-29): what a kernel written in\n  Nova needs from Nova. First K4's decision and K2a (for the roadmap's SA1), then K1 and K3 (for SA2 and SA3).\n\n- **M15** ([plan](milestone-15.md), in progress): what a compiler written in Nova (OD-33) needs. S1–S6 are done on\n  `main` (2026-10-30):\n  - the fixes for #1, #2, #4 and #5;\n  - the nesting limit, with the compiler's own 256 MiB stack and a check-elimination fact budget;\n  - `StringView.fromUtf8`.\n\n  S7 is done too ([ADR 0028](decisions/0028-m15-host-services.md), accepted 2026-10-30): `Process.capture`,\n  `File.kind`, `Environment` and `Directory`. Next: a release (0.3.2 for the fixes, 0.4.0 for the rest), and the\n  emitter gap milestone-15.md notes under S7.\n\n- **CosmoOS roadmap 1.2.** A5's kernel lines in Nova are done (#461, #476, #477; `cosmoos/a5-report.md`); the rest of\n  A5 (OpenSSH and git with their test suites) and A2 are CosmoOS ports, not Nova work. Further components in Nova\n  follow C9's rule: a per-component decision with the owner.\n- **The open decisions M13 left:** OD-25 (ADR 0018), OD-9a's performance need (ADR 0019), OD-24 (ADR 0021) and OD-9c\n  (ADR 0020) are done; OD-9a's language question (closure types, `Fn` bounds) and OD-26 were re-examined on 2026-10-29\n  and stay deferred, as do OD-10a and OD-10b (`open-decisions.md`).\n- **What is left of the slow workloads:** `vector-growth` is at parity with C since M13's G1, and `string-building`\n  is 1.26× (clang) and 1.40× (gcc) C since the ASCII boundary checks and short appends (2026-10-28,\n  `optimisations.md` §2.9).\n- When the owner wants it, the S1 in-tree port for C1 (OD-20).\n\nWhere things are: CosmoOS's Nova sources live in CosmoOS beside the C they generate (`kernel/block/gpt.nova`,\n`kernel/process/cputime.nova`, `kernel/process/nice.nova`, `kernel/scheduler/fair.nova`), built by its `make nova`\nwith a pinned novac (`cosmoos/build-integration.md` §3.1). `integrations/cosmoos/` keeps the Nova-side scripts that\ncheck them (`c1.py` to `c9.py`) and no CosmoOS source.\n\n## Progress log\n\nCompleted work, oldest first; the table above has each milestone's status and report.\nEach entry, and each milestone report's \"Not done\" list, records the state when it was written; a later entry or\nreport says what changed since. Where the same report later supersedes its own list (M13), the list says so.\n\n**M1 and C1 are complete (2026-10-22).**\n- Nova compiles its M1 subset to C.\n- The test suite passes on macOS (clang) and Linux CI (clang and GCC).\n- All 70 run-corpus programs run natively inside CosmoOS on x86-64 and AArch64\n  (`cosmoos/c1-report.md`).\n\n**M2, ownership and borrowing, is done** (2026-10-22",
    236 =>
        "; `milestone-2.md`). It delivered:\n- 2a, structs;\n- 2b, references and the NLL borrow checker;\n- 2c, move types, `drop`, move checking on NIR, drop elaboration with flags, `using`, `defer`;\n- 2d, enums with payloads and `match`, fixed arrays, slices, bounds checks, `for`, and the\n  mutation suite.\n\n**M3, the safe heap, is done** (2026-10-22; `milestone-3.md`; ADR 0003). It delivered:\n- 3a, `Result<T, E>`, `?`, `!`;\n- 3b, `Box<T, A>`, `SystemHeap`, `FailingAllocator`, runtime allocation accounting, and the\n  fault-injection sweep;\n- 3c, moves out of boxes, consuming `match`, and tail-iterative destruction.\n\nHeap programs run inside CosmoOS on both arches with its libc `malloc`.\n\n**C2 is done** (2026-10-22; `cosmoos/c2-report.md`): `integrations/cosmoos/c2.py` runs the whole\ncorpus and a guest-side fault-injection sweep inside CosmoOS on both arches.\n\n**M4, regions, is done** (2026-10-22; `milestone-4.md`; ADR 0004):\n- scoped results;\n- `Arena`, `FixedBufferAllocator`, LIFO scratch frames and `Pool`/`PoolBox`, all following R1/R2;\n- the fault-injection sweeps, which cover region allocations.\n\n**M6, generics and collections, is done** (`milestone-6.md`; ADR 0005):\n- 6a, generics (checked once, monomorphised), done 2026-10-22;\n- 6b, interfaces, `impl`, bounds, done 2026-10-22;\n- 6c, optionals `T?` and `StringView`, done 2026-10-23;\n- 6d, `Vector<T, A>` and `String<A>` (Ex9, Ex10), done 2026-10-23;\n- 6e, `SlotMap<T>`/`Handle<T>` and `StableVector<T>`, done 2026-10-23.\n\n- 6f, model-based property tests, the invalidation suite and the OD-3 design\n  ([`od3-design.md`](od3-design.md)), done 2026-10-23. **M6 is complete** (acceptance results in\n  `milestone-6.md`).\n\n**M6x, region parameters (OD-3), is done** (2026-10-23; `milestone-6x.md`; ADR 0006): references\nin structs, enums, generic instances and collections, checked by signature flows and one loan set\nper value.\n\n**C3 is done** (2026-10-23; `cosmoos/c3-report.md`): files, TCP sockets, processes and program\narguments (type-system §26), and `nwc`, a Nova word counter that matches BusyBox `wc` byte for byte\ninside CosmoOS on both arches (`integrations/cosmoos/c3.py`).\n\n**M7, the production C backend, is complete** (2026-10-24; `milestone-7.md`). 7a, source maps, is done\n(2026-10-23): `#line` directives (`--line-directives`, `-g`), host-path-free source paths, and a\nline-table test. 7b, structured C, is done (2026-10-23): `if`/`else`, loops and `switch`\nreconstructed from the NIR graph, with the flat form kept as an oracle (`--flat-c`).\n\n7c, the NIR reference interpreter, is done (2026-10-23): `novac run --interp` agrees with native\ncode on the whole run corpus, the allocation-failure sweep and the I/O programs, and faults on\nuses of ended or uninitialised memory.\n\n7d, the random program generator, is done (2026-10-23): `tests/Nova.Fuzz`. Its first runs found a\nC name collision with the runtime, a borrow-checker false positive and four kinds of warnings in\ngenerated C, all fixed.\n\n7e is done (2026-10-24): generated C builds with MSVC in CI (`test-windows`), and a 24-hour campaign\n(423,773 programs through clang and GCC at `-O0`/`-O2`, the flat form and the interpreter) found no\nmiscompile; its one finding kind, a clang warning on a constant slice index, is fixed.\n\n**M5**, escape and placement analysis, was done in parallel (2026-10-23; `milestone-5.md`):\nnon-escaping `SystemHeap` boxes on the stack with `--placement`, `--explain-allocations`, equal\noutputs and identical drop traces.\n\n**M8, systems and freestanding support, is complete** (2026-10-24; `milestone-8.md`, ADR 0007: a\nhybrid core library). 8a is done (2026-10-24): `unsafe` blocks, expressions and functions, raw pointers `*T` and\n`*mut T`, the `Safety:` rule, and interpreter faults for invalid accesses through pointers. 8b is\ndone (2026-10-24): `extern \"C\"` declarations, `@export`, `@repr(C)` structs and `@repr` enums, a\ngenerated `nova_exports.h`, and C files in a build.\n\n8c is done (2026-10-24): `--profile freestanding` r",
    237 =>
        "ejects what needs the hosted runtime, links only\nthe core runtime, and `--emit obj` makes one object whose only undefined symbols are `nova_panic`,\nthe memory functions and the package's own C functions, on the host and both CosmoOS triples.\n\n8d is done (2026-10-24): `lib/core` (bundled Nova sources: `Volatile<T>`, `MmioPtr<T>`, address\ntypes), volatile accesses, C function pointers, `@align`, `@noncopy`, `Cpu.spinHint()` and target\nqueries; Ex18 runs, and builds as a freestanding object.\n\n8e, the acceptance, is done (2026-10-24): freestanding objects for both CosmoOS triples have only\nallowlisted undefined symbols and no constructor, TLS or unwind sections, mutable globals or COMMON\nsymbols.\n\n**C4 is done** (2026-10-24; `cosmoos/c4-report.md`): a freestanding Nova object built with\nCosmoOS's own module flags meets every structural rule of its module loader (ELF, relocations,\nexported symbols), keeps frames under 2 KiB and touches no FP register, on both arches; eight\nnegative controls are rejected.\n\n**C5 is done** (2026-10-24; `cosmoos/c5-report.md`): user-defined allocators (`Allocator`, `Layout`\nin `lib/core`; for `Box`), `KernelHeap` over `kmalloc`/`kfree`, and an out-of-tree, signed test module\nwhose in-kernel selftest (allocation, real and injected exhaustion, ownership transfer, cleanup)\npasses in CosmoOS on both arches. `ObjectCache<T>` waits for statics or scoped cache boxes.\n\n**C6a is done** (2026-10-24; `cosmoos/c6-report.md`): CosmoOS's LZ4 codec in safe Nova, beside\n`kernel/core/lz4.c`. CosmoOS's own host test and fuzz target pass for both, a differential test finds no\nmismatch in 20,000 cases, and the object meets the kernel checks on both arches. A bug planted in the\noffset check panics in Nova and goes unnoticed in sanitizer-free C. Performance is measured\n(`benchmarks/lz4/`): 0.48–0.78 of C for compression, lower for literal-heavy decompression.\n\n**C6b is done, and with it C6** (2026-10-24; `cosmoos/c6-report.md`): imported C struct types\n(type-system §31, ADR 0008) and CosmoOS's framebuffer validator in Nova over CosmoOS's own\n`kernel/bootinfo.h`. CosmoOS's host test and fuzz target pass for both. The differential test reaches\nall 11 answers with no mismatch, and the object meets the kernel checks on both arches. A header that\ndisagrees with a Nova declaration fails to compile, naming the field. With clang the port costs what C\ncosts (`benchmarks/fbvalid/`).\n\n**C7 is in progress** (`cosmoos/c7-report.md`, ADR 0009; the owner chose C state embedded by value). Its increments:\nC7a context effects, C7b embedded C state, C7c lock guards, C7d kernel objects and quiescence, and C7e the in-kernel\nstress test under lockdep. `QuiesceGuard` in modules (blocked on KQ-2), `PerCpu<T>` and statics are left out, with\nreasons. **C7a is done** (2026-10-24): `sleep`/`alloc` effects inferred over the package and checked in\n`@context(atomic|irq)` functions, under live `@context(atomic)` values, and against `@effects` bounds (type-system §32).\n\n**C7b is done** (2026-10-24): C state embedded by value (type-system §33): imported C types as fields, C-laid-out types made\nin place in boxes and never moved, zero-filled C fields, and `@placed` initialisers at the final address.\n\n**C7c is done** (2026-10-24): `SpinLock<T>`, `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `Mutex<T>` over CosmoOS's own locks, with private fields\n(type-system §12) so the data is reached only through a guard; sleeping under a spin guard does not compile; a host test\nover CosmoOS's spinlock shim passes, and the objects meet the module loader's rules on both arches (`c7.py`).\n\n**C7d is done** (2026-10-25): `@callback` and raw boxes (type-system §34), `KRef<T>` over CosmoOS's own `struct kobject`, and\ngrace periods (`Quiesce.synchronize`, `deferDrop`). A final put under a spin guard does not compile, and the host test\nruns against CosmoOS's real `object.c`.\n\n**C7e is done, and with it C7** (2026-10-25; `cosmoos/c7-report.md`): a Nova module in CosmoOS's debug build runs four\nkernel",
    238 =>
        " threads over 4 CPUs on Nova locks, kernel objects and grace periods, with every total exact, no lockdep report,\nCosmoOS's 507 selftests passing and a clean shutdown, on both arches. Lockdep catches the lock-order inversion that\nNova does not check, and an unlocked control counter loses updates. `QuiesceGuard` (KQ-2) and `PerCpu` are left out.\n\n**C8a is done, C8b validated against the proposed hook** (2026-10-25; `cosmoos/c8-report.md`): a Nova module (a timer, a\nkernel thread and a shared kernel object, torn down by dropping its state) loaded at boot on both arches; 1,000\nload/unload cycles with nothing left behind in a hook-patched copy of CosmoOS; a leaked reference makes the module a\nzombie. Three findings for CosmoOS. C8 closes when the hook is in CosmoOS.\n\n**M9a is done** (2026-10-25; `milestone-9.md`, `benchmarks.md`): fifteen workloads in Nova, C, C++, Rust, Zig and\nGoose with matching checksums, a runner measuring time, memory, allocations, compile time and size, and a baseline on\nthe Ryzen under clang and gcc. Nova is within 1.25× of C on 7 of 15 workloads with clang; its largest gaps are the\nbyte-loop copy (13–17×), vector growth and string building (1.9–4.4×), and the larger nodes of optional boxes. Writing\nthe suite also fixed a parser gap and a trigraph in emitted layout assertions.\n\n**M9b is done** (2026-10-25; `milestone-9.md`, type-system §16.5): checked bulk operations `copyFrom` and `fill` on\n`SliceMut` (one length check, then `memmove`; a byte fill is a `memset`), and `memcpy` in vector growth. On the Ryzen:\n- `memory-copy` is 1.06× C under clang and 1.67× under gcc, where gcc inlines C's constant-size copy;\n- vector growth under gcc is 2.11× C, down from 4.41×;\n- LZ4's literal decompression is 0.89–0.97 of C, up from 0.02–0.03.\n\nIt also found a flaw in the 9a workload: clang had merged C's block copies into one, so the blocks are now permuted.\n\n**M9c is done** (2026-10-25; `optimisations.md` §2, ADR 0010): bounds-check elimination, a NIR dataflow that removes\nindex checks earlier checks or loop and guard tests prove, with `--no-bce` and `--explain-bounds-checks`.\n- The interpreter checks every removal on every run, and the run corpus and fuzzer also build with the analysis off.\n- The owner added a rule to the unsafe contract: a pointer made from a reference cannot write after its target's\n  next direct use. It is what makes vector lengths trackable.\n- The analysis removes 14 of LZ4's 21 checks, which makes its decompression 1.32× faster under gcc. Under clang\n  nothing changes: clang already removed them, and gives byte-identical binaries.\n- The suite shows no change beyond noise.\n\n**M9d is done** (2026-10-25; `optimisations.md` §3, `c-backend.md` §5–§6): overflow-check elimination in the same\nanalysis (`--no-oce`, `--explain-overflow-checks`), and the cause of the gcc gap found and fixed.\n- The analysis removes 35 of LZ4's 50 overflow checks and 91 of the suite's 151. With them gone, clang vectorises LZ4's\n  match copy: `records` decompression is 3.44× faster under clang, 1.24× under gcc.\n- fbvalid's gcc gap (1.76× C since C6b) was the zero-initialisation of every aggregate local, which gcc keeps. Locals\n  are now zeroed only where needed, and the port is at 1.06× C under gcc.\n- Check locations are per-site constants passed by pointer, so a passing check no longer loads its location under\n  gcc (`data-oriented` 0.76 of its 9c time).\n- The fuzz campaign found a gcc `-Werror` warning, now fixed. A rerun of 11,581 programs found nothing.\n- Recorded, not fixed:\n  - `memory-copy` under gcc (C's constant-size `memcpy` is inlined);\n  - LZ4 under gcc moves by up to 25% with code placement alone;\n  - `numeric` measured 1.14× slower under clang in the suite, which did not reproduce outside it.\n\n**M9e is done** (2026-10-25; `memory-model.md` §5.1, `optimisations.md` §4): placement is on by default\n(`--no-placement`).\n- As M5 left it, placement placed no box in the suite. It now also pla",
    239 =>
        "ces boxes created in a loop when they die\n  within the iteration.\n- `heap-alloc` makes 1 allocation instead of 10,000,001, and runs 1.30× faster under clang (0.92 of C) and 1.16× under\n  gcc. The other workloads' C is unchanged.\n- The interpreter now reports a placed site reused while its box is alive, and a placed box destroyed twice.\n- Allocation counts are the one thing placement changes, allowed for `SystemHeap` only (`optimisations.md` §4.2).\n\n**M9e′ is done** (2026-10-25; type-system §22.1, `c-backend.md` §2): 9a's F4 and F6.\n- `Box<T>?` and `borrow T?` are now one pointer, null for `null`. Nodes and peak heaps are C's size: `binary-trees`\n  peaks at 12 MiB (was 20), and `arena` at 3.1 (was 4.6).\n- `Box.create` builds its argument in the box's memory, with no temporary and no by-value copy. `large-objects` under\n  gcc is 3.49× faster, now 0.84 of C.\n- Gains under gcc: `arena` 1.77×, `heap-alloc` 1.31×, `linked-list` 1.22×. Under clang: `arena` 1.39×,\n  `binary-trees` 1.17×.\n\n**M9 is complete** (2026-10-25; `milestone-9.md`, 9f). The whole suite was rerun in eight languages and modes.\n- Nova takes 0.93–2.44× C's time under clang (median 1.17), 1.02–3.54× under gcc (median 1.40), and 0.99–2.27× under\n  MSVC on ARM64 (median 1.18). At 9a it was up to 17× (`memory-copy`).\n- Each switch's share was measured. LZ4's decompressor needs both check eliminations to vectorise under clang (3.4×),\n  and placement gains 1.16–1.29× on `heap-alloc`. Differences under about 15% are this shared machine's noise.\n- `string-building` and `vector-growth` remain the slowest (2–3.5×).\n\n**M11 is complete** (2026-10-26; `milestone-11.md`). The owner put language gaps before tooling.\n- Done: nested patterns, `for` over non-`Copy` elements, borrow-checker precision (F1), character literals, explicit\n  overflow arithmetic, `String.appendNumber`, `if`/`match` expressions, references to optionals (F2), `f32`/`f64`\n  (ADR 0011) and function values, closures, effect lists on function types and closure-taking library methods\n  (ADR 0012), with `benchmarks/sort` and `nbody`, a sixteenth suite workload.\n- The acceptance re-ran every gap's probe; one found an older backend bug (`Result<S, IoError>` with a struct `S`), fixed.\n- Against M9's acceptance nothing regressed: Nova takes 0.89–2.45× C's time under clang (median 1.19) and 0.96–3.50×\n  under gcc (median 1.46), and the rows that moved were the same with 9f's compiler, interleaved. A 60-minute\n  campaign found nothing.\n\n**M10 is complete** (2026-10-26; `milestone-10.md`). Six tools, each with a spec and tests, kept passing by the\nrepository's own code:\n- `novac fmt`;\n- `novac doc` (`docs/api/core.md`);\n- `novac lsp` and `editors/vscode`, built with Vite+;\n- the lldb and gdb commands of `editors/debug`;\n- names across packages and `nova.toml` packages with path and git dependencies (ADR 0013).\n\nThe C generated for every existing program is the same as at M11's acceptance, apart from one source line number.\n\n**C9a is complete** (2026-10-26; `cosmoos/c9-report.md`, ADR 0014). CosmoOS's GPT parser and writer in Nova:\n- the language additions: array fields of imported C structs, arrays of imported types, `@cname`, pointer arithmetic\n  on imported types, constant arrays and exported constants;\n- `integrations/cosmoos/c9/gpt.nova`, with gpt.h's own structs, `unsafe` only in the exported wrappers;\n- `c9.py`: CosmoOS's `test_gpt` and `fuzz_gpt` pass with both implementations, the differential test finds no\n  mismatch in 20,000 cases (23 of 24 refusal reasons reached; the 24th cannot be reported, finding 1), and the\n  object meets C4's checks on both arches;\n- `benchmarks/gpt`: parity with C under clang, faster under gcc.\n\n**M12 is complete** (2026-10-26; `milestone-12.md`), at the owner's request (\"MCP or any better feature AI agents can\ndepend on\"):\n- diagnostics as JSON with fixes the compiler is sure of, `novac fix` and `novac explain`, and quick fixes in editors;\n- `novac m",
