ls`.\n\n**`string-building`'s gap** (2026-",
    241 =>
        "10-28; `optimisations.md` §2.9, c-backend §2). Two causes, each found by timing a\nhand-edited copy of the generated C first: a character-boundary check on every slice of the ASCII digit text, which\ncannot fail and is now removed under `--no-bce`'s switch, and gcc's `memcpy` call for every one-byte append, now a\nbyte loop up to 16 bytes. On the Ryzen box the workload went from 1.58× to 1.26× C under clang and from 2.58× to\n1.40× under gcc.\n\n**An independent review of closures and threads** (2026-10-29; type-system §13.2–§13.3, risks R-1). Three reviewers,\neach given the code and not a conclusion, wrote programs against M13's closures, `dyn`, threads and `Thread.both`.\nTwo holes were real, each shown by a program the checker accepted and ASan or ThreadSanitizer then caught: a\n`borrow mut` or `SliceMut` field read through a shared reference gave mutable access (present in 0.1.1), and the\nfield reborrow of 2026-10-28 did the same. Both are refused now (N0248). The reviewers were the same model that wrote\nthe code, so this is not the human or outside audit the priorities ask for.\n\n**P3: atomic orderings and closure-argument effects** (2026-10-29; ADR 0021, ADR 0020). `benchmarks/atomics` showed the\nneed for weaker orderings: a ring between two threads spent most of its time in x86-64's full barrier on every\nsequentially consistent store. lib/std's atomics gained `loadWith`, `storeWith` and the other `…With` methods, and the\nring became 4.5× (clang) and 6.0× (gcc) faster on the Ryzen box. Inline atomics, a further 1.8×, are OD-27.\n`@effectsOf(p)` gives a function the effects of the closure it is given, so `sortBy`, `any` and the others may run in\natomic and interrupt code with a closure that neither sleeps nor allocates. Adapters of named functions now call them\ndirectly. Closure types with `Fn` bounds, and several versions of one package, were re-examined and stay deferred.\n\n**novac 0.1.2** (2026-10-29; `release.md`, \"Point releases\"). The shared-path soundness fix, backported to the\n`release-0.1` branch from 0.1.1, whose compiler accepts the reviewers' program and whose C for it is a use after free\nunder ASan.\n\n**M14 begins** (2026-10-29; `milestone-14.md`). CosmoOS adopted Nova prerequisites in its roadmap 2.0 (#487), and the\nowner started M14. K2a: `novac build --split` writes a C file per Nova file, so a 202,000-line program builds in 14.0 s\non ten cores instead of 33.8 s on one, and in 2.4 s after a one-file change (ADR 0022). K4: CosmoOS source releases\ncarry the generated C (ADR 0023). K1: `static` and `static mut` items, built at compile time by `@constant`\nconstructors and C constant macros, and used through a hidden reference with the static region, so no new borrow\nrule was needed (ADR 0024, type-system §39).\n\n**M14 K3 and novac 0.2.0** (2026-10-29; ADR 0025). `lib/kernel`, the library for a kernel with CosmoOS's C API, ships\ninside novac and is selected by `--kernel cosmoos` or `kernel = \"cosmoos\"`: the kernel heap, spin locks and mutexes\nthat hand out their data only through a guard (spin locks also in statics), preemption and interrupt guards, per-CPU\nvalues, counted kernel objects and quiescence. `k3.py` checks it against CosmoOS's headers on both architectures; it\nhas not yet run in a booted kernel. A static mutex and `ObjectCache` are open (ADR 0025 §7). novac 0.2.0 carries\nM13, ADR 0018–0021, `--split`, statics and `lib/kernel`.\n\n**M14 K5 and K7, ADR 0027, and novac 0.3.0** (2026-10-29). The atomic types are in lib/core for every profile, their\noperations static inline in `rt.h` (ADR 0026): the ordered ring within 1.24–1.34× of inline C. Enum values whose\npayload may hold a pointer are built by constructors, so clang's analyzer follows a box's allocation. ADR 0027:\n`p.read()`/`p.write(v)` for any type, `layoutOf<T>()`, `here` in static initialisers, and lib/kernel's static `Mutex`\nand `ObjectCache<T>`. The random program generator writes statics and kernel-style programs built freestanding\nag",
    242 =>
        "ainst their hosted twin. CosmoOS's `nova-kcore` self-test runs lib/kernel in every debug boot (CosmoOS #489).\nnovac 0.3.0 carries all of it. 0.3.1, a point release the same day, fixes `ObjectCache.setUp`'s alignment, which CosmoOS's boot found.\n\n**M15 and novac 0.4.0** (2026-10-30; `milestone-15.md`). What a compiler written in Nova (OD-33, the CeeSafe port)\nneeds:\n- the fixes for #1–#5:\n  - condition temporaries are destroyed (and `RunCorpusTests` checks `live=0`);\n  - an instance that does not check is N0222, not a crash;\n  - `truncate` is two-phase;\n  - `borrow(this)` is honoured with a generic parameter;\n- a 1,000-level nesting limit (N0110), with the passes on their own 256 MiB stack and a 128-fact budget for check\n  elimination;\n- `StringView.fromUtf8`;\n- ADR 0028's host services: `Process.capture`, `File.kind`, `Environment`, `Directory`.\n\nnovac 0.4.0 carries all of it: tag `v0.4.0` at `66b6bda`, six platforms, macOS signed and notarized. Its lib/std\nhelpers took names a program could not redeclare (N0201, found by the port's re-pin); 0.4.1, a point release\nfrom `release-0.4` the same day, keeps them inside `HostServices`.\n\n**SA2's object layer and novac 0.5.0** (2026-10-30). CosmoOS's kernel object header is Nova\n(`kernel/object/object.nova`, CosmoOS #493): counts, release, discard and the object census, over a few lines of C for\ncalls through function pointers, `panic`'s message and the module exports; the recorded 10,000-operation handle trace\ngives C's digest. The handle table, next, is a type C embeds by value, so ADR 0029 lets a `@repr(C)` struct hold C\nstate (then C-laid-out, §33) and arrays, defined alike in `main.c` and `nova_exports.h`\n(`ffi/exported-c-state.nova`). novac 0.5.0 carries it: tag `v0.5.0` at `af071d4`, six platforms.\n\n",
    243 =>
        "# Safety audit pack\n\nStatus: prepared 2026-10-29 for an independent audit of Nova's memory-safety and data-race guarantees (the roadmap's\nP0). This page is the starting point for an auditor: what is claimed, where it is enforced, what the tests show, where\nholes are most likely, and what the first review found. It is explanatory; the normative rules are in the documents it\ncites.\n\n## 1. What is claimed\n\nSafe Nova (no `unsafe` block or function, no `extern` call) has no use after free, double free, out-of-bounds access,\nuse of uninitialised memory, or data race, and no undefined behaviour in the generated C (`ownership.md` §0, §2;\n`c-backend.md` §5). Integer overflow, bounds and character-boundary failures panic. `unsafe` code is outside the\nguarantee and must keep the contract in `unsafe.md`.\n\nThe invariants, by family (`ownership.md` §2):\n\n| Family | Subject | Where enforced |\n|---|---|---|\n| O* | ownership, moves, drops | `Analysis/MoveChecker.cs`, `MovePaths.cs`, `DropElaborator.cs` |\n| B* | borrowing (shared xor mutable, two-phase, reborrows) | `Analysis/BorrowChecker.cs`; mutability of places in `Semantics/TypeChecker.cs` (`MutabilityError`, `Coerce`) |\n| E* | escape: no reference outlives its referent | `Semantics/TypeChecker.cs` (region and signature flows), `BorrowChecker.cs` |\n| R* | regions, arenas and guards | `TypeChecker.cs`, `BorrowChecker.cs` |\n| H* | the heap: `Box`, `Vector`, `String`, allocators | `runtime/core/nova_rt_core.c`, `CEmitter.cs`, fault-injection tests |\n| T* | threads: `Send`/`Share`, joins, `Thread.both` | `Semantics/Types.cs` (`IsSend`, `IsShare`, `IsForkSafe`), `TypeChecker.cs` (`CheckThreadSpawn`, `CheckThreadBoth`), `runtime/hosted/nova_rt_thread.c`, `lib/std` |\n| U* | unsafe and FFI | `TypeChecker.cs` (N0280, N0283), `unsafe.md` |\n\n## 2. What the tests show, and what they do not\n\n- The corpus (`testing.md` §3) holds at least two compile-fail tests per rule, and runs every run-pass program in each C\n  configuration, ASan+UBSan included, and since 2026-10-29 every program that starts threads under ThreadSanitizer.\n- The M2 mutation suite (`MutationTests.cs`) turns accepted programs into rejected ones, with verdicts computed\n  independently of the checker.\n- The fault-injection sweep (`FaultInjectionTests.cs`) fails each allocation of each allocating program in turn.\n- The NIR interpreter is a dynamic oracle: it faults on an invalid access, an uninitialised read, or a removed check\n  that would have failed (`InterpreterTests`, `CheckEliminationTests`).\n- The random program generator (`testing.md` §5) compares the interpreter with every C configuration. Since 2026-10-29\n  it runs nightly (`.github/workflows/fuzz.yml`).\n\nNone of these proves the checker sound. They catch accepted programs that misbehave *when a test happens to write\none*. The generator writes only programs it believes correct, so it tests the backend and the optimisations, not the\nchecker's rejections. There is no formal model and no borrow tracking at run time (Stacked Borrows style): an aliasing\nviolation that does not corrupt memory in a given run is invisible (R-1).\n\n## 3. Where holes are most likely\n\n1. **Place mutability and reborrows** (`TypeChecker.cs`, `MutabilityError`, `Coerce`, `ToPlace`). Both holes of §4\n   were here. Mutable access is decided on paths of projections; every projection kind added (fields of references,\n   `SliceMut` fields, optionals, closures' captures) is a new way in.\n2. **Field-precise closure captures** (M13 G5; `TypeChecker.cs`, captures). A closure captures a path, not a variable,\n   so conflicts between captures depend on path disjointness, and `Thread.both`'s fork-safety on how each path is used.\n3. **`Send`/`Share` and `IsForkSafe`** (`Types.cs`). Structural and conservative, but each new type kind needs a rule.\n4. **Region parameters** (M6x; `od3-design.md`): references stored in structs and collections, and the signature\n   flows that bound them.\n5. **The generated C's own safety*",
    244 =>
        "*: `CEmitter.cs` (3,454 lines) and `nova_rt_core.c`. The checklist in\n   `c-backend.md` §5 is enforced by construction, not checked.\n6. **Optimisations that remove checks** (`CheckElimination.cs`, `ClosureSpecialisation.cs`, `PlacementAnalysis.cs`).\n   The checker runs before them; each relies on written preconditions (`optimisations.md`), checked dynamically by the\n   interpreter (R-8).\n7. **The libraries' `@threadSafe` types** (lib/std's `Arc` and `Mutex`, lib/core's atomics, lib/kernel's locks,\n   `PerCpu` and `ObjectCache`): `unsafe` code whose contracts are argued in comments.\n8. **Moves through raw pointers** (ADR 0027): `p.read()`/`p.write(v)` of types that are not `Copy`. The move checker\n   lets a value leave `*p` (`MoveChecker.cs`, `NirPlace.IsRawDeref`), the drop elaborator never drops the old contents\n   of `*p` (`DropElaborator.cs`), and a write of C state is a placement (`InPlaceChecker.cs`, `PtrPlace`). Each is an\n   exception to an ownership rule that the unsafe code's `Safety:` argument must make good (`unsafe.md` U2);\n   lib/kernel's `CacheBox<T>` is the first user.\n\n## 4. The first review (2026-10-29)\n\nThree reviewers were given the code and the specification, not a conclusion, and asked to write programs that the\nchecker accepts and that misbehave. Their programs are kept outside the repository (`out/audit-closures`,\n`out/audit-threads`, `out/audit-opt`). **The reviewers were instances of the same model that wrote the compiler, so this\nis not an independent audit**; it is a first pass that an outside reviewer should not rely on.\n\n| Area | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Closures, captures, `dyn`, owning closures | **One hole, fixed.** A `borrow mut` or `SliceMut` field read through a shared reference was reborrowed mutably (introduced 2026-10-28): two live mutable views of one vector, a heap use after free under ASan. |\n| Threads and `Thread.both` | **One hole, fixed** (same root, older: in 0.1.1). A method needing `borrow mut` called on such a field through a shared reference was accepted: a use after free on one thread (ASan), and with `Thread.both` a data race on a `Vector` (TSan). |\n| Optimisations (closure specialisation, BCE, OCE, ASCII boundaries, placement) | No finding. Two invariants noted as load-bearing: a closure parameter of a specialised variant is never reassigned, and a view is assigned only by `let`; both are language rules today (N0211, N0246). |\n\nBoth holes are refused now (N0248; type-system §13.2–§13.3) and covered by `check-fail/shared-path-mutation.nova`.\nSuspected and not confirmed: locking a `Mutex` twice on one thread is undefined in POSIX for a default mutex\n(`nova_rt_thread.c` uses `pthread_mutex_init(m, NULL)`), though Nova's guard types make it a deadlock in practice; user\nallocators behind `Box<dyn I + Send>` were not explored.\n\n## 5. For an outside auditor\n\nStart with `ownership.md` §2 and `type-system.md` §13 and §38, then the files in §3 in that order. Each claim in the\nspecification names its diagnostic code; `docs/diagnostics.md` lists the codes and `tests/corpus/check-fail` the\nprograms refused for each. `novac check --dump-hir` shows the checker's view of a program, and `novac run --interp` runs\nit in the faulting interpreter (on small programs only). A finding is most useful as a program that `novac check`\naccepts, with the sanitizer or interpreter output that shows it misbehaving.\n",
    245 =>
        "# Testing Strategy and Acceptance-Test Matrix\n\nStatus: M0/C0 (CosmoOS deliverable 11). **No test in this document has been\nexecuted yet.** Every row is *proposed*. When a row is first executed, it is marked with\nthe date and result. Tests that were performed are never mixed with proposals.\n\n## 1. Test kinds\n\n| Kind | Location | Oracle |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unit | `tests/Nova.Compiler.Tests` | xUnit assertions per pass |\n| parse/check pass/fail | `tests/corpus/*` | `// error: Nxxxx @line:col` annotations |\n| compile-fail (ownership) | `tests/corpus/check-fail` | `// error[Nxxxx]` annotations, as for the other check-fail files |\n| Fault-injection sweep (M3) | `tests/Nova.Compiler.Tests/FaultInjectionTests.cs` | every run-pass program that allocates, built with ASan+UBSan, is run once per allocation attempt *k* with attempt *k* failing. Each run must return from `main` with `live=0`, or panic with \"unwrapped an Err value\"; no sanitizer report |\n| Mutation suite (M2) | `tests/Nova.Compiler.Tests/MutationTests.cs` | mutants of seed programs; the expected (code, line) diagnostics come from an independent oracle, not from the checker |\n| run-pass / run-fail | `tests/corpus/run-*` | `// expect-stdout:`, `// expect-panic: overflow`, exit status |\n| IR snapshots | `tests/corpus/*/` + `.nir` files | reviewed text dumps |\n| Differential | runner | clang×gcc×O0/O2×sanitizers agree |\n| Fault injection | runner + `FailingAllocator` | no leaks or double frees (ASan), expected `AllocError` paths |\n| Fuzz (M7) | `tests/Nova.Fuzz` (§5) | generator + NIR interpreter + native agreement |\n| CosmoOS boot | `integrations/cosmoos` (S0), later `run_boot_test.py` markers (S1) | `NOVATEST: PASS` marker |\n| Kernel selftest (C5+) | CosmoOS selftest framework (owner-approved unit) | selftest pass + lockdep clean |\n\nSanitizer results are **supporting evidence**, never proof of language safety\n(master prompt §18).\n\n## 3. Corpus format (implemented in increment 1a)\n\nRunner: `tests/Nova.Compiler.Tests/CorpusTests.cs`, run by `dotnet test`.\n\n- `tests/corpus/parse-pass/*.nova`: must parse with **no** diagnostics. The AST dump\n  (`novac parse --dump-ast`) must equal the sibling `.ast` snapshot.\n  To create or update snapshots, run `NOVA_BLESS=1 dotnet test`. **Every blessed snapshot must\n  be reviewed by hand before it is committed.** A snapshot is an expected value, not a recording.\n- `tests/corpus/parse-fail/*.nova`: annotate each expected diagnostic on the line where it\n  is reported, as `// error[N0100]`, or `// error[N0100]@14` to also pin the column. The test\n  fails on any missing **or extra** diagnostic, so it also checks error recovery.\n- `tests/corpus/check-pass/*.nova`: must type-check with no diagnostics. The HIR dump\n  (`novac check --dump-hir`) must equal the sibling `.hir` snapshot (also blessed and reviewed).\n- `tests/corpus/check-fail/*.nova`: must **parse** cleanly and produce exactly the annotated\n  semantic diagnostics.\n- A file with a line `// profile: freestanding` is checked in the freestanding profile (cosmoos/profiles.md §4),\n  and one with `// option: no-float` as if built with `--no-float` (type-system.md §35.9).\n- Every parse-pass file is also run through the type checker to check that it never crashes\n  (semantic errors are expected there).\n- `tests/corpus/run-pass/*.nova` and `run-fail/*.nova` (`RunCorpusTests`) are compiled to C,\n  built with **every available C configuration** with strict warnings and `-Werror`, then run.\n  Directives:\n  - `// expect-stdout: text` — one per output line, in order. `// expect-stdout:` alone is an empty line.\n  - `// expect-exit: N` — the exit status (default 0).\n  - `// expect-panic: reason` — placed on the line where the panic must be reported. The test\n    requires exit status 134 (SIGABRT) and `nova panic: <reason> at <file>:<line>:` on stderr.\n- C configurations (`CToolchains`), each built with `-Werror`:\n  - clang `-O0`;\n  - clang `-O2`;\n  - clang `-O2 -DNV_NO_BUILTIN_OVERFLOW` (the port",
    246 =>
        "able fallback);\n  - clang `-O1` with ASan+UBSan and `-fno-sanitize-recover`;\n  - clang `-O2` with the C split into a unit per Nova file (`--split`, c-backend.md §2.4);\n  - clang `-O1` with ThreadSanitizer, only for programs that start threads (the generated C includes\n    `nova_rt_thread.c`), where it links (`CToolchains.ThreadSanitizer`; reported missing otherwise);\n  - the same three without sanitizers for a **real GCC** when one is found (`gcc`, `gcc-12`…`gcc-15`),\n    plus `NOVA_TEST_CC` if set.\n\n  Every configuration must match the expectations, so the run corpus is also a differential test.\n- The examples in the repository's `README.md` are corpus files named `readme-*` (run-pass, run-fail, check-fail).\n  `ReadmeTests` requires each one's code, without its header comment and test annotations, to appear verbatim in the\n  README, so the README cannot show code the compiler does not accept, or output it does not print.\n- `FormatterTests` (M10 T1, `formatting.md` §6): one case per style rule, and every parseable `.nova` file of\n  `benchmarks/`, `integrations/`, `lib/`, `programs/` and `tests/` formatted twice (a fixed point). Each formatting\n  re-parses its own output and fails unless the tree, the tokens (commas aside) and the comments are the original's.\n  The repository's own code and the `readme-*` files must already be formatted.\n- `DocTests` (M10 T2, `tooling.md` §1): the page and the `--missing` lines of a package with every kind of item, and\n  `docs/api/core.md` up to date with `lib/core`.\n- `PackageTests` (M10 T5, type-system §2) and `PackageManagerTests` (M10 T6, `tooling.md` §4): names across packages,\n  with a two-package program built and run; manifests, resolution, the lock, and path and git dependencies (git from\n  repositories the test makes; nothing when git is absent).\n- `SiteTests`: every Nova example of the documentation site's guide (`site/guide`) type-checks, runs with the output\n  the page shows, or fails with exactly the errors the page shows (`site/README.md`).\n- `DebuggerTests` (M10 T4, `tooling.md` §3.4): a `-g` build under lldb (macOS) or gdb (Linux CI installs it), with\n  breakpoints at Nova lines and on `nova_panic` and the `editors/debug` commands; nothing when neither is installed.\n- `editors/vscode` has its own tests, run with Vite+ (`vp test`, `tooling.md` §2.4): the grammar under\n  `vscode-textmate`, and `novac lsp` through `vscode-jsonrpc`. They are not part of `dotnet test`.\n- `LspTests` (M10 T3, `tooling.md` §2): the language server through in-memory streams (diagnostics, hover,\n  definition, symbols, formatting, errors), and the VS Code grammar's keywords against the compiler's.\n- `tests/corpus/emit-c/*.nova` have reviewed `.c` snapshots of the generated `main.c`.\n- `RuntimeSemanticsTests` generates a C harness that calls every checked helper in `nova/rt.h` on\n  edge values of every integer type (~9,000 cases) and compares results and panic kinds with\n  `IntegerSemantics`, under every C configuration. Planting a bug in `nv_rem_*` was checked to\n  fail this test (2026-10-22).\n- `BackendTests`: generated C is byte-identical across runs and contains no host paths. It also\n  compiles freestanding (`-ffreestanding -nostdinc`) for `x86_64-` and `aarch64-unknown-none-elf`.\n- `CollectionPropertyTests` (M6f; M6 acceptance criterion 2) are model-based property tests of the\n  built-in collections. For each collection (a `Vector` of items whose `drop` prints, `String`,\n  `SlotMap` with stored `Handle`s, `StableVector`) and each of a fixed set of seeds, a generator\n  writes a Nova program of random operations (`System.Random` with the seed, so the program is\n  the same on every machine). A C# model of the collection predicts the program's exact output:\n  values read back, lengths, capacities (the growth rule of `type-system.md` §23.1), stale-handle\n  results, slices at character boundaries, and the order of every `drop`. The program then runs\n  under every C configuration (as run-pass, in",
    247 =>
        "cluding ASan+UBSan) and must print exactly that,\n  and goes through the fault-injection sweep. Any disagreement is reported with the seed, so it\n  can be reproduced.\n\n## 5. Random program generator (M7d)\n\n`tests/Nova.Fuzz` is a Csmith-style generator of Nova programs. It is a library and a campaign\ntool: `dotnet run --project tests/Nova.Fuzz -c Release -- [--seed S] [--count N | --minutes M]\n[--jobs J] [--out DIR]`. `FuzzTests` runs a fixed set of seeds in the test suite.\n\n**Programs.** Each seed gives one program, the same on every machine (`System.Random` with a seed).\nEvery program is meant to be:\n- **well-typed and borrow-correct**, so the checker accepts it;\n- **terminating**:\n  - calls go only to later-declared functions (a call DAG, no recursion);\n  - loops have constant trip counts (`for (i in 0..N)`, counted `while`);\n  - the total iteration budget is bounded;\n- **deterministic**: no I/O except standard output, no addresses printed.\n\nIt uses:\n- integers of every width (`i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`, `usize`) and `bool`;\n- literals at the edges of each range;\n- `as` (lossless only), `truncate<T>`, and every operator;\n- structs (Copy, and with a `drop` block that prints), with field reads and writes;\n- enums with payloads, `match` on enums, integers and booleans;\n- fixed arrays (indexing, `for` over them) and slices;\n- `borrow`/`borrow mut` parameters;\n- `Box`, `Vector` (`push`, indexing, `length`, `pop`), optionals (`== null`, `!`), and `Result` with `?`\n  and `!`;\n- `if`/`else`, `for`, `while`, `loop` with `break`/`continue`, and early `return`;\n- statics (type-system §39; M14 K7): immutable scalars, arrays and structs (some built by a `@constant`\n  constructor), read anywhere like variables; a `static mut` read and changed in `unsafe` blocks, and static atomics\n  changed by two threads at once (`Thread.both`). The last two are used only in `main`, so every run prints the same.\n\n**Arithmetic.** Most operations go through *guarded* helper functions written in Nova, as Csmith\ndoes (its `safe_add` and friends):\n- `add`, `sub`: compare against the limits first;\n- `mul`: widen narrow types; reduce 64-bit operands below 2³² (unsigned) or 3·10⁹ (signed);\n- `div`, `rem`: skip a zero divisor and `MIN / -1`;\n- shifts: amount `% width`;\n- `neg`: skip `MIN`.\n\nA guard returns its left operand instead of panicking. A small fraction of operations, bounds\nand unwraps are *raw*. Those may panic, so the panic path (message, location, exit status) is\ncompared too. The generated programs print running checksums of their variables throughout, and\n`main` returns a value derived from them.\n\n**Oracle.** For each program:\n1. `novac` must accept it. A rejection is a finding: of the generator, or a checker bug.\n2. The NIR interpreter's outcome (standard output, standard error, exit status) is the\n   reference. An interpreter fault is a finding.\n3. Every C configuration must compile it warning-free (`-Werror`, strict warnings) and give the\n   same outcome: clang `-O0`, clang `-O2`, clang `-O2` flat form, and, where a real GCC exists,\n   GCC `-O0` and `-O2`. A program that starts threads (`Thread.both`) is also built with clang's\n   ThreadSanitizer where it links, so a race in the generated C is a finding. A run is limited to 10 s.\n\n**Kernel-style programs** (M14 K7; `--freestanding`, `GenerateDual`). The generator writes a program with only what\nthe freestanding profile has (no system heap, `Console`, `Process`, threads or floats), printing through `out_*`\nfunctions, in two variants with the same lines: hosted, whose `main` and printers use `Console`; and freestanding,\nwhose entry is `@export(\"fuzz_main\")` and whose printers call a C harness (`Oracle.HarnessSource`), which also defines\n`nova_panic` to print what the hosted runtime prints and exit as it does. The hosted variant, interpreted, is the\nreference; the freestanding variant must be accepted with `--profile freestanding --no-float` and, built with the\nharness, agree in every C configu",
    248 =>
        "ration. `FuzzTests` checks 16 seeds; the nightly campaign spends a quarter of its\ntime here. lib/kernel's guards need CosmoOS's C, so they are not generated: `k3.py` covers them.\n\nEach finding keeps the program and a report (`DIR/finding-<seed>.nova`, `.txt`). A finding that\nreveals a compiler bug is fixed, and its program, reduced by hand, joins the corpus.\n\n## 4. Execution log\n\n| Date | What ran | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-10-30 | After 0.4.0: lib/std's host helpers kept inside `HostServices`, so a program may use their names; run-pass `names-beside-lib-std` (every former top-level name declared by the program); CeeSafe's probes, support tests and fault sweep with the fixed compiler | 3,689 passed and 1 failed, `check-pass/effects-of.hir`, whose closure numbers all move down by one (lib/std's code makes one fewer instance before the test's own), re-blessed after review; CeeSafe 16/16 probes, 6/6 support, fault sweep 573/573; with 0.4.0 the new test fails with nine N0201 |\n| 2026-10-30 | M15 S7 (ADR 0028): the full suite on macOS with run-pass `host-services`, check-fail `host-services`, `HostServicesTests` (capture natively and in the interpreter, with a child writing 300,000 bytes to each stream; the sandbox's refusal), `ProgramTests` `c3-host`; `c3.py --arch aarch64 --ssh` against a running CosmoOS guest (v1.0.14) with `c3-host`; the run corpus through `run_corpus.py --check-leaks` | 3,678 passed; c3.py all PASS after one fix (CosmoOS's `S_ISDIR` takes `st_type`); a first version broke every hosted build (a lib/std `sortBy` instance's typedefs emitted without their pruned types) and put `HostText` in every `nova_exports.h`, both fixed before commit |\n| 2026-10-30 | M15 S1–S6: the full suite on macOS (clang) with check-fail `generic-void-instance`, `truncate-invalidation`, `borrow-this-generic-key`, `string-view-from-utf8`, `string-view-from-utf8-loans`; parse-fail `nesting-too-deep`; run-pass `condition-temporaries`, `truncate-two-phase`