        "ility vs reference output | C3 | ○ | ○ | ● | ● |\n| Fuzz campaign (24 h) | M7 | ○ | ● | — | — |\n| Reproducible build (byte-identical C and ELF) | C1+ | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n\n### Profile B (kernel)\n\n| Test group | Milestone | Kernel build x86-64 | Kernel build AArch64 | QEMU x86-64 | QEMU AArch64 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Freestanding object: symbols / relocs / sections allowlist | C4 | ● | ● | — | — |\n| Frame ≤ 2 KiB, call-chain ≤ budget | C4 | ● | ● | — | — |\n| No FP registers (disassembly) | C4 | ● | ● | — | — |\n| Kernel allocator adapter selftest (incl. exhaustion) | C5 | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n| Component parity vs C original | C6 | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n| Effect compile-fail suite | C7 | ● | ● | — | — |\n| Lockdep-clean stress (debug build) | C7 | — | — | ● | ● |\n| Module load/unload ×1000, no zombie, signed | C8 | ● | ● | ● | ● |\n\nPreservation requirement for all C milestones (prompt §11): existing CosmoOS\n`make test` on both arches, host tests, sanitizer tests and the analyzer gate stay\ngreen, and CosmoOS's own build targets are unchanged unless an approved unit changes them.\n",
    260 =>
        "# Tooling (M10)\n\nThe commands of `novac` beside `build`, `check` and `run`. The formatter has its own document (`formatting.md`).\n\n## 1. Documentation generator (T2)\n\n```sh\nnovac doc file.nova...                 # Markdown for the files' public items, on standard output\nnovac doc lib/core -o docs/api/core.md --title \"lib/core\"\nnovac doc --missing file.nova...       # list the public items without a doc comment; exit 1 if there are any\n```\n\nThe files are one package, as for `check` (a directory means every `.nova` file under it, in ordinal path order).\n`lib/core` is documented only when it is named. A file with a syntax error stops the command: its diagnostics are\nprinted and nothing is written. The package does not have to type-check: documentation is made from the syntax, so it\ncan be written for code that does not build yet.\n\n### 1.1 What is documented\n\n- Every item declared `public`: a function, a constant, a struct, an enum, an interface, and a function in an\n  `extern \"C\"` block.\n- Inside a public struct: its `public` fields, its `public` methods, and whether it has a `drop` block. Inside a public\n  enum: every variant. Inside a public interface: every method.\n- An `impl I for T` block, listed under `T` as \"Implements `I`\" when `T` is a public struct or enum of the package.\n\nNothing else is listed. Items in a `namespace` are listed under their simple names, as the language names them today\n(type-system.md §1).\n\n### 1.2 The page\n\nThe output is deterministic: the same files give the same bytes.\n- A title (`--title`, or the first file's name without its extension) as a level-1 heading, then a list of the\n  documented items, each a link to its section.\n- One level-2 section per item, in the order of the files as given and of the items in each file. Its heading is the\n  item's kind and name (`struct Volatile`, `fn sortBy`).\n- The item's **signature** in a `nova` code block: the item's attributes and its declaration up to its body (the\n  `{ … }` or `=> …` of a function, the members of a struct), as `novac fmt` prints them (formatting.md). A struct's\n  block lists its public fields; an enum's, its variants; an interface's, its methods.\n- The item's **doc comment** (grammar.md §1.2): its lines as written, which are Markdown. An item without one has\n  only its signature.\n- A struct's public methods follow as level-3 sections (`StructName.method`), each with its signature and doc\n  comment; then \"Implements `I`\" for each `impl`.\n\n### 1.3 Missing documentation\n\n`--missing` writes nothing but a line per public item without a doc comment, `path:line: kind name`, and exits 1 if\nthere is any. It is a lint for a package's public surface; nothing requires it to pass.\n\n### 1.4 In the repository\n\n`docs/api/core.md` is generated from `lib/core` with `novac doc lib/core -o docs/api/core.md --title \"lib/core\"`. The\ntest suite fails if it is out of date (`DocTests`), as it does for the README's examples.\n\n## 2. Language server (T3)\n\n```sh\nnovac lsp        # a Language Server Protocol server on standard input and output\n```\n\nThe server speaks LSP 3.17 over JSON-RPC 2.0, with `Content-Length` framing, on standard input and output. It is part\nof `novac` and has no dependency beyond .NET's own libraries. The editor starts it; `editors/vscode` is a VS Code\nextension that does (§2.4).\n\n### 2.1 What a document is checked with\n\nA document that is a source of a package (a `nova.toml` above it whose sources include it) is checked with its\npackage, as `novac check` in the package would check it (§4.4): the editor's text for the document, the package's\nother files from disk, and the dependencies its `nova.lock` names, never fetching; the manifest's profile and `float`\napply. Any other document, or one whose package does not resolve, is checked as a package of its own, with\n`lib/core`, as `novac check file.nova` would check it, using the text the editor holds. Its profile is hosted, unless\nits first line is `// profile: freestanding` (the corpus con",
    261 =>
        "vention, `testing.md` §3).\n\n### 2.2 Capabilities\n\n| Request or notification | What the server does |\n|---|---|\n| `initialize` | Answers with the capabilities below: full-text synchronisation, hover, definition, document symbols, formatting, quick fixes |\n| `textDocument/didOpen`, `didChange`, `didClose` | Keeps the text; after an open or change, checks the document (§2.1) and publishes its diagnostics. A close clears them |\n| `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` (sent) | Every diagnostic of the check: its range, severity, code (`N0205`) and message, with each note as related information |\n| `textDocument/hover` | On a name or an expression: its type, as Nova writes it. On a local or parameter, `let x: T` or `var x: T`; on a function or method, its signature; on a field, `name: T` |\n| `textDocument/definition` | On a local, parameter, function, method, field, struct, enum or interface name: the location of its declaration, if it is in the document |\n| `textDocument/documentSymbol` | The document's items and their members, as a tree: functions, structs (fields and methods), enums (variants), interfaces, constants |\n| `textDocument/formatting` | The document formatted by `novac fmt` (`formatting.md`), as one edit; nothing if it has a syntax error |\n| `textDocument/codeAction` | The fixes (§5.2) of the diagnostics in the requested range, as `quickfix` actions (§5.5) |\n| `shutdown`, `exit` | Stops |\n\nAnything else is answered with the JSON-RPC error `MethodNotFound` (requests) or ignored (notifications).\n\nPositions are LSP positions: zero-based lines, and columns in UTF-16 code units, which is how .NET indexes strings, so\nno conversion is needed. Hover and definition use the typed tree of the last check; if the document does not parse,\nthey answer null and only the diagnostics are shown.\n\n### 2.3 Errors\n\nThe server never stops on a bad document. A request that fails inside the compiler is answered with the JSON-RPC\nerror `InternalError` and the exception's message, and the server goes on. A message that is not valid JSON-RPC is\nanswered with `ParseError` when it has an id.\n\n### 2.4 The VS Code extension\n\n`editors/vscode` holds:\n- a TextMate grammar for `.nova` files (keywords, literals, comments, attributes), whose keyword list is the\n  compiler's (`Keywords.Table`; `LspTests` compares them);\n- a language configuration (comments, brackets, auto-closing pairs);\n- the client, which starts `novac lsp` (the `nova.server.path` setting, default `novac` on the `PATH`) through the\n  `vscode-languageclient` library, bundled with it into `dist/extension.cjs`.\n\nIt is built with [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/) (`vp`), not with the .NET build:\n\n```sh\ncd editors/vscode\nvp install            # the dependencies, pinned by pnpm-lock.yaml\nvp test               # the grammar and the server tests below\nvp run check          # lint and formatting\nvp run package        # bundle the client (vp pack) and make nova-language-0.1.0.vsix (vsce)\n```\n\nIts tests: `test/grammar.test.mjs` tokenizes Nova lines with `vscode-textmate`, as VS Code does, and checks the scope\neach token gets; `test/server.test.mjs` drives a built `novac lsp` through `vscode-jsonrpc`, the library under VS\nCode's client (diagnostics, hover, definition, an unknown method, shutdown and exit).\n\n## 3. Debugging (T4)\n\nA Nova program is debugged as the C it compiles to, with the C mapped back to Nova lines. No debugger needs to\nknow about Nova.\n\n### 3.1 A debug build\n\n`novac build -g file.nova -o program` writes `#line` directives into the C (`c-backend.md`, source maps) and compiles\nit with `-g -O0`. A debugger then shows Nova files and lines: breakpoints set by Nova line stop there, and stepping\ngoes from one Nova statement to the next. A line can take more than one step, since one Nova statement can be several\nC statements (a checked addition, a temporary).\n\n| | lldb | gdb |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop at a Nova line | `breakpoint set -f demo.nova -l 12` (`b demo.nova:12`) | `break demo.nova:12` |\n",
    262 =>
        "| Stop when a check fails, before the program aborts | `b nova_panic` | `break nova_panic` |\n| Where am I | `bt` | `bt` |\n\n### 3.2 Names\n\nThe C names follow `c-backend.md` §2.1, so a Nova name can be found in the debugger:\n\n| Nova | C |\n|---|---|\n| function `length` | `nvF_length` |\n| method `Counter.bump` | `nvM_7Counter_bump` (the type's name, prefixed by its length) |\n| local or parameter `sum` | `v_sum_1` (a number makes each local's name unique in its function) |\n| a temporary the compiler made | `t_3` |\n| field `x` | `f_x` |\n| struct `Point` | `nvT_Point` |\n\n### 3.3 Nova names in the debugger\n\n`editors/debug/nova_lldb.py` and `editors/debug/nova_gdb.py` add two commands, which print with Nova names:\n- `nova-locals`: the current frame's locals and parameters, `name = value`, without the compiler's temporaries; struct\n  values show their fields by their Nova names;\n- `nova-bt`: the backtrace, one frame a line, `Nova name at file:line` (`Counter.bump at demo.nova:3`).\n\nLoad them with `command script import editors/debug/nova_lldb.py` (lldb) or `source editors/debug/nova_gdb.py` (gdb).\n\nSplit C (`--split`, c-backend.md §2.4) renames the program's functions to `nvF_<program>_…`: set `NOVA_PROGRAM` to\nthe program's name before starting the debugger, and the helpers read them as before.\n\n### 3.4 What is checked\n\n`DebuggerTests` builds a program with `-g` and runs it under lldb (macOS) or gdb (Linux), in batch mode. A breakpoint\nat a Nova line must stop at that line, `nova-locals` must show the locals by their Nova names, and a breakpoint on\n`nova_panic` must give a backtrace whose frames are Nova functions at Nova lines. On a machine with neither debugger,\nthe test does nothing, and the run log says which debugger ran.\n\n## 4. Packages (T6, ADR 0013)\n\n### 4.1 The manifest\n\nA package is a directory with `nova.toml`. Its sources are the `.nova` files under the directories `sources` names\n(default `src`), in ordinal path order. The manifest is a subset of TOML: `[table]` headers, `key = value` lines\nwhose value is a string, a boolean, an array of strings or an inline table of strings, and `#` comments.\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"app\"                 # required: an identifier, not a keyword and not \"core\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"            # optional; required to publish (§4.8), MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\nkind = \"executable\"          # \"executable\" (default), \"library\", or \"object\" (cosmoos/profiles.md §4.3)\nprofile = \"hosted\"           # or \"freestanding\"\nfloat = true                 # false is --no-float (type-system.md §35.9)\nkernel = \"cosmoos\"           # with profile = \"freestanding\": include lib/kernel, as --kernel cosmoos (ADR 0025)\ntrusted = true               # its types may be declared @threadSafe (ADR 0025 §3.2); listed for reviewers\nsources = [\"src\"]\nc = [\"src/shim.c\"]           # C files compiled and linked with an executable (type-system.md §28.6)\n\n[dependencies]\ngeometry = { path = \"../geometry\" }\nlz4 = { git = \"https://example.org/lz4-nova.git\", tag = \"v1.2.0\" }      # or rev = \"<commit>\", or branch = \"main\"\nshapes = \"2.1\"               # from the registry (§4.6): a version requirement\njson = { version = \"0.9\", index = \"https://example.org/my-index.git\" }   # from another index\n```\n\nA dependency's key is its package's `name`: a dependency is known by the name its own manifest gives it. A\ndependency of kind `executable` is N0321: only a library's items are used. Errors in the manifest are N0320 (its\nsyntax) and N0321 (a missing, unknown or invalid field), with the line.\n\n### 4.2 Resolving dependencies\n\nFrom the package being built, every dependency is resolved, and then its dependencies, recursively:\n- `path` is a directory relative to the manifest that names it, holding the dependency's `nova.toml`;\n- `git` is a repository; `rev` names a commit, `tag` and `branch` are resolved to one (§4.3). The commit is\n  checked out into the cache, `$NOVA_HOME/git/checkouts/<repository>/<commit>` (default `~/.nova`), and the\n  depende",
    263 =>
        "ncy is the package at the checkout's root.\n\nA package name reached by two different sources (two paths, two commits, a path and a commit) is N0326, naming both\ndependents. A package that depends on itself, directly or not, is N0327. A missing directory or manifest is N0322.\nNothing runs during resolution except `git` (ADR 0013 D5).\n\n### 4.3 The lock file\n\n`nova.lock`, beside `nova.toml`, records the resolution so that every build uses the same code:\n\n```toml\n# Written by novac fetch. Commit it with nova.toml.\n[[package]]\nname = \"lz4\"\nsource = \"git+https://example.org/lz4-nova.git\"\ncommit = \"3f2a9c0d…\"         # the full commit id\nhash = \"sha256:…\"            # of the checkout's manifest and sources\n\n[[package]]\nname = \"geometry\"\nsource = \"path+../geometry\"\n```\n\n- `novac fetch` resolves every dependency, fetching git repositories and resolving tags and branches (`git\n  ls-remote`, `git fetch`), and writes the lock. A `rev` is used as written; a `tag` or `branch` is the commit it names\n  at that moment, until the next `novac fetch`.\n- Every other command reads the lock. It fails with N0324 if the manifest has a dependency the lock lacks or\n  records differently (run `novac fetch`), and with N0325 if a git checkout's manifest and sources no longer have the\n  lock's hash. A missing checkout of a locked commit is fetched, unless `--offline` is given (N0323 if it cannot be).\n- A path dependency has no hash: it is code being worked on beside the package.\n- The hash is SHA-256 over, for the manifest and each source file in ordinal order, the path relative to the package\n  root, a zero byte, the file's bytes and a zero byte.\n\n### 4.4 Commands\n\n- `novac new <name>` makes `<name>/nova.toml` and `<name>/src/main.nova` (a `main` that prints a line); `--lib`\n  makes a library with `src/lib.nova` instead. It also writes `.gitignore` (`out/`) and the agent instructions of\n  §7: `AGENTS.md` by default, others with `--agent`, none with `--no-agent`.\n- `novac agents [--agent …]` writes or refreshes the agent instructions of the package in the current directory or\n  above it (§7.3).\n- `novac fetch [--offline] [--update [name]]` resolves and writes `nova.lock` (§4.7).\n- `novac publish` checks that the package can be published and prints its index entry (§4.8).\n- `novac build`, `check`, `run`, `doc` and `fmt` without file arguments use the package of the nearest `nova.toml` in\n  the current directory or above it. `build` writes `out/<name>` (an executable, or `out/<name>.o` for `object`), or\n  the C sources with `--emit c`; the manifest's profile, `float` and C files apply, and command-line options add to\n  them. `fmt` formats the package's own sources, not its dependencies'.\n- The language server (§2.1) checks a document that belongs to a package (a `nova.toml` above it, whose sources\n  include it) with that package: the open document's text, the package's other files from disk, and its locked\n  dependencies. Without a lock, or outside a package, the document is checked alone as before.\n\n### 4.5 Errors\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| N0320 | `nova.toml` or `nova.lock` is not valid in the TOML subset of §4.1 |\n| N0321 | A manifest field is missing, unknown, or has an invalid value |\n| N0322 | A path dependency's directory or manifest is missing, or a dependency is named differently from its key |\n| N0323 | A git dependency cannot be fetched or resolved, or is needed with `--offline` and absent |\n| N0324 | The lock file is missing or does not match the manifest: run `novac fetch` |\n| N0325 | A git checkout's manifest and sources do not match the lock's hash |\n| N0326 | One package name reached through two different sources |\n| N0327 | A dependency cycle |\n| N0328 | An invalid version, version requirement, or index entry |\n| N0329 | No versions satisfy the requirements: the chain of requirements that conflict |\n| N0335 | `novac publish`: the package cannot be published as it is |\n\n### 4.6 The registry (M13 G6, ADR 0017)\n\nA **registry depe",
    264 =>
        "ndency** names a package and a **version requirement**. The package is found in an **index**: a git\nrepository, by default `https://github.com/vkuttyp/nova-index`, or `$NOVA_INDEX`, or the dependency's `index`.\n\n- **Versions** are `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, three decimal numbers with no leading zeros, compared numerically. There\n  are no pre-release or build suffixes in v1.\n- **Requirements** are one or more comparators separated by commas, all of which must hold:\n  - `\"1.4\"`, `\"^1.4\"` and `\"^1.4.2\"` are caret requirements: at least that version, below the next major version, or\n    below the next minor for `0.x` (`\"0.3\"` is `>=0.3.0, <0.4.0`) and the next patch for `0.0.x`.\n  - `\"=1.4.2\"` means exactly that version.\n  - `\">=1.4\"`, `\">1.4\"`, `\"<2\"` and `\"<=1.9.3\"` compare. A missing minor or patch is 0.\n  - `\"*\"` means any version.\n- **The index** holds `packages/<name>.toml`, with one `[[version]]` table per published version, in publishing order:\n  ```toml\n  [[version]]\n  version = \"1.4.0\"\n  git = \"https://github.com/someone/geometry.git\"\n  commit = \"3f2a9c0d…\"                      # a full commit id\n  hash = \"sha256:…\"                         # of the commit's manifest and sources (§4.3)\n  dependencies = { shapes = \"2.1\" }         # its registry requirements, as its nova.toml writes them\n  yanked = false                            # optional; a yanked version is never chosen for a new resolution\n  ```\n  Entries are only appended. A published package's dependencies are all registry dependencies, and those without\n  an `index` come from the index that published the package.\n\n### 4.7 Version solving\n\n`novac fetch` resolves path and git dependencies as before (§4.2), gathering the registry requirements of the root\nand of every path and git package. It then chooses one version of each registry package, transitively, from the\nindex's entries:\n\n- The **newest** version that satisfies every requirement on it is chosen, and so on through its own dependencies.\n  On a conflict, the solver backtracks.\n- A version the lock already records is preferred while it satisfies the requirements, so `novac fetch` changes\n  nothing that needs no change. `--update` prefers the newest for every package, and `--update <name>` for that one.\n- **One version per package name** (OD-26). A name reached through a path or git dependency and through the registry\n  is N0326.\n- If no choice satisfies every requirement, N0329 shows the requirements on the package that could not be chosen,\n  and where each came from:\n  `no version of 'shapes' satisfies ^2.1 (from geometry 1.4.0) and ^1.0 (from plot 0.3.0)`.\n- Each chosen version's commit is checked out as for a git dependency (§4.2). Its hash must be the index's (N0325),\n  and its manifest's name and version must be the entry's (N0328).\n- The lock records each as `source = \"registry+<index>\"` with `version`, `repository` (the git URL), `commit` and\n  `hash`. Other commands use the lock: an entry whose version no longer satisfies the manifest's requirement is\n  N0324.\n\n### 4.8 Publishing\n\n`novac publish` (no network but `git`, and nothing written) checks the package in the current directory:\n\n- it is a library with a valid `version`;\n- every dependency is a registry dependency;\n- it type-checks with its dependencies;\n- its git working tree is clean, and its commit is on a remote branch.\n\nIt then prints the index entry for the version: the remote's URL, the commit, the hash and the requirements. To\npublish, add that entry to `packages/<name>.toml` in the index by pull request. The index's continuous integration\nruns `novac index-check`, which checks every new entry:\n- the commit exists;\n- its manifest has the entry's name and version;\n- its hash and dependencies are the entry's;\n- the version is newer than every earlier entry.\n\nA new package name is reviewed by the index's maintainers. Later versions from the same repository merge after the\nchecks pass.\n\n## 5. Diagnostics for programs (M12 A1)\n\nPeople read diagn",
    265 =>
        "ostics as text (`diagnostics.md`, \"Format\"). Programs, AI agents and editors among them, read the\nsame diagnostics as JSON, and can apply the fixes the compiler is sure of.\n\n### 5.1 `--diagnostics-format=json`\n\n`check`, `build`, `run` and `parse` accept `--diagnostics-format=json` (or `=text`, the default). With `json`,\ndiagnostics are not written to standard error; instead one JSON document is written to standard output, as one line,\nafter the command's own work. `build` still writes its outputs, and `run` still runs the program, if there are no\nerrors; the program's own output then precedes the document. The exit status is unchanged.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"errors\": 1,\n  \"warnings\": 0,\n  \"diagnostics\": [\n    {\n      \"code\": \"N0249\",\n      \"severity\": \"error\",\n      \"file\": \"main.nova\",\n      \"start\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 },\n      \"end\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 11 },\n      \"message\": \"this parameter is 'borrow mut': write 'mut' before the argument to let the call modify it\",\n      \"summary\": \"`mut` argument marker missing or misplaced\",\n      \"notes\": [ { \"file\": \"main.nova\", \"start\": { … }, \"end\": { … }, \"message\": \"…\" } ],\n      \"fixes\": [\n        { \"title\": \"write 'mut' before the argument\",\n          \"edits\": [ { \"file\": \"main.nova\", \"start\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 }, \"end\": { \"line\": 7, \"column\": 10 },\n                       \"newText\": \"mut \" } ] }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `version` is the format's version: fields may be added within a version, never removed or changed.\n- Positions are 1-based lines and columns, columns in UTF-16 code units, as in the text form. `end` is exclusive.\n- `file` is the path as the text form prints it.\n- `summary` is the code's \"Meaning\" in `diagnostics.md`, which `novac` embeds.\n- Diagnostics are in the order the text form prints them. Keys are written in the order shown.\n\n### 5.2 Fixes\n\nA fix is a list of edits that the compiler is sure resolves the diagnostic. It is offered only where the\nintent is unambiguous; a diagnostic with several plausible repairs offers none. Applying a fix removes that\ndiagnostic and introduces none at the edited place (checked by `FixTests` for every kind below, on every corpus\ncase that offers it). The kinds:\n\n| Code | When | Edit |\n|---|---|---|\n| N0249 | an argument for a `borrow mut` parameter lacks `mut`, and is a place that can be borrowed mutably | insert `mut ` before the argument; if the place is a `let` local, also `let` → `var` at its declaration (one fix) |\n| N0249 | an argument for a shared parameter has `mut` | delete the `mut ` |\n| N0211 | assignment to a `let` local declared in the function | `let` → `var` at its declaration |\n| N0248 | `borrow mut` (or a `borrow mut this` call) of a `let` local declared in the function | `let` → `var` at its declaration |\n| N0208 | `e as T` between integer types that can lose information | `truncate<T>(e)` (wrap-around, as the message says) |\n| N0100 | `expected ';'`, with no earlier error in the file, after a declaration, assignment, `return`, or a call statement (not after an expression that cannot be a statement, N0225) | insert `;` after the previous token |\n\n### 5.3 `novac fix`\n\n`novac fix [FILES]` (or the package, without files) applies every fix, writes the files, checks again, and repeats\nuntil no fix applies or after 8 rounds. It then prints the remaining diagnostics, as text or with\n`--diagnostics-format=json`, and exits 1 if errors remain. With `--dry-run` it changes no file and prints the\nedits of the first round. Edits to one file never overlap: when two fixes would, the later one waits for the next\nround.\n\n### 5.4 `novac explain`\n\n`novac explain N0249` prints the code's row of `diagnostics.md` (its meaning and example) and the paragraphs of\n`diagnostics.md` that mention it. An unknown code is exit status 2.\n\n### 5.5 In the language server\n\n`textDocument/codeAction` returns the fixes of the diagnostics that touch the requested range as `quickfix` actions,\neach with its diagn",
    266 =>
        "ostic and a `WorkspaceEdit` for the document (§2.2). A fix that would edit another file is not\noffered.\n\n## 6. The MCP server (M12 A2)\n\n`novac mcp` serves the [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) on standard input and output, so an\nAI agent's host (Claude Code, an IDE, any MCP client) can use Nova's compiler, interpreter and documentation as\ntools. It is part of `novac`, with no other dependency, and serves the documents embedded in that `novac`, so the\nanswers always match the compiler that gives them.\n\n```json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"nova\": { \"command\": \"novac\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"] } } }\n```\n\n### 6.1 Protocol\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0, one message per line of UTF-8 on standard input and output; nothing else is written to standard\n  output. The server stops at the end of its input.\n- `initialize` negotiates the protocol version: the client's, if it is one the server supports (`2025-06-18`,\n  `2025-03-26`, `2024-11-05`), otherwise the newest of those. The server declares `tools`, `resources` and `prompts`,\n  none of which change while it runs, and gives brief usage `instructions`.\n- `ping`, `tools/list`, `tools/call`, `resources/list`, `resources/read`, `prompts/list` and `prompts/get` are\n  answered; other requests are `MethodNotFound` (-32601); notifications are ignored.\n- With version `2025-06-18`, each tool declares an `outputSchema` and its result carries `structuredContent`, with the\n  same data as JSON text in `content`. With earlier versions, only `content`.\n- A tool call that the tool cannot carry out (a file it cannot read, a source that does not parse when it needs to)\n  is a result with `isError: true` and the reason. Unknown tools and invalid arguments are `InvalidParams` (-32602).\n  The server never stops on a bad request.\n\n### 6.2 Sources\n\nEvery tool that compiles takes its program as either\n- `files`: an array of `{ \"path\": \"main.nova\", \"text\": \"…\" }`, compiled as one package, with nothing read from disk; or\n- `paths`: an array of `.nova` file paths, read from disk relative to the server's working directory;\n\nplus `profile` (`hosted`, the default, or `freestanding`) and `noFloat` (default false). Paths in results are the\npaths given.\n\n### 6.3 Tools\n\n| Tool | Input (besides §6.2) | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nova_check` | | the diagnostics document of §5.1, with fixes |\n| `nova_fix` | | `files`: each file's text with every fix applied, over rounds as `novac fix` does (§5.3); `applied`: the number of fixes; `diagnostics`: what remains. Nothing is written to disk |\n| `nova_format` | `text`, `path` (optional) | `text`: formatted (`formatting.md`); or an error with the syntax diagnostics |\n| `nova_run` | `stdin` (text), `args`, `maxSteps` (default 10,000,000), `maxOutputBytes` (default 65,536) | `outcome` (`exited`, `panicked`, `fault`, `not-compiled`), `exitCode`, `stdout`, `stderr`, `truncated`, and the diagnostics if it did not compile |\n| `nova_emit_c` | `explain`: any of `allocations`, `bounds-checks`, `overflow-checks` | `files`: the generated C (`main.c`, and `nova_exports.h` when there is one; not the runtime); `explanations`: the `--explain-*` text |\n| `nova_explain` | `code` | `code`, `meaning`, `example`, `paragraphs` of `diagnostics.md` (§5.4) |\n| `nova_spec_search` | `query`, `limit` (default 10) | `results`: the sections whose heading or text contain every word of the query, best first: `document`, `heading`, `snippet`, `uri` |\n| `nova_spec_section` | `document` (`type-system`, `grammar`, …), `section` (`31.2`, or heading text) | `text` of that section, with its subsections |\n| `nova_symbol` | `path`, `line`, `column` (1-based) | the type or signature under the position, and its declaration's location, as hover and definition give them (§2.2) |\n\n`nova_run` runs the program in the interpreter (`run --interp`) **sandboxed**:\n- files, sockets and processes are refused (a fault that names the refusal);\n- `maxSteps` statements at most (a fault), and `maxOutputBytes` of each output kept (`truncate",
    267 =>
        "d` says if more was\n  written; the program goes on);\n- 256 MiB of interpreter memory at most: the heap then reports out of memory, which the program sees as\n  `AllocError.OutOfMemory`; a local or static allocation beyond it is a fault;\n- standard input is `stdin`, the arguments are `args`, and the run has no access to the server's environment.\n\nA fault's exit code is 70 and a panic's 134, as for `run --interp`.\n\n### 6.4 Resources\n\nEach embedded document is a resource `nova://docs/<path>` (for example `nova://docs/docs/type-system.md`,\n`nova://docs/site/guide/basics.md`), `text/markdown`, named by its first heading. `resources/read` returns its text.\nAn unknown URI is the error -32002 (resource not found).\n\n### 6.5 Prompts\n\n| Prompt | Arguments | What it asks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `write-nova` | `task` | To write Nova for the task: the agent guide (`site/guide/for-agents.md`), then the task, with the edit-check loop of `nova_check` and `nova_fix` |\n| `port-c-to-nova` | `file`, `api` | To port a C file to Nova as C6 and C9 did: imported C structs from the header, safe code over slices, `unsafe` only at the exported boundary, and the C's own tests, a differential test and a benchmark (`cosmoos/c9-report.md`) |\n\n## 7. Agent instructions in packages (M12 A4)\n\nAI coding agents read a file of instructions at the root of a project. `novac new` writes one, so that an agent\nworking in a new Nova package knows from the start what Nova is, which commands check its work, and the mistakes to\navoid. The convention follows Vite+'s (`vp create`).\n\n### 7.1 The Nova section\n\n`AGENTS.md` holds the **Nova section**, between the lines `<!--NOVA START-->` and `<!--NOVA END-->`: what Nova is and\nwhere its guide for agents is; the package's layout; the commands (`check`, `fix`, `run --interp`, `build`, `fmt`,\n`explain`, `--diagnostics-format=json`); how to use the MCP server; a checklist before finishing; and the common\nmistakes of the agent guide. The text is embedded in `novac` (`templates/AGENTS.md`) and is the same for every\npackage. Everything outside the markers belongs to the package's authors: novac never changes it.\n\n### 7.2 Agents\n\n`--agent` takes one or more of these names, separated by commas or by repeating the option; the default is `agents`.\nEach name writes the files below; `AGENTS.md` is written whenever any agent is chosen, since the others refer to it.\n\n| Name | Agent | Instructions | MCP configuration (`novac mcp`, §6) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `agents` | Codex, Amp, OpenCode and others that read `AGENTS.md` | `AGENTS.md` | — |\n| `claude` | Claude Code | `CLAUDE.md`: `@AGENTS.md` (Claude Code's import) | `.mcp.json` |\n| `gemini` | Gemini CLI | `GEMINI.md`: `@AGENTS.md` (Gemini CLI's import) | `.gemini/settings.json` |\n| `copilot` | GitHub Copilot | `.github/copilot-instructions.md`: a copy of the Nova section | `.vscode/mcp.json` |\n| `cursor` | Cursor | `.cursor/rules/nova.mdc`: a copy of the Nova section, applied always | `.cursor/mcp.json` |\n\nAn unknown name is a usage error (exit status 2), and so is `--agent` with `--no-agent`. The MCP configurations name\nthe server `nova`, with command `novac` and argument `mcp`, in each tool's format: `{\"mcpServers\": {\"nova\": …}}`, or\n`{\"servers\": {\"nova\": {\"type\": \"stdio\", …}}}` for VS Code.\n\n### 7.3 `novac agents`\n\nIn a package, `novac agents [--agent …]` writes the chosen agents' files (default `agents`):\n- a missing file is written as `novac new` writes it;\n- in an instructions file that has the markers, the text between them is replaced with the current Nova section,\n  and the rest is kept; a file without them gets the Nova section appended, after a blank line; a file that only\n  imports `AGENTS.md` (`@AGENTS.md`) is left as it is;\n- an MCP configuration that exists is left as it is, and the command says so: it may hold the authors' other servers.\n\nIt prints what it wrote and what it left. Run it after updating `novac`, so the instructions match the compiler.\n\n## 8. The C compiler (`nov",
    268 =>
        "ac build`)\n\n`novac build` writes C and gives it to a C compiler. It needs no configuration when a usual compiler is installed.\n\n### 8.1 Which compiler\n\n`--cc <compiler>` names it; otherwise `$CC`; otherwise:\n- on Linux and macOS, `cc`;\n- on Windows, the first of these that exists: `clang` on `PATH`; `%ProgramFiles%\\LLVM\\bin\\clang.exe` (where the LLVM\n  installer, `winget install LLVM.LLVM`, puts it); `gcc` on `PATH` (MinGW-w64); `cl` on `PATH`; Visual Studio's `cl`\n  (§8.2). clang and gcc are fully supported; MSVC is best effort (`c-backend.md` §1.1).\n\nWith none, the error names what was looked for and how to install one.\n\n### 8.2 Visual Studio's environment (Windows)\n\nclang on Windows and `cl` compile against Visual Studio's C library and the Windows SDK, which they find through the\nenvironment that Visual Studio's developer prompt sets (`INCLUDE`, `LIB`, `PATH`). When that environment is missing\n(`INCLUDE` unset) and the compiler is clang or `cl`, `novac build` sets it up for the compiler, as the developer prompt\nwould:\n1. find Visual Studio, or its Build Tools, with the C++ tools for this machine: `vswhere -latest -products *\n   -requires Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.ARM64` on an ARM64 Windows,\n   `…VC.Tools.x86.x64` otherwise (other products that `vswhere` knows, such as SQL Server Management Studio, have no\n   C++ tools and are not taken);\n2. run its `VC\\Auxiliary\\Build\\vcvarsall.bat` for the machine's architecture (`arm64` or `x64`) and take the\n   environment it leaves;\n3. run the compiler in that environment. The user's own environment is not changed.\n\nWithout Visual Studio, clang still works where the environment is already set; otherwise the error says to install\nVisual Studio's \"Desktop development with C++\" workload (or the Build Tools), or MinGW-w64.\n\n### 8.3 Flags\n\n| Compiler | Flags |\n|---|---|\n| clang, gcc, `cc` | `c-backend.md` §1's: `-std=c11`, the strict warnings, `-ffp-contract=off -fno-math-errno`, `-O2` (or `-g -O0`), `--cflag`s, `-o <output>`; on Windows also the 8 MiB stack, `-Wl,/STACK:8388608` for clang and `-Wl,--stack,8388608` for gcc |\n| `cl` (MSVC) | `/nologo /std:c11 /W3 /O2` (or `/Zi /Od` with `-g`), `--cflag`s, `/Fo:` the build directory (so no `.obj` lands in the project), `/Fe:<output>`, `/link /STACK:8388608` (`c-backend.md` §1.1) |\n\nA compiler is MSVC when its file name is `cl` or `cl.exe`. `--emit obj` (one relocatable object) needs clang or gcc.\n\n### 8.4 The output\n\nThe executable is `out/<name>` in a package, or `-o <path>`. On Windows, a path without an extension gets `.exe`, so\n`novac build` makes `out\\hello.exe`. The output's directory is created if it does not exist.\n",
    269 =>
        "# Type System and Static Semantics — M1 subset\n\nStatus: normative for Milestone 1 (increment 1b implements it). Later milestones extend these\nrules. Where `language-spec.md` describes a later feature, it does not apply until then. Error\ncodes are listed in `diagnostics.md`.\n\n## 1. Types\n\n| Type | Values | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `i8 i16 i32 i64` | two's complement, −2^(n−1) … 2^(n−1)−1 | |\n| `u8 u16 u32 u64` | 0 … 2^n−1 | |\n| `isize` / `usize` | pointer width of the **target** (64 on every M1 target) | from `TargetInfo`, never the host |\n| `bool` | `true`, `false` | |\n| `void` | no value | function return type only |\n| *never* | no value; the expression does not complete | internal only (`Process.exit`); not nameable |\n\nNot in M1 (error N0216): floats, `char`, strings as values, user-defined types, references. Floats came in M11\n(`f32`, `f64`, §35); character literals are integers (§4).\nIn M1 a string literal was allowed **only** as a direct argument of `Console.write`/`writeLine`;\nsince M6c it is a `StringView` value (§22.2).\n\n`void` may appear only as a return type (writing `-> void` is the same as omitting the\nreturn type). A *never* expression is accepted wherever any type is expected.\n\n## 2. Names and scopes\n\nA **package** is the unit of compilation and of naming (ADR 0013, `tooling.md` §4): its files, its dependencies, and\n`lib/core`. A build checks the package being built together with every package it depends on, directly or not.\n\n- **Package scope.** All files of a package share one item scope. Items are referenced by simple name. Two items of\n  one package with the same name are an error (N0201), even in different files. `namespace` declarations are accepted\n  and have no effect (retired by ADR 0013; they will become an error).\n- **Dependencies** (M10 T5). A package names its dependencies in its manifest (`tooling.md` §4). A dependency's\n  **public** items are reached by qualifying them with the dependency's name:\n  - in a type, `geometry.Point`, `geometry.Pair<u8>`;\n  - in an expression, `geometry.area(p)`, `geometry.LIMIT`, `geometry.Shape.Circle(2)`, a struct literal\n    `geometry.Point { x: 1, y: 2 }`; in a pattern, `geometry.Shape.Circle(r)`.\n\n  A static function of a generic type of a dependency is called through an import (`Pair<u8>.make()`), since\n  `geometry.Pair<u8>.make()` does not parse (grammar.md §2.1: in an expression, generic arguments follow a simple\n  name only).\n- **Dependency names are reserved.** In a package, nothing else may have the name of one of its dependencies: no\n  item, parameter, local, loop variable or pattern binding (N0315). So `geometry.x`, with `geometry` a dependency,\n  always means the dependency's item, and the compiler resolves it before type checking (`PackageNames`).\n- **Imports.** `import geometry;` at the top of a file brings every public item of the dependency `geometry` into\n  that file's scope; `import geometry.Point;` brings one (N0311 if it has none of that name, N0312 if it is not\n  public). An import applies to its file only. The name before the `.` must be a dependency of the package (N0310).\n- **Visibility between packages.** Only `public` items are visible to other packages, and of a public struct only its\n  `public` fields and methods; `internal`, the default, keeps an item within its package, and `private` within its\n  file (§12). Naming another package's non-public item is N0312. A public function, constant, field or struct whose\n  signature names a type its dependents cannot see (a public function returning an internal struct) is N0314. An\n  `impl I for T` applies wherever `I` and `T` are both visible.\n- **Built-in scope** (outside the package scope, so a user item hides a built-in): `Console.write`,\n  `Console.writeLine`, `Process.exit`, `truncate`, and the primitive type names.\n- **`lib/core`** is in scope in every package, unqualified, and no package may redeclare one of its names (N0201,\n  §29.2).\n- **Function scope.** Parameters, t",
    270 =>
        "hen nested block scopes. A local is visible from the end of\n  its declaration to the end of its block.\n- **No shadowing of locals.** Declaring a local or parameter whose name is already a local or\n  parameter in scope is an error (N0203), as in C#. Shadowing an *item* with a local is allowed.\n- **Lookup order** for a simple name: locals (innermost first), the package's items, the file's imports, `lib/core`,\n  built-ins. Two imports that both provide a name are an error where the name is used (N0313), not where it is\n  imported. For `a.b`: if `a` is a local or an item of the package or of `lib/core`, `.b` is a member as before;\n  otherwise, `a` being a dependency (and so not a local or item, N0315), `a.b` is that dependency's item `b`.\n- **Messages and C names** write an item of a dependency with its package's name, `geometry.Point`; the C backend\n  gives it a prefixed name, `nvT_geometry_Point` (`c-backend.md` §2.1).\n\n## 3. Bindings and mutability\n\n- `let x = e;` declares an immutable local and **requires** an initialiser (N0220).\n- `var x = e;` / `var x: T = e;` / `var x: T;` declare a mutable local. Without an initialiser\n  the type is required (N0221), and the variable must be definitely assigned before every read (§7).\n- Without an annotation, a local's type is the type of its initialiser. An initialiser of type\n  `void` is an error (N0224).\n- Parameters and `const` items are immutable. Assigning to them, or to a `let`, is N0211.\n- Assignment targets in M1 are locals only. Member targets (`a.b = …`) are N0216.\n\n## 4. Integer literals\n\n- A suffixed literal (`7u8`) has the suffix's type.\n- An unsuffixed literal takes the **expected type** from context (§5) if that type is an\n  integer type. With no integer expectation it defaults to `i32`.\n- The value must be representable in the literal's type (N0206). A literal directly under\n  unary minus is checked as a negative value, so `-128i8` and `-9223372036854775808` (as `i64`) are valid.\n- **Character literals** (M11, G4a). `'k'` is an integer literal whose value is the character's Unicode scalar value\n  (`grammar.md` §1.6, escapes included: `'\\n'` is 10). It takes its type from context like an unsuffixed literal,\n  so `c == 'k'` with `c: u8` compares bytes, and `'0'..'9'` is a range. With no integer expectation it is a `u32`.\n  In an 8-bit type it must be ASCII (at most `0x7F`), since a larger scalar value is more than one byte in UTF-8 and\n  would not equal any byte of the text (N0206). It is a pattern too (§15.2). Nova has no `char` type: text is UTF-8\n  bytes (§22.2), and a character type is left for when text gains character iteration.\n\n## 5. Expected types (bidirectional checking)\n\nEach expression is checked against an optional expected type:\n\n- local initialiser → the annotated type; assignment value → the target's type;\n  argument → the parameter type; `return` value → the return type; `const` value → its\n  declared type; `if`/`while` condition → `bool`; each value branch of an `if` or `match` expression → the\n  expression's expected type (§15.3).\n- An expression's *actual* type must equal the expected type, unless it is *never* (N0205).\n- **Flexible operands.** An expression is *flexible* if it consists only of unsuffixed integer\n  literals combined with unary `-`, parentheses and the arithmetic/bitwise binary operators, or (M11) if it is an\n  `if` or `match` expression whose expression branches are all flexible (§15.3). Since M11 (G7), unsuffixed float\n  literals combined the same way are flexible too; they take an expected `f32` or `f64` instead (§35.2).\n  For a binary operator whose operands must have the same type:\n  1. if the operator's result type is the operand type and an integer type is expected, both\n     operands are checked against it;\n  2. otherwise, if exactly one operand is flexible, the other operand is checked first\n     (without expectation) and its type becomes the flexible operand's expected type;\n  3. otherwise both are check",
    271 =>
        "ed without expectation (flexible ones default to `i32`).\n\n  So `x + 1` with `x: u8` types `1` as `u8`, and `let y: u64 = 2 * 3;` types both literals as `u64`.\n\n## 6. Operators\n\nAll binary operators require both operands to have the **same type**. There are no implicit\nconversions.\n\n| Operators | Operand types | Result | Runtime behaviour (implemented in 1c) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `+ - *` | integer | same | overflow → panic |\n| `/ %` | integer | same | zero divisor → panic. `MIN / -1` → panic (overflow). `/` truncates toward zero, `%` has the sign of the dividend, and `MIN % -1` is `0` |\n| `& \\| ^` | **unsigned** integer | same | |\n| `<< >>` | left: **unsigned** integer; right: any integer type | left type | amount `< 0` or `≥ bit width` → panic. `>>` is logical |\n| `== !=` | integer or `bool` | `bool` | |\n| `< <= > >=` | integer | `bool` | |\n| `&& \\|\\|` | `bool` | `bool` | short-circuit |\n| unary `-` | **signed** integer | same | `-MIN` → panic |\n| unary `!` | `bool` | `bool` | |\n\n**Explicit overflow behaviour** (M11, G4b; OD-6). The operators panic on overflow. Where a program wants another\nbehaviour, every integer type has nine methods. Each takes one argument of the receiver's type, and a flexible\nliteral adapts:\n\n| Method | Result | On overflow |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x.wrappingAdd(y)`, `x.wrappingSub(y)`, `x.wrappingMul(y)` | `T` | the result modulo 2^bits, in two's complement for signed types |\n| `x.checkedAdd(y)`, `x.checkedSub(y)`, `x.checkedMul(y)` | `T?` | `null` (otherwise `Some` of the result) |\n| `x.saturatingAdd(y)`, `x.saturatingSub(y)`, `x.saturatingMul(y)` | `T` | the nearest of `T`'s minimum and maximum |\n\nNone of them panics. Their names are reserved on integer types: an `impl` for an integer type cannot define a method\nwith one of these names (N0228). In C they are `static inline` helpers of `rt.h` with no undefined behaviour: a\nwrapping operation computes in the unsigned type of the same width and converts back with `nv_as_iN`\n(`c-backend.md` §6).\n\nBitwise operators and shifts are restricted to unsigned types in M1. This avoids the\nimplementation-defined and undefined parts of C's signed bit operations. Reinterpret\nwith `truncate<u32>(x)`. Relaxing this later is backward compatible. A shift amount that is a\nflexible literal is typed as `u32`. Other mismatches are N0207.\n\n## 7. Conversions\n\n- `e as T` is allowed only when **every** value of the source type is representable in `T`\n  (N0208 otherwise):\n  - identity;\n  - `uN → uM` and `iN → iM` for M ≥ N;\n  - `uN → iM` for M > N;\n  - `bool →` any integer type (`false`→0, `true`→1).\n  `isize`/`usize` use the target width. Floats (M11, §35.4): `f32 → f64`, and the integers that a float type holds\n  exactly.\n- `truncate<T>(e)`: `e` of any integer type, `T` any integer type. The result is `e`\n  modulo 2^bits(T), reinterpreted in `T` (two's complement). It is always defined. Use it for\n  narrowing and for signed↔unsigned reinterpretation.\n\n## 8. Calls and statements\n\n- A callee must be a package function, or one of the built-ins below (N0210 otherwise).\n  Functions are not values in M1 (since M11 they are, §36.1). The argument count must match (N0209), and each argument is\n  checked against its parameter type.\n- Built-ins:\n  - `Console.write(x)` and `Console.writeLine(x)`: `x` is an integer, a `bool`, or a string\n    literal. `writeLine()` with no argument writes a newline. Both return `void`.\n  - `Process.exit(code: i32)`: has type *never*.\n  - `truncate<T>(e)`: see §7. Exactly one type argument (N0216 for other generic arguments).\n- An expression statement must be a call (N0225). `1 + 2;` is rejected.\n- `break`/`continue` must be inside `while` or `loop` (N0214).\n- `return;` only in a `void` function. `return e;` only in a non-`void` function (N0215).\n\n## 9. Control flow facts\n\n- **Definite assignment.** A local may be read only if it is assigned on every path from its\n  declaration to the read (N0212). Compound assignment (`x += 1`) r",
    272 =>
        "eads `x`. Code that is\n  unreachable is not checked. M1 has no moves, so the analysis is a forward must-analysis\n  over the structured statements; it moves to NIR in M2.\n- **Termination.** A statement *may complete* unless it is a `return`, `break` or\n  `continue`, or a call of type *never*. It also may not complete if it is a block containing\n  such a statement, an `if` with an `else` whose two branches both cannot complete, or a `loop`\n  with no `break` that targets it. A `while` loop may always complete, whatever its condition:\n  write `loop` for infinite loops. An `if` or `match` expression (§15.3) cannot complete when none of its\n  branches can: its condition or scrutinee does not complete, or every branch is a block that cannot complete.\n- **Missing return.** If the end of a non-`void` function body may be reached, that is N0213.\n\n## 10. Constants\n\n- `const N: T = e;` where `e` is a constant expression: literals, other constants, and unary,\n  binary, `as` and `truncate` applied to constant expressions. Calls are not constant (N0217).\n- Constants are evaluated at compile time with the runtime semantics of §6. An operation that\n  would panic at runtime (overflow, division by zero, bad shift) is a compile error (N0217).\n- A constant that depends on itself is N0218.\n- Uses of a constant are replaced by its value, typed as the constant's declared type.\n- **Constant arrays** (C9, ADR 0014): `T` may be `[E; N]` with `E` an integer type or `bool`, and `e` an array\n  literal `[e1, …, eN]` or repeat `[v; N]` of constant expressions. A use is the whole array's value, like an\n  array literal: it can be indexed and iterated, and bound to a local to be sliced or borrowed. Arrays of other\n  types, and of arrays, are N0216.\n- `@export` on a constant makes it C data (§28.4).\n\n## 11. `main`\n\nIf a function named `main` exists, it must take no parameters and return `i32` or\n`void` (N0219). Whether `main` is *required* is decided by `novac build` (increment 1c).\n\n## 12. Structs (M2a)\n\n```nova\nstruct Point {\n    x: i64;\n    y: i64;\n\n    fn sum(this) -> i64 => this.x + this.y;                    // method: `this` by value\n    fn origin() -> Point => Point { x: 0, y: 0 };             // static function\n}\n```\n\n- **Declaration.** Top level only. Field names and member names (fields and functions) are unique\n  within the struct (N0228). Field types are any non-`void` M1 type or another struct. A struct\n  must not contain itself by value, directly or through other structs (N0227: infinite size).\n  Recursive data needs indirection (`Box`, M3). Empty structs are allowed.\n- **Names.** A struct name is a package item (unique with functions and constants, N0201) and a\n  type name. In expressions, `S.f(...)` calls the static function `f` of `S`.\n- **Copy.** In M2a every struct is a Copy type: its fields are Copy and `drop` does not exist yet.\n  Assignment, argument passing and return copy the value. OD-7's rule (\"Copy iff all fields Copy\n  and no `drop`\") takes effect in 2c.\n- **Literals.** `S { f1: e1, …, fn: en }` must initialise **every** field exactly once (missing:\n  N0229, unknown: N0230, duplicate: N0231), in any order. Each `ei` is checked against the field's\n  type. Initialisers are **evaluated in source order** (`intermediate-representation.md`), not in\n  declaration order.\n- **Field access.** `e.f` where `e` has struct type `S` and `f` is a field of `S` has `f`'s type\n  (unknown field: N0230; `e` is not a struct: N0234). An access chain `a.b.c` is a *place* if `a`\n  is a local or `this`.\n- **Field assignment.** `a.f = e` and `a.f op= e` require the root local `a` to be a `var` (N0211).\n  `this` is immutable in a by-value method. The root must be definitely assigned *as a whole*\n  first (N0212): M2a has no partial initialisation.\n- **Methods.** A function in a struct whose first parameter is `this` is a method. `this` has the\n  struct's type and is an immutable binding (a copy for Copy structs). It is called as `e.m(args)`,\n  where `e` i",
    273 =>
        "s evaluated first and passed as `this`. A static function (no `this`) is called as\n  `S.f(args)`. Calling a method as `S.m()`, or a static function as `e.f()`, is N0233. `this`\n  anywhere other than a method's receiver position or body is N0232.\n- **Private fields (C7c).** A field declared `private` (`private data: T;`) is accessible only in the\n  source file that declares its struct (`language-spec.md` §5: \"visible within the declaring type or\n  file\"), and in any instance of a generic one there: read, assigned, borrowed and given in a literal.\n  Anywhere else, naming it is N0296. A struct with private fields can therefore only be made by code in\n  its own file, so types that cooperate, like a lock and its guard, can share their fields. This is the one place where a visibility\n  modifier takes effect within a package (§2). It lets a type keep an invariant that safe code cannot break,\n  such as \"the data is reached only through a guard\" (`integrations/cosmoos/c7/sync.nova`).\n  `public` and `internal` fields, and the default, stay visible throughout the package.\n- **Operators.** No operator applies to struct values in M2a (`==` included: N0207).\n  `Console.write` cannot print structs (N0207).\n- **Layout.** Fields are laid out in declaration order with C's rules (`@repr(C)` by default,\n  `memory-model.md` §2). The compiler computes size, alignment and field offsets from `TargetInfo`.\n  The generated C checks them with `_Static_assert` (`c-backend.md` §2), so a disagreement with the\n  real C compiler fails the build instead of miscompiling.\n\n## 13. References (M2b)\n\nThe algorithm that enforces these rules is in `lifetimes.md`.\n\n### 13.1 Types\n- `borrow T` (shared) and `borrow mut T` (exclusive), where `T` is any non-reference, non-`void`\n  type. `borrow borrow T` is rejected (N0246). At runtime a reference is a non-null pointer.\n- **Where references may appear (v0):**\n  - parameter types;\n  - `let` locals with an initialiser;\n  - return types (with a source list, §13.5).\n\n  They may **not** appear in struct fields (E3), constants, or `var` locals (N0246). Struct\n  fields of reference type need lifetime parameters (OD-3).\n- `borrow T` is Copy. A `borrow mut T` value is never copied: every use reborrows it (§13.3).\n\n### 13.2 Creating references\n- `borrow p` / `borrow mut p`, where `p` is a **place**: a local, `this`, a field path, or a path\n  through a reference such as `r.f` (`(*r).f`). Borrowing anything else is N0247; bind the value\n  to a local first.\n- `borrow mut p` requires `p` to be mutable: rooted at a `var` local, or reached through a\n  `borrow mut` reference (N0248). A `let` binding, a parameter, a by-value `this`, and anything\n  reached through a shared reference are immutable. That holds through a `borrow mut` or\n  `SliceMut` stored where a shared reference leads: in `h.v` with `h: borrow Holder` and\n  `v: borrow mut Vector<T>`, the vector cannot be changed, as with Rust's `& &mut T`.\n- **Call sites.**\n  - A `borrow T` parameter accepts a place or value of type `T`, borrowed implicitly. A value\n    becomes a temporary that lives until the end of the statement.\n  - A `borrow T` parameter also accepts `borrow T` or `borrow mut T`; the latter is coerced by a\n    shared reborrow.\n  - A `borrow mut T` parameter accepts `mut p`, where `p` is a mutable place of type `T`, or a\n    `borrow mut T` value (reborrowed).\n\n  The `mut` marker is required for a plain place and is rejected where the parameter is not\n  `borrow mut` (N0249). It makes mutation visible at the call site, as C#'s `ref` does.\n- **Receivers.** `fn m(borrow this)` and `fn m(borrow mut this)` declare reference receivers;\n  `this` then has type `borrow S` / `borrow mut S`. In `e.m(…)` the receiver is borrowed\n  implicitly, with no marker. For `borrow mut this`, `e` must be a mutable place or a\n  `borrow mut S` value (N0248).\n\n### 13.3 Using references\n- **Auto-deref.** Where a value of type `T` is needed (an operand, a by-value argument, an\n  initialiser o",
    274 =>
        "f type `T`, a return of type `T`, `Console.write`), a `borrow T` or\n  `borrow mut T` operand is read through: `*r`. Field access `r.f` and method calls\n  `r.m()` also look through references.\n- **Writes through references.** `r = v`, `r op= v` and `r.f = v` write to the referent. This\n  requires `borrow mut` (N0211: \"cannot assign through a shared reference\"). A reference local\n  itself can never be re-pointed.\n- **Reborrow of `borrow mut`.** Using a `borrow mut T` value as a call argument, an initialiser\n  or a return value creates a new mutable loan of `*r`. The original reference is unusable while\n  the new one is live, and usable again afterwards (B4).\n  This holds for a field of type `borrow mut T` or `SliceMut<T>` (`s.left`) as for a variable: it is\n  reborrowed, not moved out of its struct, and inside a closure it is a mutable use of the struct.\n  Its referent must be mutable from where it is used (§13.2): a field reached through a shared\n  reference cannot be reborrowed mutably (N0248).\n- References support no operators of their own: `==` compares the referents (via auto-deref).\n\n### 13.4 Borrow rules (summary of `lifetimes.md`)\nWhile a loan is live (the reference, or something derived from it, is used later), the borrowed\nplace may not be:\n- written, mutably borrowed, or go out of scope, if the loan is shared;\n- accessed at all, if the loan is `borrow mut`.\n\nDiagnostics: N0240 (write), N0241 (conflicting borrow), N0242 (read while mutably borrowed),\nN0243 (out of scope or temporary dropped while borrowed).\n\n### 13.5 Returning references\n- A function returning `borrow T` / `borrow mut T` with exactly one reference parameter (counting\n  a reference receiver) returns something derived from that parameter.\n- Otherwise the return type names the sources: `fn longer(a: borrow T, b: borrow T) ->\n  borrow(a, b) T`. Missing, unknown or non-reference sources are N0245, and so is a\n  `borrow mut` result naming a shared parameter. After `borrow`, a `(` begins a source list only when it holds\n  names separated by commas and a type follows the `)`; otherwise it begins a parenthesised type (M11, §22.1), so\n  `borrow (T?)` is a reference to an optional and `borrow(a) (T?)` names a source.\n- The body may only return references derived from the named parameters (N0245). A reference to\n  a local or temporary of the function is N0244.\n\n## 14. Move types, destruction, `using` and `defer` (M2c)\n\nNormative for 2c. It implements `ownership.md` O1–O5 and R3. The NIR analyses are described in\n`intermediate-representation.md`.\n\n### 14.1 Copy and move types (OD-7)\n- **Copy:** integers, `bool`, and `borrow T`. A struct is Copy iff it has no `drop` block and every\n  field is Copy.\n- **Move types:** structs with a `drop` block or a move-type field, and `borrow mut T` (whose uses\n  are reborrows, §13.3, so in practice it is never moved out of a variable).\n- **Needs drop:** a struct with a `drop` block, or one with a field that needs drop. Every type that\n  needs drop is a move type. The converse does not hold.\n\n### 14.2 `drop` blocks\n```nova\nstruct File {\n    fd: i32;\n    drop { Sys.close(this.fd); }      // `this` is `borrow mut File`\n}\n```\n- At most one `drop` block per struct (N0228). Inside it, `this` has type `borrow mut S`. The block\n  cannot be called explicitly. It runs exactly once when a value of `S` is destroyed (O4), and the\n  fields that need drop are destroyed **after** it, in declaration order.\n- Moving a field out of a value whose type has a `drop` block is N0252: the `drop` block needs the\n  whole value. Moving out of `this` inside `drop` is impossible anyway (§14.3).\n\n### 14.3 Moves\n- **Using a move-type place by value moves it**:\n  - an initialiser or right-hand side;\n  - a by-value argument or a by-value `this` receiver;\n  - a struct-literal field;\n  - a `return` value.\n\n  The place is then uninitialised. Reading, borrowing or moving it again before it is reassigned is\n  **N0250** (\"use of moved value\"); this is O2 and O3.\n-",
    275 =>
        " **`move p`** explicitly moves out of the place `p`, even for a Copy type. Afterwards `p` is\n  uninitialised, which ends a value's use early.\n- **Partial moves** (O2): moving `p.f` out of a local is allowed if no struct on the path from\n  `p` to `f` has a `drop` block. `p` is then partially initialised. `p.f` may be reassigned, after\n  which `p` is whole again. Reading `p` as a whole, or borrowing it, needs all of it initialised\n  (N0250).\n- **Not allowed:**\n  - moving out of a place reached through a reference (N0251), because the referent must stay\n    valid; this includes `this` in `borrow`/`borrow mut` methods;\n  - moving out of a `using` binding (N0253).\n- **Moves and borrows:** moving a place while it is borrowed is a conflict, treated like a write\n  (N0240).\n\n### 14.4 Initialisation (replaces §9's first bullet)\nThe analysis runs on NIR move paths (`intermediate-representation.md`):\n- A place must be **definitely initialised** to be read, borrowed or moved. If it may never have\n  been assigned, that is N0212. If it may have been moved, that is N0250.\n- Assigning a field `p.f` requires the rest of `p` to be initialised (no piecewise construction\n  of an uninitialised struct). Re-initialising a moved-out field is allowed.\n  - If some enclosing struct of `p.f` has a `drop` block, that whole struct must be initialised,\n    `f` included: such a struct is never partially moved (N0252), so a missing field means the\n    whole value was moved. This also covers a struct whose only field is `f` (found by the\n    mutation suite, M2d).\n\n### 14.5 Destruction (O4)\n- A value that needs drop is destroyed exactly once, at the first of these:\n  - the end of its variable's scope: the block end, a `break`/`continue` leaving the block, or a\n    `return`;\n  - the end of its statement, for a temporary. An `if` or `while` condition is its own statement:\n    its temporaries are destroyed once it is evaluated, before the body runs, on every evaluation\n    (M15 S2; before M15 they were never destroyed, #1);\n  - being **overwritten** by an assignment, in which case the old value is destroyed first.\n- **Order:** locals in reverse declaration order; then a struct's `drop` block; then its fields in\n  declaration order. By-value parameters are destroyed when the function returns, after the locals.\n- **Moved values are not destroyed.** A value that has been moved on *some* paths gets a runtime\n  **drop flag**. A partially moved struct destroys only the fields it still has.\n- **`Process.exit` and panics do not run destructors or deferred statements** (panic = abort,\n  OD-5).\n\n### 14.6 `using`\n`using x = e;` declares a local that cannot be moved out of (N0253) and is destroyed at the end\nof its block, like any local (R3). It documents that the value's lifetime *is* the scope: a lock\nguard, an arena, a file.\n\n*Since M4b:* a `using` binding is **pinned, not immutable**. Its contents may be changed and it may\nbe borrowed mutably (`arena.reset()`, `frame.enter()`). It cannot be assigned as a whole (N0211)\nor moved (N0253). This also lets scoped values that must be mutably borrowed, such as scratch\nframes, live in a `using` binding, since they cannot be `var`.\n\n### 14.7 `defer`\n`defer s;` schedules the statement `s` to run when the enclosing block exits on any path:\n- the block end;\n- `break`/`continue` out of it;\n- `return` (after the return value is computed).\n\nDeferred statements and local destructions run in reverse order of their position, so a `defer`\nruns before the locals declared before it are destroyed, and after the locals declared after it.\n`s` may not contain `return`, `break`, `continue` or another `defer` (N0254).\n\n## 15. Enums and `match` (M2d)\n\n```nova\nenum Shape {\n    Empty,\n    Circle(i64),\n    Rect(i64, i64),\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -> i64 {\n    match (s) {\n        Shape.Empty => { return 0; }\n        Shape.Circle(r) => { return 3 * r * r; }\n        Shape.Rect(w, h) => { return w * h; }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 15.1 Declarations and values\n- `enum E {",
    276 =>
        " V1, V2(T1, …), … }` at the top level. Variants are unique (N0228) and have zero or\n  more positional payload types: any non-reference, non-`void` type (E3 applies to payloads too).\n  An enum must not contain itself by value (N0227).\n- **Values.** `E.V` for a variant without payload; `E.V(e1, …)` with exactly the payload arity\n  (N0209). Each `ei` is checked against its payload type.\n- **Copy and drop.** An enum is Copy iff every payload type is Copy. It needs drop iff some payload\n  needs drop; destroying it destroys the payloads of the active variant. Enums have no `drop`\n  block (v0).\n- **Layout.**\n  - A tag (`u8` for ≤ 256 variants, else `u16`/`u32`), then a union of the variants' payloads, each\n    laid out like a struct.\n  - Size and alignment follow C's rules for `struct { tag; union { … }; }`. The generated C\n    asserts them.\n  - Nova does not reorder or niche-optimise (v0).\n\n### 15.2 `match`\n```text\nMatchStmt = 'match' '(' Expr ')' '{' Arm* '}'\nArm       = Pattern '=>' Block ','?\nPattern   = '_' | IntLiteral | '-' IntLiteral | 'true' | 'false' | 'null'\n          | QualifiedName ('(' Payload (',' Payload)* ')')?        -- E.V, E.V(x, _), Ok(E.A(x)) (M11)\nPayload   = Binding | Pattern                                      -- a nested pattern: M11 (G1)\nBinding   = Identifier | 'var' Identifier | '_'        -- 'var': M3c (§18.4)\n```\nA payload that is a single identifier is a binding; `Some(…)`, `Ok(…)`, `Err(…)` and `E.V…` there are patterns.\n- This section's `match` is a **statement**; §15.3 has the expression form. Arms are tried in order; the first\n  that matches runs its block.\n- **Scrutinee types:**\n  - an enum: variant patterns;\n  - an integer: literal patterns of that type;\n  - `bool`: `true`/`false`;\n  - or a reference to one of these.\n- **Exhaustiveness** (N0256): enum matches must cover every variant or end with `_`. Integer\n  matches need `_`. `bool` matches need both values or `_`. An arm that can never match (a\n  repeated variant or literal, or anything after `_`) is N0257.\n- **Bindings.** A variant pattern binds each named payload to a new immutable local in the arm:\n  - **Scrutinee of type `E` (a value):** the binding is a *copy* of the payload. Since M3c a\n    non-Copy payload is moved out, and the match consumes its scrutinee (§18.4).\n  - **Scrutinee of type `borrow E` / `borrow mut E`:** the binding is a `borrow T` /\n    `borrow mut T` to the payload, and the borrow checker treats it like any reborrow of the\n    scrutinee.\n  - `_` binds nothing.\n- The scrutinee is evaluated once. When it is a place it is not moved (only Copy payloads are\n  read). A temporary scrutinee lives until the end of the `match` statement.\n- **Nested patterns** (M11, G1). In a payload's position, a pattern instead of a binding matches the payload itself:\n  a variant pattern on an enum payload (`Ok(E.A(x))`, `Some(Some(_))`), a literal on an integer or `bool` payload\n  (`Some(3)`), or `null` on an optional payload (`Ok(null)`). Each is checked against the payload's type exactly as\n  a whole `match` is checked against the scrutinee's, at any depth.\n  - **Order.** Arms are tried in order. Within an arm, the outer variant is tested first, then each nested pattern,\n    left to right and depth first. Testing only reads tags and values: an arm that does not match moves nothing.\n  - **Bindings** inside nested patterns follow the rules above. On a value scrutinee they copy, or move (§18.4).\n    On a reference scrutinee they are references to the nested payload.\n  - **A payload that is itself a reference** (`borrow E`) takes only a binding or `_`, not a nested pattern. Matching\n    through it is a separate `match` on that reference (N0205 otherwise).\n  - **Exhaustiveness and unreachable arms** are decided over the whole patterns. A match is exhaustive when every\n    value of the scrutinee's type matches some arm: N0256, whose message names one value that matches none, such\n    as `Ok(E.B)`. An arm is unreachable when e",
